\mt1 The Book of Psalms.
\is Concerning interpretation of holie Scriptures.
\ip As Prophecie \it (or other holie Scripture)\it* was not at anie time by mans wil, but the \it (Prophetes)\it* holie men of God ſpake , inſpired by the Holie Ghoſt: ſo no prophecie \it (nor explication)\it* of Scripture, is \f - \ft Scriptures are to be expounded by the common ſpirit of the Church, not by priuate men.\f* made by priuate interpretation. \it 2. Pet. 1.\it* but by the ſame Spirite wherwith it was written, which our Sauiour gaue to the Church, to abide for euer, the Spirite of truth, to teach al truth. \it Ioan. 14. 16. Neither\it* perteyneth it to euerie one, to diſcerne the true ſpirite, but to ſome. \it 1. Cor. 12.\it*
\ip Holie Scriptures conſiſt not in reading, but in vnderſtanding. \it S. Ierom Dialogo aduers. Luciferianos.\it* The wordes of holie Scripture are ſo to be vnderſtood , as holie men, the Sainctes of God, haue \f - \ft They conſiſt in vnderſtanding.\f* vnderſtood them , \it S. Aug. Ser. 18. de verb. Domini.\it* Men muſt lerne of men, not expect \f - \ft Holy Fathers do beſt expound them.\f* knowlege immediatly of God, nor only by Angels. \it Idem, in Prologo Doct. Chriſt.\it*
\ip There be ſome thinges, mentioned in holie Scripture , which God wil haue hidden, and thoſe are \f - \ft Some Myſteries are hidden.\f* not to be curiouſly ſearched. \it S. Amb. li. 1. c. 7. de vocat. Gentium.\it*
\ip By thoſe thinges, which to vs are hidden in holie Scripture, our humilitie is proued . \it S. Greg. ho. 17.\it* \f - \ft They proue our humilitie.\f* \it ſuper Ezech.\it*
\is PROEMIAL ANNOTATIONS VPON THE BOOKE OF PSALMES.
\ip The authoritie of this Booke was euer authentical, and certaine, as the aſſured word of God, and Canonical Scripture . But concerning the Author, there be diuers opinions. For although it be \f - \ft This booke vndoubtedly is canonical Scripture.\f* manifeſt by the teſtimonie of Philo and Ioſephus, that in their time, and alwaies before, only King Dauid was by al Hebrew Doctors holden for Author of al the Pſalmes; yet after that lerned \f - \ft Late Hebrew Doctors and ſome Catholiques hold diuers Authors of ſundry Pſalmes.\f* Origen, and other Chriſtian Doctors, expounded manie Pſalmes of Chriſt, the Jews being preſſed therwith, begane to denie that al were Dauids: alleaging for their new opinion the title of diuers Pſalmes, and ſome other difficulties, miniſtring occaſion of much needles diſpute, ſtil acknowledging the whole booke to be Canonical. Wherupon S. Ierom and S. Auguſtin , \f - \ft \+it Prefat. in Pſal. Epiſt. 134. 139.\+it*\f* ſometimes admitted thoſe as Authores of certaine Pſalmes, whoſe names are in the title thereof. S. Cyprian, S. Cyril, S. Athanaſius, and others agree in general, that Dauid writte not al: but differ much in particular, touching other ſuppoſed authores. In ſo much that \it Melchiſedech, Moyſes, Aſaph, Eman, Idithun, The ſonnes of Core, Salomon, Ieremie, Ezechiel, Aggaeus, and Zacharias, are al\it* (with more or leſſe probabilitie) \it reputed Authores of ſeueral Pſalmes\it*. Neuertheles it ſemeth that S. \f - \ft It is much more probable that Dauid was Author of al.\f* Ierom rather related other mens iudgement, then shewed his owne; as we shal note by and by. And S. Auguſtin \it (l. 17. c. 14. de ciuit.)\it* maturely diſcuſſing this doubt, ſaith plainly, that \it their Iudgement ſemed to him more credible, who attribute al the hundred fiftie Pſalmes to Dauid alone\it*. Further explicating that wheras ſome Pſalmes haue Dauids owne name in their titles, ſome \f - \ft Proued by S. Auguſtin, S. Chryſoſtom and greateſt part of Doctors.\f* haue other mens names, ſome none at al, \it this diuerſitie importeth not diuers Authores, but ſignifieth other thinges, either perteyning to the ſame perſons, or by interpretation of their names, belonging to the preſent matter, as our Lord inſpired him\it*. Likewiſe S. Chryſoſtome reſolutely iudgeth, that only King Dauid was Author of this whole Booke. Moued eſpecially by this argument, for that Chriſt and the Apoſtles alleaging the Pſalmes, do oftentimes name Dauid as Author, and neuer anie other . \f - \ft \+it Matt. 22. v. 16. Act. 4. v. 24. Ro. 4. v. 6. Ro. 11. v. 9. Heb. 4. v. 7.\+it*\f* Alſo Origen, S. Baſil, Saint Ambroſe, S. Hilarie, Theodoretus, Caſſiodorus, Beda, Eutimius, and moſt part of ancient and late writers, with the moſt common voice of Chriſtians , cal this booke \f - \ft The common voice of Chriſtians & ſome general Councels, cal it Dauids Pſalter.\f* the Pſalmes of Dauid: and the General Councels of Carthage, Florence, and Trent, in the Catalogue of Canonical Scriptures recite this booke by the name of Dauids Pſalter.
\ip Moreouer it is clere, \it Act. 2.\it* that the ſecond Pſalme, though it want his name, is Dauids. And other Scriptures , \it 2. Paral. v. 6.\it* and \it 1. Eſd. 3. v. 10.\it* ſay plainly, that Dauid made the Pſalmes, \it 104. 105.\it* \f - \ft Proued by other Scriptures.\f* \it 106. 117. 135.\it* beginning: \it Confeſſe to our Lord, becauſe he is good, becauſe his mercie is for euer\it*. VVhich he appointed the Leuites to ſing, or play on inſtruments, \it 1. Paral. 15. & 16.\it* and yet they haue not his name in their title. Againe, \it 2. Reg. 23. Dauid\it* is only intitled \it the egregious\it*, or excellent \it Pſalmiſt of Iſrael\it*. Neither were Aſaph, Eman, and Idithun anie where called Prophetes (as are al the writers of holie Scriptures) but only maſters of muſike, \it 1. Paral. 25.\it* And the ſonnes of Core were only porters, \it 1. Paral. 26.\it* Finally, S. Ierom (whoſe iudgement the whole Church ſingularly eſtemeth in al queſtions belonging to holie Scriptures (ſemeth as much inclined, that only the Royal Prophet Dauid was Author of this whole booke, as to the contrarie . For in his Epiſtle to Paulinus, \f - \ft S. Ierom attributeth the ſumme of this booke to Dauid only. key of each Pſalm.\f* prefixed before the Latin Bible, comprehending the principal arguments of ſeueral bookes, when he cometh to the Pſalmes, without mention of other Authors, ſaith: \it Dauid our Simonides, Pindarus, and Alceus, Flaccus alſo, Catullus, and Cerenus, ſoundeth out Chriſt, with harpe & tenne ſtringed Pſalter, riſing vp from hel:\it* ſo attributing the ſumme of this whole booke to the Royal Prophet Dauid, as if he ſuppoſed no other Author.
\ip Touching therfore the argument or contentes of this diuine Pſalter, al Catholique Doctors \f - \ft The Pſalmes are a Summe of al other Scriptures.\f* vniformly agree that it is the abridgement, ſumme, and ſubſtance of al holie Scriptures, both old and new Teſtament. As may firſt be probably collected, by that Chriſt himſelfe often \f - \ft \+it Mat. 5. 7. 11. 22. Luc. 16.\+it*\f* comprehending al the old Teſtament by the termes of the Law and Prophetes, in one place \it (Luc. 24.\it*
\ip \it v. 44.)\it* ſemeth not onlie to reduce al to \it the Law and Prophetes iointly\it*, but alſo to \it the Pſalmes alone\it*, or ſeuerally. But whether this be our Sauiours diuine meaning or no in that place, out of this and manie other places, al the ancient Fathers teach expreſly, that the Pſalmes are an Epitome of al other holie Scriptures. For examples, S. Denys. \it li. de Eccles. Hierar. contemplat. 2.\it* after brief recital of the contents of other holie Scriptures, ſaith: \it This ſacred booke of diuine Canticles, doth exhibite both a general ſong, and expoſition of diuine thinges\it*. S. Baſil calleth the \it Pſalmodie of Dauid the common and moſt plentiful ſtore-houſe of al ſacred doctrine, the treaſure of perfect Theologie\it*. S. Ambroſe accounteth it \it The regiſter of the whole Scripture\it*. Origen, S. Cyprian, S. Ierom, S. Chryſoſtom, \f - \ft \+it S. Greg. in Pſal. penitent.\+it*\f* S. Gregorie, S. Beda, S. Bernard, Caſſiodorus, Eutimius, and others vſe the ſame, or very like termes. S. Auguſtin particularly diſtinguishing al the Scriptures into foure ſortes of bookes, sheweth that the Pſalmes contain al : \it The Law\it* (ſaith he) \it teacheth ſome things, the Hiſtorie ſome things, the\it* \f - \ft They conteine the ſumme of Legal, Hiſtorical, Sapiential, and Prophetical doctrine.\f* \it Prouerbes alſo and Prophetes teach ſome things: but the Booke of Pſalmes teacheth al. It propoſeth the law, recounteth thinges of old, preſcribeth the due ordering of mens actions, and prophecieth thinges to come. Briefly, it is a common treaſure of good doctrine, aptly adminiſtring that is neceſſarie to euerie one\it*. And a litle after, exemplifying in particular points: \it Is not here\it* (ſaith he) \it al greatnes of vertue, and is not here the right ſquare of iuſtice? Is not the comlines of chaſtitie, the conſummation of prudence, is not whatſoeuer may be called good, lerned in the Pſalmes? Here is the knowlege of God, the clere pronunciation of Chriſt to come in flesh, the hope of general Reſurrection, feare of torments, promiſe of glorie, reuelation of myſteries. Euen al good thinges are here, as in a common great treaſure laide vp and heaped together\it*.
\ip See then and obſerue here (Chriſtian reader) the admirable wiſdom and goodnes of God . The \f - \ft Gods prouidence in ſweetly drawing our conſent & cooperation of freewill, which is neceſſarie to ſaluation.\f* meanes of mans ſaluation being ſo diſpoſed, that his owne free conſent and cooperation is therto neceſſarily required, according to that moſt approued doctrin of the ſame S. Auguſtin: \it Qui creauit te ſine te, non iuſtificat te ſine te . He that created thee without thee, doth not iuſtifie thee\it* \f - \ft \+it Ser. 15. de verb. Apoſt.\+it*\f* \it without thee\it*: to helpe our weaknes, and ſweetly to draw our mindes, otherwiſe auerſe from travail and paine, the Holie Ghoſt hath ordained that in ſmal room , and in pleaſant maner, we may \f - \ft \+it S. Baſil in prolog.\+it*\f* attaine neceſſarie knowlege of God, & ourſelves, eaſily kepe the ſame in memorie, and dayly put in practiſe our chiefeſt dutie, in ſeruing and praiſing God, by ſinging, reading, or hearing theſe diuine Pſalmes. Which one booke (as euerie one shal be able to lerne it, more or leſſe perfectly) openeth and sheweth the way to vnderſtand al other Scriptures, and ſo to finde, & enioy the hidden treaſures of Gods word, in like maner as a key openeth a lock. For the whole ſacred \it Bible is a ſealed Booke\it*, \f - \ft Holie Scripture a ſealed booke.\f* and not rightly vnderſtood, til the ſeale , or lock be opened, by the key of Gods ſpirite, giving \f - \ft \+it Apoc. 5.\+it*\f* knowlege; which the Holie Ghoſt, amongſt other wayes, inſpireth very often by ſacred Muſike or Pſalmodie. As S. Gregorie noteth in holie Scripture \it (4. Reg. 3. v. 15.)\it* where Eliſeus not yet \f - \ft \+it li. 4. dialogi. c. 42.\+it*\f* \f - \ft The Pſalter is the key of other Scriptures.\f* knowing Gods wil in a particular caſe, called for a Pſalmiſt (or player on inſtruments) and \it when the Pſalmiſt ſang, the hand of our Lord came vpon Eliſeus\it*, and preſently he preſcribing what should be donne, procured plentie of water without rayne, where was none before, and prophecied victorie againſt the enimies. Reaſon alſo and experience teach, that as \it men of cheerful hart are apt to ſing\it*, ſo the exerciſe of reading, ſinging, or playing Pſalmes, is a conuenient and a ſpecial meanes, to \f - \ft \+it Iac. 5.\+it*\f* attaine quietnes or cheerfulnes of mind.
\ip But as this holie Pſalter is the key of other Scriptures, ſo itſelf is moſt eſpecially a ſealed, and locked Booke , requiring manie keyes. \it Euerie Pſalme\it* (ſaith S. Hilarie) \it hath a peculiar key, and\it* \f - \ft But itſelf is alſo ſealed.\f* \it oftentimes there be ſo manie lockes and keyes of one Pſalme, as there be diuers perſons that ſpeake, to diuers endes and purpoſes\it*. For albeit diuers myſteries are ſometimes connected , and ſo require \f - \ft But one principal \+it merit.\+it*\f* ſundrie keyes, yet there is but one principal & proper key of ech Pſalme: otherwiſe it should be diuided into manie Pſalmes. Our firſt endeuour therfore muſt be, to find the proper key of euery Pſalme, that is, to know what is principally therein conteyned. To this purpoſe the lerned \f - \ft Tenne keyes of the Pſalter.\f* Expoſiters of this booke, haue obſerued tenne general pointes, or ſeueral matters, to which al the contents may be reduced, as it were, ſo manie keyes, and meanes of entrance into the ſenſe, and true vnderſtanding of al the Pſalmes. And the ſame may likewiſe be called the tenne ſtringes of this diuine inſtrument . Vpon one of which, euery Pſalme principally playeth, touching the reſt more or \f - \ft Alſo tenne ſtringes.\f* leſſe, as cauſe requireth, for more melodious harmonie and perfect muſike.
\ip The firſt key or ſtring is God himſelf , One in Subſtance, Three in Perſons, Almightie, \f - \ft 1. Key. One God the B. Trinitie.\f* All-perfect, Power, VViſdom, Goodnes, Maieſtie, Iuſtice, Mercie, & other Diuine Attributes. The ſecond is Gods workes of Creation , Conſeruation, and Gouerning of the whole world. The \f - \ft 2. Gods workes.\f* third, Gods Prouidence , eſpecially towards man, in protecting and rewarding the iuſt, in \f - \ft 3. Gods prouidence.\f* permitting and punishing the wicked. The fourth, is the peculiar calling of the Hebrew people , \f - \ft 4. The Hebrew people.\f* their beginning in Abraham, Iſaac, and Iacob: their maruelous increaſe in Ægypt: diuers eſtates, manie admirable and miraculous thinges donne amongſt them, with their ingratitude, reiection, and reprobation. The fifth principal key and ſtring is Chriſt , the promiſed Redemer of mankind: \f - \ft 5. Chriſt our Redemer.\f* prophecying his Incarnation, Natiuitie, Travails, Sufferings, Death, Reſurrection, Aſcenſion, and Glorie. The ſixt is the propagation of Chriſts name and Religion , with Sacrifice and \f - \ft 6. Conuerſion of Gentiles, the Catholique Church.\f* Sacraments, in the multitude of Gentiles beleeuing in him, euen to the vttermoſt coaſtes of the earth: the Catholique Church euer viſible. The ſeuenth is the true maner of ſeruing God , with ſincere \f - \ft 7. Faith & good workes.\f* faith and good workes. The eight, holie Dauid interpoſeth manie thinges concerning himſelfe . As \f - \ft 8. Dauids owne actes.\f* Gods ſingular benefites towards him, for which he rendereth thankes and diuine praiſes, recounteth his enimies, dangers, and afflictions of mind & bodie, namely by Saul, Abſalon, and others, humbly beſeeching and obtaining Gods protection. He alſo expreſſeth in himſelfe a perfect image and patterne of a ſincere and hearty penitent: bewayling, confeſſing, and punishing his owne ſinnes. The ninth is the end and renouation of this world , with the general Reſurrection, and Iudgement. \f - \ft 9. General Reſurrection, & Iudgement.\f* The tenth is eternal felicitie, and punishment, according as euerie one deſerueth in this life . \f - \ft 10. Eternal glory and paine.\f* Theſe are the tenne keyes of this holie Booke; and tenne ſtringes of this Diuine Pſalter.
\ip Moreouer to finde which of theſe is the proper key and principal ſtring of euerie Pſalme , lerned \f - \ft Foure wayes to find the proper key of euerie pſalme.\f* Diuines vſe foure eſpecial wayes. Firſt , by the title added by Eſdras, or the Seuentie two \f - \ft 1. By the title.\f* Interpreters, for an introduction to the ſenſe of the ſame Pſalme. So it appeareth that the third Pſalme treateth literally of Dauids danger, and deliuerie from his ſonne Abſalon: which is the eighth key: though myſtically it ſignifieth Chriſts Perſecution, & Reſurrection, which is the fifth key. Secondly , if there be no title, or if it declare not ſufficiently they key, or principal matter \f - \ft 2. Allegation in the new Teſtament.\f* conteyned, it may ſometimes be found by allegation and application of ſome ſpecial part thereof in the new Teſtament. So it is euident \it Act. 4. v. 25. c. 13. v. 33. Heb. 1. v. 5. & Heb. 5. v. 5.\it* that the ſecond Pſalme perteyneth to Chriſt, impugned and perſecuted by diuers aduerſaries. VVhich is the fift key. Thirdly , when greater thinges are affirmed of anie perſon, or people, as of Dauid, \f - \ft 3. Greatnes of thinges affirmed.\f* Salomon, the Iewish nation, or the like, then can be verified of them, it muſt neceſſarily be vnderſtood of Chriſt, or his Church, in the new Teſtament or in Heauen. So the \it concluſion\it* of the \it 14. Pſalme: He that doth theſe thinges, shal not be moued for euer\it*, cannot be verified of the tabernacle, nor temple of the Jews, but of eternal Beatitude in heauen. VVhich is the tenth key. Though the greater part of the Pſalme sheweth, that iuſt and true dealing towards our neighboures, is neceſſarie for attayning of eternal Glorie. Fourtly , when both the title and Pſalme, or part thereof ſeme \f - \ft 4. Conference of places.\f* hard and obſcure, ſome part being more cleare, the true ſenſe of al may be gethered by that which is more euident. According to S. Auguſtins rule \it li. 2. c. 9. & li. 3. c. 26. Doct. Chriſt\it*. So the title \f - \ft \+it li. 3. c. 4. de pecca.\+it* more pleaſant.\f* and former part of the fifth Pſalme, being more obſcure, are explaned by the laſt verſes, shewing plainly that God wil iuſtly iudge al men, both iuſt and wicked, in the end of this world. VVhich is the ninth key. By theſe and like meanes the principal key being found, it wil more eaſily appeare, what other keyes belong to the ſame, and what other ſtringes are alſo touched. At leaſt the ſtudious may by theſe helpes make ſome entrance, and for more exact knowlege ſearch the iudgement of ancient Fathers and other learned Doctours.
\ip But beſides this ſingular great commoditie, of compendious handling much Diuine matter in ſmal room , this booke hath another ſpecial excellencie, in the kind of ſtile, and maner of vttering, \f - \ft The ſtile of this booke is Poetrie.\f* which is Meeter, and Verſe, in the original Hebrew tongue. And though in Greke, Latin, and other languages, the ſame could not in like forme be exactly tranſlated, yet the number, and diſtinction of verſes is ſo obſerued, that it is apt for muſike, as wel voices as inſtruments, and to al other vſes of Gods ſeruants. Neither is muſical maner of vttering Gods word and praiſes leſſe to be eſteemed , becauſe profane Poetes haue in this kind of ſtile vttered light, vaine, and falſe thinges. For the \f - \ft Abuſe derogateth not from good thinges.\f* abuſe of good thinges, doth not derogate from the goodnes therof, but rather commendeth the ſame, which others deſire to imitate. And clere it is, that this holie Pſalmodie was before anie profane poetrie now extant . For Homer, the moſt ancient of that ſorte, writte his poeme at leaſt \f - \ft Dauids Pſalter more ancient then any profane poetrie now extant.\f* two hundred and forty yeares after the deſtruction of Troy, as Apolidorus witneſſeth; others, namely Solinus, Herodotus, and Cornelius Nepos ſay longer. VVheras King Dauid our Diuine Pſalmiſt, reigned within one hundred years after the Troianes warres. There were indeed Amphion, Orpheus, and Muſcus before Dauid, but their verſes either were not written, or shortly perished, only a confuſe memorie remaining of them, recited, altered, and corrupted by word of mouth: but before them were the ſacred Hiſtorie of Iob, almoſt al in verſe; and the two Canticles of Moyſes, \it Exodi. 15.\it* and \it Deut. 32.\it* It is moreouer recorded that Iubal (long before Noes floud) was the \f - \ft \+it Gen. 4.\+it*\f* father of them , that ſang on harpe, and organ. Muſike therfore is maruelous ancient. But ſacred \f - \ft Muſic very ancient.\f* Poetrie is in manie other reſpectes moſt excellent, and moſt profitable. \it This holie Pſalmodie\it* (ſaith \f - \ft Sacred poetrie moſt excellent.\f* S. Auguſtin ) \it is a medecine of old ſpiritual ſores, it bringeth preſent remedie to new woundes: it\it* \f - \ft \+it Prefat.\+it*\f* \it maketh the good to perſeuere in wel doing, it cureth at once al predominating paſſions, which vexe mens ſoules\it*. A little after: \it Pſalmodie driueth away euil ſpirites, inuiteth good Angels to helpe vs, it is a shield in night terrors, a refreshing of day travails, a guard to children, an ornament to yongmen, a comforte to oldmen, a moſt ſeemlie grace to wemen. Vnto beginners it is an introduction, an augmentation to them that goe forward in vertue, a ſtable firmament to the perfect: It conioyneth the whole Church militant in one voice, and is the ſpiritual eternal ſwete perfume of the celeſtial Armies, al Sainctes and Angels in heauen.\it*
\ip To al this we may adde other cauſes, which moued the Royal Prophete to write this diuine poetrie.
\ip Firſt, he had from his youth (by Gods ſpecial prouidence) a natural inclination to Muſike ; \f - \ft VVhy King Dauid writte diuine poetrie\f* \f - \ft The firſt cauſe his natural inclination to muſike.\f* wherin he shortly ſo excelled, that before al the Muſitians in Iſrael, he was ſelected to recreate King Saul, whom an euil ſpirite vexed. And his skil, together with his deuotion, had ſuch effect, that \it when he played on the harpe , Saul was refreshed, and waxed better. For the euil ſpirite departed\it* \f - \ft \+it 1. Reg. 16. v. 23.\+it*\f* \it from him\it*, ſaith the holie text. VVherfore he made theſe Pſalmes, that himſelf and others might by ſinging them, imploy this gift of God to his more honour. Secondly, verſe being more eaſie to \f - \ft 2. Verſe more eaſie &\f* lerne, more firmly kept in mind, and more pleaſant in Practiſe (for \it as wine, ſo muſike doth recreate the hart of man\it* ) the Holie Ghoſt condeſcending to mans natural diſpoſition, inſpired Dauid \f - \ft \+it Eccli. 40.\+it*\f* to write theſe Pſalmes in meeter, \it mixing the powre of diuine doctrin, with delectable melodie of ſong, that whiles the eare is allured with harmonie of muſike, the hart is indued with heauenlie knowlege, pleaſant to the mind, and profitable to the ſoule.\it* Thirdly, Dauid ſingularly illuminated with \f - \ft \+it S. Aug. S. Baſil. in Præfat.\+it*\f* \f - \ft 3. Moſt ſpecial, great, and memorable thinges writte in verſe.\f* knowlege of great, and moſt diuine Myſteries, indued alſo with moſt gracious diſpoſition of mind, \it the man choſen according to Gods owne hart (1. Reg. 13.)\it* would vtter the ſame Myſteries, with godlie inſtructions, and praiſes of God, in the moſt exquiſite kind of ſtile; that is in verſe. For otherwiſe he was alſo very eloquent in proſe, as wel appeareth by ſundrie his excellent, and effectual diſcourſes, in the books of Kinges, and Paralipomenon. For which cauſe Moyſes alſo deſcribed the paſſage of Iſrael forth of Ægypt through the red ſea in a Canticle , after that he \f - \ft \+it Exo. 15.\+it*\f* had related the ſame whole hiſtorie more at large in proſe, that al might ſing, and ſo render thankes with melodious voice, and muſical inſtruments praiſing God. Likewiſe in another Canticle he compriſed the whole law, a litle before his death . So alſo Barac and Debora, and after them \f - \ft \+it Deut. 32.\+it*\f* Iudith , ſung praiſes to God for their victories in verſe. Salomon writte the end of his Prouerbes, \f - \ft \+it Iudic. 5. Iudith. 16. Prou. 21. 2. Reg. 2. Iſa. 38. Iſa. 12. 26. Ezech. 38. Ioan. 2. Abac. 3. Dan. 3. Luc. 1. 2.\+it*\f* and a whole booke (intituled Canticles) & the Prophet Ieremie his Lamentations in verſe. Anna hauing obtained her prayer for a ſonne, gaue thankes to God with a Canticle. The like did King Ezechias for recouerie of health. The Prophets Iſaias, Ezechiel, Ionas, Abacuc, and the three children in the fornace: againe in the new Teſtament , the B. Virgin-Mother, iuſt Zacharie, & \f - \ft Canticles in the new Teſtament.\f* deuout Simeon gaue thankes, & ſang praiſes to God in Canticles.
\ip Fourthly, albeit the holie King was not permitted to build the gorgious Temple for Gods \f - \ft 4. Both diuine muſike and dittie in Gods temple.\f* ſeruice, as he greatly deſired to haue done, yet he prouided both ſtore of muſitians (foure thouſand in number, of which 288. were maiſters to teach) & made theſe Pſalmes as godlie ditties, for this \f - \ft \+it 1. Paral. 23. 25.\+it*\f* holie purpoſe, in al ſolemnities of feaſtes, and daylie ſacrifice, when the Temple should afterward be built.
\ip Fiftly, he made theſe Pſalmes not only for his owne, & others priuate deuotion, nor yet ſo \f - \ft 5. The great vſe of theſe Pſalmes in the Catholique Church.\f* eſpecially for the publique Diuine ſeruice in the Temple, and other Synagogues of the Jews, but moſt principally for the Chriſtian Catholique Church, which he knew should be ſpread in the whole earth. Forſeing the maruelous, great, and frequent vſe therof in the Chriſtian Clergie, and Religious people of both ſexes. As he prophecieth in diuers Pſalmes. \it Al the earth ſing to thee: ſing Pſalmes to thy name.\it* Againe , \it I wil ſing Pſalmes to thee\it* (o God) \it in the Gentiles, in al peoples and Nations\it*. \f - \ft \+it 56. 117. 65.\+it*\f* VVhich himſelf neuer did, but his Pſalmes are euer ſince Chriſt, ſung by Chriſtians, conuerted from gentilitie, as we ſee in the Churches Seruice. For the whole Pſalter is diſtributed to be ſung, in \f - \ft The whole Pſalter in the ordinarie office euerie weke.\f* the ordinarie office of our Breuiarie euerie weke. And though extraordinarily, for the varietie of times, and feaſtes, there is often alteration, yet ſtil the greater part is in Pſalmes. Certayne alſo of the ſame Pſalmes , are without change or intermiſſion repeted euerie day . And ſuch as haue \f - \ft Certaine Pſalmes euerie day.\f* \f - \ft 4. \+it (30. ad v. 7) 53. 62. 66. 90. 94. 118. 133. 148. 149. 150.\+it*\f* obligation to the Canonical Houres, muſt at leaſt read the whole Office priuatly, if they be not preſent where it is ſung. The Office alſo of Maſſe , ordinarily beginneth with a Pſalme. In \f - \ft Many Pſalmes in other Eccleſiaſtical offices.\f* Litanies, and almoſt al publique Prayers, and in adminiſtration of other Sacraments, and Sacramentals, either whole Pſalmes, or frequent verſes are inſerted. Likewiſe the greateſt part of the Offices of our B. Ladie, and for the dead, are Pſalmes. Beſides the ſeuen Penitential, and fiftene Gradual Pſalmes, at certaine times. So that Clergie mens daily office conſiſteth much in ſinging, or reading Pſalmes. And therfore al Byshops eſpecially, are ſtrictly bound by a particular Canon \it (Diſt. 38. cap. Omnes pſallentes)\it* to be skilful in the Pſalmes of Dauid: and to ſee that other Clergy \f - \ft Bishops bound to be skilful in Dauids Pſalter. Other Prieſtes to haue competent knowlege therin.\f* men be wel inſtructed therin. According to the Holie Ghoſts admonition, by the pen of the ſame Royal Prophet \it (Pſal. 46.) Pſallite ſapienter\it*, or, \it intelligenter\it*, that is: \it Sing Pſalmes with knowlege, and vnderſtanding them\it*. Not that euerie one is bound to know, and be able to diſcuſſe al difficulties, but competently, according to their charge vndertaken in Gods Church. Otherwiſe euerie one that is, or intendeth to a Prieſt, may remember what God denounceth to him, by the Prophet Oſee \it (c. 4.) Becauſe thou haſt repelled knowlege, I wil repel thee, that thou do not the function of Prieſthood vnto me.\it* Thus much touching the Author, the contentes, the poetical ſtile, & final cauſe of this holie Pſalter.
\ip As for the name , S. Ierom, S. Auguſtin, and other Fathers teach, that wheras amongſt \f - \ft VVhy this booke is called the pſalter.\f* innumerable muſical inſtruments, ſix were more ſpecially vſed in Dauids time, mentioned by him in the laſt Pſalme, \it Trumpet, Pſalter, Harpe, Timbrel, Organ, and Cimbal\it*: This booke hath his name of the inſtrument called Pſalter, which hath tenne ſtrings, ſignifying the tenne commandements, and is made in forme (as S. Ierom, and S. Bede ſuppoſe) of the Greke letter Δ \it delta\it*, becauſe as that inſtrument rendreth ſound from aboue, ſo we should attend to heauenlie vertues, which come from aboue: Likewiſe vſing the harpe, which ſignifyeth mortification of the flesh, & other inſtruments , which ſignifie and teach other vertues, we muſt finally referre al to Gods glorie, \f - \ft Other inſtrumentes make conſorte with the Pſalter.\f* reioyce ſpiritually in hart, and render al praiſe to God. \f - \ft Al vertues are referred to Gods honour.\f*
\c 1
\cd The Royal prophet Dauid placed this Pſalme as a Preface to the reſt, conteyning, 1 true happines, which conſiſteth in flying ſinnes, and ſeruing God. 3. The good doe proſper, 5. not the Wicked: 6. as wil appeare in the end of this world. \f - \ft The right maner of ſeruing God. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 BLESSED \f a \fr 1.1 \ft He iſ in the right way to eternal felicitie.\f*\f 1 \fr 1.1 \ft Mat. 5.\f* is the man, that hath \f b \fr 1.1 \ft not conſented to euil ſuggeſtions.\f* not gone in the counſel of the impious, & hath \f c \fr 1.1 \ft not continued in ſinne.\f* not ſtoode in the way of ſinners, and hath \f d \fr 1.1 \ft not finally perſiſted in wicked life.\f* not ſitte in the chayre of peſtilence:
\v 2 But \f e \fr 1.2 \ft Ios. 1. · He is wholly occupied & delighted in keeping Gods commandments.\f* his wil is in the way of our Lord, and in his law he wil meditate day and night. \x - \xt Ios. 1. 1. Hath not gone, not ſtood, not ſit.]\x*
\v 3 And he shal be as a tree, that is planted nigh to \f f \fr 1.3 \ft Iere. 17. · To him that vſeth Gods grace wel, more grace is continually geuen.\f* the ſtreames of waters, which shal geue his fruite in his time: \x - \xt Jere. 17.\x* And \f g \fr 1.3 \ft Through ſuch grace he shal perſeuer.\f* his leafe shal not fal: and \f h \fr 1.3 \ft al thinges worke to the good of them that loue God ſincerely.\f* al thinges whatſoeuer he shal doe, shal proſper.
\v 4 The impious not ſo: but \f i \fr 1.4 \ft The wicked are carried with euery light tentation.\f* as duſt, which the winde driueth from the face of the earth.
\v 5 Therfore the impious shal \f k \fr 1.5 \ft Al ryſing at the laſt day, the wicked shal not riſe with hope nor comforth, but in deſolation.\f* not riſe againe in iudgement: nor ſinners in the \f l \fr 1.5 \ft the happie congregation of the bleſſed.\f* councel of the iuſt.
\v 6 For our Lord \f m \fr 1.6 \ft approueth & rewardeth.\f* knoweth the way of the iuſt, and the way of the impious \f n \fr 1.6 \ft in eternal damnation.\f* shal perish.
\c 2
\cd Chriſts glorie, the World repining therat, 4. shal be propagated in al the world. 7. His diuine powre as wel ſpiritual in conuerting mens hartes, as external, in ſeuere iuſtice, is prophecied. \f - \ft Of Chriſt, The 5. key. Alſo of his Church, The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 WHY did the \f a \fr 2.1 \ft Both gentiles.\f* Gentiles rage, and \f b \fr 2.1 \ft and Iewes ſtriue invaine againſt Chriſt.\f* peoples meditate vaine things? \x - \xt Act. 4.\x*
\v 2 The \f c \fr 2.2 \ft Pilate and Herod.\f* kings of the earth ſtood vp, and the \f d \fr 2.2 \ft Annas and Caiphas.\f* princes came together in one againſt our Lord, and againſt his Chriſt.
\v 3 Let \f e \fr 2.3 \ft The voice of wicked men,\f* vs breake their bondes a ſunder: and let vs caſt away their \f f \fr 2.3 \ft & eſpecially libertines ſtriuing to shake of al diſcipline.\f* yoke from vs.
\v 4 He that dwelleth in the heauens, shal \f g \fr 2.4 \ft God for al this wil turne the hartes of manie.\f* laugh at them: and our Lord shal ſcorne them.
\v 5 Then shal he ſpeake to them in his \f h \fr 2.5 \ft ſeuerly reprehend,\f* wrath, & in his \f i \fr 2.5 \ft and iuſtly punish the obſtinate.\f* furie he shal truble them.
\v 6 But \f k \fr 2.6 \ft Chriſt shal reigne in his kingdome the Church.\f* I am appoynted king by him ouer Sion his holie hil, preaching his precept.
\v 7 The \f l \fr 2.7 \ft God the Father ſpeaketh to\f* Lord ſaid to me; Thou art my \f m \fr 2.7 \ft God the Sonne.\f* Sonne, I this day haue begotten thee. \x - \xt Act. 13. Heb. 1. & 5.\x*
\v 8 Aske of me, and I wil geue \f n \fr 2.8 \ft Chriſt as man hath the Church for his inheritance.\f* thee the Gentiles, for thyne inheritance, and thy poſſeſſion \f o \fr 2.8 \ft Spred through the whole world.\f* the endes of the earth.
\v 9 Thou shalt rule them in \f p \fr 2.9 \ft Gods inflexible wil & powre.\f* a rod of yron, and as a potters veſſel thou shalt breake them in peeces. \x - \xt Apoc. 3. & 19.\x*
\v 10 And \f q \fr 2.10 \ft A prophecie that kinges shal be conuerted, and ſubmit themſelues to Chriſts diſcipline.\f* now ye kings vnderſtand: take inſtruction you that iudge the earth.
\v 11 Serue our Lord in \f r \fr 2.11 \ft None is ſecure before death.\f* feare: and reioyce to him with trembling.
\v 12 Apprehend diſcipline \f s \fr 2.12 \ft Some fal from the way of ſaluation.\f* leſt ſometime our Lord be wrath, and you perish out of the iuſt way.
\v 13 When his wrath \f t \fr 2.13 \ft God wil iudge iuſtly in the end of this short life both the euil and good. So this Pſalme concludeth with the ninth key.\f* shal burne in short time, bleſſed are al, that truſt in him.
\c 3
\cd King Dauid recounteth his danger, when his ſonne Abſalom conſpired againſt him: 4. and thanketh God for his deliuerie. 9, acknowledging al helpe to be from God. Miſtically, Chriſts perſecution, Death, Burial, and Reſurrection. \f - \ft David Perſecuted by his ſon. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 The Pſalme of Dauid, when he fled from the face of Abſalom his ſonne. (2. Reg. 15.)
\v 2 LORD \f a \fr 3.2 \ft O God let me know how greuiouſly I haue ſinned.\f* why are they \f b \fr 3.2 \ft that al Iſrael (1.Reg. 15.v.13.) with al their hart foloweth Abſolom. So againſt Chriſt, the Prieſtes, the People, & Gentiles al conſpired.\f* multiplied that truble me? manie riſe vp againſt me. \x - \xt Ioan. 2.\x*
\v 3 Many ſay to \f c \fr 3.3 \ft my life\f* my ſoule: There is \f d \fr 3.3 \ft he can not eſcape\f* no ſaluation for him in his God.
\v 4 But \f e \fr 3.4 \ft But I auouch that God alwaies defendeth me,\f* thou Lord art my protectour, my \f f \fr 3.4 \ft geuing me victorie,\f* glorie, & exalting \f g \fr 3.4 \ft & confirming my kingdom.\f* my head.
\v 5 With my voice I haue cried to our Lord: and he hath heard me from his \f h \fr 3.5 \ft heauen.\f* holie hil.
\v 6 I haue \f i \fr 3.6 \ft I lay downe\f* ſlept, and haue \f k \fr 3.6 \ft and reſted in expectation of thy helpe.\f* bene at reſt; and haue \f l \fr 3.6 \ft And am deliuered. Chriſt dyed, was buried, & roſe againe.\f* riſen vp, becauſe our Lord hath taken me.
\v 7 I wil not feare thouſandes of people compaſſing me: \f m \fr 3.7 \ft I know thou wilt help me, and ſo I beſeech thee to do.\f* ariſe Lord, ſaue me my God.
\v 8 Becauſe thou haſt ſtroken al that are my aduerſaries without cauſe: thou haſt broken the \f n \fr 3.8 \ft The ſtrength and furie.\f* teeth of ſinners.
\v 9 Saluation \f o \fr 3.9 \ft health and ſaftie cometh from God.\f* is our Lordes: and thy \f p \fr 3.9 \ft Abundance of grace promiſed to Gods ſeruantes.\f* bleſſing vpon thy people.
\c 4
\cd The holie prophet teacheth, by his owne example, to flee to God in al tribulation. 3. that other refugies are inſufficient, 9. and Gods helpe moſt aſſured. \f - \ft Confidence in God neceſſary. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, in \f a \fr 4.1 \ft In an inſtrument apt for verſes.\f* ſongues, the Pſalme \f b \fr 4.1 \ft This Pſalme perteyneth to the beloued, ſignified by the word Dauid. S. Aug: li. 17. c. 14. ciuit. S. Beda in Pſal.\f* of Dauid.
\v 2 WHEN \f c \fr 4.2 \ft VVhen Saul vniuſtly perſecuted iust Dauid, God heard his prayers.\f* I inuocated, the God of my iuſtice heard me: in \f d \fr 4.2 \ft being ſtraictly beſeeged \+it (1. Reg. 23. 26\+it*.)\f* tribulation thou haſt enlarged to me. \f e \fr 4.2 \ft Likewiſe helpe me when ſoeuer I shal nede.\f* Haue mercie on me, and heare my prayer.
\v 3 Ye ſonnes of men how long are you of \f f \fr 4.3 \ft Why do you ſtil harden your hartes?\f* heauie hart? why loue you \f g \fr 4.3 \ft honour, and tranſitorie glorie,\f* vanitie, and ſeeke \f h \fr 4.3 \ft falſe and deceiptful riches?\f* lying? \x - \xt Eph. 4.\x*
\v 4 And know ye that our Lord hath made his \f i \fr 4.4 \ft Euery godly ſoule.\f* holie one \f k \fr 4.4 \ft Rich with vertues.\f* meruelous: \f l \fr 4.4 \ft Euerie iuſt ſoule hath confidence in God, that he wil heare his crie.\f* our Lord wil heare me, when I shal crie to him.
\v 5 Be ye \f m \fr 4.5 \ft Iuſt anger is good & neceſſarie againſt ſinne.\f* angrie, and \f n \fr 4.5 \ft but then is moſt nede to beware not to excede in paſſion. haue therfore a continual purpoſe neuer to ſinne.\f* ſinne not: the thinges that you ſay in \f o \fr 4.5 \ft Euil cogitations.\f* your hartes, in your \f p \fr 4.5 \ft bewaile & repent before you ſleepe.\f* chambers be ye ſorie for.
\v 6 Sacrifice ye the ſacrifice of \f q \fr 4.6 \ft Not only external but moſt eſpecially internal ſacrifice of iuſtice, and obſeruation of Gods commandments is moſt neceſſarie\f* iuſtice, and hope in our Lord. Manie ſay: \f r \fr 4.6 \ft The ſolide rewardes promiſed by God?\f* Who sheweth vs good thinges?
\v 7 The \f s \fr 4.7 \ft reaſon and grace are freely geuen to man, wherby he may know that God wil reward the iuſt. \+it Heb. 11. v. 6\+it*.\f* light of thy countenance ô Lord is ſigned vpon vs: thou haſt geuen \f t \fr 4.7 \ft VVherin a iuſt man inwardly reioyceth.\f* gladneſſe in my hart.
\v 8 By the fruite of their \f v \fr 4.8 \ft For example and in figure of heauenlie rewardes, God gaue temporal wealth in the old teſtament.\f* corne, and \f w \fr 4.8 \ft See previous note\f* wine, and \f x \fr 4.8 \ft See previous note\f* oile they are multiplied.
\v 9 In \f y \fr 4.9 \ft In this confidence the iuſt may reſt contented.\f* peace in the ſelfe ſame I wil ſleepe, and reſt:
\v 10 Becauſe thou Lord haſt \f z \fr 4.10 \ft God ſo promiſeth euerie iuſt perſon in particular.\f* ſingularly ſetled me in hope.
\c 5
\cd Iuſt men in affliction appeale to God, the reuenger of iniuries. 5. knowing and profeſſing that God hateth iniquity. 9. therfore remitte their cauſe to him. 11. recite certaine enormious vices of the wicked. 13. and expect Gods final iudgement of the good and bad. \f - \ft The general judgement. The 9. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, for \f a \fr 5.1 \ft The faithful iuſt ſoule that ouercometh her enimies by vertue.\f* her that obtaineth the \f b \fr 5.1 \ft eternal glorie.\f* inheritance. The Pſalme of Dauid.
\v 2 RECEIVE ô Lord \f c \fr 5.2 \ft The praier of the whole Church, or of anie faithful ( euer beloued ) ſoule.\f* my wordes with thine eares, vnderſtand my crie.
\v 3 Attend to the voice of my prayer, my king and my God.
\v 4 Becauſe I wil pray to thee: Lord in \f d \fr 5.4 \ft Gods helpe is preſently granted of his part, though it be ſometimes differred for the more good of his ſeruantes.\f* the morning thou wilt heare my voice.
\v 5 In \f e \fr 5.5 \ft Before al other affayres we muſt pray to God. S. Ciprian. in fine orat. Dominicæ.\f* the morning I wil ſtand by thee and wil ſee: becauſe thou art not a God that wilt iniquitie.
\v 6 Neither shal the malignant \f f \fr 5.6 \ft The wicked and wickednes haue noe conuerſation with God.\f* dwel neere thee: neither shal the vniuſt abide \f g \fr 5.6 \ft in the day of iudgement.\f* before thine eies.
\v 7 Thou hateſt al that worke iniquitie: thou wilt \f h \fr 5.7 \ft by final ſentence of eternal damnation.\f* deſtroy al that ſpeake lie.
\v 8 The bloudie and deceitful man our Lord wil abhorre:
\v 9 But I in the multitude of \f i \fr 5.9 \ft Not in mans powre, but in Gods mercie muſt the iuſt man truſt.\f* thy mercy. I wil enter into thy houſe: I wil adore toward \f k \fr 5.9 \ft In the Church of God.\f* thy holie temple in thy \f l \fr 5.9 \ft with reuerential feare as in Gods preſence.\f* feare. Lord conduct me in thy iuſtice: becauſe of mine enimies direct my way in thy ſight.
\v 10 Becauſe there is \f m \fr 5.10 \ft No true nor ſolide goodnes in the wicked.\f* no truth in their mouth: their hart is \f n \fr 5.10 \ft They thinke nothing but vanitie, and miſchiefe.\f* vayne.
\v 11 \x - \xt Pſal. 13. Rom. 3.\x* Their throte is an \f o \fr 5.11 \ft yelding lothſome ſtinch, bitternes, and rancor,\f* open ſepulchre, they did \f p \fr 5.11 \ft yet they flatter with feaned good wordes.\f* deceitfully with their tongues, \f q \fr 5.11 \ft Albeit the iuſt deſire the conuerſion of the wicked, yet if they wil not repent, then the iuſt conforme their deſires to Gods iuſt iudgement: which shal be manifeſted in the end of the world.\f* iudge them o God.
\v 12 Let them faile of their cogitations, according to the multitude of their impieties expel them, becauſe they haue prouoked thee ô Lord.
\v 13 And let al be glad, that hope in thee, they shal reioyce for euer: and thou shalt dwel in them. And al that loue thy name shal glorie in thee, becauſe thou wilt \f r \fr 5.13 \ft The iuſt shal receiue ſentence of eternal glorie.\f* bleſſe the iuſt.
\v 14 Lord, as with a shield of thy good wil, thou haſt crowned vs.
\c 6
\cd Dauids earneſt and hartie praier after he had grieuouſly ſinned. 5. which being grounded in filial, not ſeruil feare, 9. concludeth with aſſured hope, and confidence in Gods mercie. \f - \ft A pathetical prayer of a ſinner & the firſt penitential Pſalm, The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 6.1 \ft This Pſalme perteyneth alſo to penitentes in the new teſtament.\f* the end in ſongs, the Pſalme of Dauid for the octaue. \x - \xt Pſa. 37.\x*
\v 2 LORD, \f b \fr 6.2 \ft condemne me not eternally\f* rebuke me not in thy furie; nor \f c \fr 6.2 \ft Pſal. 37. · Spare me alſo for part of the temporal paine, which I deſerue.\f* chaſtiſe me in thy wrath.
\v 3 Haue mercie on me Lord, becauſe I am weake: \f d \fr 6.3 \ft Geue me the medicine of grace.\f* heale me Lord, becauſe al \f e \fr 6.3 \ft My ſorow hath inwardly pearced me euen to the bones.\f* my bones be trubled.
\v 4 And my ſoule is \f f \fr 6.4 \ft with feare of thy iuſt wrath.\f* trubled exceedingly: but thou Lord \f g \fr 6.4 \ft leaueſt thou me in this calamitie?\f* how long?
\v 5 \f h \fr 6.5 \ft Shew againe thy fauorable contenance.\f* Turne thee o Lord, and \f i \fr 6.5 \ft from this fearful affliction.\f* deliuer my ſoule: ſaue me for \f k \fr 6.5 \ft Though my ſinnes haue deſerued the contrary yet shew thy mercy.\f* thy mercie.
\v 6 Becauſe there is not in \f l \fr 6.6 \ft This life is the time of repentance, after death no cenuerſion.\f* death, that is mindful of thee: and in \f m \fr 6.6 \ft In hel nothing but blaſphemie.\f* hel who shal confeſſe to thee?
\v 7 I \f n \fr 6.7 \ft I haue in part lamented.\f* haue labored in my ſighing, I \f o \fr 6.7 \ft I wil adde more ſorow & penance.\f* wil euerie night washe my bed; I wil \f p \fr 6.7 \ft I wil perſiſt in my penance, til I be throughly watered with thy grace.\f* water my couche with my teares.
\v 8 My \f q \fr 6.8 \ft myñ eyes are dimme with weeping, for feare of thy iust iudgement.\f* eye is trubled for furie: I haue waxen \f r \fr 6.8 \ft my heares are gray with ſorrow.\f* old \f s \fr 6.8 \ft wherat myn enemies reioyce.\f* among al myne enemies.
\v 9 \f t \fr 6.9 \ft After due ſorow the true penitent hath confidence in God, against his enimies.\f* Depart from me al ye that worke iniquitie: becauſe our Lord \f v \fr 6.9 \ft Wil most certainly accept of true repentance.\f* hath heard the voice of my weeping. \x - \xt Mat. 7. & 25. Luc. 13.\x*
\v 10 Our Lord hath heard my petition, our Lord hath receiued my prayer.
\v 11 Let al myne enemies be \f w \fr 6.11 \ft Theſe are not imprecations, but threatninges, that the wicked may amend, or els predictions if they perſist in ſinne.\f* ashamed, & very ſore trubled: let them be conuerted and ashamed very quicly.
\c 7
\cd Dauid in confidence of his iuſt cauſe, and vniuſt perſecution. prayeth for Gods helpe, 7. and iuſt reuenge of his enemies. 15. deſcribing their malitious intention, and ruine. \f - \ft David's prayer in tribulation. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 The Pſalme of Dauid which he ſong to our Lord, for the wordes of \f a \fr 7.1 \ft This Chuſi defeated the counſel of Achitophel. as S. Aug. S. Baſil & S. Chris. expound it.\f* Chuſi the ſonne Iemini. (2. Reg. 16.) \f 1 \fr 7.1 \ft \+it (2. Reg. 16.)\+it*\f*
\v 2 O Lord my God I haue hoped in thee: ſaue me from al that perſecute me, and deliuer me.
\v 3 Leſt ſometime \f b \fr 7.3 \ft Abſolom, or anie other enemie, if God doe not reſiſt and hinder him.\f* he as a Lyon violently take \f c \fr 7.3 \ft my life.\f* my ſoule, whiles there is none to redeme, nor to ſaue.
\v 4 O Lord my God if I haue done \f d \fr 7.4 \ft Such euil as myne enemies fayne & obiect againſt me.\f* this, if there be iniquitie in my handes;
\v 5 If I haue rendred to them that repayd me euils, let me worthely fal \f e \fr 7.5 \ft Let me not haue the victorie of them.\f* emptie from myne enemies.
\v 6 Let the enemie perſecute my ſoule, and take it, and \f f \fr 7.6 \ft Let me dye with ignominie.\f* treade downe my life in the earth, and bring downe my glorie into the duſt.
\v 7 Ariſe Lord in thy wrath: and \f g \fr 7.7 \ft Shew thy powre.\f* be exalted in the coaſtes of myne enemies. And ariſe ô Lord my God in the \f h \fr 7.7 \ft Seing thou diddeſt command to make me king, it behoueth thee to protect me.\f* precept which thou haſt cõmanded:
\v 8 and a \f i \fr 7.8 \ft manie wil ſerue thee the only true God.\f* ſinagogue of peoples shal compaſſe thee. And \f k \fr 7.8 \ft For this increaſe of faithful people.\f* for it returne on high:
\v 9 our Lord iudgeth peoples. Iudge me ô Lord according to \f l \fr 7.9 \ft my iuſt cauſe againſt my particular enemies.\f* my iuſtice, and according to my innocencie \f m \fr 7.9 \ft let fal vpon me.\f* vpon me.
\v 10 The wickedneſſe of ſinners shal be conſumed, and thou shalt direct the iuſt, which ſearcheſt the hart and raynes ô God. \x - \xt 1. Par. 28. Iere. 11. 17.\x*
\v 11 My iuſt helpe is from our Lord, who ſaueth thoſe that be right of hart.
\v 12 God is a iuſt iudge, ſtrong, & patient: is he angrie \f n \fr 7.12 \ft God doth not punish al ſinners preſently, but often differreth.\f* euerie day?
\v 13 \f o \fr 7.13 \ft expecting if they wil amend.\f* Vnleſſe you wil be conuerted, he shal shake his ſword; he hath bent his bow, and prepared it.
\v 14 And in it he hath prepared the veſſels of death: he hath made his arrowes for them that burne. \x - \xt Iſa. 59. Iob. 15.\x*
\v 15 Behold \f p \fr 7.15 \ft The iniurious perſecutor purpoſing iniuſtice, conceiueth enuie, and bringeth forth iniquitie. · Iſa. 59. Iob. 15.\f* he hath bredde with iniuſtice: he hath conceiued ſorow, and brought forth iniquitie.
\v 16 He hath opened a pit, and digged it vp: and he is fallen into the diche, which he made.
\v 17 His \f q \fr 7.17 \ft Enuie, & deſire to hurt others turneth to the ruine of the enuiers.\f* ſorrow shal be turned vpon his head: and his iniquitie shal deſcend vpon his crowne.
\v 18 I wil confeſſe to our Lord according to his iuſtice: and wil ſing to the name of our Lord moſt high.
\c 8
\cd God is magnified & praiſed for his meruelous worke of creatures, 5. but eſpecially of mankind, ſingularly exalted by the Incarnation of Chriſt. \f - \ft Chriſt's Incarnation. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 8.1 \ft Belonging to the newe Teſtament.\f* the end \f b \fr 8.1 \ft Suffering of Christ, and of Chriſtians.\f* for preſſes, the Pſalme of Dauid.
\v 2 O LORD \f c \fr 8.2 \ft God the lord of al by creation, is our ſingular lord, that beleue and truſt in him.\f* our Lord, how meruelous is thy name in the whole earth! Becauſe thy magnificence is eleuated, aboue \f d \fr 8.2 \ft God more excelent then the heauens, he being the creator, they a creature.\f* the heauens.
\v 3 Out of the mouth of \f e \fr 8.3 \ft fulfilled whē Chriſt coming into Ieruſalem, children ſang \+it Ozanna Mat. 21\+it*. · Mat. 21.\f* infantes and ſucklinges, thou haſt perfected praiſe becauſe of thine enemies, that thou mayeſt deſtroy the enemie and reuenger. \x - \xt Mat. 21.\x*
\v 4 Becauſe I shal ſee thy heauens, the workes of thy fingers: the moone and the ſtarres, which thou haſt founded.
\v 5 What is man, that thou art mindful of him? or the ſonne of man, that thou viſiteſt him?
\v 6 Thou haſt \f f \fr 8.6 \ft The Sonne in aſſumpted humāe nature became leſſe then Angels.\f* minished him a litle leſſe then Angels; with \f g \fr 8.6 \ft But in him mans nature is exalted aboue Angels.\f* glorie and honour thou haſt crowned him: \x - \xt Heb. 2.\x*
\v 7 and haſt appointed him \f h \fr 8.7 \ft Chriſt the Lord of al creatures.\f* ouer the worke of thy handes.
\v 8 Thou haſt ſubiected \f i \fr 8.8 \ft yea of Angels. \+it Heb. 2\+it*.\f* al thinges vnder his feete, al \f k \fr 8.8 \ft Not only al reaſonable creatures, but al beaſtes, and other thinges obey him. The ſea and the windes obey him. \+it Mat. 8\+it*.\f* sheepe and oxen: moreouer alſo the beaſtes of the field.
\v 9 The birdes of the ayre, and fishes of the ſea; that walke the pathes of the ſea.
\v 10 \f l \fr 8.10 \ft The ſame end & beginning ſignifie, that as God was meruelous in creating man in ſo happie ſtate, that if he would, he might haue auoyded both ſinne & death: ſo he is meruelous in that he ſo rewardeth the bleſſed in the reſurrection, that they can neither ſinne non dye.\f* O Lord our Lord, how meruelous is thy name in the whole earth!
\c 9
\cd The Church prayſeth God for her protection, 4. in repelling the enemies force, 8. in punishing the wicked, and rewarding the iuſt. \f - \ft God's providence in protecting the good and permitting evil. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, for the \f a \fr 9.1 \ft Chriſts coming in humilitie, and Chriſtians afflictions, are hidden from the world, in Gods prouidence.\f* ſecrets of the ſonne, the Pſalme of Dauid.
\v 2 I WIL \f b \fr 9.2 \ft geue thanks.\f* confeſſe to thee ô Lord with al my hart: I wil tel al thy meruelous thinges.
\v 3 I wil be \f c \fr 9.3 \ft in mind.\f* glad and \f d \fr 9.3 \ft and bodie.\f* reioyce in thee: I wil ſing to thy name ô moſt High.
\v 4 In \f e \fr 9.4 \ft God repelleth the enemie, when man is not able to reſiſt.\f* turning mine enemie backward: they shal be weakened, and perish before thy face.
\v 5 Becauſe thou haſt done \f f \fr 9.5 \ft A iuſt man doth his endeuour, not of him ſelfe, but by Gods grace ouercometh the enemie.\f* my iudgement and my cauſe: thou haſt ſitte vpon the throne which iudgeſt iuſtice.
\v 6 Thou haſt rebuked the \f g \fr 9.6 \ft Al ſinners called gentils, becauſe they were generally accounted wicked.\f* Gentiles, and the impious hath perished: their \f h \fr 9.6 \ft The vaine glorious fame of ſinners partly decayeth in this world but moſt eſpecially in the world to come.\f* name thou haſt deſtroyed for euer, and for euer and euer.
\v 7 The ſwordes of the enemie haue fayled vnto the end: and their cities thou haſt deſtroyed.
\v 8 Their memorie hath perished wirh a ſound: and our Lord abideth for euer. He hath prepared his throne in \f i \fr 9.8 \ft Iudicial ſeates of men are often corrupted but Gods neuer.\f* iudgement:
\v 9 & he wil iudge the whole world in equitie, he wil iudge the people in iuſtice.
\v 10 And our Lord is made a refuge for the poore: an helper \f k \fr 9.10 \ft God doth not preſently deliuer the good from afflictions: but when it is to their ſpiritual profite.\f* in opportunities, in tribulation.
\v 11 And let them hope in thee that know thy name: becauſe thou haſt not forſaken them that ſeeke thee ô Lord.
\v 12 Sing to our Lord, which dwelleth in Sion: declare his \f l \fr 9.12 \ft His precepts which men ought chiefly to ſtudie.\f* ſtudies among the Gentiles:
\v 13 Becauſe he \f m \fr 9.13 \ft God reuengeth the blood of Martyrs.\f* requiring bloud remembred them: he hath not forgotten the crie of the poore.
\v 14 Haue mercie on me ô Lord: See my humiliation \f n \fr 9.14 \ft procured by mine enemies.\f* by my enemies.
\v 15 Which exalteſt me from the gates of death, that I may declare al thy prayſes in \f o \fr 9.15 \ft In the publique view of the Church.\f* the gates of the daughter of Sion.
\v 16 I wil reioyce in thy ſaluation: the Gentiles are \f p \fr 9.16 \ft The wicked are intangled in the ſnares which they lay for others.\f* faſtened in the deſtruction, which they made. In this ſnare, which they hid, is their foote taken.
\v 17 Our Lord shal be knowen doing iudgements: the ſinner is taken in the workes of his owne handes.
\v 18 \f q \fr 9.18 \ft As the firſt workes of Chriſt in al humility and patience were ſtrange, and hidden to the world: \+it (v. 1\+it*.) ſo his laſt iudgement shal be in maieſty and manifeſt to al in exalting the bleſſed and ſuppreſſing the wicked.\f* Let ſinners be turned into hel, al nations that forget God.
\v 19 Becauſe to the end there shal not be obliuion of the poore man: the patience of the poore, shal not perish in the end.
\v 20 Ariſe Lord, let not man be ſtrengthned: let the \f r \fr 9.20 \ft By Gentiles is often vnderſtood al great ſinners. For the Iewes deſpiſed Gentiles: as the Romans did al Barbarous nations.\f* Gentiles be iudged in thy ſight.
\v 21 Appoint Lord \f s \fr 9.21 \ft Suffer a tyrant to rule ouer them that thereby they may lerne what it is to vſe others vniuſtly. It ſemeth to S. Auguſtine a prophecie, that ſuch as receiue not Chriſt, shal beleue Antichriſt.\f* a lawgeuer ouer them: that the Gentiles may know that they be men.
\v 22 \f v \fr 9.22 \ft In great perſecution it ſemeth to the weake, that God differreth his aſſiſtance very long.\f* Why Lord haſt thou departed far of, deſpiſeſt in opportunities, in tribulation?
\v 23 Whiles the impious is proude, the poore is \f w \fr 9.23 \ft Extremely vexed & tormented.\f* ſet on fyre: \f x \fr 9.23 \ft The Prophet anſwereth to the complaint of the iuſt, that in deede the wicked are caught in their owne ſnares.\f* they are caught in the counſels which they deuiſe.
\v 24 Becauſe the ſinner is prayſed in the deſires of his ſoule: and the vniuſt man is bleſſed.
\v 25 The ſinner hath exaſperated our Lord, according to the multitude of his wrath he shal \f y \fr 9.25 \ft not ſeeke to recouer Gods fauour.\f* not ſeeke.
\v 26 There is no God in his ſight: his waies are defiled at al time. Thy iudgementes are taken away from his face: he shal \f z \fr 9.26 \ft The wicked doth dominier for a time, and thinketh he shal do ſo ſtil.\f* rule ouer al his enemies
\v 27 For he hath ſayd in his hart: I wil not be moued from generation vnto generation, \f a \fr 9.27 \ft and neuer fal into any aduerſitie but ſtil remaine without miſerie or anie euil.\f* without euil.
\v 28 Whoſe mouth is ful of curſing, and bitterneſſe, and guile: vnder his tongue labour and ſorrow. \x - \xt Rom. 3.\x*
\v 29 He ſitteth in waite with the rich in ſecrete places, to kil the innocent.
\v 30 His eyes looke vpon the poore: he lyeth in wayte in ſecret, as a lyon in his denne.
\v 31 He lyeth in wayte to take the poore man violently: violently to take the poore man whiles he draweth him. In his ſnare he wil humble him ſelfe, and shal fal when he shal haue dominion ouer the poore.
\v 32 For he hath ſayed in his hart; God hath forgotten, he hath turned away his face not to ſee for euer.
\v 33 \f b \fr 9.33 \ft The prayer of the iuſt in tribulation.\f* Ariſe Lord God, let thy hand be axalted: forget not the poore.
\v 34 Wherfore hath the impious prouoked God? for he hath ſaid in his hart; He wil not enquire.
\v 35 Thou ſeeſt, that thou conſidereſt labour and ſorrow: that thou mayeſt deliuer them into thy handes. To thee is the poore left: to the orphane thou wilt be an helper
\v 36 Breake the arme of the ſinner and malignant: his ſinne shal be ſought, and shal not be found.
\v 37 Our Lord shal reigne for euer, and for euer and euer: ye \f c \fr 9.37 \ft ye vileſt men.\f* Gentiles shal perish from his land.
\v 38 Our Lord hath heard the deſire of the poore: thy eare hath heard the \f d \fr 9.38 \ft The iuſt ought alwayes to be readie prepared in hart, to ſuffer patiently al that shal happen vnto them.\f* preperation of their hart,
\v 39 To iudge \f e \fr 9.39 \ft As the firſt workes of Chriſt in al humility and patience were ſtrange, and hidden to the world: (v. 1.) ſo his laſt iudgement shal be in maieſty and manifeſt to al in exalting the bleſſed and ſuppreſſing the wicked.\f* for the pupil and the humble, that man adde no more to magnifie him ſelfe vpon the earth.
\c 10
\cd Dauids freindes aduiſing him to flee from the perſecution of Saul, he anſwereth, that his truſt is in Gods protection. 2. Though the perſecutor be very malitious, 4. yet God wil ouerthrow him, 5. and deliuer the iuſt. \f - \ft God's provident care of the juſt. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 10.1 \ft S. Auguſtin applieth it to heretikes, perſwading Catholiques to repare vnto their ſeparate congregation, falſly calling it the montayne.\f* the end the pſalme of Dauid I TRVST in our Lord: how ſay \f b \fr 10.1 \ft Ye my freindes ſay thus.\f* ye to my ſoule: Paſſe ouer vnto the mountayne as a ſparrow?
\v 2 For behold ſinners \f c \fr 10.2 \ft Perſecuters vſing al rigor and force,\f* haue bent the bow, they haue prepared their arrowes in the quiuer, that they may shoote in \f d \fr 10.2 \ft falſly pretēd other cauſes againſt the innocent to deſtroy them.\f* the darke, at them that be right of hart.
\v 3 For they haue \f e \fr 10.3 \ft It is the maner of perſecuters, and eſpecially of heretikes, to deſtroy and pul downe that others haue built.\f* deſtroyed the thinges, which thou didſt perfite: but the iuſt what hath he done?
\v 4 Our Lord is in his holie temple, our Lord his ſeate is in heauen. \x - \xt Abac. 2.\x*
\v 5 His eies haue reſpect vnto the poore: his \f f \fr 10.5 \ft Though God ſeme to winke or ſleepe, yet his prouidence ſtil watcheth, and obſerueth al mens actions.\f* eieliddes examine the ſonnes of men.
\v 6 Our Lord \f g \fr 10.6 \ft Proueth by tribulations.\f* examineth the iuſt, and the impious: but he that \f h \fr 10.6 \ft Continuance in ſinne bringeth damnation to the ſoule.\f* loueth iniquity, hateth his owne ſoule.
\v 7 \f i \fr 10.7 \ft God ſparing for the time at laſt muſt needes of iuſtice punish ſeuerely.\f* He shal rayne ſnares vpon ſinners: fyre and brimſtone, and blaſt of ſtormes the portion of their cuppe.
\v 8 Becauſe our Lord is iuſt and hath loued iuſtice: his countenance hath ſeene equitie.
\c 11
\cd The Prophet deſcribeth the paucity of iuſt men, and abundanc of wicked, both at Chriſts firſt coming in flesh, 6. and ſecond in maieſtie, in the end of the world. \f - \ft The ſtate of the Church in the firſt and laſt times of Chriſt. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 11.1 \ft Chriſts firſt,\f* the end for \f b \fr 11.1 \ft and laſt comming,\f* the octaue, the \f c \fr 11.1 \ft wil bring ioy to the elect.\f* Pſalme of Dauid.
\v 2 SAVE \f d \fr 11.2 \ft Chriſt calleth his myſtical bodie, him ſelfe. \+it Act. 9. v. 4\+it*.\f* me Lord, becauſe the holy hath fayled \f e \fr 11.2 \ft Falſe and duble dealing hinder from true faith.\f* becauſe verities are diminished from among the children of men.
\v 3 They haue ſpoken vaine thinges euerie one to his neighbour, deiceitful lippes, they haue ſpoken in hart and hart,
\v 4 Our Lord deſtroy al deceitful lippes, & the tongue that ſpeaketh \f f \fr 11.4 \ft Inſolent & arrogant.\f* great thinges
\v 5 Which haue ſaid: We wil magnifie our tongue, our lippes are of vs, who is our Lord?
\v 6 For the miſerie of the needie, and mourning of the poore, now wil I ariſe, ſaith our Lord: I wil put in \f g \fr 11.6 \ft VVhen ſinne moſt abunded Chriſt came into this world: and in like caſe wil come to iudge.\f* a ſaluation: I wil do confidently in him.
\v 7 Wordes of our Lord, be chaiſt wordes: ſiluer examined by fire, tryed from the earth, purged ſeuen fold. \x - \xt Pro. 30.\x*
\v 8 Thou Lord wilt \f h \fr 11.8 \ft yet ſtil there remaine ſome iuſt whom God preſerueth.\f* preſerue vs: and keepe vs from this generation for euer.
\v 9 The impious walke round about: according to thy highnes thou haſt \f i \fr 11.9 \ft God ſometimes ſuffereth the wicked to do what euil they deſire.\f* multiplied the children of men.
\c 12
\cd A general prayer of the Church, in tribulation, either temporal or ſpiritual. \f - \ft A prayer in tribulation. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 12.1 \ft It more perteyneth to the new teſtament then to the old.\f* the end, the Pſalme of Dauid. HOw long ô Lord wilt thou forget me vnto the end? How long doeſt thou turne away thy face from me?
\v 2 How long shal I put \f b \fr 12.2 \ft Carful and almoſt perplex cogitatiōs by reaſon of long perſecution and mans weaknes.\f* counſels in my ſoule, ſorrow in my hart \f c \fr 12.2 \ft Very often euerie day.\f* by day?
\v 3 How long shal mine enemies be exalted ouer me? Regard and heare me ô Lord my God.
\v 4 Illuminate mine eies that I ſleepe not \f d \fr 12.4 \ft fal not into mortal ſinne.\f* in death at any time: leſt ſometime mine enemie ſay: I haue preuailed againſt him.
\v 5 They that truble me, wil reioyce if I be moued: but I haue hoped in thy mercie.
\v 6 My hart shal reioyce in thy ſaluation: I wil ſing to our Lord which geueth me \f e \fr 12.6 \ft Patience in tribulation, and reward for victory.\f* good thinges: and I wil ſing to the name of our Lord moſt high.
\c 13
\cd After general groſſe ignorance and impiety in the World, 7. Chriſt shal be incarnate, the Redemer of mankind. \f - \ft Of Chriſt's Incarnation. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, the Pſalme of Dauid. THE \f a \fr 13.1 \ft wicked men drowned in ſinne are at laſt ſo beſotted in their vnderſtanding, that they thinke in their hart (though they dare not vtter it) that there is no God: that is, none that hath diuine prouidence in gouerning the world, nor that wil iudge al in the end.\f* foole hath ſaid in his hart: There is no God. They are corrupt, and are become \f b \fr 13.1 \ft defiled with al ſortes of ſinne.\f* abominable in their ſtudies: there is \f c \fr 13.1 \ft not only the moſt wicked, but alſo al mankind were vnable without a Redemer to do good.\f* not that doth good no not one. \x - \xt Pſa. 52.\x*
\v 2 Our Lord hath looked forth from heauen vpon the children of men, to ſee if there be that vnderſtandeth, and ſeeketh after God.
\v 3 Al haue declined, they are become \f d \fr 13.3 \ft Without faith in Chriſt none had meritorious workes. · Theſe three verſes being not in the Hebrew, nor Greke, yet are in the Engliſh. an. \+it 1577\+it*. and are three diſtinct verſes in other pſalmes. \+it 5. 9. & 35\+it*.\f* vnprofitable together: there is not that doth good, no not one. Their throte is an open ſepulchre: with theit tongues they did deceitfully, the poyſon of aſpes vnder their lippes. Whoſe mouth is ful of curſing and bitterneſſe: their feete ſwift to sheed bloud. \f e \fr 13.3 \ft They are wholly occupied in vexing others.\f* Deſtruction and infelicitie in their waies, and the way of peace they haue not knowen: there is no feare of God before their eies. \x - \xt Iſa. 59. v. 8.\x*
\v 4 \f f \fr 13.4 \ft The Prophet ſpeaketh this in the perſon of God.\f* Shal not al they know that worke iniquitie, that deuoure my people \f g \fr 13.4 \ft With greedines to hurt the good.\f* as foode of bread?
\v 5 They haue not inuocated our Lard, \f h \fr 13.5 \ft Not beleeuing in God, they feared Idols, that is, diuels:\f* there haue they trembled for feare, \f i \fr 13.5 \ft who in dede can not hurt Gods ſeruants.\f* where no feare was:
\v 6 Becauſe our Lord is in \f k \fr 13.6 \ft Though innumerable be very wicked, yet ſome are iuſt.\f* the iuſt generation, you haue \f l \fr 13.6 \ft mocked and derided thoſe that trust in God.\f* confounded the counſel of the poore man: becauſe our Lord is his hope.
\v 7 \f m \fr 13.7 \ft The Prophet wisheth, and withal prophecieth that Christ our Sauiour wil come, who is promiſed to Iſrael.\f* Who wil geue from Sion the ſaluation of Iſrael? when our Lord shal haue \f n \fr 13.7 \ft Redemed man from the captiuitie of the diuel.\f* turned away the captiuitie of his people, \f o \fr 13.7 \ft thoſe that ſupplant vice\f* Iacob shal reioyce, and \f p \fr 13.7 \ft and contemplate God.\f* Iſrael shal be glad.
\c 14
\cd For attayning eternal glorie in heauen, it is neceſſarie to flee from ſinnes, and do good workes. \f - \ft Of eternal Beatitude. The 10. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 The Pſalme of Dauid. LORD who shal dwel in \f a \fr 14.1 \ft In heauē, as appeareth by the laſt verſe.\f* thy tabernacle? or who shal reſt in thy holie hil?
\v 2 He that walketh \f b \fr 14.2 \ft One requiſite thing is to be free, or cleanſed from ſinne.\f* without ſpot, and \f c \fr 14.2 \ft the ſecond is to do good.\f* worketh iuſtice.
\v 3 He that ſpeaketh truth in his \f d \fr 14.3 \ft Sincerely in thought,\f* hart, that hath not done guile in his \f e \fr 14.3 \ft word, and\f* tongue. Nor hath \f f \fr 14.3 \ft dede:\f* done euil to his neighbour, and hath not taken \f g \fr 14.3 \ft nor harkened to detraction.\f* reproch againſt his neighbour,
\v 4 The malignant is brought to nothing in his ſight; but them that feare our Lord, he \f h \fr 14.4 \ft Glorie is the reward of good workes.\f* glorifieth: he that ſweareth to his neighbour, and deceiueth not,
\v 5 that hath not geuen his money to \f i \fr 14.5 \ft Vſurie excludeth from heauen.\f* vſurie, and hath not taken \f k \fr 14.5 \ft Likewise doing wrong for bribes.\f* giftes vpon the innocent: He that doeth theſe thinges, shal not be moued for euer.
\c 15
\cd Chriſt, by the mouth of Dauid, declareth his future victory, and triumph ouer the world, 9. and death. \f - \ft Of eternal Beatitude. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 The \f a \fr 15.1 \ft Stylographia ſignifieth a thing moſt worthie to be noted, towitte, Chriſt crucified.\f* inſcription of the title \f b \fr 15.1 \ft and moſt worthie of the prophets conſideration.\f* to Dauid him ſelf. PRESERVE \f c \fr 15.1 \ft Chriſt as man did often pray, as appeareth in the Goſpel.\f* me ô Lord, becauſe I haue hoped in thee.
\v 2 I haue ſaid to our Lord: Thou art my God, becauſe thou \f d \fr 15.2 \ft Chriſts paſſion was not needful nor profitable to God, but to man.\f* needeſt not my goods.
\v 3 \f e \fr 15.3 \ft God ſpeaketh shewing that Chriſt should make his meruelous charity knowen to his Apoſtles, and other ſeruantes.\f* To the ſainctes, that are in his land, he hath made al my willes meruelous in them.
\v 4 Their infirmities were \f f \fr 15.4 \ft Men feeling their infirmities and miſeries,\f* multiplied: afterward they \f g \fr 15.4 \ft make haſt in ſeeking remedies.\f* made haſt. I wil not aſſemble their \f h \fr 15.4 \ft Sacrifices to idols shal ceaſe in Gentiles.\f* conuenticles of bloud: neither wil I be mindful of their \f i \fr 15.4 \ft Their names shal be changed from heathen to be called Chriſtians.\f* names by my lippes.
\v 5 \f k \fr 15.5 \ft Eternal glorie conſiſteth in ſeing God.\f* Our Lord the portion of myne inheritance, and of my \f l \fr 15.5 \ft God is the reward of ſuffering paines for Chriſt.\f* cuppe: thou art he, that wil reſtore myne inheritance vnto me.
\v 6 \f m \fr 15.6 \ft In diuiſion of temporal inheritance, land is meaſured by cordes: as Ioſue 10. ſo portions in heauen are geuen with large meaſure.\f* Cordes are fallen to me in goodly places: for \f n \fr 15.6 \ft Chriſt alſo receiued al nations for his inheritance.\f* mine inheritance is goodlie vnto me.
\v 7 I wil bleſſe our Lord, who hath \f o \fr 15.7 \ft Wiſdome to make good election of ſpiritual thinges rather then temporal.\f* geuen me vnderſtanding: moreouer alſo euen til \f p \fr 15.7 \ft Not only by day, but alſo by night.\f* night, my \f q \fr 15.7 \ft Alſo my corporal paines geue me inſtruction.\f* veines haue rebuked me.
\v 8 I \f r \fr 15.8 \ft Chriſt had God continually before his eyes: euerie man ought to thincke frequently of God.\f* forſaw our Lord in my ſight alwaies: becauſe he is \f s \fr 15.8 \ft for God ſtil protecteth the iuſt.\f* at my right hand, that I be not moued. \x - \xt Act. 2. v. 25.\x*
\v 9 For this thing my hart hath beene glad, and my tongue hath reioyced: moreouer alſo my flesh shal reſt in hope.
\v 10 Becauſe thou wilt not leaue my ſoule in \f t \fr 15.10 \ft in limbo patrum.\f* hel: neither wilt geue \f v \fr 15.10 \ft Chriſts body corrupted not in the graue.\f* thy holie one to ſee corruption. \x - \xt Act. 2. & 3.\x*Thou haſt made \f w \fr 15.10 \ft death and reſurrection is the way to life.\f* the waies of life knowen to me, thou shalt make me ful of ioy with \f x \fr 15.10 \ft Perfect glorie conſiſteth in ſeing God.\f* thy countenance: delectations on thy right hand, euen to \f y \fr 15.10 \ft in eternity.\f* the end.
\c 16
\cd A iuſt mans prayer in tribulation, 10. deſcribing his enemies cruelty, 13. by way of imprecation foresheweth their deſtruction, 15. and declareth that the iuſt shal be ſatisfied in glorie. \f - \ft God's providence protecting the juſt. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 The prayer of Dauid. HEARE ô Lord my \f a \fr 16.1 \ft in my iuſt cauſe heare my petition.\f* iuſtice: attend my petition: With thine eares heare my prayer, \f b \fr 16.1 \ft ſeing I pray ſincerly, not in feaned affection.\f* not in deceitful lippes.
\v 2 From \f c \fr 16.2 \ft Thou that ſeeſt al thinge declare my right againſt mine aduerſaries.\f* thy countenanee let my iudgement procede: let thine eies ſee equities.
\v 3 Thou haſt proued \f d \fr 16.3 \ft my intention\f* my hart, and viſited it \f e \fr 16.3 \ft moſt ſecret cogitations.\f* by night: \f f \fr 16.3 \ft by tribulations\f* by fire thou haſt examined me, \f g \fr 16.3 \ft whoſe conſcience is pure from greuous ſinne, may pray with this confidence, otherwiſe repentance is firſt neceſſarie. But the whole Church may euer pray in this maner, becauſe there be alwayes ſome iuſt and holy, in reſpect of whom it is truly called holy.\f* and there is no iniquitie found in me.
\v 4 That my mouth ſpeake not the workes of men: for the \f h \fr 16.4 \ft for thy precepts.\f* wordes of thy lippes I haue kept the \f i \fr 16.4 \ft the narrow way of vertue.\f* hard wayes.
\v 5 \f k \fr 16.5 \ft none can of them ſelues walke rightly, but by Gods helpe.\f* Perfite my paſes in thy pathes: that my ſteppes be not moued.
\v 6 I haue cried, becauſe thou haſt heard me ô God: incline thyne eare to me, and heare my wordes.
\v 7 Make thy mercies meruelous, which ſaueſt them that hope in thee.
\v 8 From them that reſiſt \f l \fr 16.8 \ft againſt thy omnipotent powre.\f* thy right hand keepe me, as the apple of the eie. Vnder the shadowe of thy winges protect me:
\v 9 from the \f m \fr 16.9 \ft from their cruel and furious countenance.\f* face of the impious, that haue afflicted me. Mine enemies haue compaſſed my ſoule,
\v 10 they haue shut vp their \f n \fr 16.10 \ft they haue shut out al pittie or commiſeration.\f* fatte; their mouth hath ſpoken pride.
\v 11 Caſting me forth now haue they compaſſed me: they haue ſette their eies to bend them \f o \fr 16.11 \ft They intend vtterly to deſtroy me euen to the ground.\f* vnto the earth.
\v 12 They haue taken me as a lion readie to the pray: and as a lions whelpe dwelling in hid places.
\v 13 Ariſe Lord, \f p \fr 16.13 \ft Except God preuent, mans induſtry is not ſufficient.\f* preuent him, and ſupplant him: deliuer my ſoule from the impious, \f q \fr 16.13 \ft reſtraine their powre, which they haue by thy permiſſion, that they may not perſecute ſo much as they intend.\f* thy ſword
\v 14 from the enemies of thy hand. Lord from \f r \fr 16.14 \ft This is a prophecy, that the wicked which are many, shal at the day of iudgement be ſeparated from the elect.\f* a few out of the land diuide them, \f s \fr 16.14 \ft Which iudgement beginneth ſometimes in this life.\f* in their life: their bellie is filled of \f t \fr 16.14 \ft the pleaſures of this world, which God approueth not, nor acknowledgeth amongst good thinges.\f* thy ſecretes. They are filled with children: and they haue leaft their remnantes to their litle ones.
\v 15 But \f v \fr 16.15 \ft the iuſt shal be approued.\f* I in iuſtice shal appeare to thy ſight: I shal be \f w \fr 16.15 \ft Nothing doth ſatiate mans mind, but the ſight of God in eternal glorie.\f* filled when thy glorie shal appeare?
\c 17
\cd King Dauids thankes to God for his often deliuerie from great dangers, firſt in general, 9. then more particularly deſcribeth Gods terrible maner of fighting for him, 18. againſt his cruel, and otherwiſe potent enimies: 22. attributing the ſame to Gods good pleaſure, and iuſtice of his cauſe, 31. praiſeth God, 33. his only protector. 41. and depreſſer of his enemies. \f - \ft David ſingularly protected by God. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 17.1 \ft Though literally this Pſalme perteyned to Dauid, yet in figure of Chriſt: and of the Church, or euerie iuſt ſoule.\f* the end, \f b \fr 17.1 \ft The Holie Ghoſt inſpired Dauid to render theſe thankes for his often deliuerie from dangers.\f* to the ſeruant of our Lord Dauid, who ſpake to our Lord the wordes of this canticle, in the day, that our Lord deliuered him out of the hand of al his enemies, and out of the hand of \f c \fr 17.1 \ft Saul is ſpecially named becauſe he was his moſt potent worldlie enimie.\f* Saul, and he ſaid: (2. Reg. 22.) \f 1 \fr 17.1 \ft (2. Reg. 22.)\f* I wil \f d \fr 17.1 \ft Theſe firſt wordes ( as alſo diuers others ) are added, and many changed in this and other Pſalmes by the Septuagint, who often leauing the Hebrew text render the ſenſe, and ſo this agreeth in ſubſtance with the ſame Pſalme recorded. 2. Reg. 22.\f* loue thee ô Lord \f e \fr 17.1 \ft by whom I am ſtrong.\f* my ſtrength:
\v 2 Our Lord is my firmament, and my refuge, and my deliuere.
\v 3 My God is my helper, and I wil hope in him. My protectour and the \f f \fr 17.3 \ft high & firme ſaluation.\f* horne of my ſaluation, and my receiuer. \x - \xt Heb. 2.\x*
\v 4 Prayſing I wil inuocate our Lord: and I shal be ſaued from mine enemies.
\v 5 The \f g \fr 17.5 \ft This is aptly applied to al mankind after his fal, declaring our ſtate in ſinne, and inducing to penance, in the office of Maſſe on Septuageſima ſunday.\f* ſorrowes of \f h \fr 17.5 \ft mortal flesh ſubiect to death.\f* death haue compaſſed me: and \f i \fr 17.5 \ft Violent incurſions of tentions to ſinne.\f* torrentes of iniquitie haue trubled me.
\v 6 The ſorrowes of \f k \fr 17.6 \ft exceding great afflictiōs of mind, like to torments of hel, which I alſo feare.\f* hel haue compaſſed me: the \f l \fr 17.6 \ft ſecrete tentations haue deceiued me.\f* ſnares of death haue preuented me.
\v 7 In my tribulation I haue inuocated our Lord, and haue \f m \fr 17.7 \ft Earneſt prayer is the beſt remedie in al tribulations.\f* cried to my God: And \f n \fr 17.7 \ft As it is certaine that God heard Dauids prayers; ſo he aſſuredly heareth al that ſincerly flee vnto him.\f* he hath heard my voice from his holie temple: and my crie in his ſight, hath entered into his eares.
\v 8 The earth was shaken & trembled: the fundations of mountaines were trubled, and were moued, \f o \fr 17.8 \ft Gods anger againſt ſinne maketh high and loftie thinges to shake, euen the moſt obſtinate preſumptuous ſinners.\f* becauſe he was wrath with them.
\v 9 \f p \fr 17.9 \ft Diuine wrath is like to ſmoke of the noſethrles, or flaming fire, and burning coles.\f* Smoke aroſe in his wrath: and fire flamed vp from his face: coles were kindled from him.
\v 10 He \f q \fr 17.10 \ft Gods punishment ſometimes cometh ſo ſwiftly, as if the heauens bowed towards the earth.\f* bowed the heauens, and deſcended: and \f r \fr 17.10 \ft Gods furie is as a darke deſolate night, or horrible miſt.\f* darkeneſſe vnder his feete.
\v 11 And he \f r \fr 17.11 \ft Yet whẽ ſinners repēt God moſt ſpeedely, as fleing with winges of mercie, comforteth & protecteth them.\f* aſcended vpon the cherubs, and flew: he flew vpon the wings of windes.
\v 12 And he put darkeneſſe his \f s \fr 17.12 \ft God being in him ſelfe incõprehenſible, is alſo ſecret in his determinations, and couert in his procedinges or actes.\f* couert, his tabernacle is round about him: darkeſome water in the cloudes of the aire.
\v 13 Becauſe of the \f t \fr 17.13 \ft Gods ſplēdor oppreſſing mãs ſenſe, yet inſtructeth him by his meruelous vvorkes. VVhich myſtically ſignifieth, that Chriſt illuminateth the vvorld by his Apoſtles, and other preachers denouncing his iuſtice, peace, and his vvil in al thinges perteyning to man.\f* brightneſſe in his ſight the cloudes paſſed, hayle and coles of fire.
\v 14 And our Lord thundered from heauen, and the Higheſt gaue his voice: haile and coles of fire.
\v 15 And he shot his arrowes, and diſſipated them: he multiplied lightnings, and trubled them.
\v 16 And the fountaynes of waters appeared, and the fundations of the world were reueled. At thy rebuke ô Lord, at the blaſt of the ſpirit of thy wrath.
\v 17 He ſent from on high, and tooke me: and he receiued me out of manie \f v \fr 17.17 \ft from tribulations.\f* waters.
\v 18 He deliuered me \f w \fr 17.18 \ft From Saul, Abſolō, Moabites, Ammonites, and al temporal and ſpiritual enemies.\f* from my moſt ſtrong enemies, and from them that hated me: becauſe they were made ſtrong ouer me.
\v 19 \f 1 \fr 17.19 \ft So in the reſt of this Pſalme the Prophet ſpeaketh for moſt part in proper termes, vvithout Metaphores or other figures. Yet in the myſtical ſenſe of Chriſt, and Chriſtians.\f* They preuented me in the day of mine affliction: and our Lord was made my protectour.
\v 20 And he brought me out into largeneſſe: he ſaued me, becauſe he \f x \fr 17.20 \ft Of his good pleaſure without my deſertes.\f* would me.
\v 21 And our Lord wil reward me according to my iuſtice, and according to the purity of my handes he wil reward me.
\v 22 Becauſe I haue kept the waies of our Lord, neither haue I done impiouſly from my God.
\v 23 Becauſe al his iudgementes are in my ſight: and his iuſtices I haue not repelled from me.
\v 24 And I shal be immaculate \f y \fr 17.24 \ft by his grace\f* with him; and shal keepe me from mine iniquitie.
\v 25 \f z \fr 17.25 \ft He repeteth the 21. verſe, inculcating that God wil render to euerie one as they deſerue.\f* And our Lord wil reward me according to my iuſtice: and according to the puritie of my handes in the ſight of his eies.
\v 26 With the holie thou shalt be holie, and with the innocent man thou shalt be innocent.
\v 27 And with the elect thou shalt be elect: and with the peruerſe thou shalt be peruerted.
\v 28 Becauſe thou wilt ſaue the humble people: and the eies of the proud thou wilt humble.
\v 29 Becauſe thou doſt illuminate my lampe ô Lord: my God illuminate my darkeneſſe.
\v 30 Becauſe in thee I shal be deliuered from tentation, and in my God I shal \f a \fr 17.30 \ft paſſe ouer al difficulties.\f* goe ouer the wal.
\v 31 My God his \f b \fr 17.31 \ft Gods precepts.\f* way is vnpolluted: the wordes of our Lord are examined by fire: he is protector of al that hope in him.
\v 32 For \f c \fr 17.32 \ft One only God, Creator and Sauiour of al.\f* who is God but our Lord? or who is God but our God?
\v 33 God that girded me with ſtrength: and made my way immaculate.
\v 34 That perfited my feete as it were of hartes: and ſetting me vpon high thinges.
\v 35 That teacheth my handes to battel: and haſt put mine armes, \f d \fr 17.35 \ft Amongſt other actes, Dauid killed a lion, and a beare, & Goliath. 1. Reg. 17.\f* as a bow of braſſe.
\v 36 And haſt geuen me the protection of thy ſaluation: and thy right hand hath receiued me: And thy diſcipline hath corrected me vnto the end: and thy diſcipline the ſame shal teach me.
\v 37 Thou haſt enlarged my paſes vnder me: and my ſteppes are not weakened:
\v 38 I wil purſew myne enemies, and ouertake them: and wil not returne til they faile.
\v 39 I wil breake them, neither shal they be able to ſtand: they shal fal vnder my feete.
\v 40 And thou haſt girded me with ſtrength to battel: and haſt ſupplanted them that riſe againſt me vnder me.
\v 41 And \f e \fr 17.41 \ft As God geueth ſtreingth to his ſeruāts; ſo he diminisheth the natural ſtreingth and corage of his enemies.\f* myne enemies thou haſt geuen me their backe, and them that hate me thou haſt deſtroyed.
\v 42 They cried, neither was there that would ſaue them, to our Lord; neither did he heare them.
\v 43 And I wil breake them to powder, as the duſt before the face of winde: as the durt of the ſtreates I wil deſtroy them.
\v 44 Thou wilt deliuer me from the contradictions of the people: thou wilt appoynte me to be head of the Gentiles.
\v 45 A people, \f f \fr 17.45 \ft Conuerſion of Gentiles to Chriſt, as the Moabites, Idumeans, and others were ſubdued by Dauid. 1. Par. 11. 14. 18. 19. 20.\f* which I knew not, hath ſerued me: in the hearing of the eare it hath obeyed me.
\v 46 The \f g \fr 17.46 \ft The reuolting and reprobation of the Iewes prefigured by Abſolons rebellion and others. \+it 2. Reg. 15. 16\+it*.\f* children being alienes haue lyed to me, the children alienes are inueterated, and haue halted from their pathes.
\v 47 Our Lord liueth, and bleſſed be my God, and the God of my ſaluation be exalted.
\v 48 O God \f h \fr 17.48 \ft God ſtil protecteth the Church of Chriſt, as he preſerued Dauid.\f* which geueſt me reuenges, & ſubdeweſt peoples vnder me, my deliuerer from mine angrie enemies.
\v 49 And from them that riſe vp againſt me thou wilt exalt me: from the vniuſt man thou wilt deliuer me.
\v 50 Therfore wil I confeſſe to thee among nations ô Lord: and wil ſay \f i \fr 17.50 \ft Vſe of Pſalmes is most frequent in the Church of Gentiles. See the proemial Annotations. \+it page. 12\+it*.\f* a pſalme to thy name,
\v 51 Magnifying the ſaluations of his king, and doing mercie to his Chriſt Dauid, and to his ſeede for euer.
\c 18
\cd Gods perfect goodnes and glorie is shewed by his great workes, and by his Apoſtles ſent with heauenlie commiſſion, to preach in al tongues to al nations. 6. Chriſt coming into the world, and returne vnto heauen. 8. his immaculate Law: 13. Wherin notwiſtanding the iuſt shal haue nede to pray for remiſſion of ſmaller and daylie ſinnes. \f - \ft Propagation of the Catholic faith.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 18.1 \ft perteyning to the beloued of the new Teſtament.\f* the end, the pſalme of Dauid.
\v 2 THE \f b \fr 18.2 \ft The ſilent workes of God declare his Maieſtie to them that conſider therof; his preachers declare the ſame by wordes, to al that wil heare.\f* heauens shew forth the glorie of God, and the firmament declareth the workes of his handes.
\v 3 \f c \fr 18.3 \ft The conſtant courſe of times sheweth that the ſame was diſpoſed by Gods powre, and dayly propagation of faith, eſpecially of Chriſtian doctrine sheweth Chriſts powre, & aſſured perpetuitie of his Church.\f* Day vnto day vttereth word: and night vnto night sheweth knowledge.
\v 4 There are \f d \fr 18.4 \ft Some of euery language or natiõ haue beleued in Chriſt, receiuing the Catholique Religion.\f* no languages, nor ſpeaches, whoſe voyces are not heard.
\v 5 \f e \fr 18.5 \ft S. Paul affirmeth that this hath bene fulfilled by the Apoſtles preaching in al the world. Rom. 10.\f* Their ſound hath gone forth into al the earth; and vnto the endes of the round world the wordes of them.
\v 6 He put his tabernacle in \f f \fr 18.6 \ft By the ſunne a moſt excellent and ſuperelemental creature, the Prophet deſcribeth Chriſt, making his courſe through this world, illuminating, comforting and ſtreingthning the Church his tabernacle, wherein he perpetually dwelleth.\f* the ſunne: & himſelf as a \f g \fr 18.6 \ft Chriſt the bridgrome, and the Church his bride are neuer diuorſed; his loue, wiſdome, and powre, euer conſeruing her by his immaculute law.\f* bridgrome coming forth of his bridechamber. He hath reioyced as a giant to runne the way,
\v 7 his comming forth from the toppe of heauen: And his recourſe euen to the toppe therof: neither is there that can hide him ſelfe from his heate.
\v 8 The law of our Lord is immaculate \f h \fr 18.8 \ft The old law was likewiſe pure in it ſelfe and holy, but the new alſo maketh the obſeruers immaculate.\f* conuerting ſoules: the teſtimonie of our Lord is faithful, geuing wiſedome to litle ones.
\v 9 The iuſtices of our Lord be right, making hartes ioyful: the precept of our Lord lightſome; illuminating the eies.
\v 10 The feare of our Lord is holie, permanent for euer and euer: the iudgmentes of our Lord be true, iuſtified in themſelues.
\v 11 To be deſired aboue gold and much pretious ſtone: and more ſweete aboue honie and the honie combe.
\v 12 For thy ſeruant \f i \fr 18.12 \ft How ſwete the law of God is, his ſeruantes finde not by reading, or by hearing only, but by keping it.\f* keepeth them, in keeping them is \f k \fr 18.12 \ft Conformably to this text the prophete profeſſeth (Pſal. 118. v. 12.) that he kept them for reward, in which place the heretikes tranſlation is corrupted.\f* much reward.
\v 13 Sinnes \f l \fr 18.13 \ft None in this life knoweth and feareth perfectly his owne eſtate, whether he be worthie of loue or hate, Eccle. 9 but hopeth.\f* who vnderſtandeth? from my ſecrete ſinnes cleanſe me:
\v 14 and from other mens ſpare thy ſeruant. If \f m \fr 18.14 \ft If mortal ſinnes haue not dominion in the ſoule it is iuſt: and shal be in time immaculate from al ſinne.\f* they shal not haue dominion ouer me, then shal I be immaculate; and shal be cleanſed from the greateſt ſinne.
\v 15 And the wordes of my mouth shal be ſuch as may pleaſe: and the meditation of my hart in thy ſight alwayes. O Lord \f n \fr 18.15 \ft Gods helping grace is ſtil neceſſarie to perſeuer,\f* my helper, and my \f o \fr 18.15 \ft as his firſt grace redeeming man is neſſarie for our firſt conuerſion,\f* redemer.
\c 19
\cd Faithful people ioyne their prayer, for their Prince or Prelate, 4. with ſacrifice offered for his preſeruation, and proſperous ſucceſſe. \f - \ft The ſubiects prayer for their ſuperior. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 19.1 \ft Though ſuch a prayer was very fitly made for Dauid, Ezechias, or other kinges of Iuda, yet it more properly ſerueth for Chriſtian Kinges and Prelates.\f* the end, the Pſalme of Dauid.
\v 2 OVr Lord heare \f b \fr 19.2 \ft The king, or other ſuperior praying for him ſelfe, his ſubiects pray with him, and for him. It may alſo be applied to Chriſt, praying whiles he vvas mortal, or novv praying for his myſtical body the Church, but in both theſe caſes, our prayers are only neceſſary for his ſeruantes not for him.\f* thee in the day of tribulation: the name of the God of Iacob protect thee.
\v 3 Send he ayde to thee from the holie place: and from Sion defend he thee.
\v 4 Be he mindeful of al thy \f c \fr 19.4 \ft The Hebrevv vvord MINCA ſignifieth ſacrifice of floure, and vnbloudy, ſo perteyneth to the Euchariſt, in forme of bread, and vvine.\f* ſacrifice: and be thy holocauſt made \f d \fr 19.4 \ft be acceptable for him, for vvhom it is offered.\f* fatte.
\v 5 Geue he vnto thee according to thy hart: and confirme he al thy counſel.
\v 6 We shal reioyce \f e \fr 19.6 \ft in the proſperous ſtate of thee our ſuperiour.\f* in thy ſaluation: and in the name of our God we shal be magnified.
\v 7 Our Lord accomplish al thy petitions: now haue I knowen that our Lord hath ſaued \f f \fr 19.7 \ft his anointed king, or Prieſt, Myſtically faithful people acknovvlege the victory of Chriſt our Sauiour ouer death and al enemies.\f* his \sc CHRIST\sc*. He shal heare him from his holie heauen: the ſaluation of his righthand is, \f g \fr 19.7 \ft in great ſtreingth, or heauenlie forces.\f* in powers.
\v 8 Theſe in chariotes, and theſe in horſes: but we wil inuocate in the name of the Lord our God.
\v 9 \f i \fr 19.9 \ft men truſting in humane and worldly powre fal into captiuitie.\f* They are bound, and haue fallen: but we haue riſen and are ſet vpright.
\v 10 Lord \f k \fr 19.10 \ft By protection of the head the body is alſo conſerued.\f* ſaue the king, and heare vs in the day, that we shal inuocate thee.
\c 20
\cd Praiſe to God for Chriſts exaltation after his paſſion: 9. and depreſſion of his enemies. \f - \ft Chriſt's exaltation. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 20.1 \ft Perteyning to the nevv Teſtament, principally to Chrriſt, partly to godly and victorious kinges, and generally to al the bleſſed, vvhich ouercome ſpiritual enemies.\f* the end, the Pſalme of Dauid.
\v 2 LOrd in \f b \fr 20.2 \ft Chriſt our king as man ouercoming his enemies by his diuine povvre, reioyceth in victorie.\f* thy power the king shal be glad: and vpon thy ſaluation he shal reioyce excedingly.
\v 3 The \f c \fr 20.3 \ft Chriſts moſt ſpecial deſire was the ſaluation of his people.\f* deſire of his hart thou haſt geuen him: and of the wil of his lippes thou haſt not defrauded him.
\v 4 Becauſe thou haſt \f d \fr 20.4 \ft This moſt principally verified in Chriſt, is alſo applied to Martyres, vvhich ſuffer or are readie in preparation of mind to ſuffer death for the truth.\f* preuented him in bleſſinges of ſweetneſſe: thou haſt put on his head a crowne of pretious ſtone.
\v 5 He asked life of thee: and thou gaueſt him length of daies for euer, and for euer and euer.
\v 6 Great is his glorie in thy ſaluation: glorie and great beautie thou shalt put vpon him.
\v 7 Becauſe thou shalt geue him \f e \fr 20.7 \ft Al Sainctes receiue bleſſing of glorie, but only Chriſt imperteth ſuch grace to others: for in him al are bleſſed that are eternally glorified. \+it Gen. 21. Ioan. 1\+it*.\f* to be a bleſſing for euer and euer: thou shalt make him ioyful in gladeneſſe with thy countenance.
\v 8 Becauſe the king hopeth in our Lord: and in the mercie of the Higheſt he shal not be moued.
\v 9 Let \f f \fr 20.9 \ft The iuſt conforming their deſires to Gods vvil, do pray that the vvicked may be punished. It is alſo a prophecie, that ſo it shal come to paſſe.\f* thy hand be found of al thy enemies: let thy righthand find al, that hate thee.
\v 10 Thou \f g \fr 20.10 \ft And ſo it folovveth in the next verſe: \+it Thou shalt put them\+it* &c.\f* shalt put them as an ouen of fyre \f h \fr 20.10 \ft This alſo can not be vnderſtood of anie but of Chriſt, vvho in the general or particular iudgement ſhal geue ſentence vpon the vvicked.\f* in the time of thy countenance: our Lord in his wrath \f i \fr 20.10 \ft And immediatly hel fire shal deuoure them.\f* shal truble them, and fyre shal deuoure them.
\v 11 Their \f k \fr 20.11 \ft Al the wicked, and namely the Iewes who perſecuted Chriſt, faile in their deuiſes.\f* fruite thou shalt deſtroy from the land: and their ſeede from the children of men.
\v 12 Becauſe they haue turned the euils vpon thee: they haue deuiſed counſels, which they could not eſtablish.
\v 13 Becauſe thou shalt put them backe: in thy remnantes thou shalt prepare their countenance.
\v 14 Be exalted Lord in thy powre: \f l \fr 20.14 \ft whiles the wicked perish, the iuſt reioyce and praiſe God in ſongue and Pſalme.\f* we wil chaunte and ſing thy powers.
\c 21
\cd Chriſt prayeth in his Paſſion, 7. deſcribeth the acerbitie therof; 20. foresheweth by way of prayer his Reſurrection: 23. more clerly the fundation and propagation of his Church ( 27. & 30. interpoſing the ſingular foode of the moſt bleſſed Sacrament ) euen to the endes of the earth in al nations. \f - \ft Chriſt's Paſſion & effects thereof. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end for the \f a \fr 21.1 \ft Redemptiõ of mankind vndertakē by Chriſt, and performed by his death, beganne to be shewed by his Reſurrection in the morning of the third day. \+it S. Aug\+it*.\f* morning enterpriſe, the Pſalme \f b \fr 21.1 \ft in figure of Chriſt the \+it beloued of God\+it*.\f* of Dauid.
\v 2 GOD \f c \fr 21.2 \ft God is God of al his creatures, but in more peculiar ſorte God of Chriſt by perſonal vnion.\f* my God haue reſpect to me: \f d \fr 21.2 \ft Thou neither deliuereſt me frõ dying: nor yeldeſt me ſuch comforth, as thou voutſafeſt to other Sainctes. · \+it v. 6. Mat. 27.\+it*\f* why haſt thou forſaken me? \f e \fr 21.2 \ft I am neither deliuered from dying, nor comforted in my paſſion, hauing vndertaken to dye for the ſinnes of mankind, & reputed them as myn owne wordes or factes.\f* far from my ſaluation are the wordes of my ſinnes.
\v 3 My God I shal crie \f f \fr 21.3 \ft Albeit I crie by day,\f* by day, \f g \fr 21.3 \ft and by night, on the croſſe and in the garden, to haue the chalice of death remoued from me, and shal not be heard,\f* and thou wilt not heare: and by night, and \f h \fr 21.3 \ft Yet there is no follie, nor fault in this petition, which is with ſubmiſſion of mans wil to Gods wil.\f* not for follie vnto me.
\v 4 But \f i \fr 21.4 \ft Thou from heauen wilt heare when it is conuenient.\f* thou dwelleſt in the holie place, \f k \fr 21.4 \ft Whoſe wiſdome and goodnes the Church worthely praiſeth.\f* the prayſe of Iſrael.
\v 5 In thee \f l \fr 21.5 \ft Patriarkes, Prophets, and other holy mẽ praying in their diſtreſſes obtained their requeſtes.\f* our fathers haue hoped: they hoped, and thou didſt deliuer them.
\v 6 They cried to thee, and were \f m \fr 21.6 \ft Were temporally deliuered by thy mightie hand from their perſecuters.\f* ſaued: they hoped in thee, and were \f n \fr 21.6 \ft not fruſtrate of their humble prayers.\f* not confounded.
\v 7 \f o \fr 21.7 \ft God that comforted his ſeruantes in their tribulations, leift Chriſt without his ordinarie conſolation, to ſuffer more then euer any other did.\f* But I am a \f p \fr 21.7 \ft wicked perſecuters reſpected not Chriſt as a man, but contemned him, as a very worme\f* worme and no man: a \f q \fr 21.7 \ft as moſt reprochful of al men.\f* reproch of men and \f r \fr 21.7 \ft as the baſeſt of al the people.\f* outcaſt of the people.
\v 8 \f s \fr 21.8 \ft Almoſt al became Chriſts cruel enimies at the time of his paſſion, afflicting, blaſpheming, and ſcorning him, as the Euangeliſtes record.\f* Al that ſee me haue ſcorned me: they haue ſpoken with the lippes, and wagged the head.
\v 9 \f t \fr 21.9 \ft The Euangeliſtes write how al theſe thinges were fulfilled by wicked men ſpeaking theſe blaſphemies, and reproches.\f* He hoped in the Lord, let him deliuer him: let him ſaue him, becauſe he \f v \fr 21.9 \ft God ſemed to be wel pleaſed with Chriſt, as with his owne Sonne: if it be ſo, let him deliuer him from theſe afflictions, ſay theſe blaſphemers.\f* wil him.
\v 10 Becauſe thou art he that \f w \fr 21.10 \ft diuine powre without man formed me in the wombe of my mother a virgin.\f* haſt drawen me out of the wombe: my hope from the breſtes of my mother.
\v 11 Vpon thee I \f x \fr 21.11 \ft As I haue no father but thee O God: ſo without intermiſſion from myn incarnation to this time, I haue had thee my protector:\f* haue bene caſt from the matrice: \f y \fr 21.11 \ft leaue me not now without comforte ſeing I muſt dye as thou haſt determined, and I freely conſented: yet leaue me not in death but raiſe me againe to life. \+it Pſal. 15. v. 9. 10\+it*.\f* from my mothers wombe thou art my God,
\v 12 depart not from me. Becauſe tribulation is verie nigh: becauſe there is \f z \fr 21.12 \ft Almoſt al are become myn enemies, and thoſe few that would, can not helpe me.\f* not that wil helpe.
\v 13 Manie \f a \fr 21.13 \ft Delicate laſciuious yongmen,\f* calues haue compaſſed me: \f b \fr 21.13 \ft and the ſcribes Phariſes and elders of the people haue al conſpired againſt me.\f* fatte bulles haue beſieged me.
\v 14 They haue \f c \fr 21.14 \ft condemning me and perſwading the people to crie: \+it Crucifie, crucifie him\+it*.\f* opened their mouth vpon me, as a lion rauening and roaring.
\v 15 As \f d \fr 21.15 \ft So weakned with paines of torments, as fluide water not able to conſiſt.\f* water I am powred out: and al \f e \fr 21.15 \ft My bones and ſtrongeſt partes of my bodie are weakned, verified, when our Sauiour fel downe vnder his croſſe.\f* my bones are diſperſed. \f f \fr 21.15 \ft the part that firſt and laſt liueth is weakened as ſoft waxe by heat of the fire, and ready to faile.\f* My hart is made as waxe melting in the middes of my bellie.
\v 16 My ſtrength is withered as a \f g \fr 21.16 \ft al my powres, and radical humiditie is dried vp, as a potters veſſel is baked in the furnace.\f* pot-shard, and my tongue \f h \fr 21.16 \ft Through exceding great drught, which our Sauiour profeſſed on the croſſe ſaying: \+it I thirſt\+it*.\f* cleaued to my iawes: and thou haſt \f i \fr 21.16 \ft thus thou O God haſt ſuffered me to come to the laſt breath of life, next to death. Yet finally our Sauiour gaue vp his ſpirite, before he ſhould haue died. \+it v. 21\+it*.\f* brought me downe into the duſt of death.
\v 17 Becauſe manie \f k \fr 21.17 \ft Agane this royal Prophet recounteth by whom, and how our B. Sauiour ſhould ſuffer, euen as clere as the Euangeliſtes afterwards haue written the hiſtorie.\f* dogges haue compaſſed me: the counſel of the malignant hath beſieged me. They haue digged my handes and my feete:
\v 18 they haue \f l \fr 21.18 \ft Ioan. 19. · Our Sauiours body was ſo racked on the croſſe, that his bones might be ſeene and counted.\f* numbered al my bones. But themſelues haue \f m \fr 21.18 \ft The perſecuters vvittingly determined al this crueltie, beheld it vvith their eyes, and vvithout al compaſſion perſiſted in malice, reioyced, and blaſphemed.\f* conſidered and beheld me:
\v 19 they haue \f n \fr 21.19 \ft the ſouldiars that crucified our Sauiour, taking his garmentes for their praye:\f* deuided my garmentes among them, and vpon my \f o \fr 21.19 \ft yet in myſterie of his Church, diuided not his coate.\f* veſture they haue caſt lot.
\v 20 But thou Lord \f p \fr 21.20 \ft He prophecieth Chriſts ſpeedy reſurrection.\f* prolong not thy helpe from me: looke toward my defenſe.
\v 21 Deliuer, ô God, \f q \fr 21.21 \ft Chriſts ſaul vvas not ſeperated from his bodie by force of the torments, but he preuenting death freely yelded vp his ſpirite. \+it Ioan. 10. v. 9. & 10\+it*.\f* my ſoule from the ſword: and myne \f r \fr 21.21 \ft the moſt pure, and ſanctified ſoule, of vvhoſe fulnes al other iuſt ſoules are ſanctified:\f* onlie one from \f s \fr 21.21 \ft that it ſtay not in hel, vvhich deuoured al other ſoules in the old Teſtament.\f* the hand of the dog.
\v 22 Saue me out of the lions mouth: and my humilitie from the hornes of vnicornes.
\v 23 I wil \f t \fr 21.23 \ft The propagation of the Church of Chriſt in al nations.\f* declare thy name to my bretheren: in the middes of the Church I wil prayſe thee.
\v 24 Ye that feare our Lord praiſe him: al the \f v \fr 21.24 \ft not the carnal but ſpiritual children of Iacob, Iſaac, and Abraham. \+it Rom. 9. v. 8\+it*.\f* ſeede of Iacob glorifie ye him:
\v 25 Let al the ſeede of Iſrael feare him: becauſe he hath not contemned, nor deſpiſed the petition of the poore. Neither hath he turned away his face from me: and when I cried to him he heard me.
\v 26 With thee is my praiſe in \f w \fr 21.26 \ft the Church gethered both of Ievves and Gentiles is very great and vniuerſal.\f* the great Church: I \f x \fr 21.26 \ft Our Sauiour promiſed to geue his ovvne bodie, the bread of life \+it Ioan. 6\+it*. and performed the ſame at his laſt ſupper.\f* wil render my vowes in the ſight of them that feare him.
\v 27 The \f y \fr 21.27 \ft thoſe that be faithful, humble and poore in ſpirit participat the fruict of this moſt excellent Sacrament.\f* poore shal eate, and shalbe filled: and they shal praiſe our Lord that ſeeke after him: their hartes \f z \fr 21.27 \ft The effect of this B. Sacrament is the reſurrection in glorie, and life euerlaſting.\f* shal liue for euer and euer.
\v 28 Al the endes of the earth \f a \fr 21.28 \ft Gentiles which haue bene idolaters shal recollect themſelues, when they heare Chriſt preached, and shal turne to true Religion.\f* shal remember, and be conuerted to our Lord: And al the families of the Gentiles shal adore in his ſight.
\v 29 \f b \fr 21.29 \ft Although men can neither deſerue to be conuerted, nor to perſeuere in iuſtice; yet Chriſt meriteth to haue a continual kingdom; which is the perpetual viſible Catholique Church.\f* Becauſe the kingdome is our Lords: and he shal haue dominion ouer the Gentiles.
\v 30 Al the \f c \fr 21.30 \ft Not only the poore ſorte, but alſo the mightie ones of the world shal be conuerted to Chriſt, participate his B. Bodie in the Sacrament:\f* fatte ones of the earth haue eaten, and \f d \fr 21.30 \ft and religiouſly adore the ſame.\f* adored: in his ſight shal al fal, \f e \fr 21.30 \ft Al that adore God, ſhad adore him in this Sacraments.\f* that deſcend into the earth.
\v 31 And \f f \fr 21.31 \ft Death being once ouercome, it shal haue no more powre.\f* my ſoule shal liue to him: and my \f g \fr 21.31 \ft Againe the prophet inculcateth the continuance of the Catholique Church.\f* ſeede shal ſerue him.
\v 32 The generation to come shal be shewed to our Lord: and the \f h \fr 21.32 \ft Apoſtles, and other preachers of Chriſt.\f* heauens shal shew forth his iuſtice to the people that shal be borne, whom our Lord hath made.
\c 22
\cd A forme of thankeſgeuing for al ſpiritual benefites ( deſcribed vnder the metaphor of temporal proſperitie ) euen from a ſinners firſt conuerſion, to final perſeuerance, and eternal beatitude. \f - \ft Thanks-giving for God's protection. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 The Pſalme of Dauid. OVr \f a \fr 22.1 \ft Chriſt the good paſtor, gouerneth, protecteth,\f* Lord ruleth me, and nothing shal be wanting to me:
\v 2 in place \f b \fr 22.2 \ft and feedeth his faithful flocke.\f* of paſture there he hath placed me \x - \xt Iſa. 40. Iere. 23. Ezech. 34. Ioan. 10. 1. Pet. 2. & 5.\x*. Vpon \f c \fr 22.2 \ft Baptiſme of regeneration,\f* the water of refection he hath brought me vp:
\v 3 he hath \f d \fr 22.3 \ft which is the firſt iuſtification.\f* conuerted my ſoule. He hath conducted me vpon \f e \fr 22.3 \ft Gods precepts which the baptiſed muſt obſerue. \+it Mat. 28. v. 20\+it*.\f* the pathes of iuſtice, \f f \fr 22.3 \ft Saluation is in the name and powre of Chriſt, not in mans owne merites.\f* for his name.
\v 4 For, although I shal walke in \f g \fr 22.4 \ft in great dangers of tentations to mortal ſinne,\f* the middes of the shadow of death, I wil \f h \fr 22.4 \ft yet by Gods grace we may reſiſt.\f* not feare euils: becauſe thou art with me. Thy \f i \fr 22.4 \ft Gods direction and law is ſtreight,\f* rod and thy \f k \fr 22.4 \ft and ſtrong.\f* ſtaffe: they haue comforted me.
\v 5 Thou haſt prepared in my ſight \f l \fr 22.5 \ft Chriſt hath prepared for our ſpiritual foode the B. Sacrament of the Euchariſt. \+it S. Cyprian. Epiſt. 63. Eutim. in hunc Pſal\+it*.\f* a table, \f m \fr 22.5 \ft againſt al ſpiritual enemies, the world, the flesh, and the diuel.\f* againſt them; that truble me. Thou \f n \fr 22.5 \ft Chriſtian ſoules are alſo ſtreingthned by the Sacraments of Confirmation, Penance, holie Orders, Matrimonie, and Extreme Vnction.\f* haſt fatted my head with oyle: and my \f o \fr 22.5 \ft The B. Sacrament and Sacrifice of Chriſts bodie and bloud.\f* chalice inebriating how goodlie is it!
\v 6 And thy mercie shal folow me \f p \fr 22.6 \ft continual and final peſeuerance is by Gods ſpecial grace.\f* al the dayes of my life: And that I may dwel in the houſe of our Lord, \f q \fr 22.6 \ft in eternal life.\f* in longitude of dayes.
\c 23
\cd Chriſt is Lord of the whole earth, being Creatour, and Redemer of man. 3. Good life (with faith in him) is the way to heauen. 7. whither Chriſt aſcending with triumph Angels admire him. \f - \ft Chriſt Lord of al the world. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 The \f a \fr 23.1 \ft Chriſt riſing from death the firſt day of the weeke, had al powre geuen him in heauen and in earth. \+it Mat. 28\+it*.\f* firſt of the Sabbath, the Pſalme of Dauid. THE earth is our Lordes, and \f b \fr 23.1 \ft Not only the ſoile it ſelfe but al the fruict, and al that dwel therin are Gods.\f* the fulneſſe therof: the round world, and al that dwel therein.
\v 2 Becauſe he hath founded it vpon the ſeas: and vpon the riuers hath prepared it.
\v 3 Who \f c \fr 23.3 \ft Though Chriſt created and redeemed al, yet only the iuſt shal inherite heauen.\f* shal aſcend into the mount of our Lord? or who shal ſtand in his holie place?
\v 4 The innocent of handes, and of cleane hart, that hath not taken his ſoule \f d \fr 23.4 \ft not occupied himſelfe in vaine and vnprofitable thinges, but in commendable workes.\f* in vayne, nor ſworne to his neighbour in guile.
\v 5 He shal receiue bleſſing of our Lord: and \f e \fr 23.5 \ft Gods mercy goeth before iuſtifications iuſt workes folow, and ſo glorie is the reward of al.\f* mercie of God his Sauiour.
\v 6 This is \f f \fr 23.6 \ft This ſorte of people thus ſeruing God, shal receiue euerlaſting bliſſe.\f* the generation of them that ſeeke him, of them that ſeeke the face of the God of Iacob.
\v 7 Lift vp your gates \f g \fr 23.7 \ft The prophet contemplating in ſpirite Chriſts Aſcenſion, inuiteth Angels to receiue him, and ( by proſopopeia ) ſpeaketh alſo to the gates of heauen, by which he is to enter.\f* ye princes, and be ye lifted vp ô eternal gates: and the king of glorie shal enter in,
\v 8 \f h \fr 23.8 \ft Angels anſwer admiring, & demanding as in a dialogue, how Chriſt is become ſo glorious!\f* Who is this king of glorie? Our Lord \f i \fr 23.8 \ft The Prophet anſwereth that Chriſt by his powre hath ouercome al enemies in battel.\f* ſtrong & mightie, our Lord mightie in battel.
\v 9 \f k \fr 23.9 \ft Againe he willeth Angels to open the gates, and biddeth the gates to enlarge them ſelues.\f* Lift vp your gates ye princes, and be ye lifted vp ô eternal gates: and the king of glorie shal enter in.
\v 10 \f l \fr 23.10 \ft the Angels demand as before:\f* Who is this king of glorie? \f m \fr 23.10 \ft the prophet anſwereth: that Chriſt is Lord alſo of Angels, and al heauenlie powres vnder God.\f* The Lord of powers he is the king of glorie.
\c 24
\cd A general prayer of the faithful againſt al enemies, 4. with deſire to be directed in the way of godlines, 7. and to be pardoned for ſinnes paſt. 9. acknowledging Gods meeknes, 17. our weaknes, neceſſitie of helpe, and hope in God: 22. concludeth with prayer for the whole Church. \f - \ft A prayer of the faithful. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 24.1 \ft This Pſalme perteyneth more properly to the new teſtament. And is artificially compoſed: the verſes beginning with diſtinct letters in order of the Hebrew Alphabet, to the laſt verſe.\f* the end, the Pſalme of Dauid. TO THEE ô Lord I haue lifted vp \f b \fr 24.1 \ft my mind, to be attentiue.\f* my ſoule:
\v 2 my God in thee is my confidence, let me \f c \fr 24.2 \ft not be fruſtrate of my petition.\f* not be ashamed:
\v 3 Neither let mine enemies ſcorne me: for al \f d \fr 24.3 \ft that patiently expect the time when God wil aſſiſt.\f* that expect thee, shal not be confounded.
\v 4 \f e \fr 24.4 \ft This maner of praying is frequent in the Pſalmes, ſignifying as a prophecie, that ſo it wil come to paſſe and the conformitie of the iuſt to Gods iuſtice.\f* Let al be confounded that do vniuſt thinges in vayne. Lord shew me thy wayes: and teach me thy pathes.
\v 5 Direct me \f f \fr 24.5 \ft in true faith and religion.\f* in thy truth, and teach me: becauſe thou art God my Sauiour, and thee haue I expected \f g \fr 24.5 \ft al our life we muſt deſire more and more knowlege of true doctrin.\f* al the day.
\v 6 Remember ô Lord thy commiſerations, and thy mercies: that are from the beginning of the world.
\v 7 The ſinnes \f h \fr 24.7 \ft from the firſt vſe of reaſon, at which time manie are careles,\f* of my youth, and \f i \fr 24.7 \ft & negligent to lerne how to ſerue God.\f* my ignorances doe not remember. According to thy mercie remember thou me: for thy goodneſſe ô Lord.
\v 8 Our Lord is \f k \fr 24.8 \ft As God is ſweete in geuing good motions:\f* ſweete, and \f l \fr 24.8 \ft ſo he is ſeuere to them that reſiſt his grace.\f* righteous: for this cauſe he wil geue a law to them that ſinne in the way.
\v 9 He wil direct the milde in iudgement: he wil teach the meeke his wayes.
\v 10 Al the wayes of our Lord, be \f m \fr 24.10 \ft God mercifully preuenteth with his grace:\f* mercie and \f n \fr 24.10 \ft and iuſtly rewardeth good workes.\f* truth, to them that ſeeke after his \f o \fr 24.10 \ft Gods law is his couenant with man:\f* teſtament and his \f p \fr 24.10 \ft and teſtimonie of his wil.\f* teſtimonies.
\v 11 For thy name ô Lord thou wilt be propitious to my ſinne: for \f q \fr 24.11 \ft ſinne in reſpect of auerſion from God is great & nedeth his grace.\f* it is much.
\v 12 Who is the man that \f r \fr 24.12 \ft He that feareth God which is the beginning of wiſdome, receiueth fiue ſpiritual commodities by his law here mentioned:\f* feareth our Lord? he \f 1 \fr 24.12 \ft 1. God inſtructeth him by his law:\f* appoynteth him a law in the way, that he hath choſen.
\v 13 His ſoule \f 1 \fr 24.13 \ft 2. beſtoweth al neceſſaries vpon him:\f* shal abide in good things: and \f 2 \fr 24.13 \ft 3. others shal imitate his good example.\f* his ſeede shal inherite the land.
\v 14 Our Lord is \f 1 \fr 24.14 \ft 4. God wil protect him:\f* a firmament to them that feare him: & \f 2 \fr 24.14 \ft 5. According to Gods couenant he shal enioy the manifeſt ſight of God for his eternal reward.\f* his teſtament that it may be made manifeſt to them.
\v 15 Myne eies are alwayes to our Lord: becauſe he wil plucke my feete out of the ſnare.
\v 16 Haue reſpect to me, and haue mercie on me: becauſe I am \f s \fr 24.16 \ft mans weaknes without Gods helpe.\f* alone and poore.
\v 17 The tribulations of my hart are multiplied: deliuer me from \f t \fr 24.17 \ft Tribulatiõs can not be auoided, but muſt neceſſarily be ſuffered: therfore ô God geue vs grace to paſſe through them without ſinne.\f* my neceſſities.
\v 18 See my \f v \fr 24.18 \ft myn affliction.\f* humiliation, and my labour: and \f w \fr 24.18 \ft take away the cauſe and affliction wil be mitigated.\f* forgeue al my ſinnes.
\v 19 Behold mine enemies, becauſe they are multiplied, and with \f x \fr 24.19 \ft wicked men of hatred do endeuour to draw others into ſinne.\f* vniuſt hatred hated me.
\v 20 Keepe my ſoule, and deliuer me: I shal \f y \fr 24.20 \ft Thoſe that hope in God shal neuer be confounded.\f* not be ashamed, becauſe I hoped in thee.
\v 21 The innocent and righteous haue cleaued to me: becauſe I expected thee.
\v 22 \f z \fr 24.22 \ft Al The letters of the Alphebet being complete in this Pſalme, this laſt verſe beginneth with \+it Pere, Redeeme\+it*, praying God to redeme and deliuer Iſrael, that is, the whole Church from tribulations.\f* Deliuer Iſrael ô God, out of al his tribulations.
\c 25
\cd Dauid in banishment among the Philiſtines, truſteth in the iuſtice of his cauſe, 9. and prayeth God earneſtly to deliuer him, that he may with more freedom and commodity ſerue him as he deſireth. \f - \ft David'ſ prayer diſtreſſed in perſecution. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 25.1 \ft This Pſalme is alſo a conuenient prayer for anie Chriſtian in tribulation.\f* the end the Pſalme of Dauid. IVDGE \f b \fr 25.1 \ft Be thou ô God arbiter of the cauſe betwen Saul and me, thou knoweſt myn innocencie in this behalfe, though I am vniuſtly charged by Saul, and his freindes.\f* me ô Lord becauſe I haue walked in my innocencie: and hoping in our Lord I shal not be weakened.
\v 2 \f c \fr 25.2 \ft Leſt perhaps I be not ſo innocent as I deſire, and as in reſpect of Saul I hope that I am, do thou O God proue me as thou wilt by tribulations.\f* Proue me Lord, and tempt me: burne my reynes and my hart.
\v 3 Becauſe thy mercie is before mine eies: and I am wel pleaſed in thy truth.
\v 4 I \f d \fr 25.4 \ft Dauid in a good conſcience, and zele againſt the wicked, alleageth his ſincere confidence of proceding, more then ordinarie men may do, God ſo inſpiring him extraordinarily.\f* haue not ſitten with the councel of vanitie: and with them that doe vniuſt thinges. I wil not enter in
\v 5 I haue hated the Church of the malignant: and with the impious I wil not ſitte.
\v 6 I \f e \fr 25.6 \ft The reſt of this Pſalme euerie Prieſt reciteth in Maſſe, before he offer the holie Sacrifice, profeſſing, & putting him ſelfe in memorie, that he muſt only communicate with the innocent, or of pure conſcience; · Epi. 120. c. 11.\f* wil wash my handes among innocentes: \f f \fr 25.6 \ft and ſo approch to the Altar. \+it prefigured Leuit. 16. v. 4\+it*.\f* and wil compaſſe thy altar ô Lord:
\v 7 That I \f g \fr 25.7 \ft Shutting the eares of my hart from euil and vaine thoughtes I wil attend to godlie inſpirations,\f* may heare the voice of praiſe, and \f h \fr 25.7 \ft and ſo with mental prayer, and external voice (as the holie order of this ſacred office requireth) praiſe thee ô God in thy meruelous workes.\f* shew forth al thy meruelous workes.
\v 8 Lord I haue loued \f i \fr 25.8 \ft I can not but ſingularly loue the excellencie of this place dedicated to thy ſeruice: where is true faith, vnitie, and charitie of thy people, the guard of holie Angels, the adminiſtration of ſacred myſteries, aſſiſtance of the Holie Ghoſt, real preſence of Chriſt our Lord, al replenished with Diuine maieſtie.\f* the beautie of thy houſe, and the place of \f k \fr 25.8 \ft This repreſenteth vnto me the glorious heauenlie kingdom of God and al Sainctes.\f* the habitation of thy glorie.
\v 9 \f l \fr 25.9 \ft Suffer me not therfore to be contaminate by the wicked, nor to be deuoured with them.\f* Deſtroy not ô God my ſoule with the impious, and my life with bloudie men.
\v 10 In whoſe \f m \fr 25.10 \ft They are ſtil readie to committe more and more iniquities.\f* handes are iniquities: their \f n \fr 25.10 \ft themſelues being corrupted, endeuour by giftes of wordly commodities to corrupt others.\f* righthand is replenished with giftes.
\v 11 But \f o \fr 25.11 \ft Euerie one ought ſo to purge his conſcience, that he may be innocent, or free from great ſinne.\f* I haue walked in mine innocencie: \f p \fr 25.11 \ft deliuer me from this neceſſitie of dwelling among the wicked.\f* redeme me, and haue mercie on me.
\v 12 My \f q \fr 25.12 \ft I intend to walke right.\f* foote hath ſtood in the direct way: in \f r \fr 25.12 \ft I deſire to praiſe thee amongſt thy true faithful ſeruantes.\f* the Churches I wil bleſſe thee ô Lord.
\c 26
\cd Dauid being in great diſtreſſe through perſecution, and hauing aſſured confidence in God, deſcribeth the great ſecuritie of Gods protection. 7. sheweth the ſame experienced in him ſelf. 12. prayeth for continuance therof 13. and incorageth his owne ſoule, in hope of life euerlaſting, to perſeuer in vertue. \f - \ft An other confident prayer of David in tribulation. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 The Pſalme of Dauid \f a \fr 26.1 \ft Before his ſecond annointing, as is probable. \+it 2. Reg. 2\+it*.\f* before he was annoynted. OVr Lord is \f b \fr 26.1 \ft Againſt ignorance God illuminateth his ſeruantes.\f* my illumination, and \f c \fr 26.1 \ft againſt infirmitie he geueth ſtreingth.\f* my ſaluation, \f d \fr 26.1 \ft ſo he nedeth not to feare anie mans malice, ſuteltie, nor force. \+it Luc. 21. v. 15\+it*.\f* whom shal I feare? Our Lord is the protectour of my life, of whom shal I be afrayd?
\v 2 Whiles the harmeful approch vpon me, to eate my flesh. Mine enemies that truble me, them ſelues are weakened and are fallen.
\v 3 If campes ſtand together againſt me, my hart shal not feare. If battel riſe vp againſt me, in this wil I hope.
\v 4 One thing I haue asked of our Lord, this wil I ſeeke for, that I \f e \fr 26.4 \ft How ſpecial a benefite Dauid eſtemed it to be in the Catholique Church the only true houſe of God!\f* may dwel in the houſe of our Lord al the dayes of my life. That I may ſee the pleaſentnes of our Lord, and viſite his temple.
\v 5 Becauſe he hath \f f \fr 26.5 \ft Albeit the ſpiritual or carnal enemie ſeke to ouerthrow me, yet I am ſecure in the Catholique Church.\f* hid me in his tabernacle: in the day of euils he hath protected me, in \f g \fr 26.5 \ft God either ſuffereth not the enemie to find his ſeruant.\f* the ſecrete of his tabernacle.
\v 6 In \f h \fr 26.6 \ft or not to be able to hurt him ſpiritually\f* a rocke he hath exalted me: and now he hath \f i \fr 26.6 \ft when a martyr or confeſſour dieth, then he getteth the victorie againſt the perſecutors.\f* exalted my head ouer mine enemies. I \f k \fr 26.6 \ft Diligently recounting al thy benefites, I render thankes by ſacrifice and praiſe.\f* haue gone round about, and haue immolated in his tabernacle an hoſt of iubilation: I \f l \fr 26.6 \ft not only in hart, but alſo ſinging with loud voice and inſtrument.\f* wil ſing, and ſay a Pſalme to our Lord.
\v 7 Heare ô Lord my voice, wherwith I haue cried to thee: haue mercie on me, and heare me.
\v 8 My \f m \fr 26.8 \ft In my inward ſincere cogitation I deſire, and ſeeke that I may ſee thee,\f* hart hath ſayd to thee, my face hath ſought thee out: \f n \fr 26.8 \ft face to face \+it 1. Cor. 13. v. 12\+it*.\f* thy face ô Lord I wil ſeeke.
\v 9 Turne \f o \fr 26.9 \ft In the meane time ô Lord, grant me thy fauour,\f* not away thy face from me: \f p \fr 26.9 \ft leaue me not though thou be angrie with me.\f* decline not in wrath from thy ſeruant. Be \f q \fr 26.9 \ft He ſpeaketh in the perſon of orphanes.\f* thou my helper: forſake me not, neither deſpiſe me ô God my Sauiour.
\v 10 Becauſe \f r \fr 26.10 \ft Though carnal parentes forſake the iuſt man in tribulation, yet God hath then moſt ſpecial care of him.\f* my father and my mother haue forſaken me: but our Lord hath taken me.
\v 11 Geue me \f s \fr 26.11 \ft Eſtablish my hart in thy law.\f* a law ô Lord in thy way: \f t \fr 26.11 \ft conſerue me in the right way, which thou haſt already taught me, and it is the more neceſſarie, becauſe myne enemies labour to peruert me.\f* and direct me in the right path, becauſe of mine enemies.
\v 12 Deliuer me not into \f v \fr 26.12 \ft the willes.\f* the ſoules of them that truble me: becauſe \f w \fr 26.12 \ft falſe witneſſes accuſed Dauid, others accuſed Chriſt: \+it Mat. 26\+it*. others do ſtil accuſe the iuſt \+it Mat. 5\+it*.\f* vniuſt witneſſes haue riſen vp againſt me, and iniquity hath lyed \f x \fr 26.12 \ft the wicked pleaſe themſelues in lying, but the chief hurt finally turneth vpon themſelues.\f* to it ſelfe.
\v 13 I \f y \fr 26.13 \ft The prophet and al iuſt men are comforted by God, and hope of reward in heauen.\f* beleue to ſee the good things of our Lord, in the land of the liuing.
\v 14 \f z \fr 26.14 \ft The iuſt exhorteth his owne ſoule to patience,\f* Expect our Lord, doe manfully: and \f a \fr 26.14 \ft fortitude,\f* let thy hart take courage, and \f b \fr 26.14 \ft and longanimitie. · Pſal. 30.\f* expect thou our Lord. \x - \xt Pſal. 30.\x*
\c 27
\cd Dauid prayeth to be defended from the eternal deſtruction of the wicked, 4. which ( by way of imprecation, or conformitie to Gods iuſtice ) he prophecieth 6. Feeling by inſpiration that his prayer is heard, rendereth thankes to God, 9. and prayeth for al the people. \f - \ft Another prayer of David for delivery from evils. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Pſalme to Dauid him ſelfe. TO THEE ô Lord I wil crie, my God \f a \fr 27.1 \ft Omitte not to comfort me.\f* keepe not ſilence from me: leſt at any time thou hold thy peace from me, and I shal be like to them that goe downe into the lake.
\v 2 Heare ô Lord the voice of my petition whiles I pray to thee: whiles I lift vp my handes to thy holie temple.
\v 3 \f b \fr 27.3 \ft Suffer me not to be ouercome: for God tempteth none to euil. Iac. 1.\f* Draw me not together with ſinners: and with them that worke iniquitie deſtroy me not. Which ſpeake peace with their neighbour, but euils in their hartes.
\v 4 \f c \fr 27.4 \ft The iuſt in zele of iuſtice pray that ſinne may be punished.\f* Geue them according to their workes, and according to the wickedneſſe of their inuentions. According to the workes of their handes geue vnto them: render them their retribution.
\v 5 Becauſe they haue \f d \fr 27.5 \ft Ignorance doth not excuſe when men may and wil not vnderſtand.\f* not vnderſtood the workes of our Lord, and in the workes of his handes thou shalt deſtroy them, and \f e \fr 27.5 \ft God ſaueth not without our cooperation with his grace.\f* not build them vp.
\v 6 Bleſſed be our Lord: becauſe he hath heard the voice of my petition.
\v 7 Our Lord is my helper, and my protectour: in him my hart hath hoped, and I was holpen. And \f f \fr 27.7 \ft being comforted in ſpirite my bodie is as it were refreshed.\f* my flesh florished againe: and \f g \fr 27.7 \ft freely and gladly.\f* with my wil I wil confeſſe to him.
\v 8 Our Lord is the ſtrength of his people: and he is \f h \fr 27.8 \ft God protecteth and proſpereth the kings good endeuoures for his people.\f* the protector of the ſaluations of his annoynted.
\v 9 Saue \f i \fr 27.9 \ft As Pſal. 19. and often elswhere the ſubiectes pray for their Superior: ſo mutually the ſuperior prayeth for the ſubiectes.\f* thy people ô Lord, and bleſſe thine inheritance: and rule them, and extol them for euer.
\c 28
\cd The royal prophet ſeing in ſpirite the moſt ſacred Myſteries, brought by Chriſt into this world, inuiteth al to offer their beſt thinges, euen themſelues wholly, as ſacrifice of thankes, for ſo excellent benefites, preached with magnificence. 5. VVherby innumerable are gethered into his Church here replenished with grace, and in heauen with glorie. \f - \ft The Church of Chriſt endowed with excellent myſteries. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 The Pſalme of Dauid, in the conſummation \f a \fr 28.1 \ft \+it 2. Reg. 6. v. 17. 1. Par. 16. v. 1\+it*.\f* of the tabernacle. BRING \f b \fr 28.1 \ft Offer ſacrifice of thankes for the ſingular benefites after recounted in this Pſalme.\f* to our Lord ye children of God: bring to our Lord \f c \fr 28.1 \ft Rammes were of the more principal thinges that were offered in the law of Moyſes. But the ſonnes of rammes importe in myſtical ſenſe better hoſtes then rammes.\f* the ſonnes of rammes.
\v 2 Bring to our Lord \f d \fr 28.2 \ft The firſt thing in ſacrifice is to glorifie, honour, and adore God in ſinceritie of ſpirite,\f* glorie and honour, bring to our Lord glorie vnto his name: adore ye our Lord \f e \fr 28.2 \ft in his holie Catholique Churh.\f* in his holie court.
\v 3 The voice of our Lord \f f \fr 28.3 \ft Here is a greater matter intimated then happened in the bringing of the Arke into a tabernacle prepared in Sion, when Dauid danced, and offered hoſtes for ſacrifice, others ioyning with him in that ſolemnitie. But this \+it voice of out Lord vpon waters\+it* is rather verified of our Bleſſed Sauiours owne preaching with\f* vpon waters, the God of \f g \fr 28.3 \ft \+it maieſtie\+it*\f* maieſtie hath \f h \fr 28.3 \ft \+it thundering\+it*, & by his Apoſtles vpon\f* thundered: Our Lord vpon \f i \fr 28.3 \ft \+it manie waters\+it*, manie nations:\f* manie waters.
\v 4 The voice of our Lord \f k \fr 28.4 \ft \+it in powre\+it* of miracles.\f* in powre: the voice of our Lord \f l \fr 28.4 \ft \+it in magnificence\+it* preaching as \+it hauing\+it* in \+it dede powre, not as the Scribes and Phariſes. Mat. 7. v. 29\+it*.\f* in magnificence.
\v 5 The voice of our Lord \f m \fr 28.5 \ft \+it breaking cedars\+it* among innumerable others, conuerting higheſt Potentates\f* breaking ceders: and our Lord shal breake the \f n \fr 28.5 \ft \+it of Libanus\+it*, Emperoures, kinges and greateſt Princes of the world:\f* ceders of Libanus:
\v 6 And he shal breake them in peeces as a \f o \fr 28.6 \ft \+it as a calfe of Libanas\+it* ſo meekly ſubmitting them ſelues to Chriſts yoke, and ſpiritual obedience of his Church.\f* calfe of Libanus. \f p \fr 28.6 \ft Al which is done by Chriſt our Lord \+it the beloued\+it* of God,\f* and the beloued as \f q \fr 28.6 \ft \+it as the ſonne of vnicornes\+it*, is moſt tenderly beloued by the parentes.\f* the ſonne of vnicornes.
\v 7 The voice of our Lord \f r \fr 28.7 \ft This \+it voice of our Lord diuiding the flame of fire\+it*. the Holie Ghoſt proceding from the Father and the Sonne, came vpon the Apoſtles, as in diuided tongues of fire.\f* diuiding the flame of fire:
\v 8 The voice of our Lord \f s \fr 28.8 \ft wherwith \+it the deſert\+it*, the Gentiles of the wide and wild world, \+it vvere shaken\+it* and moued,\f* shaking the deſert; and our Lord shal moue \f t \fr 28.8 \ft the \+it deſert of Cades\+it*, ſome of the Iewes alſo compunct in hart with remorſe of conſcience hearing the voice of S. Peter, and other Apoſtles.\f* the deſert of Cades.
\v 9 The voice of our Lord \f v \fr 28.9 \ft \+it The ſame voice of our Lord preparing hartes\+it*, inſpiring the mindes of men with ſpede, like hyndes and does, to aſcend the high hilles of free and perfect life, in contemplatiue vertues.\f* preparing \f 1 \fr 28.9 \ft \+it ceruos\+it*.\f* hartes, and he shal \f w \fr 28.9 \ft So our Sauiour shal \+it diſcouer the thicke woodes\+it*, reueile the hidden Myſteries of the old Law, by preaching Chriſtian doctrine, and vſe of Chriſtian Religion.\f* diſcouer thicke woodes: and \f x \fr 28.9 \ft \+it in his holie Temple\+it*, the Catholique Church, wherin al true Chriſtians shal \+it glorifie God\+it*.\f* in his temple al shal ſay glorie.
\v 10 Our Lord maketh \f y \fr 28.10 \ft making the great abundance of people, who are like \+it the ſea when it ouerfloweth the land\+it*, to dwel in the ſame Church.\f* to inhabite the floud: and our Lord \f z \fr 28.10 \ft Chriſt \+it our Lord ſitting\+it*, & ruling \+it king\+it* ouer \+it al foreuer\+it*:\f* shal ſit king for euer.
\v 11 Our Lord \f a \fr 28.11 \ft by his grace \+it geuing ſtreingth to his people\+it*, to paſſe through the tentations of this life;\f* wil geue ſtrength to his people: our Lord \f b \fr 28.11 \ft and \+it bleſſe\+it*e the iuſt with eternal peace in heauen.\f* wil bleſſe his people in peace.
\c 29
\cd King Dauid by voice and inſtrument rendereth thankes to God for his peaceable ſtate in the kingdom, 5. inuiteth others to reioyce in Gods benefites, teaching by his owne example that God ſometimes geueth more conforth ſometimes sheweth his wrath, but al for our good. \f - \ft David rendereth thanks for his eſtablishment in his Kingdom. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A \f a \fr 29.1 \ft The general name of Pſalme common to this whole booke conteyning in al 150. is more particularelie appropriated to ſome, which more ſpecially were playde vpon muſical inſtruments as on the Pſalter, Harpe, &c. Others are called Canticles, which were moſt vſuallie ſongue with humaine voices. So this, called \+it a Pſalme of Canticle\+it*, ſignifieth that voyces begane the muſike and inſtruments were adioyned. As contrariwiſe others are called \+it Canticles of Pſalmes\+it*, where inſtruments begane and voices folowed.\f* Pſalme of Canticle, \f b \fr 29.1 \ft After manie great tribulations, King Dauid proſpering built an excellent houſe or palace. \+it (2. Reg. 5. v. 11. Paralip. 14. v. 1\+it*.) And at his firſt dwelling therein, made this Pſalme, beginning himſelfe to ſing the ſame with voice, other muſitians ioyned with him in the praiſes of God and thankeſgeuing for his benefites.\f* in the dedication of Dauids houſe.
\v 2 I WIL \f c \fr 29.2 \ft Though God in himſelfe is moſt high, and neither nedeth, nor can be exalted by men, yet the royal prophet knew it vvas his dutie to ſing thankes and praiſes to him,\f* exalt thee ô Lord, \f d \fr 29.2 \ft for his deliuerie from manie trubles, and dangers.\f* becauſe thou haſt receiued me: neither haſt \f e \fr 29.2 \ft not ſuffering his enemies to be delighted in his ruine.\f* delighted myne enemies ouer me.
\v 3 O Lord my God I haue cried to thee, and thou haſt \f f \fr 29.3 \ft conſerued my bodie in health amõgſt innumerable dangers.\f* healed me.
\v 4 Lord thou haſt \f g \fr 29.4 \ft Preſerued my ſoule from greater dãgers of ſinnes, and ſo from hel.\f* brought forth my ſoule out of hel: thou haſt ſaued me from them that goe downe into the lake.
\v 5 Sing to our Lord \f h \fr 29.5 \ft Ye that are iuſt and holie praiſe God for it, from vvhom it cometh, and not from your ſelues:\f* ye his ſainctes: and \f i \fr 29.5 \ft confeſſe his mere goodnes vvithout your deſertes.\f* confeſſe to the memorie of his holines.
\v 6 Becauſe \f k \fr 29.6 \ft vvhen he is angrie,\f* wrath is in his indignation: \f l \fr 29.6 \ft yet he meaneth vvel vnto vs.\f* and life in his wil. At \f m \fr 29.6 \ft The ſtate of a iuſt mans life is often changed from ſorovv to comforte, and from comforte to ſorovv.\f* euening shal weeping abide: and in the morning gladneſſe.
\v 7 And I ſaid in my abundance: \f n \fr 29.7 \ft Though vve ſuppoſe our ſelues firmly eſtablished:\f* I wil not be moued for euer.
\v 8 O Lord \f o \fr 29.8 \ft yet God of his good vvil tovvardes vs ſometimes geueth \+it ſtrength\+it*, and corege,\f* in thy wil, thou haſt geuen ſtrength to my beautie. Thou haſt \f p \fr 29.8 \ft ſometimes ſuffereth vs to our ovvne vveakenes,\f* turned away thy face from me, and I became trubled.
\v 9 To thee ô Lord \f q \fr 29.9 \ft therfore we muſt ſtil \+it crie and pray\+it* for Gods helpe,\f* I wil crie: and I wil pray to my God.
\v 10 \f r \fr 29.10 \ft in manner here expreſſed or the like.\f* What profite is in my bloud, whiles I deſcend into corruption? Shal duſt confeſſe to thee, or declare thy truth?
\v 11 Our Lord hath heard, and had mercie on me: our Lord is become my helper.
\v 12 Thou haſt turned my mourning into ioy vnto me: thou haſt cut my ſackcloth, and haſt compaſſed me with gladnes.
\v 13 That \f s \fr 29.13 \ft finally in this my good ſtate\f* my glorie may ſing to thee: and I be not compunct: Lord my God for euer \f t \fr 29.13 \ft I shal alvvayes confeſſe and praiſe thee.\f* wil I confeſſe to thee.
\c 30
\cd A prayer of a iuſt man excedingly afflicted ſtil confident in God, 11. deſcribing his manie calamities (in reſpect of his enemies) vndeſerued, 18. prayeth for his owne deliuerie, and their iuſt punishment. 20. praiſeth and thanketh God for his goodnes. 24. exhorteth al others to do the ſame. \f - \ft How to pray in affliction. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 30.1 \ft Perteyning to the new Teſtament.\f* the end, the Pſalme of Dauid, for \f b \fr 30.1 \ft eſpecially to the iuſt trubled and almoſt diſtracted in mind in great affliction. \+it See v. 23\+it*.\f* exceſſe of minde.
\v 2 IN THEE ô Lord \f c \fr 30.2 \ft How greuouſly ſoeuer I am afflicted yet I truſt in thee,\f* haue I hoped, \f d \fr 30.2 \ft therfore I pray thus.\f* let me not be confounded for euer: in thy iuſtice deliuer me. \x - \xt Pſa. 70.\x*
\v 3 Incline thine eare to me, make haſt to deliuer me. Be vnto me for a God protector: and for a houſe of refuge, that thou mayſt ſaue me.
\v 4 Becauſe thou art my ſtrength, and my refuge: and for thy name thou wilt conduct me, and wilt nourish me.
\v 5 Thou wilt bring me out of this ſnare, which they haue hid for me: becauſe thou art my protector.
\v 6 Into \f e \fr 30.6 \ft I offer and reſigne my ſelfe to thee:\f* thy handes I commend my ſpirit: thou haſt \f f \fr 30.6 \ft Luc. 23. · Thou haſt often deliuered me. This verſe is moſt aptly applied in al reſignatiõs of our ſoules to Gods wil, namely whenſoeuer we receiue the B. Sacrament; likewiſe in the houre of death, and at manie other times.\f* redeemed me ô Lord God of truth. \x - \xt Luc. 23.\x*
\v 7 Thou haſt hated them that obſerue vanities, vnprofitably. But I haue hoped in our Lord:
\v 8 I shal reioyce and be ioyful in thy mercie. Becauſe thou haſt reſpected my humilitie, thou haſt ſaued my ſoule out of neceſſities.
\v 9 Neither \f g \fr 30.9 \ft not ſuffered me to be shut vp.\f* haſt thou shut me vp in the handes of the enemie: thou haſt ſet me feete in a large place.
\v 10 Haue mercie vpon me ô Lord becauſe I am in tribulation: myne eie is trubled for wrath, my ſoule, and my bellie:
\v 11 Becauſe \f h \fr 30.11 \ft al my partes external and internal, body and mind are trubled.\f* my life is decayed for ſorrowe: and my yeares for gronings. My ſtrength is weakened for pouertie; and by bones are trubled.
\v 12 Aboue al myne enemies I am made a reproch both to my neighbours excedingly: and \f i \fr 30.12 \ft My freindes dare not conuerſe with me, leſt they incurre diſpleaſure for my ſake.\f* a feare to my acquantance. They that ſaw me, fled forth from me:
\v 13 I am forgotten, from the hart as one dead. I am made as a veſſel deſtroyed:
\v 14 becauſe I haue heard the reprehenſion of manie that abide round about: In that whiles, they aſſembled together againſt me, they conſulted to take my ſoule.
\v 15 But I haue hoped in thee ô Lord: I ſayd: Thou art my God:
\v 16 my lottes are in thy handes. Deliuer me out of the handes of my enemies, and from them that perſecute me.
\v 17 \f k \fr 30.17 \ft Make thy light ſo ſhine in my ſoule, that I may vnderſtand that is right.\f* Illuſtrate thy face vpon thy ſeruant, \f l \fr 30.17 \ft and through thy mercie deliuer me from the force of myne aduerſaries.\f* ſaue me in thy mercie:
\v 18 Lord let me not be confounded, becauſe I haue inuocated thee. Let the impious be ashamed, and brought downe into hel:
\v 19 let the deceitful lippes be made mute. Which ſpeake iniquitie againſt the iuſt, \f m \fr 30.19 \ft ſo proudly behauing them ſelues, as if they had no ſuperior, neither in earth nor in heauen, to whom they shal at laſt render account:\f* in pride, and \f n \fr 30.19 \ft and abuſing their preſent powre and authoritie, which they haue of God.\f* abuſe.
\v 20 How great is the multitude of thy ſweetneſſe ô Lord, which thou \f o \fr 30.20 \ft As yet in this preſent life the reward of the iuſt is hidde:\f* haſt hid for them that feare thee. Thou haſt perfited it for them that hope in thee, \f p \fr 30.20 \ft but shal be made manifeſt in ſight of al men.\f* in the ſight of the children of men.
\v 21 Thou \f q \fr 30.21 \ft In the meane time the iuſt is in great eſtimation in the ſecrete knowledge of God.\f* shalt hide them in the ſecrete of \f r \fr 30.21 \ft title of honoure, as we ſpeake to a king; \+it your maieſtie\+it*: or to a noble man, \+it your Lordshippe\+it*.\f* thy face from the diſturbance of men. Thou shalt protect them in thy tabernacle from the contradiction of tongues.
\v 22 Bleſſed be our Lord: becauſe he hath made his mercie merueilous to me in the fenſed citie.
\v 23 But I haue ſaid in \f s \fr 30.23 \ft in myn extreme affliction being almoſt diſtracted in my mind, I ſaid that in reaſon I would not haue ſayd. Holie Iob ſpake ſome thinges in ſuch ſtate of affliction. \+it ch. 3. & 42\+it*.\f* the exceſſe of my minde: I am caſt away from the ſight of thine eies. Therfore thou haſt heard the voice of my praier, whiles I cried to thee.
\v 24 \f t \fr 30.24 \ft the prophet, or other iuſt perſon exhorteth al the ſeruants of God\f* Loue our Lord al ye his ſainctes: becauſe our Lord wil require truth, & wil repay them abundantly that doe proudly.
\v 25 Do ye \f v \fr 30.25 \ft to conſtancie.\f* manfully, and let your \f w \fr 30.25 \ft longanimitie,\f* hart take courage, al ye that \f x \fr 30.25 \ft and final perſeuerance to the end.\f* hope in out Lord.
\c 31
\cd Forgeuenes of ſinnes is a happie thing: 3. wherto manie are brought by affliction geuing them vnderſtanding, ſo mouing them to confeſſe their ſinnes, 6. pray for remiſſion, 10, not deſpaire, but hope in Gods mercie, and ſo reioyce with ſincere hart. \f - \ft The ſecond penitential Pſalm. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 To \f a \fr 31.1 \ft This Pſalme ſheweth how Dauid was brought to vnderſtand his ſinnes, to confeſſe, bewayle, and obtaine remiſſion of them.\f* Dauid him ſelfe vnderſtanding. BLESSED are they, \f b \fr 31.1 \ft The firſt bleſſing of a ſinner is the forgeuenes of his ſinnes.\f* whoſe iniquities are forgeuen: and whoſe ſinnes \f c \fr 31.1 \ft by charitie, which couereth the multitude of ſinnes. \+it 1. Pet. 4.\+it*\f* be couered. \x - \xt Rom. 4. 1. Pet. 4.\x*
\v 2 Bleſſed is the man, to whom \f d \fr 31.2 \ft Satisfaction being made.\f* our Lord hath not imputed ſinne, neither is there \f e \fr 31.2 \ft VVhen ſinners repent ſincerly without guile, then God forgeueth: without which cooperation none is iuſtified.\f* guile in his ſpirit.
\v 3 Becauſe \f f \fr 31.3 \ft becauſe I acknowledged not my greuous ſinnes, I was ſtil ſore afflicted.\f* I held my peace, my bones are \f 1 \fr 31.3 \ft \+it vvaxed as if they vvere old\+it*.\f* inueterated, \f g \fr 31.3 \ft though otherwiſe I ceaſed not to pray but without any fruict or good effect.\f* whiles I cried al the day.
\v 4 Becauſe day and night thy hand is made heauie vpon me: I \f h \fr 31.4 \ft thy diuine prouidence reducing me,\f* am turned in my anguish, whiles \f i \fr 31.4 \ft by remorſe of myn owne conſcience which telleth me that I deſerue al this affliction.\f* the thorne is faſtened.
\v 5 I \f k \fr 31.5 \ft therfore I do no longer diſsẽble with men nor am ſilent to thee, but expreſly acknowlege my ſinnes.\f* haue made my ſinne knowen to thee: and my iniuſtice I haue not hid. I ſaid: I wil confeſſe againſt me my iniuſtice to our Lord: and thou haſt forgeuen the impietie of my ſinne.
\v 6 For this \f l \fr 31.6 \ft As I do now recal my ſelfe being ſtricken with Gods heauie hand; ſo muſt euerie one that wil be purged from his ſinnes and ſanctified \+it pray to thee\+it*, when he is afflicted.\f* shal euerie holie one pray to thee, in time conuenient. But yet \f m \fr 31.6 \ft Though calamities be meruelous great like \+it to a diluge\+it*:\f* in the floud of manie waters, they shal \f n \fr 31.6 \ft yet they ſhal not opreſſe him, that relieth vpon God.\f* not approche to him.
\v 7 Thou art my refuge from tribulation, which hath compaſſed me: my exultation, deliuer me from them that compaſſe me.
\v 8 I \f o \fr 31.8 \ft God ſpeaketh: promiſing by theſe tribulations to geue his ſeruants \+it vnderſtanding\+it*, and inſtruction:\f* wil geue thee vnderſtanding, and wil inſtruct thee in the way, that thou shalt goe; I \f p \fr 31.8 \ft with perpetual protection.\f* wil faſten mine eies vpon thee.
\v 9 Doe \f q \fr 31.9 \ft Be not therfore careles, like to brute beaſtes, but conſideratiue of your actions.\f* not become as horſe and mule, which haue no vnderſtanding. In \f r \fr 31.9 \ft The Prophet or anie iuſt ſoul beſecheth God to hold this ſtraict hand of diſcipline ouer ſinners, for their conuerſion.\f* bit and bridle binde faſt their cheekes, that approch not to thee.
\v 10 Manie \f s \fr 31.10 \ft sinners deſerue much punishment.\f* are the ſcourges of a ſinner, but \f t \fr 31.10 \ft but repenting, and truſting in God shal finde his mercie.\f* him that hopeth in our Lord mercie shal compaſſe.
\v 11 Be \f v \fr 31.11 \ft The end of true penance is ioy to which therfore the prophet inuiteth al penitents.\f* ioyful in our Lord and reioyſe ye iuſt, and glorie al ye right of hart.
\c 32
\cd The prophet exhorteth to praiſe God, 4. deſcribing his powre, prouidence, mercie, and wiſdom. 16. no ſaluation but by him. 20. and therfore prayeth for his helpe. \f - \ft The perfection of God's works deſcribed. The 2. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 REIOYCE ye iuſt \f a \fr 32.1 \ft not in your ſelues\f* in our Lord: \f b \fr 32.1 \ft praiſe is not comelie in the mouth of a ſinner. Eccli. 15.\f* prayſing becometh the righteous.
\v 2 Confeſſe ye to our Lord on \f c \fr 32.2 \ft in mortificacion offering your bodies a liuing hoſte, Rom. 12.\f* the harpe: on \f d \fr 32.2 \ft of this inſtrument this booke is called the Pſalter and it ſignifieth the obſeruation of the tenne commandments, withoutwhich no praiſe pleaſeth God.\f* a pſalter of ten ſtrings ſing to him.
\v 3 Sing ye to him \f e \fr 32.3 \ft praiſing God for the grace of Chriſt in the new teſtament.\f* a new ſong: ſing wel to him in iubilation.
\v 4 Becauſe the \f f \fr 32.4 \ft Gods rules and precepts are moſt iuſt and right.\f* word of our Lord is right, and al his workes are \f g \fr 32.4 \ft he performeth whatſoeuer he promiſeth.\f* in faith.
\v 5 He loueth \f h \fr 32.5 \ft God euer ioyneth theſe vertues together.\f* mercie and iudgement: the earth is ful of the mercie of our Lord.
\v 6 By \f i \fr 32.6 \ft Gods word is omnipotent.\f* the word of our Lord the heauens are eſtablished: and by the ſpirit of his mouth al the power of them.
\v 7 Gathering together the waters of the ſea, as it were in a bottel: putting the depthes \f k \fr 32.7 \ft vnknowen, as riches hid in ſecrete places.\f* in treaſures.
\v 8 Let al the earth feare our Lord: and let al the inhabitantes of the world be moued at him.
\v 9 Becauſe he ſaid, and they were made: he commanded and they were created,
\v 10 Our Lord \f l \fr 32.10 \ft As in Babylon he confounded their tongues.\f* diſſipateth the counſels of nations: and he reproueth the cogitations of people, and he reproueth the counſels of princes.
\v 11 But the counſel of our Lord abydeth for euer: the cogitations \f m \fr 32.11 \ft his abſolute wil is alwayes fulfilled.\f* of his hart in generation and generation.
\v 12 Bleſſed is the nation, whoſe God is our Lord: the people \f n \fr 32.12 \ft the people of Iſrael in the old teſtament: and Chriſtians of al nations in the new.\f* whom he hath choſen for his inheritance.
\v 13 Our \f o \fr 32.13 \ft Gods perpetual prouidence.\f* Lord hath looked from heauen: he hath ſene al the children of men.
\v 14 From his prepared habitation he hath looked vpon al, that inhabite the earth.
\v 15 Who \f p \fr 32.15 \ft Gods wiſdom infinite.\f* made their hartes ſeuerally: who vnderſtandeth al their workes.
\v 16 The \f q \fr 32.16 \ft No powre in earth is of anie force without God.\f* king is not ſaued by much powre: and the gyant shal not be ſaued in the multitude of his ſtrength.
\v 17 The horſe fayleth \f r \fr 32.17 \ft of his maiſter\f* to ſafetie, \f s \fr 32.17 \ft or of him ſelfe.\f* and in the abundance of his force he shal not be ſaued.
\v 18 Behold the eies of our Lord be vpon them that feare him: and on them, that hope vpon his mercie.
\v 19 That he may deliuer their ſoules from death: and nourish them in famine.
\v 20 Our ſoule expecteth our Lord: becauſe he is our helper and protector.
\v 21 Becauſe in him our hart shal reioyce: and we haue truſted in his holie name.
\v 22 \f t \fr 32.22 \ft O God which haſt al perfection, shew thy mercie in protecting, and ſauing al that truſt in thee.\f* Let thy mercie ô Lord be made vpon vs: as we haue hoped in thee.
\c 33
\cd King Dauid by his owne example being deliuered from danger, exhorteth al men to render thankes for Gods benefites. 12. shewing wherin iuſtice conſiſteth, 16. and Gods ſpecial prouidence towards the iuſt. \f - \ft God'ſ providence. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 To \f 1 \fr 33.1 \ft This Pſalme is alſo compoſed in order of the Alphabet.\f* Dauid, when he changed his countenance before \f a \fr 33.1 \ft His proper name was Achis \+it 1. Reg. 21\+it*. but al kinges of Paleſtina were called Abimelech, as Pharao in Ægypt, Nabuchodonoſor in Babylon.\f* Abimelech, and he diſmiſt him, and he went away. (1. Reg. 21.) \f 2 \fr 33.1 \ft \+it (1. Reg. 21)\+it*\f*
\v 2 I \f b \fr 33.2 \ft I prayſe God\f* WIL bleſſe our Lord at \f c \fr 33.2 \ft both in proſperitie and aduerſitie.\f* al time: his prayſe alwayes in my mouth.
\v 3 In our Lord \f d \fr 33.3 \ft when I ſerue our Lord, my ſoule shal be praiſed in his ſeruice.\f* my ſoule shal be praiſed: let the milde heare, and reioyce.
\v 4 Magnifie ye our Lord with me: and let vs exalt his name for euer.
\v 5 I haue ſought out our Lord, and he hath heard me: and from al my tribulations he hath deliuered me.
\v 6 Come ye to him, and be illuminated: and your faces shal not be confounded.
\v 7 This \f e \fr 33.7 \ft Euerie man be he how poore ſoeuer when he prayeth shal be heard.\f* poore man hath cried, and our Lord hath heard him: and from al his tribulations he hath ſaued him.
\v 8 The \f f \fr 33.8 \ft the proper guardian Angel of euerie one.\f* Angel of our Lord shal put in him ſelfe about them that feare him: and shal deliuer them.
\v 9 Taſt ye, and ſee that our Lord is ſweete: bleſſed is the man, that hopeth in him.
\v 10 Feare \f g \fr 33.10 \ft with filial feare.\f* ye our Lord al ye his ſainctes: becauſe there is no lacke to them that feare him.
\v 11 The \f h \fr 33.11 \ft the rich of this world ſetting their mind vpon their wealth, are poore in ſpiritual giftes.\f* rich haue wanted, and haue bene hungrie: but they that ſeeke after our Lord shal not be diminished of any good.
\v 12 Come children, heare me: I wil teach you the feare of our Lord.
\v 13 Who is the man \f i \fr 33.13 \ft Euerie one deſireth to be happie, but he in dede shal be happie, that fleeth from euil, and doth good.\f* that wil haue life: loueth to ſee good daies.
\v 14 Stay thy tongue from euil: and thy lippes that they ſpeake not guile.
\v 15 Turne away from euil, and do good: ſeeke after peace, and purſewe it.
\v 16 The eies of our Lord vpon the iuſt: and his eares vnto their prayers
\v 17 But the \f k \fr 33.17 \ft God ſeing al mens actiõs & intentions, wil render as they deſerue.\f* countenance of our Lord is vpon them that doe euil things: to deſtroy their memorie out of the earth.
\v 18 The iuſt haue cried, and our Lord hath heard them: and out of al their tribulations he hath deliuered them.
\v 19 Our Lord is nigh to them, that are of a contrite hart: and the humble of ſpirit he wil ſaue.
\v 20 Manie are the tribulations of the iuſt: and out of al theſe our Lord wil deliuer them.
\v 21 Our Lord keepeth al their \f l \fr 33.21 \ft Though the iuſt ſeme for a time to be forſaken, yet God that geueth them internal ſtreingth, wil at laſt reward and crowne in them his owne giftes.\f* bones: there shal not one of them be broken.
\v 22 The death of ſinners is verie il: and they that hate the iuſt \f m \fr 33.22 \ft for their ſinne they are ſuffered to fal into more ſinne.\f* shal offend.
\v 23 Our Lord wil redeme the ſoules of his ſeruantes: and al that hope in him \f n \fr 33.23 \ft contrariwiſe thoſe that accept of his grace, shal finally not offend.\f* shal not offend.
\c 34
\cd Dauid, in figure of Chriſt, prophetically by way of inuocating Gods helpe, forsheweth his perſecution, and the iuſt reuenge vpon his perſecutors, 9. with praiſe to God. 13. his charitie towards his cruel aduerſaries. 17. whom neuertheles God punisheth, 20. for pretending peace in wordes, and in fact perſecuting, 23. rendering to al as they deſerue. \f - \ft Chriſt's perſecution. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 To \f a \fr 34.1 \ft Dauid ſignifieth beloued deſiderable, or ſtrong of hand, that is Chriſt, aboue al beloued of God, deſired of man, the ſtrong conquerour of death and hel. S. Aug. in hunc locum.\f* Dauid himſelf. IVDGE \f b \fr 34.1 \ft By way of imprecation he prophecieth that God wil ouerthrow the perſecutors of Chriſt, and of Chriſtians.\f* bô Lord them that hurt me: ouerthrow them that impugne me.
\v 2 Take \f c \fr 34.2 \ft offenſiue\f* armour and \f d \fr 34.2 \ft denfenſiue.\f* shield: and riſe vp to helpe me.
\v 3 Bring forth the ſword, and \f e \fr 34.3 \ft preoccupate and preuent the malice of the perſecutor.\f* shut vp againſt them, that perſecute me: ſay to my ſoule: I am thy ſaluation.
\v 4 Let them \f f \fr 34.4 \ft Such a punishment and confuſion shal fal in the end vpon al the malicious, after that the iuſt shal haue ouercome tribulations.\f* be counfounded & ashamed, that ſeeke my ſoule. Let them be turned backward, and be confounded that thinke euil againſt me
\v 5 Be they made as duſt before the face of winde; and the angel of our Lord ſtraictning them.
\v 6 Let their way be made darkeneſſe and ſlippernes: and the angel of our Lord purſewing them.
\v 7 Becauſe they haue hid the deſtruction of their ſnare for me without cauſe: in vaine haue they vpbrayded my ſoule.
\v 8 Let the ſnare which he knoweth not, come on him; and the net, which he hath hid, catch him: and let him fal into the verie ſame ſnare.
\v 9 But \f g \fr 34.9 \ft In the time of truble in hope,\f* my ſoule shal reioyce in our Lord: and shal be delighted vpon \f h \fr 34.9 \ft after deliuerie, in eternal ſaluation,\f* his ſaluation.
\v 10 Al my bones shal ſay: Lord, who is like to thee? Deliuering the needie from the hand of them that are ſtronger then he: the needie and poore from them that ſpoile him.
\v 11 \f i \fr 34.11 \ft ſuch falſe witneſſes did riſe againſt Chriſt Mat. 26.\f* Vniuſt witneſſes ryſing vp, asked me things that \f k \fr 34.11 \ft thinges that were not; for God himſelfe, that knoweth al thinges knoweth not that which neither was, is, nor can be.\f* I knew not.
\v 12 They repayed me euil things for good, \f l \fr 34.12 \ft they made priuation of my life, verified in Chriſt, not in Dauid, for he was killed by his enemies.\f* ſterilitie to my ſoule.
\v 13 But I when they were trubleſome to me, \f m \fr 34.13 \ft Al our Sauiours life was penance for others, needing none for himſelfe.\f* did put on cloth of heare. I humbled my ſoule in faſting: and my prayer shal be turned into my boſome.
\v 14 As a \f n \fr 34.14 \ft Chriſt the good Samaritane that releeued the wounded man. Luc. 10.\f* neighbour, as our brother, ſo did I pleaſe: as mourning and ſorowful ſo was I humbled.
\v 15 And they \f o \fr 34.15 \ft al this was fulfilled according to the letter in our Sauiours paſſion.\f* reioyced againſt me, and \f p \fr 34.15 \ft See previous note\f* came together: \f q \fr 34.15 \ft See previous note\f* ſcourges were gathered together vpon me, and I was \f r \fr 34.15 \ft Our Lord knoweth not anie iuſt cauſe, why the Iewes ſo perſecuted him, for they had no iuſt cauſe but meere malice.\f* ignorant.
\v 16 They were diſſipated, and not compunct, they tempted me, they ſcorned me with ſcorning: they gnashed vpon me with their teeth.
\v 17 Lord \f s \fr 34.17 \ft as Pſal. 21. and Mat. 27. God why haſt thou forſaken me? not deliuered me from temporal death, nor yelded me ſuch conſolation, as thou gaueſt other Sainctes in their agonies?\f* when wilt thou regard? \f t \fr 34.17 \ft a prophecie of Chriſts reſurrection.\f* reſtore thou my ſoule from their malignitie, myne only one from the lions.
\v 18 I wil confeſſe to thee in \f v \fr 34.18 \ft Prophecie of the Catholique Church. as Pſal. 21.\f* the great Church, in a graue people I wil prayſe thee.
\v 19 Let them not reioyce ouer me that are my aduerſaries vniuſtly: \f w \fr 34.19 \ft This place is applied by our Sauiour to himſelfe. Ioan. 15.\f* that hate me without cauſe, and twinckle with the eies.
\v 20 Becauſe they ſpake in deede \f x \fr 34.20 \ft The Phariſes and Herodians ſaid: Maſter we know that thou art a true ſpeaker &c. meaning to intrappe him with treaſon. \+it Mat. 22\+it*.\f* peaceably to me: and in the anger of the earth ſpeaking they meant guiles.
\v 21 And they \f y \fr 34.21 \ft The ſame Phariſes and Prieſtes iudged him woorthy of death, and procured the people to crie: Crucifie him, crucifie, him.\f* opened their mouth a wide vpon me: they ſaid; Wel, wel, our eies haue ſeene.
\v 22 Thou haſt ſene ô Lord, keepe not ſilence: \f z \fr 34.22 \ft Againe his Reſurrection is prophecied.\f* Lord depart not from me.
\v 23 Ariſe and attend to my iudgement: my God, and my Lord vnto my cauſe.
\v 24 Iudge me according to thy iuſtice ô Lord my God, and let them not reioyce ouer me
\v 25 Let them not ſay in their hartes: Wel, wel, to our ſoule: neither let them ſay: We haue deuoured him.
\v 26 Let \f a \fr 34.26 \ft At the day of iudgement the Wicked shal receiue ſentence of damnation.\f* them blush and be ashamed together, that reioyce at my euils. Let them be clothed with confuſion and shame; that ſpeake great things vpon me.
\v 27 Let \f b \fr 34.27 \ft the bleſſed of eternal glorie.\f* them reioyce and be glad, that wil my iuſtice: and let them ſay alwayes: Our Lord be magnified, that wil the peace of his ſeruant.
\v 28 And my tongue shal meditate thy iuſtice, thy prayſe al the day.
\c 35
\cd The prophet deſcribeth the wicked malice of obſtinate ſinners 6. Againſt which he oppoſeth Gods infinite goodnes, 9. with his prouident mercie towards the worſt, and iuſt reward of the good; 12. praying to eſcape the dangerous gulfe of pride. \f - \ft God's providence. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 \f a \fr 35.1 \ft More ſpecially deſcribing the ſtate of men in the new teſtament, then in the old.\f* Vnto the end, to \f b \fr 35.1 \ft inſtruction for Dauid not as a king, or a prophet, but as the poore ſeruant of God.\f* the ſeruant of our Lord Dauid him ſelfe.
\v 2 THE vniuſt hath ſaid within him ſelfe, that he \f c \fr 35.2 \ft wittingly and reſolutely preferring wicked life before vertuous.\f* would ſinne: there is no feare of God before his eies
\v 3 Becauſe he hath done deceitfully in his ſight: \f d \fr 35.3 \ft God ſo hateth ſinne committed of meere malice, that he commonly reiecteth ſuch ſinners, and more often offereth new grace to thoſe, that ſinne of frailitie, or ignorance.\f* that his iniquitie may be found vnto hatred.
\v 4 The wordes of his mouth are iniquitie, and guile: he \f e \fr 35.4 \ft Some ignorance is inuincible whē one hath a good wil to lerne, doing his endeuour to knovv the truth in doctrin, & his dutie in manners, but can not get knowlege therof, and then he is excuſed before God; though he erre in opinion, or in fact: others are negligent to lerne, and their error is groſſe ignorance and is a ſinne, greater or leſſe, according to the importance of the thing, which they ouhgt to knovv. Others are more vvilful, deſiring to be ignorant; that they may ſinne vvith the leſſe remorſe, or repining of their ovvne conſcience, and this is affected ignorance, and moſt hainous, and odious ſinne. For which God often leauing them deſtitute of ordinarie grace, which he geueth to others, they fal into reprobate ſenſe, and into more horrible ſinnes.\f* would not vnderſtand that he might doe wel.
\v 5 He hath meditated iniquitie in his bed: he hath ſet himſelfe on euery way not good, and malice he hath not hated.
\v 6 Lord \f f \fr 35.6 \ft God doth not vtterly ſhut vp his mercie from the moſt wilful & wicked ſinners, but geueth them ſometimes good motions, and ſufficient helpe, thar they may repent, be iuſtified & ſaued, if they do not wittingly harden their owne hartes, and ſtil wilfully repel Gods grace.\f* thy mercie is in heauen: and \f g \fr 35.6 \ft For ſo God pomiſeth (vvho is moſt faithful) that he vvil forgeue ſinners, and receiue them into his fauoure againe, whenſoeuer they reſoluing to ſerue him, repent and ceaſe to ſinne.\f* thy truth euen to the clowdes.
\v 7 Thy iuſtice as the hilles of God: thy iudgementes are great depth. Men & \f h \fr 35.7 \ft Thou ô God that haſt care of al creatures, not only of men but alſo of brute beaſtes, art euer readie of thy part to ſaue both moderate men, in vvhom the light of reaſon remaineth, and alſo groſſe ſenſeles perſons, vvhich are become brutish like horſe and mule or other beaſtes.\f* beaſtes thou wilt ſaue ô Lord:
\v 8 as thou \f i \fr 35.8 \ft For ſo our Lord multiplieth his mercie.\f* haſt multiplied thy mercie ô God. But \f k \fr 35.8 \ft Yet with condition that ſenſles or brutish men, muſt become reaſonable men, the children of men, not coltes, whelpes, pigges &c.\f* the children of men, \f l \fr 35.8 \ft Sinners thus conuerted shal not only haue al neceſſaries in this life, as al liuing creatures haue in this world, but alſo shal hope of ſpiritual, heauenly, & eternal glorie prepared for Angels, and children of men: as in the verſes folovving.\f* shal hope in the couert of thy winges.
\v 9 They shal be inebriated with the plentie of thy houſe: and with the torrent of thy pleaſure thou shalt make them drinke,
\v 10 Becauſe with thee is the fountaine of life: and in thy light we shal ſee light.
\v 11 Extend thy mercie to them that know thee, and thy iuſtice to them, that are of a right hart.
\v 12 Let \f m \fr 35.12 \ft Leſt anie impediment hinder the obtayning and poſſeſſion of eternal revvard, the iuſt muſt ſpecially pray not to be infected vvith pride:\f* not the foote of pride come to me: and let not \f n \fr 35.12 \ft nor be ouercome by the forcible tentations of other ſinners, by perſvvaſion, nor euil example.\f* the hand of a ſinner moue me.
\v 13 There \f o \fr 35.13 \ft The firſt ſinne, tovvit of diuels, vvas pride, and mans ſinne vvas by perſvvaſion of the diuel.\f* haue they fallen that worke iniquitie: they were expelled, \f p \fr 35.13 \ft neither of which could eſcape punishment.\f* neither could they ſtand.
\c 36
\cd An exhortation not to enuie nor imitate the euil, who for moſt part proſper in this world, and are damned eternally: but to flee euil and doe good, duly conſidering that God diuerſly permitteth and punisheth the wicked, and likewiſe comforteth and afflicteth the iuſt, al for their good. \f - \ft An exhortation to contempt of this world. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 \f 1 \fr 36.1 \ft This Pſalme is compoſed in order of the Alphabet, euerie diſtick beginning vvith a diuers letter, to moue the reader to diligent attention, which may ſerue in place of a larger cōmentarie,\f* A Pſalme \f a \fr 36.1 \ft For Dauids and euery iuſt mans inſtruction.\f* to Dauid him ſelf. HAVE \f b \fr 36.1 \ft Neither be thou offended that the wicked do proſper in this world, nor imitate them that thou maiſt alſo proſper.\f* no emulation toward the malignant: neither enuie them that doe iniquitie.
\v 2 Becauſe \f c \fr 36.2 \ft For al this life, and conſequently the proſperitie therof is shorte, and vncertaine.\f* they shal quickely wither as graſſe: and as the bloſſomes of herbes they shal ſoone fal.
\v 3 \f d \fr 36.3 \ft Put thy truſt in God, liuing content in this world:\f* Hope in our Lord and doe good: and inhabite the land, and thou \f e \fr 36.3 \ft & he wil geue thee that is neceſſarie.\f* shalt be fed in the riches therof.
\v 4 Be delighted in our Lord: and he wil geue thee the petitions of thy hart.
\v 5 \f f \fr 36.5 \ft Commend al thyn affaires to God.\f* Reuele thy way to our Lord, and hope in him: and he wil doe it.
\v 6 And he wil \f g \fr 36.6 \ft Partly making vertue appeare to the comfort of the vertuous, and example of others in this life, but eſpecially in the next world.\f* bring forth thy iuſtice as light: and thy iudgement as midday:
\v 7 be ſubiect to our Lord, and pray him. Haue no emulation in him, that proſpereth in his way: in a man that doth iniuſtices.
\v 8 Ceaſe from wrath, and leaue furie: haue not emulation that thou be malignant.
\v 9 Becauſe they that are malignant, shal be caſt out: but they that expect our Lord, the ſame shal inherite \f h \fr 36.9 \ft the land of the liuing.\f* the land.
\v 10 And yet a litle while, and the ſinner shal not be: and thou shalt ſeeke his place, and shal not find it.
\v 11 \x - \xt Mat. 5.\x* But the meeke shal inherite the land, and shal be delighted in multitude of peace.
\v 12 The ſinner shal obſerue the iuſt: and shal gnash vpon him with his teeth.
\v 13 But our Lord shal ſcorne him: becauſe he foreſeeth that his day shal come.
\v 14 Sinners haue drawen out the ſword; they haue bent their bowe; That they may deceiue the poore and needie: that they may murder the right of hart.
\v 15 Let \f i \fr 36.15 \ft By way of imprecation (as in manie other places) the prophet forsheweth that wicked men shal fal into the euils, which they prepare for others.\f* their owne ſwordes enter into their hartes: and let their bowe be broken.
\v 16 Better is a litle to the iuſt, aboue much riches of ſinners.
\v 17 Becauſe the armes of ſinners shal be broken in pieces: but our Lord comfirmeth the iuſt.
\v 18 Our Lord knoweth the daies of the immaculate: and their inheritance shal be for euer.
\v 19 They shal not be confounded in the euil time, and in the dayes of famine they shal be filled:
\v 20 becauſe the ſinners shal perish. But the enemies of our Lord forthwith as they shal be honoured and exalted, vanishing shal vanish as ſmoke.
\v 21 The ſinner shal borrow, and not pay: but the iuſt is merciful and wil geue.
\v 22 Becauſe they that bleſſe him shal inherite the land: but they that curſe him shal perish.
\v 23 With our Lord the ſteppe of man shal be directed: and he shal like wel of his way.
\v 24 When \f k \fr 36.24 \ft Though the iuſt fal of frailtie or ignorance into venial ſinne, yet Gods grace shal ſtay him that he fal not into mortal. The iuſt falleth ſeuen times in the day, & riſeth. Prou. 24.\f* he shal fal, he shal not be bruſed: becauſe our Lord putteth his hand vnder.
\v 25 I haue bene yong, for I am old: and I haue not ſene the iuſt forſaken, \f l \fr 36.25 \ft So king Dauid obſerued: and it very rarely happeneth, that the iuſt or their children are deſtitute of neceſſarie ſuſtenance in this vvorld. If it chance in ſome, it is to their greater merite, and is manifeſtly recompenſed in ſpiritual giftes. In which ſenſe S. Baſil expoundeth, that it is alwayes verified For God euer rewardeth good workes either temporally, or ſpiritually, or both wayes. S. Auguſtin alſo (conc. 3. in hunc Pſal.) exemplifieth in Abraham, Iſaac, and Iacob with al his familie, who were forced to goe into other countries by reaſon of famine, and by Gods prouidence were there ſuſtained, Gen. 12. 26. 46. and S. Paul among his other tribulations mentioneth famine and thirſt. 2. Cor. 11. v. 27. Seing therfore theſe ſo iuſt perſons ſought their bread in neceſſitie, he expoundeth this holie Scripture in the Allegorical ſenſe, that the Church from her beginning in Ieruſalem to the end of the world, neuer wanteth the true word of God, true faith and doctrin, which is the ſpiritual bread vvhervvith the ſoule is nourished.\f* nor his ſeede ſeeking bread.
\v 26 Al the day he is merciful and lendeth: and his ſeede shal be in bleſſing.
\v 27 \f m \fr 36.27 \ft In theſe tvvo prīciples, declining from euil and doing good, true iuſtice conſiſteth.\f* Decline from euil, and doe good: and \f n \fr 36.27 \ft and he that finally obſerueth theſe tvvo pointes, meriteth and shal poſſeſſe heauen.\f* inhabite for euer and euer.
\v 28 Becauſe our Lord loueth iudgement, and he wil not forſake his ſainctes: they shal be preſerued for euer. The vniuſt shal be punished: and the ſeede of the impious shal perish.
\v 29 But the iuſt shal inherite the land: and shal inhabite for euer and euer vpon it.
\v 30 The mouth of the iuſt shal meditate wiſedome, and his tong shal ſpeake iudgement. \x - \xt Pro. 31. Iſa. 51.\x*
\v 31 The law of his God in his hart: and his ſteppes shal not be ſupplanted.
\v 32 The ſinner conſidereth the iuſt: and ſeeketh to \f o \fr 36.32 \ft to draw him to mortal ſinne, which is death of the ſoule.\f* murder him.
\v 33 But our Lord wil not leaue him in his handes: neither wil he condemne him, when iudgement shal be geuen of him.
\v 34 Expect our Lord, and keepe his way: and he wil exalt thee, that thou mayſt inherite the land: when the ſinners shal perish thou shalt ſee.
\v 35 I haue ſeene the impious highly exalted, and aduanced as the ceders of Libanus.
\v 36 And I paſſed by, and behold he was not: and I ſought him, and his place was not found.
\v 37 Keepe innocencie, and ſee equitie: becauſe there are \f p \fr 36.37 \ft rewardes.\f* remaynes for the peaceable man.
\v 38 But the vniuſt shal perish together: the remaines of the impious shal perish.
\v 39 But the ſaluation of the iuſt is \f q \fr 36.39 \ft mans iuſtice and wel doing is not of his owne powre but of Gods grace.\f* of our Lord: and he is their potector in the time of tribulation.
\v 40 And our Lord wil helpe them, and deliuer them: and he wil take them away from ſinners, and ſaue them: becauſe they haue hoped in him.
\c 37
\cd King Dauid, or anie other penitent, earneſtly prayeth God to remitte his ſinnes, and mitigate the paines which he acknowledgeth him ſelfe to haue deſerued, 12. lamenting the afflictions which he ſuffereth by ſuch as ſometimes were his freindes, 14. whoſe tentations he now reſiſteth, truſting in God, reſigning himſelfe to Gods wil, confeſſing his owne iniquitie, and humbly praying for Gods helpe. \f - \ft The third penitential Pſalm. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Pſalme of Dauid, in \f a \fr 37.1 \ft In remembrance that by ſinne we loſt the reſt and peace, which man had in the ſtate of innocencie; ſecondly we loſt the peace of conſcience; thirdly the reſt and peace of eternal felicitie.\f* recordation of the ſabbath.
\v 2 LORD \f b \fr 37.2 \ft Condemne me not to eternal paine:\f* rebuke me nor in thy furie: \f c \fr 37.2 \ft nor punish me in purgatorie fire; but purge me ſo in this life, that the purging fire be not needful. By which fire (ſaith S. Auguſtin) though ſome ſhal be ſaued ( grauior tamen erit ille ignis, quam quicquid poteſt homo pati in hac vita) yet that fire shal be more greuous, then whatſoeuer a man can ſuffer in this llife. S. Gregory alſo expoundeth this ſame place, as if Dauid ſayd thus: I know it wil come to paſſe, that after the end of this life, ſome shal be cleanſed by purging flames, ſome ſhal be vnder the ſentence of eternal damnation. But becauſe I do eſteme that tranſitorie fire more intolerable then al preſent tribulation, I deſire not only not to be rebuked in furie of eternal damnation, but alſo I feare to be purged in the wrath of tranſitorie correption. Thou therfore ô Lord whom I ſerue in my ſpirite, whom I know to be the Sauiour of al men, rebuke me not in furie of perpetual damnation, nor chatiſe me in wrath of purging puniſhment. See Annotat. Pſal. 6.\f* nor chaſtiſe me in thy wrath.
\v 3 Becauſe \f d \fr 37.3 \ft Afflictions of mind and bodie ſent by thy iuſt iudgement.\f* thy arrowes are faſt ſticked in me: and thou haſt \f e \fr 37.3 \ft thou haſt ſtrooke me with an heauie hand.\f* faſtened thy hand vpon me.
\v 4 There is \f f \fr 37.4 \ft I already feele in my flesh, in al my bones, and powres great affliction,\f* no health in my flesh, at \f g \fr 37.4 \ft conſidering thy iuſtice,\f* the face of thy wrath: my bones haue no peace at \f h \fr 37.4 \ft and my ſinnes.\f* the face of my ſinnes.
\v 5 Becauſe mine iniquities are gone \f i \fr 37.5 \ft which are excedingly increaſed, almoſt ouerwhelming my ſpirite.\f* ouer my head: and as a \f k \fr 37.5 \ft ſinnes not washed away by penance by their weight carie the ſoule into more and more wickednes.\f* heauie burden are become heauie vpon me.
\v 6 My \f l \fr 37.6 \ft ſtil corrupting thoſe partes which were whole before, as a peſtered ſore that is not cured.\f* ſcarres are putrified and corrupted, becauſe of my folishnes.
\v 7 I am become miſerable, and am made \f m \fr 37.7 \ft not able to goe ſtreight to do anie good worke, being guiltie of greuous ſinne.\f* crooked euen to the end: I went ſorowful al the day.
\v 8 Becauſe \f n \fr 37.8 \ft concupiſcence ſtriuing in me.\f* my loynes are filled with illuſions: and there is no health in my flesh.
\v 9 I am afflicted and am humbled excedingly: I \f o \fr 37.9 \ft from the ſorrow of my hart, my voice hath broken out into clamour.\f* rored for the groning of my hart.
\v 10 Lord, \f p \fr 37.10 \ft ô God thou knoweſt my deſire, to be reſtored to thy fauour.\f* before thee is al my deſire: and my groning is not hid from thee.
\v 11 My hart is trubled, my ſtrength hath forſaken me: and the light of mine eies, and the ſame is not with me.
\v 12 My \f q \fr 37.12 \ft thoſe that were my freindes and companions in ſinne are become myn enemies, becauſe I forſake them:\f* frendes, and my neighbores haue approched, & ſtood againſt me. And they that were neere me, ſtood far of:
\v 13 and they did violence which ſought my ſoule. And they that ſought me euils, \f r \fr 37.13 \ft ſought by al meanes to intangle me againe.\f* ſpake vanities: and meditated guiles al the day.
\v 14 But I as \f s \fr 37.14 \ft I now renoũce al ſinne.\f* one deafe did not heare: and as one dumme not opening his mouth.
\v 15 And I became as a man not hearing: and not hauing reproofes in his mouth.
\v 16 Becauſe \f t \fr 37.16 \ft I now relie vpon thee ô God.\f* in thee ô Lord haue I hoped, thou wilt heare me ô Lord my God.
\v 17 \f v \fr 37.17 \ft for this cauſe I am returned to thee and doe pray that mine enemies may not preuail againſt me.\f* Becauſe I ſaid: Leſt ſometime mine enemies reioyce ouer me: and whiles my feete are moued, they ſpeake great thinges vpon me.
\v 18 Becauſe I \f w \fr 37.18 \ft I reſigne my ſelfe to thee.\f* am readie for ſcourges: and my ſorow is in my ſight alwaies:
\v 19 Becauſe \f x \fr 37.19 \ft though thou knoweſt al yet with mouth confeſſion is made to ſaluation.\f* I wil declare my iniquitie: and I wil \f y \fr 37.19 \ft and I meditate of that which my ſinne hath deſerued.\f* thinke for my ſinne.
\v 20 But mine enemies liue, and are confirmed ouer me: and they are multiplied that hate me vniuſtly.
\v 21 They that repay euil thinges for good, \f z \fr 37.21 \ft one kind of detraction is in reueling ſecrete faultes, an other in feaning and imputing falſe crimes, the third ( here mentioned ) in calling vertue vice, as penance, hypochriſie.\f* detracted from me: becauſe I folowed goodnes.
\v 22 \f a \fr 37.22 \ft Graunt me Lord final perſeuerãce in thy grace, and ſeruice.\f* Forſake me not ô Lord my God, depart not from me.
\v 23 Attend vnto my help, ô Lord the God of my ſaluation.
\c 38
\cd A iuſt man in remediles perſecution reſolueth to ſuffer al with peace and ſilence: 5. praying God to take him from this world, confeſſing the vanitie therof. 8. and relying on Gods prouidence (11. who punisheth man for his ſinnes) prayeth for releaſe. \f - \ft Gods prouidence. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 38.1 \ft Some expound this Pſalme of the Iewes in captiuitie in Babylon, but this title, and the matter conteyned shew, that it rather peteineth to the new Teſtament.\f* the end, to \f b \fr 38.1 \ft to be ſongue by Idithun and his ſcholars & ſucceſſors, or rather by Chriſtianes.\f* Idithun him ſelfe, a canticle of Dauid.
\v 2 I \f c \fr 38.2 \ft weake men in affliction not able to deliuer them ſelues, ſeeke reuenge, by murmuring and other euil ſpeaches, but the perfect reſolue to rule their tongues:\f* HAVE ſaid: I wil keepe my waies: that I offend not in my tongue. I haue ſet a gard to my mouth, when the ſinner ſtood againſt me.
\v 3 I was dumme and humbled, and kept ſilence \f d \fr 38.3 \ft euen to forbeare ſometimes from their owne iuſt defence:\f* from good thinges: and \f e \fr 38.3 \ft though therby they indure more perſecution.\f* my ſorrow was renewed.
\v 4 My \f f \fr 38.4 \ft ſorow ſuppreſſed maketh the hart to burne with zele, and indignation.\f* hart waxed hote within me: and in my meditation a fyre shal burne.
\v 5 I haue ſpoken in my tongue: Lord \f g \fr 38.5 \ft If it may pleaſe thee let me know how long I shal liue, deſiring to dye; as Elias deſired. \+it 3. Reg. 19\+it*.\f* make mine end knowne to me. And the number of my daies what it is: that I may know what is lacking to me.
\v 6 Behold thou haſt put my daies meaſurable, and my \f h \fr 38.6 \ft my life and al that I haue is as nothing compared to thy eternitie.\f* ſubſtance is as nothing before thee. Doubtles al things are vanitie, euerie man liuing.
\v 7 Surely man paſſeth as \f i \fr 38.7 \ft as a shadow or image appearing in a glaſſe, which is quickly forgotte.\f* an image; yea and he is trubled \f k \fr 38.7 \ft therfore there is no cauſe man should be trubled in mind for temporal miſeries.\f* in vayne. He gathereth treaſure; and knoweth not to whom he shal gather them.
\v 8 And now what is my expectation? is not our Lord? and my ſubſtance is with thee.
\v 9 From al mine iniquities deliuer me: \f l \fr 38.9 \ft Thou haſt ſuffered me to be reproched by the foolish that proſper in this world.\f* a reproch to the foolish thou haſt geuen me.
\v 10 I was dumme, and opened not my mouth, \f m \fr 38.10 \ft I know my tribulation is by thy prouidence.\f* becauſe thou didſt it:
\v 11 Remoue thy ſcourges from me.
\v 12 By the ſtrength of thy hand I haue faynted in reprehenſions: for iniquitie thou haſt chaſtiſed man. And thou haſt made his \f o \fr 38.12 \ft my life decayeth as a ſpider hauing ſpent al her moyſture.\f* ſoule pyne away as a ſpider: but vaynly is euerie man trubled.
\v 13 Heare my prayer ô Lord, and my petition; with thyne eares receiue my teares. Keepe not ſilence: becauſe I am a \f p \fr 38.13 \ft Al men are ſtrangers in this life, heauen being our home.\f* ſtranger with thee, and a pilgrime, as my fathers.
\v 14 Forgeue me, that I may \f q \fr 38.14 \ft that I may recouer ſpiritual ſtreingth in this life:\f* be refreshed before I depart: and \f r \fr 38.14 \ft after which I shal not be in ſtate to do free workes of ſatisfaction nor merite.\f* shal be no more.
\c 39
\cd Chriſts faithful members after long expectation congratulate his coming in flesh. 6. He directing his ſpeach to his Father, profeſſeth to performe the Redemption of mankind, and to denounce the ſame in the whole world: 12. prayeth for his ſeruantes, vndertaking to ſatisfie for their ſinnes. \f - \ft Chriſts comming and redeming of mankind The 5 key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 39.1 \ft perteyning vnto the new Teſtament.\f* the end, a Pſalme to Dauid himſelfe.
\v 2 EXPECTING \f b \fr 39.2 \ft the faithful of the old and new Teſtament reioyce in the coming of Chriſt.\f* I expected our Lord, and he hath attended to me.
\v 3 And he heard my prayers, and brought me out of the lake of miſerie, and from the myre of dregges. And hath ſet my feete vpon a rocke: and hath directed my ſteppes.
\v 4 And he hath put a new canticle into my mouth: a ſong to our God. Manie shal ſee, and shal feare: and they shal hope in our Lord.
\v 5 Bleſſed is the man, whoſe hope is in the name of our Lord: and hath not had regard to vanities and falſe madnes.
\v 6 Thou haſt done manie thy meruelous thinges ô Lord my God: & in thy cogitations there is none that may be like to thee. I \f c \fr 39.6 \ft Chriſt by him ſelfe and by others preached the Goſpel of ſaluation.\f* haue declared and haue ſpoken: they \f d \fr 39.6 \ft the multiplication of Chriſtians therby.\f* multiplied aboue number.
\v 7 \f e \fr 39.7 \ft No ſacrifice of the old teſtament ſufficed to ſatisfie Gods iuſtice for the ſinne of man.\f* Sacrifice and oblation thou wouldeſt not: but \f f \fr 39.7 \ft Chriſt by the eare of obedience performed the redemption of man by his death, as was determined from eternitie. S. Paul for [eares] ſaith [bodie] See Annotations. Heb. 10.\f* eares thou haſt perfited to me. \x - \xt Heb. 10.\x* Holocauſt and for ſinne thou didſt not require:
\v 8 then ſaid I, Behold I come. In the \f g \fr 39.8 \ft The ſumme of holie Scripture is of Chriſts Incarnation & death for redemption of man.\f* head of the booke it is written of me,
\v 9 that I should doe thy wil: my God I would, and thy law in the middes of my hart.
\v 10 I haue \f h \fr 39.10 \ft Againe Chriſt inculcateth the preaching and receiuing of his Goſpel in the whole world.\f* declared thy iuſtice in the great Church, loe I wil not ſtay my lippes: Lord thou haſt knowen it.
\v 11 Thy iuſtice I haue not hid in my hart: thy truth and thy ſaluation I haue ſpoken. I haue not hid thy mercie, and thy truth from the \f i \fr 39.11 \ft In the greateſt and wiſeſt congregations of this world, Chriſt concealeth not his mercie and truth. So himſelfe profeſſed before Annas, Caiphas, Pilate, and their councels. S. Paul preached Chriſt at Athens, and in manie nations, and ſo the other Apoſtles. For their voice went into al the coaſtes of the earth.\f* great councel.
\v 12 \f k \fr 39.12 \ft The prophet now ſpeaketh in the name of Chriſts myſtical bodie the Church: praying to be made partaker of mercie, and to be deliuered from euiles.\f* But thou ô Lord make not thy commiſerations farre from me: thy mercie and thy truth haue alwayes receiued me.
\v 13 Becauſe euils haue compaſſed me, which haue no number: mine iniquities haue ouertaken me, & I was \f l \fr 39.13 \ft the ſinnes alſo of thoſe which beleue in Chriſt are ſo manie, that they can not be fully ſene in particular.\f* not able to ſee. They are multiplied aboue the heares of my head: and \f m \fr 39.13 \ft I almoſt faint in conſidering ſo manie and ſo great iniquities amongst thoſe that profeſſe Chriſt.\f* my hart hath forſaken me.
\v 14 It may pleaſe thee ô Lord to deliuer \f n \fr 39.14 \ft The whole Church prayeth in the name of al for the infirme members.\f* me: Lord haue reſpect to helpe me.
\v 15 Let \f o \fr 39.15 \ft The prophet foresheweth that the reprobate for their obſtinate malice, ſeeking to hurt others shal be confounded.\f* them be confounded and ashamed together, that ſeeke my ſoule, to take it away. Let them be turned backward, and be ashamed that wil me euils.
\v 16 Let them forthwith receiue their confuſion, that \f p \fr 39.16 \ft that skornfully ſay: \+it wel, wel\+it*: wishing al euil to good men.\f* ſay to me: Wel, wel.
\v 17 Let al \f q \fr 39.17 \ft which not only in mouth and outward profeſſion, but alſo in ſinceritie of hart ſeeke thee, may with confidence reioyce, and praiſe God.\f* that ſeeke thee, reioyce and be glad vpon thee: and let them that loue thy ſaluation, ſay alwayes: Our Lord be magnified:
\v 18 But \f r \fr 39.18 \ft Chriſt ſpeaketh in the name of ſinners truly repenting, whoſe ſinnes he vndertaketh to redeme, and wash away by his paſſion.\f* I am a begger, and poore: Our Lord is careful of me. Thou art \f s \fr 39.18 \ft The faithful of the old teſtament pray for Chriſts firſt coming into this world, and the faithful now pray for his ſecond coming to purge his Church, and to reward the good.\f* my helper, & my protector: my God be not ſlacke.
\c 40
\cd The prophet pronounceth them happie that wil beleue in Chriſt, coming in humilitie and pouertie. 5. Chriſt deſcribeth his owne poore afflicted ſtate in this life, by reaſon he is to ſatisfie for the ſinnes of the world; the malice of his aduerſaries, 10. eſpecially of Iudas, 11. and by way of prayer, prophecieth his owne Reſurrection. \f - \ft Chriſt's Paſſion and Reſurrection. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 40.1 \ft Perteyning to the new teſtament, as appeareth by the 10. verſe alleaged by our Sauiour. This Pſalme is alſo applied by the Church in the office of the ſick, whom whoſoeuer aſſiſteth in that caſe, may hope to haue aſſiſtance in their owne like neceſſitie.\f* the end, a Pſalme to Dauid him ſelfe. \x - \xt Io. 13. v. 18. This Pſalm is alſo applied by the Church in the office of the ſick, whom whoſoever aſſiſteth in that caſe, may hope to have aſſiſtance in their own like neceſſity.\x*
\v 2 BLESSED is the man that vnderſtandeth concerning \f b \fr 40.2 \ft He is happie that is not ſcandalized in Chriſt (Luc. 7 v. 23.) coming in pouertie, and ſuffering extreme afflictions.\f* the needie, and the poore: in \f c \fr 40.2 \ft He that truſteth in Chriſt, notwithſtanding the contrarie motiues of his wordlie miſerie, shal be deliuered by him in al diſtreſſe.\f* the euil day our Lord wil deliuer him.
\v 3 Our Lord \f d \fr 40.3 \ft Our Lord wil geue to ſuch ſeruantes more grace in this life, and glorie in the next.\f* preſerue him, and geue him life, and make him bleſſed in the land: and \f e \fr 40.3 \ft not ſuffer him to be ouercome in tentations.\f* deliuer him not vnto the wil of his enemies.
\v 4 Our Lord helpe him \f f \fr 40.4 \ft When ſuch conſtant ſeruantes are ſick to death, Chriſt wil moſt eſpecially comforte and helpe them.\f* vpon the bed of his ſorow: thou haſt turned al his couche in his infirmitie.
\v 5 I ſaid: \f g \fr 40.5 \ft Chriſt in the behalf of his myſtical bodie confeſſeth their ſinnes, and prayeth for them.\f* Lord haue haue mercie on me: heale my ſoule, becauſe I haue ſinned to thee.
\v 6 Mine enemies haue ſpoken euils to me: When shal he die, \f h \fr 40.6 \ft After death ſuffered for mankind Chriſt riſeth, and his name and kingdom is glorious.\f* and his name perish?
\v 7 And if \f i \fr 40.7 \ft Thoſe that came not of good wil, but of malice to obſerue Chriſts deedes and wordes, carped at both, ſometimes ſaying, he taught againſt the law, and againſt Moyſes; ſometymes that he caſt out diuels in the powre of Beelſebub.\f* he came in to ſee, he ſpake vayne thinges: his hart hath gathered together iniquitie to him ſelfe. He went forth. and ſpake together.
\v 8 Al mine enemies whiſpered againſt me: they did thinke euils to me.
\v 9 They \f k \fr 40.9 \ft At laſt they reſolued that he should die.\f* haue determined an vniuſt word againſt me: \f l \fr 40.9 \ft But they could not ſo ſuppreſſe his powre, for he roſe againe in glorie.\f* Shal not he that ſleepeth adde to ryſe againe?
\v 10 For \f m \fr 40.10 \ft By our Sauiours application of this verſe, it is certaine that the traitor Iudas is here deſcribed. Ioa. 13. v. 18.\f* the man alſo of my peace, in whom I hoped: who did eate my breades, hath greatly troden me vnder foote \x - \xt Io. 13. Act. 1.\x*
\v 11 But thou ô Lord haue mercie vpon me, and raiſe me vp againe: and I \f n \fr 40.11 \ft in the day of iudgement Chriſt Iudge of al wil render to euerie one as they deſerue.\f* wil repay them.
\v 12 In this I haue knowen that thou wouldeſt me: becauſe mine enemie shal not reioyce ouer me.
\v 13 But me thou haſt receiued \f o \fr 40.13 \ft As before in reſpect of ſinners, Chriſt Iudge of al wil render to euerie one: ſo here in his owne perſon he auoucheth his owne innocencie, which made him apt to ſatisfie for others.\f* becauſe of innocencie: and thou haſt confirmed me in thy ſight for euer.
\v 14 Bleſſed be our Lord the God of Iſrael \f p \fr 40.14 \ft For this mercie of Almightie God in ſauing the elect by his Sonnes death, he is to be praiſed for euer eternally.\f* from the beginning of the world, and for euermore: \f q \fr 40.14 \ft Al the bleſſed agree in this, that God is eternally to be praiſed and therto ſay \+it Amen,\+it* So be it, ſo be it. Some diuide the Pſalmes into fiue bookes, ſuppoſing the firſt booke to end here with theſe wordes; \+it Be it, be it:\+it* not obſeruing that the laſt Pſalme hath not this ending. S Ierom confuteth this opinion by our Sauiours, and S. Peters naming it the booke, not bookes of Pſalmes. Luc 20. v. 42. Act. 1. Moreouer if this were the end of one booke, then the Pſalme folowing ſhould not be called the 41. Pſalme, but the firſt Pſalme of the ſecond booke.\f* Be it, be it.
\c 41
\cd The feruent deſire of the iuſt, 6. much afflicted in this life, 12. and aſſured hope of eternal ioy. \f - \ft Eternal glory. The 10. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, vnderſtanding to the ſonnes of \f a \fr 41.1 \ft The ſonnes of Core repented, and departed from their fathers ſchiſme, and ſo eſcaped miraculoſly the horrible pitte of damnation, into which their father and his complices fel. Num. 26. v. 10. By which example al ſeduced, and deceiued Chriſtians are admonished, not to perſiſt in ſchiſme or other ſinnes. And wordlie men, ambicious of honour be warned to deſire & ſeke God aboue al thinges, firſt of al the kingdome of heauen (to be liuing members of the Catholique Church) and the iuſtice therof: to ſeke thinges which are aboue, not which are vpon the earth; leſt hel deuoure them, as it deuoured the complices of Core. Num. 16 v. 31.\f* Core.
\v 2 EVEN as the harte \f b \fr 41.2 \ft A harte waxing old, and burdened with much heare, and great hornes, draweth a ſerpent into his noſethrels, ſo being infected with poyſon, deſireth moſt ardently to drinke, and afterwards caſteth his hornes, and heare, and becometh as it were yong againe.\f* deſireth after the fountaines of waters: \f c \fr 41.2 \ft with ſuch feruent deſire a true penitent, feeling him ſelfe infected with poyſou of ſinnes, ſeeketh the water of Gods grace.\f* ſo doth my ſoule deſire after thee ô God.
\v 3 My ſoule hath thirſted after God \f d \fr 41.3 \ft God is omnipotent, and in dede the only-true liuing God: diuels who are honored in idols, can do no more then God permitteth, and ſo they can kil the ſoules, that conſent vnto their tentations, but can not reſtore ſpiritual life againe.\f* the ſtrong \f e \fr 41.3 \ft See previous note\f* liuing: \f f \fr 41.3 \ft The ſoule being iuſtified, and ſtil aſſaulted with new tentations deſireth to be with God.\f* when shal I come and appeare before the face of God?
\v 4 My \f g \fr 41.4 \ft I haue had no other refection, but to lenifie my ſorow with weping.\f* teares haue beene breades vnto me day and night: whiles it is ſaid to me dayly: \f h \fr 41.4 \ft The wicked exprobate the iuſt, as though God would neuer helpe them, becauſe he ſuffereth them to be ſometimes long in tribulation.\f* Where is thy God?
\v 5 Theſe thinges haue I remembred, and haue powred out my ſoule in me, becauſe I shal paſſe into the place \f i \fr 41.5 \ft King Dauid was not permitted to build the temple, much leſſe did he enter into anie ſuch meruelous tabernacle in his mortal life, but muſt nedes be vnderſtood, to ſpeake here of the heauenlie tabernacle, prepared by Chriſt for his ſeruants.\f* of a meruelous tabernacle, euen to the houſe of God. In the yoyce of exultation, and confeſſion: the ſound of one feaſting.
\v 6 Why \f k \fr 41.6 \ft The prophet conforteth him ſelfe, or anie iuſt ſoule, in the hope of euerlaſting ioy.\f* art thou ſorowful my ſoule? and why doſt thou truble me? Hope in God, becauſe yet I \f l \fr 41.6 \ft I render thankes and praiſes.\f* wil confeſſe to him: the ſaluation of my countenance,
\v 7 and my God. My ſoule is trubled toward my ſelfe; therfore wil I be mindful of thee from the land \f m \fr 41.7 \ft Al this life is like to the ſmal ſtraict place betwene Iordan and a litle hil called Hermoniim; but from this ſtraictnes the hope of the iuſt is, to be placed in heauen.\f* of Iordan, and Hermoniim from the litle mountaine.
\v 8 Depth \f n \fr 41.8 \ft One tentation ſtil ſuccedeth an other:\f* calleth on depth, in the voice of \f o \fr 41.8 \ft and the ſame ſo great, as if God opened the gates; and ſuffered them to ouerflow like fluddes of water.\f* thy flould-gates. Al thy high thinges, and thy waues haue paſſed ouer me.
\v 9 In \f p \fr 41.9 \ft But God helpeth in opportunitie, not ſuffering his ſeruantes to be tempted aboue their ſtreingth, geuing them fruit with tentations:\f* the day our Lord hath commanded his mercie: and \f q \fr 41.9 \ft yea in the greateſt tribulation, he geueth ordinarily moſt comfort, making them ſing ſpiritually in hart, if not alſo in voice.\f* in the night a ſong of him. With me \f r \fr 41.9 \ft one ſpecial meanes to procure diuine conſolation is prayer in diſtreſſe.\f* is prayer to the God of my life:
\v 10 I wil ſay to God: Thou art my defender. Why haſt thou forgotten me? and why goe I ſorowful, whiles mine enemie afflicteth me?
\v 11 Whiles my bones are broken, mine enemies that truble me haue vpbrayded me: Whiles they ſay to me day by day: Where is thy God?
\v 12 Why art thou heauie ô my ſoule? & why doſt thou truble me? \f s \fr 41.12 \ft Stil the iuſt ſoule taketh comforth in aſſured hope of ſaluation, the eternal viſion of God.\f* Hope in God, becauſe yet I wil confeſſe to him: the ſaluation of my countenance, and my God.
\c 42
\cd The iuſt inuocateth Gods ſentence againſt the deceiptful, that ſeeke his ſpiritual ouerthrow, 4. acknowledgeth his helpe, from almightie God the B. Trinitie, in whoſe viſion glorie conſiſteth. \f - \ft One God the B. Trinity. The 1. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Pſalme \f a \fr 42.1 \ft Holie Dauid often prefiguring Chriſt, here repreſenteth euerie faithful ſeruant of God, and particularly when they beginne a great and holie worke; as when Prieſtes celebrate the diuine Sacrifice, they with their aſſiſtantes recite by interchangeable verſes this Pſalme.\f* of Dauid. IVDGE \f b \fr 42.1 \ft After that we haue examined, and prepared our ſelues to the moſt holie Sacrifice and Sacrament, according to S. Paules admonition (let a man proue himſelfe, and ſo eate this bread, and drinke this chalice, 1. Cor. 11.) we pray God, to iudge betwen our true ſincere intention, and the vniuſt deceipful endeuoures of our enimie: and ſo to deliuer and protect vs from ſutle malice.\f* me ô God, & diſcerne my cauſe from the nation not holie, from the vniuſt and deceitful man \f c \fr 42.1 \ft and ſo to deliuer and protect vs from ſutle malice.\f* deliuer me.
\v 2 Becauſe thou art God \f d \fr 42.2 \ft VVith thee I can do anie thing, without thee nothing,\f* my ſtrength: \f e \fr 42.2 \ft thou ſemeſt ſometimes not to regard me,\f* why haſt thou repelled me? and why goe I ſorowful, \f f \fr 42.2 \ft whiles tentations are more ſenſible then thy grace.\f* whiles the enemie afflicteth me?
\v 3 Send forth \f g \fr 42.3 \ft As thou haſt ſent Chriſt the light and truth into this world, grant vs the ſame now in particular.\f* thy light and thy truth: \f h \fr 42.3 \ft Theſe two giftes of God, the light of knowing our duties and truth, with ſincere intention to performe the ſame, haue brought vs into thy Church, and vnto thy Altar.\f* they haue conducted me, and haue brought me into thy holy hil, and into thy tabernacles.
\v 4 And \f i \fr 42.4 \ft Accompanied with light of truth, and ſincere intention, we confidently approch to thyn Altar ô God,\f* I wil goe in to the altar of God: to God, which \f k \fr 42.4 \ft vvho changeſt our old corruption into newnes of life.\f* maketh my youth ioyful.
\v 5 I wil confeſſe to thee \f l \fr 42.5 \ft But to this purpoſe we praiſe God on the harpe, mortifying our affections.\f* on the harpe \f m \fr 42.5 \ft The former word is of the plural number in hebrevv, Eloim, the other of the ſingular, ſignifying the Bleſſed Trinitie, one God.\f* ô God \f n \fr 42.5 \ft See previous note\f* my God: \f o \fr 42.5 \ft Thou needeſt not therfore my ſoule, be penſiue, or deſolate.\f* why art thou ſorowful ô my ſoule? and doſt thou trubel me?
\v 6 \f p \fr 42.6 \ft But truſt in God,\f* Hope in God, becauſe yet \f q \fr 42.6 \ft praiſe him,\f* wil I cõfeſſe to him: the ſaluation \f r \fr 42.6 \ft vvhom I hope to ſee face to face,\f* of my countenance, and \f s \fr 42.6 \ft the true eternal God.\f* my God.
\c 43
\cd The prophet deſcribeth the firſt calling, and difficulte ſtate of the Iewish nation, 6. their proſperitie at other times. 10. Againe their afflictions in captiuitie, and perſecutions. \f - \ft The ſtate of the Jews. The 4. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 43.1 \ft Though this Pſalme doth firſt and literally perteyne to the people of Iſrael, yet al thinges happening to them, vvere in figure of the Chriſtian Catholique Church, vvhich begane vvith difficulties, aftervvards proſpered, and againe ſuffereth much perſecution.\f* the end, for the ſonnes of \f b \fr 43.1 \ft Core ſignifieth caluus, bauld; alſo caluaria a ſcul, or place of ſculles: the name of the place vvhere our Sauiour vvas crucified, ſo the children of Core ſignifie the children of Chriſt. S. Aug.\f* Core to vnderſtanding.
\v 2 O GOD we haue heard with our eares: our fathers haue declared to vs. The \f c \fr 43.2 \ft The particular calling of Abraham out of Chaldea, protection of him, and Iſaac, and Iacob, the deliuerie of al Iſrael out of Ægypt, and eſtablishing them in the promiſed land of Chanaan, with innumerable, great, and ſtrange thinges donne for them.\f* worke, that thou haſt wrought in their dayes: and in the dayes of old.
\v 3 Thy hand deſtroyed the nations, and thou didſt plant them: thou didſt afflict the peoples, and expel them:
\v 4 For \f d \fr 43.4 \ft The Iſraelites conquered not by ordinarie povvre, but by the miraculous hand of God.\f* not by their owne ſword did they poſſeſſe the land, and their owne arme did not ſaue them: But thy right hand, and thyne arme, and the illumination of thy countenance: becauſe \f e \fr 43.4 \ft Not that this people deſerued of themſelues, but of Gods free election, al the vvorld being vvicked, he gaue peculiar grace to Abraham, Iſaac, Iacob and ſome others, and then for their ſakes protected the vvhole people, in them conſeruing a viſible Church.\f* thou waſt pleaſed in them.
\v 5 Thou art the ſame my king and my God: which commandeſt the ſaluations of Iacob.
\v 6 In \f f \fr 43.6 \ft As in former examples, ſo in Dauids time, not mans ſtreingth, but Gods hand gaue them great victories.\f* thee we shal turne out our enemies with \f g \fr 43.6 \ft As an oxe vvith his horne caſteth a ſmal thing into the vvind.\f* the horne, & in thy name we shal contemne them that ryſe vp against vs.
\v 7 For I wil not hope in my bowe: and my ſword wil not ſaue me.
\v 8 For thou haſt ſaued vs from them that afflict vs: and them that hate vs thou haſt confounded. \x - \xt Ios. 24. 1. Reg. 17. 2. Reg. 8.\x*
\v 9 In God we shal be prayſed al the day: and in thy name we wil confeſſe for euer.
\v 10 But \f h \fr 43.10 \ft The prophet fortelleth that after proſperitie God vvould ſuffer the Ievves to falle into captiuitie, & manie afflictions, which alſo ſignified allegorically diuers ſtates of Chriſts Church.\f* now thou haſt repelled and confounded vs: and thou wilt not goe forth ô God in our hoſtes.
\v 11 Thou haſt turned vs backe behind our enemies: and they that hated vs, ſpoyled for themſelues.
\v 12 Thou haſt geuen vs as sheepe that are to be eaten: and thou \f i \fr 43.12 \ft The Iewes are now meruelouſly diſperſed, and depreſſed.\f* haſt diſperſed vs among the nations.
\v 13 Thou haſt ſold thy people \f k \fr 43.13 \ft In the deſtruction of Ieruſalem the remnant of the people were ſold for ſmal, as it wree for no price. They had ſold Chriſt for thirtie pence;\f* without price: and there was \f l \fr 43.13 \ft and now no multitude, nor number of money at al was geuen for them, but thirtie of them were ſold for one pennie, Ioſephus de bello Iudaico.\f* no multitude in the exchanges of them.
\v 14 Thou haſt made vs a reproche to our neighbours, a ſcorne and mocking ſtocke to them, that are round about vs.
\v 15 Thou haſt made vs for a parable to the Gentiles: a wagging of the head among the peoples.
\v 16 Al the day my shame is againſt me, and the confuſion of my face hath couered me.
\v 17 At the voyce of the vpbrayder, and the reprocher: at the face of the enemie and perſecutor.
\v 18 Al theſe thinges haue come vpon vs, \f m \fr 43.18 \ft Vntil Chriſts paſſion the Iewish people did not wholly fal from God, and true religion. And of them were choſen the Apoſtles, and manie others, that founded and propagated the Church of Chriſt.\f* neither haue we forgotten thee: and we haue not done wickedly in thy teſtament.
\v 19 And our hart hath not reuolted backward: and \f n \fr 43.19 \ft The negatiue particle is here vnderſtood by zeugma, according to the hebrew thus: our hart hath not reuolted backward, neither haſt thou ſuffered our pathes to decline frō thy way.\f* thou haſt declined our pathes from thy way:
\v 20 \f o \fr 43.20 \ft An other hebrew phraiſe, If we haue, for, we haue not.\f* Becauſe thou haſt humbled vs in the place of affliction, and the shadow of death hath couered vs.
\v 21 If we haue forgotten the name of our God, and if we haue ſpred forth our handes to a ſtrange God:
\v 22 Wil not God enquire of theſe thinges? For he knoweth the ſecretes of the hart. Becauſe \f p \fr 43.22 \ft The Prophetes and others perſecuted partly before Chriſt, much more the Apoſtles, and other Chriſtians in the new Teſtament:\f* for thee we are killed al the day: we are eſtemed as sheepe of ſlaughter. \x - \xt Rom. 8.\x*
\v 23 \f q \fr 43.23 \ft A prayer in affliction.\f* Ariſe why ſleepeſt thou ô Lord? Ariſe, and expel vs not to the end.
\v 24 Why doſt thou turne away thy face, forgetteſt our pouertie and our tribulation?
\v 25 Becauſe \f r \fr 43.25 \ft we are at deaths dore, readie to become duſt.\f* our ſoule is humbled in the duſt: our \f s \fr 43.25 \ft lying as groueling ſorowing on the earth:\f* bellie is glewed in the earth.
\v 26 \f t \fr 43.26 \ft til thou deliuer vs from theſe tribulations.\f* Ariſe Lord, helpe vs: and redeme vs for thy name.
\c 44
\cd Dauid ſingularly moued in hart and tongue, 3. prophecieth Chriſts excellencie, indowing his Church with moſt worthie dowries. 11. by way of exhortation forshewing her internal and external beautie. 17. with perpetual ſucceſſion of Paſtors feeding the flock euen to the worlds end. \f - \ft Chriſt moſt excellently endowing his Church. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 44.1 \ft Perteyning to the new Teſtament.\f* the end, for them, \f b \fr 44.1 \ft Gentiles conuerted from paganiſme to Chriſtianitie:\f* that shal be changed, \f c \fr 44.1 \ft and al others returning from ſchiſme, or other ſinnes,\f* to the ſonnes of Core, \f d \fr 44.1 \ft for their inſtruction,\f* for vnderſtanding, \f e \fr 44.1 \ft this Pſalme is a mariage ſongue of the beloued bridgrome and bride: Chriſt and his Church.\f* a Canticle for the beloued.
\v 2 MY \f f \fr 44.2 \ft I haue receiued by diuine inſpirarion in my hart and cogitation,\f* hart hath vttered \f g \fr 44.2 \ft a moſt high Myſterie.\f* a good word: I tel my workes \f h \fr 44.2 \ft To the honour therfore and glorie of this king (vvhom I ſecretly ſee in my hart) I vtter and referre al my vvorkes, and this particular Canticle.\f* to the king. My \f i \fr 44.2 \ft From the abundance of my hart, my tongue alſo ſpeaketh,\f* tongue is the penne of a ſcribe, that \f k \fr 44.2 \ft & that preſently without delay\f* writeth ſwiftly.
\v 3 \f l \fr 44.3 \ft Deſcription of Chriſt, moſt excellent in al internal and external giftes.\f* Goodly of beautie aboue the ſonnes of men, grace is powred abrode in thy lippes: therfore hath God bleſſed thee for euer.
\v 4 Be \f m \fr 44.4 \ft The prophet ſeing in ſpirite the perfections vvhich he wisheth in Chriſt, in maner of congratulating, deſcribeth his fortitude, fighting againſt the diuel for the Church.\f* girded with thy ſword vpon thy thigh, ô moſt mightie.
\v 5 With thy beautie and fayrneſſe \f n \fr 44.5 \ft purpoſing,\f* intend, \f o \fr 44.5 \ft proſecuting,\f* procede proſperouſly, and \f p \fr 44.5 \ft and perfecting the conqueſt, and ſo eſtablishing thy ſpiritual kingdome.\f* reigne, Becauſe of \f q \fr 44.5 \ft Not vvith warlike armour of this world, but by aſſaulting the aduerſarie with truth:\f* truth, and \f r \fr 44.5 \ft defending thyſelfe and thy ſouldiers with the shield of mildnes,\f* mildeneſſe, and \f s \fr 44.5 \ft and ſtriking the enemie with the ſword of iuſtice. VVhich right force of ſpiritual fight hath meruelous good ſucceſſe.\f* iuſtice: and thy right hand shal conduct thee meruelouſly.
\v 6 Thy \f t \fr 44.6 \ft Preaching of Chriſts Goſpel, his grace mouing the hartes of the hearers, is liuelie and forcible, more pearcing then anie two edged ſword.\f* sharpe arrowes, the \f v \fr 44.6 \ft The example of people conuerted, shal moue the hartes of the aduerſaries to come alſo vnto the truth.\f* peoples vnderneth thee shal fal into the hartes of the kings enemies.
\v 7 Thy ſeate ô God \f w \fr 44.7 \ft Chriſts kingdom shal haue no end. Luc. 1. v. 33.\f* for euer and euer: a rod of direction the rod of thy kingdom.
\v 8 Thou \f x \fr 44.8 \ft Thou defendeſt and rewardeſt the good, finally forſakeſt and puniſheſt the wicked.\f* haſt loued iuſtice, and haſt hated iniquitie: therfore God, \f y \fr 44.8 \ft more peculiarly the God of Chriſt, by hypoſtatical vnion.\f* thy God, hath annoynted thee with the oile of gladnes \f z \fr 44.8 \ft Diuers kinges (as Dauid himſelfe, Ioſaphat, Ezechias, and Ioſias) were as godlie as Salomon, and perſeuered good to the end, which is doubted Salomon did not: but Chriſt incomparably was annointed, & indued with al graces aboue al kinges.\f* aboue thy felowes.
\v 9 \f a \fr 44.9 \ft Mortification which conſerueth from putrifying,\f* Myrrhe, and \f b \fr 44.9 \ft humilitie aſwaging pride,\f* Aloes, and \f c \fr 44.9 \ft being ſmal in the firſt ſpring grovveth great,\f* Caſſia from thy \f d \fr 44.9 \ft humanitie aſſumpted; and ſanctified perſons, in vvhom Chriſt dwelleth as in cleane, ſhining, odoriferous houſes;\f* garmentes, from houſes of yuorie; out of the which
\v 10 \f e \fr 44.10 \ft ſincere faithful ſoules more deare to their ſpouſe Chriſt, then daughrers of temporal kinges.\f* the daughter of kinges haue delighted thee in thy honour.
\v 11 The \f f \fr 44.11 \ft The Catholique Church, in faith purified as gold:\f* Queene ſtood on thy right hand in golden rayment: compaſſed with \f g \fr 44.11 \ft vvith varietie of ſtates, as Clergie, Laity and diuers ſortes of religious Orders, and other profeſſions, al vnited in the ſame faith, hope, and charitie.\f* varietie. \f h \fr 44.11 \ft carifully al that Chriſt thy ſpouſe ſpeaketh to thee by his Spirite.\f* Heare daughter, and \f i \fr 44.11 \ft diligently put the ſame in practiſe:\f* ſee, and \f k \fr 44.11 \ft vvith al obedience and readines, and returne not to former infidelitie, nor to corrupt life.\f* incline thyne eare: and forget thy people, and the houſe of thy father.
\v 12 And the king \f l \fr 44.12 \ft Chriſt loueth the Church adorned with his giftes,\f* wil couet thy beautie: becauſe he is the Lord thy God, and \f m \fr 44.12 \ft and mutually his true children loue and ſerue him.\f* they shal adore him.
\v 13 And the daughters \f n \fr 44.13 \ft Manie of al nations ſubmitte themſelues, and al that they haue to Chriſt.\f* of Tyre with giftes, al the rich of the people shal beſech thy countenance.
\v 14 Al the glorie of that daughter of the king is \f o \fr 44.14 \ft Internal vertues are moſt eſpecial ornaments:\f* within, in \f p \fr 44.14 \ft exterior are required to edifie others in diuers ſortes of vertues.\f* borders of gold
\v 15 clothed round about with varieties. \f q \fr 44.15 \ft By this meanes manie more are conuerted to chriſtianitie:\f* Virgins shal be brought to the king after her: her \f r \fr 44.15 \ft and one countrie inuiteth and draweth an other.\f* neighbours shal be brought to thee.
\v 16 They shal be brought in ioy and exultation: they shal be brought into the temple of the king.
\v 17 For \f s \fr 44.17 \ft As Apoſtles came in place of Patriarches and Prophetes: ſo ſtil Biſhops, and Prieſtes ſuccede in the Church, paſtors, and gouernours therof.\f* thy fathers there are borne ſonnes to thee: thou ſhalt make them princes ouer al the earth.
\v 18 They shal \f t \fr 44.18 \ft Theſe paſtores shal ſtil teach the true Chriſtian doctrin.\f* be mindeful of thy name in al generation and generation. Therfore shal \f v \fr 44.18 \ft and ſtil there shal be Chriſtian people that wil folow and profeſſe the ſame.\f* peoples confeſſe to thee for euer: and for euer and euer.
\c 45
\cd The Church in perſecution acknowledgeth Gods perpetual defence, 5. making her therby more glorious, 10. ſometimes granting reſt ( 11. God himſelf checking the perſecuters ) and euer protecting her. \f - \ft The Church proſpereth alſo in perſecution. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 45.1 \ft Belonging to the Church of Chriſt.\f* the end, to the ſonnes of Core, for \f b \fr 45.1 \ft As wel the cauſe, vvhy God ſuffereth his Church to be perſecuted, as his aſſured protection in difficulties, are hidden ſecretes to the world.\f* the ſecretes.
\v 2 OVr God is a refuge. \f c \fr 45.2 \ft Al refuge is not ſecure for one man is not able alwayes to defend an other: but God is a ſure and ſtrong refuge.\f* and ſtrength: an \f d \fr 45.2 \ft euer able and in conuenient time vvilling to helpe\f* helper in tribulations, which \f e \fr 45.2 \ft This whole vvorld is ful of tribulations, but the Church ſuffered the greateſt in the firſt perſecutions, & shal ſuffer as great in the time of Antichriſt. English Catholiques ſuffer moſt of al nations in this age, and can not be ſuppreſſed, but ſtil increaſe in number and fortitude.\f* haue found vs excedingly.
\v 3 Therfore wil we \f f \fr 45.3 \ft Therfore al Catholiques may aſſuredly know, that the whole Church can not faile.\f* not feare when \f g \fr 45.3 \ft though very manie, as now in England,\f* the earth shal be trubled: and \f h \fr 45.3 \ft and very eminent perſons, as ſome noblemen, and ſome Prieſtes haue reuolted, yet al vvil not.\f* mountaines tranſported into the hart of the ſea.
\v 4 Their waters haue ſounded, and were trubled: the mountaines were trubled in his ſtrength.
\v 5 The violence of the riuer \f i \fr 45.5 \ft Such bad examples make the good to recollect themſelues more diligently, and to reioyce in Gods grace, by which they ſtand faſt.\f* maketh the citie of God ioyful: the Higheſt hath ſanctified his tabernacle.
\v 6 God is in the middes therof, it shal not be moued: God wil helpe it \f k \fr 45.6 \ft before the heate of perſecution ſhal inuade al, for the elect the dayes of tribulation are shortned.\f* in the morning early.
\v 7 Nations are trubled, and \f l \fr 45.7 \ft Sometimes one nation or kingdome rebelleth againſt the Church, but can not deſtroy it.\f* kingdomes are inclined: he gaue \f m \fr 45.7 \ft by the ſpirite of Chriſt, Antichriſt, and al his members shal be deſtroyed.\f* his voice, the earth was moued
\v 8 The Lord of hoſtes is with vs: the God of Iacob is our defender.
\v 9 Come ye, and ſee the workes of our Lord, what wonders he hath put vpon the earth:
\v 10 \f n \fr 45.10 \ft The Church ſometimes hath great peace, and tranquilitie.\f* taking away warres euen vnto the end of the earth. He shal deſtroy bow, & breake weapons: and shields he shal burne with fire.
\v 11 \f o \fr 45.11 \ft God himſelfe reſtrayneth the wicked, ſuddainly abating their furie, or cutting of their forces.\f* Be quiet, and ſee that I am God: I shal be exalted among the gentiles, and I shal be exalted in the earth.
\v 12 The Lord of hoſtes is with vs: the God of Iacob is our defender.
\c 46
\cd Gentiles are called, and inuited to praiſe God for his magnificence: 6. for Chriſts Aſcenſion, and powre. \f - \ft Vocation of Gentiles. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, for \f a \fr 46.1 \ft For Chriſtians that leaue the ſinnes of their fathers, and reioyce in Chriſt crucified: See Annotation. Pſal. 41.\f* the ſonnes of Core.
\v 2 AL YE Nations \f b \fr 46.2 \ft True ioy of the hart ſheweth it ſelfe both in voice of exultation, and alſo in geſture of body, by clapping of handes, dancing ( as king Dauid did before the Arke. 2. Reg. 6.) likevviſe vvith inſtruments.\f* clappe handes: make iubilation to God in the voyce of exultation.
\v 3 Becauſe our Lord is high, \f c \fr 46.3 \ft To al the wicked,\f* terrible; a great king ouer \f d \fr 46.3 \ft not only of one or few kingdoms, but of al the earth.\f* al the earth.
\v 4 He hath made peoples ſubiect \f e \fr 46.4 \ft VVhen kinges, and countries become Chriſtians, they are made ſubiectes to the Church that vvas before, not heades and rulers therfore.\f* to vs: & gentiles vnder our feete.
\v 5 He hath choſen his inheritance in vs: the beautie of Iacob which he loued.
\v 6 \f f \fr 46.6 \ft Chriſt God & man, after his Paſſion, roſe from death and aſcended:\f* God is aſcended in \f g \fr 46.6 \ft not leauing his Church deſolate, but making her ioyful by an other comforter the Holie Ghoſt.\f* iubilation: and our Lord in the voice of trumpet.
\v 7 Sing ye to our \f h \fr 46.7 \ft The ſame Chriſt is our God, by his Diuinitie:\f* God, ſing ye: Sing ye to our \f i \fr 46.7 \ft and our king by his Humanitie.\f* king, ſing ye.
\v 8 Becauſe God is king of al the earth: ſing ye \f k \fr 46.8 \ft Doe your endeuour to vnderſtand vvhat you ſing, read, or heare in Gods word. At leaſt to know the principal Myſteries, and pointes of Chriſtian doctrin, euerie one according to their capacitie and ſtate or profeſſion.\f* wiſely.
\v 9 God shal reigne ouer the gentiles: God ſitteth vpon his holie ſeate.
\v 10 Princes of peoples are gathered together with \f l \fr 46.10 \ft The faithful of the old and nevv Teſtament are vnited in the ſeruice of one, and the ſame eternal God.\f* the God of Abraham: becauſe the ſtrong \f m \fr 46.10 \ft In reſpect of the Bleſſed Trinitie, holie Scripture here, and in manie places vſeth names of the plural number as \+it Eloim\+it* Goddes, not diuiding Gods ſubſtance, vvhich is one, but inſinuating diſtinction of Diuine Perſons. The Father, the Sonne, and the Holie Ghoſt. VVhich Myſterie is more expreſly mentioned in Baptiſme, and profeſſed by Chriſtian gentils, then it was by the people of the Ievves.\f* goddes of the earth, are excedingly aduanced.
\c 47
\cd God moſt, and euerie where laudable, is eſpecially praiſed in the Church of Chriſt ( preſigured by Sion, and there begunne ) 9. Al thinges being fulfilled in the Church, euen as they were prophecied, and promiſed, 12. the faithful are exhorted to conſider and congratulate the ſame. \f - \ft The Church founded and protected by God. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Pſalme \f a \fr 47.1 \ft Voices beginning the muſike inſtruments proſecuted;\f* of Canticle to the ſonnes of Core, the \f b \fr 47.1 \ft eſpecially for the ſecond day of the weke, the day after the ſabbath, which is our Sunday, called Dominica, our Lords day.\f* ſecond of the Sabbath.
\v 2 GREAT is our Lord, and to be prayſed excedingly in \f c \fr 47.2 \ft Ieruſalem, and mount Sion were moſt obliged to praiſe God, for greateſt benefites receiued, ſo the Catholique Church therby prefigured, and hauing receiued farre greatter, is moſt of al bonden to be gratful.\f* the citie of our God, in his holie mount.
\v 3 Mount Sion is founded with the exultation of \f d \fr 47.3 \ft This can not be affirmed of Sion, or Ieruſalem, but is only verified of the Catholique Chriſtian Church:\f* the whole earth, \f e \fr 47.3 \ft whoſe coaſtes do extend to the North, and to al quarters of the round earth.\f* the ſides of the North, the citie of the great king.
\v 4 God shal be knowen in \f f \fr 47.4 \ft The ſame one God, one Chriſt, one Faith, and one Religion in al particular Churches of the vvhole militant Church.\f* the houſes therof, \f g \fr 47.4 \ft And this Vniuerſalitie, and Vnitie shal be, after that Chriſt taking mans nature ſhal be aſcended, and ſhal ſend the Holie Ghoſt, to found & beginne this Church.\f* when he shal receiue it.
\v 5 For behold the kings of the earth \f h \fr 47.5 \ft For the aſſured certaintie of that is foreshewed, the Prophet ſpeaketh in the pretertence, as if it vvere already done in his time, which he then ſavv in ſpirite.\f* were gathered together: they aſſembled in one.
\v 6 They ſeing it ſo, were in admiration, were trubled, were moued:
\v 7 trembling tooke them. Their ſorowes \f i \fr 47.7 \ft Nothing more moueth the hart, & affecteth al the bodie and ſoule, then ſpiritual cogitations of faith and religion, and therfore it is compared to a woman traueling with child, who hath moſt careful and greuous paines.\f* as a woman traueling,
\v 8 In a vehement ſpirit \f k \fr 47.8 \ft In vvhich great confflict of mans ſpirite, God by his grace geueth force, to breake through the contrarie aſſaultes of our enimie, to remoue al impediments, and to ouercome the difficulties.\f* thou shalt breake the shippes of Tharſis.
\v 9 \f l \fr 47.9 \ft This conſideration that al is now done, that was of old prophecied, is a meruelous confirmation and conſolation to Chriſtians.\f* As we haue heard, ſo haue we ſeene in the citie of the Lord of hoſtes, in the citie of our God: God hath founded it for euer.
\v 10 We haue receiued thy mercie, ô God, \f m \fr 47.10 \ft Grace and mercie is only granted to thoſe that are vvithin, or come vnto the Catholique Church.\f* in the middes of thy temple.
\v 11 According to thy name ô God, ſo alſo is thy prayſe vnto the endes of the earth: thy right hand is ful of \f n \fr 47.11 \ft As God is praiſed for his mercie, ſo alſo for his iuſtice; which do neuer preiudice the one the other.\f* iuſtice.
\v 12 Let mount Sion be glad, and the daughters of Iuda reioyce, becauſe of thy iudgementes ô Lord
\v 13 Compaſſe Sion, and embrace ye her: \f o \fr 47.13 \ft Conſider the fortreſſes of the Church, which are the holie Fathers, and Doctors, that watch and defend her vvalles.\f* tel ye in her towers.
\v 14 Set your hartes \f p \fr 47.14 \ft So reſt you aſſured for al matters of faith in this pillar of truth.\f* on her ſtrength: and \f q \fr 47.14 \ft obſerue and marke diligently how manie particular Churches were ſpedely founded in the world,\f* diſtribute ye her houſes, that you \f r \fr 47.14 \ft and declare this to other generations, that they may alſo hold faſt the ſame faith, or returne vnto it, if they be relapſed; or at laſt embrace it, if ſowner they haue not.\f* may declare it in an other generation.
\v 15 Becauſe \f s \fr 47.15 \ft Chriſt God incarnate that vvorketh al this, is our very God and Sauiour, not for a few yeares, an hundred, ſix hundred, or a thouſand, but for euer and euer.\f* this is God, our God for euer, and for euer and euer: he \f t \fr 47.15 \ft he shal rule as a king, and conſequently haue a kingdom his militant Church euermore, to the very end of this vvorld. As he ſhal likevviſe haue his triumphant Church in eternitie.\f* shal rule vs euermore.
\c 48
\cd The royal prophet inuiting al ſtates and ſortes of men, to heare him attentiuely, 6 sheweth that al ought to feare eternal damnation, that liue wickedly; 9. vainly and foolishly ſeeking (13. euen like brute beaſtes) carnal pleaſures, which they can not long enioy, nor long eſcape hel. 16. confidently animating him ſelfe, and al good men, that truſt not in this world. \f - \ft Exhortation to fly from ſin for fear of hell. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, \f a \fr 48.1 \ft In this and diuers other titles, both before and yet enſuing, is ſaid, To the ſonnes, or, for the ſonnes of Core, a Pſalme, or Canticle, or vnderſtanding & the like ; but in no place, a Pſalme, Canticle &c. of the ſonnes of Core, vvhich no way proueth that they vvere the authores of ſuch Pſalmes, but rather the contrarie.\f* to the ſonnes of Core a Pſalme.
\v 2 HEARE theſe thinges \f b \fr 48.2 \ft Al ye nations and ſortes of people,\f* al ye Gentiles: receiue with your eares al ye, that \f c \fr 48.2 \ft that dwel vpon the earth, lerne this leſſon vvhich I wil teach you.\f* inhabite the earth.
\v 3 Al ye earthly perſons, and children of men: together in one the rich and the poore.
\v 4 My mouth shal ſpeake wiſedom, and the meditation of my hart prudence.
\v 5 I wil \f d \fr 48.5 \ft Holie Dauid harkened to God inſpiring him,\f* incline mine eare vnto a parable: I wil \f e \fr 48.5 \ft and declared to others that vvhich he receiued from God,\f* open my propoſition \f f \fr 48.5 \ft not only by his penne or tongue, but alſo for better inſtilling it into their mindes he ſounded it vpon the inſtrument called the Pſalter, vvhich had tenne ſtringes, ſignifying the obſeruation of the tenne commandments.\f* on a Pſalter.
\v 6 \f g \fr 48.6 \ft What eſpecial thing is there in this life, vvhy or for vvhich I or anie haue cauſe to feare the dreadful day of iudgement?\f* Why shal I feare in the euil day? \f h \fr 48.6 \ft Marry this we muſt feare, iniquitie, by which any ſupplanteth, defraudeth, oppreſſeth, or anie vvay wrongeth others, for that vvil inuolue the offender in the ſentence of eternal damnation.\f* the iniquitie of my heele shal compaſſe me.
\v 7 They \f i \fr 48.7 \ft Such be they that truſt in their preſent powre, riches, or other wordlie thing.\f* that truſt in their ſtrength: and glorie in the multitude of their riches.
\v 8 A \f k \fr 48.8 \ft A mans owne brother can not helpe a ſinner in that day,\f* brother doth not redeme, \f l \fr 48.8 \ft much leſſe anie other man; ſo the Hebrew phraiſe by zeugma, vnderſtandeth an other negatiue particle.\f* man shal redeme: he shal not geue vnto God his reconciliation.
\v 9 And the price of the redemption of his owne ſoule: and he shal \f m \fr 48.9 \ft ſtil ſuffer paine,\f* labour for euer,
\v 10 and \f n \fr 48.10 \ft and not dye, but liue in eternal torments.\f* shal liue yet vnto the end.
\v 11 He shal not ſee death, when he shal ſee \f o \fr 48.11 \ft Al both wiſe and foolish do dye temporally: but the wiſe liuing in eternal ioy, the foolish liue in eternal paine,\f* the wiſe dying \f p \fr 48.11 \ft thoſe that beleue not anie other life after this,\f* the vnwiſe, and \f q \fr 48.11 \ft and thoſe that beleuing an other life, yet liue badly in this, shal perish in eternal damnation.\f* the foole shal perish together. And they shal leaue their riches to ſtrangers:
\v 12 and their \f r \fr 48.12 \ft They shal neuer returne from their ſepulchers,\f* ſepulchers their \f s \fr 48.12 \ft to enioy againe their houſes and earthlie poſſeſſions.\f* houſes for euer. Their Tabernacles in generation and generation: they haue renowmed their \f t \fr 48.12 \ft which vainely they labour to eſtablish in their poſteritie.\f* names in their landes.
\v 13 And \f v \fr 48.13 \ft A moſt pithie and brief conſideration, for man to thinke, how abſurdly, he being endewed with reaſon, vnderſtanding, & free wil, like vnto Angels, and capable of eternal glorie, ſetteth his vvhole ſtudie, and care vpon corporal and temporal thinges, ſo making himſelfe like vnto brute beaſtes.\f* man, when he was in honour, did not vnderſtand: he was compared to beaſts without vnderſtanding, and became like to them.
\v 14 This their way is \f w \fr 48.14 \ft This care of wordlie thinges is the ſtumbling block, and cauſe of eternal ruine:\f* a ſcandal to them: and \f x \fr 48.14 \ft yet they ſhal be obſtinate, and praiſe their owne deſires, ſtil perſiſting therin.\f* afterward in their mouth they shal take pleaſure.
\v 15 As \f y \fr 48.15 \ft Amongſt other creatures a sheepe can leaſt helpe her ſelfe in miſerie: euen ſo the damned in hel are altogether vnable to deliuer themſelues from thence, or to get any relief,\f* sheepe they are put in hel: death shal feede vpon them. And the iuſt shal rule ouer them \f z \fr 48.15 \ft in the general reſurrection they shal be moſt of al in miſerie, as euer dying and neuer dead: the iuſt vvhom they vvronged, shal be their iudges, al freindes shal faile them, after they haue paſſed their glorie, and pleaſure in this vvorld.\f* in the morning: and their aide shal waxe old in hel from their glorie.
\v 16 Neuertheleſſe \f a \fr 48.16 \ft The confidence of the iuſt.\f* God wil redeme my ſoule out of the hand of hel, when he shal take me.
\v 17 Feare not when a man shal be made rich: and when the glory of his houſe shal be multiplied,
\v 18 Becauſe when he shal dye, he shal not take \f b \fr 48.18 \ft He shal leaue al worldlie thinges and take nothing with him.\f* al thinges: neyther shal his glorie goe downe with him.
\v 19 Becauſe his ſoule in his life shal be \f c \fr 48.19 \ft temporally:\f* bleſſed: he wil confeſſe to thee \f d \fr 48.19 \ft ſo long as he enioyeth wordlie profites he wil ſeme gratful to God;\f* when thou shalt do him good.
\v 20 He shal enter in, euen to the progenies of his fathers: and he shal \f e \fr 48.20 \ft but they shal not ſee the true light of heauen.\f* not ſee light for euer.
\v 21 \f f \fr 48.21 \ft Remember and conſider ô worldlie man, that God made thee an excellent creature: which thou neglecting makeſt thyſelf like to a beaſt. As, \+it v. 13\+it*.\f* Man, when he was in honour, did not vnderſtand: he was compared to beaſts without vnderſtanding, and became like to them.
\c 49
\cd Chriſt in his firſt coming calleth al Nations. 3. in his ſecond wil iudge the world. 7. In the meane time God exhorteth al men to ſerue him in puritie of vertue, which he much preferreth before external ſacrifice of the old law. 17. reprehending ſuch as profeſſe or teach the right way, and liue wickedly. \f - \ft General Judgement. The 9. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Pſalme \f a \fr 49.1 \ft To be ſongue or tuned by Aſaph a maiſter of muſike.\f* to Aſaph. THE \f b \fr 49.1 \ft God almightie, who is greater then are al falſly ſuppoſed goddes, or holie perſons, that participating of his goodnes are called goddes (as Kinges, Prieſtes, Iudges) coming into this world in mans nature, calleth al men to ſaluation.\f* God of goddes our Lord hath ſpoken: and he hath called the earth, from the ryſing of the ſunne euen to the going downe.
\v 2 Out \f c \fr 49.2 \ft The Church of Christ began in Sion.\f* of Syon the beauty of his comelines.
\v 3 God wil come \f d \fr 49.3 \ft Chriſt that came in humilitie, and more obſcurely to ſuffer, and to redeme vs, vvil come in maieſtie, and manifeſtly to iudge.\f* manifeſtly: our God and he wil not kepe ſilence. \f e \fr 49.3 \ft Immediately before the general iudgement, fire shal burne al tranſitorie thinges.\f* Fire shal burne forth in his ſight: and round about him a mighty tempeſt.
\v 4 He shal \f f \fr 49.4 \ft Geue ſignes in the firmament,\f* cal the heauen from aboue: and \f g \fr 49.4 \ft and in earth.\f* the earth to diſcerne his people.
\v 5 Gather ye together his ſaincts vnto him: which ordaine his teſtament \f h \fr 49.5 \ft Which know that to keepe Gods commandments in folowing vertues, is aboue the oblation of external ſacrifice.\f* aboue ſacrifices.
\v 6 And the heauens shal shew forth his iuſtice: becauſe God is Iudge.
\v 7 \f i \fr 49.7 \ft God inſtructeth his people.\f* Heare ô my people, and I wil ſpeake: Iſrael, and I wil teſtifie to thee: God thy God am I.
\v 8 I wil not rebuke thee in thy ſacrifices: and thy holocauſtes \f k \fr 49.8 \ft Sacrifices are gratful to God.\f* are in my ſight alwaies.
\v 9 I wil \f l \fr 49.9 \ft but in regard that God needeth not theſe earthly thinges, he rather requireth a gratful mind. For otherwiſe man in dede can geue nothing to God: ſeing al that is in the whole world is Gods owne in proprietie.\f* not take calues out of thy houſe: nor buckegoats out of thy flockes.
\v 10 Becauſe al the wilde beaſts of the woods be myne, the cattle in the mountaines and oxen.
\v 11 I haue knowne al the foules of the ayer: and the beauty of the fielde is with me.
\v 12 If I shal be hungrie, I wil not tel thee: for the round earth is myne, and the fulnes therof.
\v 13 Wil I eate the flesh of oxen? or wil I drinke the blood of buckegoats?
\v 14 \f m \fr 49.14 \ft Spiritual ſacrifice of prayſe.\f* Immolate to God the ſacrifice of praiſe, and \f n \fr 49.14 \ft & due payment of voluntarie vowes made in honour of God,\f* pay thy vowes to the Higheſt.
\v 15 And \f o \fr 49.15 \ft and praying to him for helpe in tribulation are most gratful.\f* inuocate me in the day of tribulation: I wil deliuer thee, and thou shalt glorifie me.
\v 16 But to the ſinner God hath ſayde: \f p \fr 49.16 \ft He that wil teach others, muſt eſpecially flee from ſinne, & ſerue God ſincerly.\f* Why doeſt thou declare my iuſtices, and takeſt my teſtament by thy mouth?
\v 17 But thou haſt hated diſcipline: & caſt my words behind thee.
\v 18 If thou didſt ſee a theeſe, thou didſt rune with him: and with adulterers thou didſt put thy portion.
\v 19 Thy mouth hath abounded with malice: and thy tongue fourged guiles.
\v 20 Sitting thou ſpakeſt againſt thy brother, and againſt thy mothers ſonne thou didſt put a ſcandal:
\v 21 theſe things haſt thou done, and I haue held my peace. Thou haſt thought vniuſtly that I wil be like thee: I wil reproue thee, and ſet it againſt thy face.
\v 22 Vnderſtand theſe things you that forget God: leſt ſometime he take you violently and there be none to deliuer you.
\v 23 The ſacrifice of prayſe \f q \fr 49.23 \ft God is honored by mans gratitude, and other good workes.\f* shal glorifie me: and there is the way, by which I wil shew him the ſaluation of God.
\c 50
\cd King Dauid in great ſorow for his ſinnes of adulterie and murder, most ſeriouſly prayeth God of his manifold mercies to remitte and purge al his offences, and paines due for them. 12. to reſtore vnto him the grace of the Holie Ghost, lost by his ſinnes; 15. that he may teach others (as in deede his ſingular example may teach the whole world true penance) 19. contrition of hart, worthely to offer ſacrifice, for the whole Church. \f - \ft The fourth penitential Pſalm. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 50.1 \ft Pertayning not only to Dauid, but alſo to al penitentes, eſpecially of the new teſtament\f* the end, a Pſalme of Dauid, \x - \xt 2. Reg. 12.\x*
\v 2 when Nathan the Prophet came to him, after that he had ſinned with Bethſabee. (2. Reg. 12.)
\v 3 HAVE mercie on me ô God, \f b \fr 50.3 \ft My ſinnes being very great, nede thy great mercie.\f* according to thy great mercie. And according \f c \fr 50.3 \ft Yea manie ſortes of thy mercies: not only remiſſion of the crimes, but alſo mitigation of the paines due for the ſame. Thy merciful grace to be truly ſorie, to make ſome part of ſatisfaction, to beware hereafter not to fal againe, to geue better example of penance, and of vertuous life, and to perſeuer to the end.\f* to the multitude of thy commiſerations, take away myne iniquitie.
\v 4 Wash me \f d \fr 50.4 \ft O God thou haſt forgeuen me, and taken away my ſinnes, as thy prophet hath told me \+it (2. Reg. 12. v. 13.)\+it* but my ſoule ſo fouly polluted, nedeth yet more waſhing.\f* more amply from mine iniquitie: & \f e \fr 50.4 \ft cleanſe alſo the dregges that remaine, and al habites and inclinations to ſinne. So our Sauiour afterwards taught. \+it (Ioan. 13. v. 10.)\+it* He that is waſhed nedeth not ſauing to wash his feete (il affections and reliques of former ſinnes) but is cleane wholy.\f* cleanſe me from my ſinne.
\v 5 \f f \fr 50.5 \ft VVhiles I did not know; not conſider nor acknowledge my ſinnes, I could not be forgeuen, but now I know and acknowledge them:\f* Becauſe I do know myne iniquitie: and my ſinne is \f g \fr 50.5 \ft and I ceaſe not to conſider of them with ſorow.\f* before me alwaies.
\v 6 To thee \f h \fr 50.6 \ft Principally (for ſo this particle [only] here ſignifieth) the enormities of my ſinnes conſiſt, in that I haue offended thy Diuine Goodnes and Maieſtie, the King of the worldes, immortal, inuiſible, onlie God, to whom is due al honour and glorie for euer and euer. \+it 1. Tim. 1. v. 17\+it*.\f* onely haue I ſinned, and haue done euil before thee: that thou mayſt \f i \fr 50.6 \ft Thou which haſt promiſed forgeuenes to al ſinners that truly conuert, ſhal herein be iuſtified by receiuing me againe to grace:\f* be iuſtified in thy words, and mayſt \f k \fr 50.6 \ft and ouerthrow thy calumniators, that iudge wickedly of thy proceedings, as if either thy iuſtice or mercie were peruerted.\f* ouercome when thou art iudged.
\v 7 For behold I \f l \fr 50.7 \ft I and al are borne in original ſinne, the reliques wherof, concupiſcence and weakenes incline vs to other ſinnes, which we haue added. In regard of which our infirmitie, thy mercie is readie to recal vs, and help vs.\f* was conceiued in iniquities: & my mother conceiued me in ſinnes.
\v 8 For behold thou \f m \fr 50.8 \ft Beſides thou haſt alſo geuen me knowledge of true faith, and right doctrine, which thou euer loueſt, and art accuſtomed to reduce, and direct ſuch into the true way of penance.\f* haſt loued truth: \f n \fr 50.8 \ft yea thou haſt moreouer ſhewed to me thinges vncertaine, or vnknowen to manie others, geuen me the gift and ſpirit of prophecie, to know hidde myſteries, and to euerie one God geueth ſome particular benefites, which he loueth in him, and is ready of his part to confirme and maintaine the ſame, that they be not loſt.\f* the vncertaine, and hidden thinges of thy wiſdome thou haſt made manifeſt to me.
\v 9 Thou shalt ſprinkle me with \f o \fr 50.9 \ft Moſt merciful Lord thou wilt (as I ſee in the ſpirit of prophecie) ſprinkle me, and al men with thy bloud, from the Croſſe, where they shal geue thee vinegre about hyſſoppe to drinke. (Ioan. 19.)\f* hyſſope, and I shal be clenſed: thou shalt wash me, and I shal be made \f p \fr 50.9 \ft by which washing I shal be cleane from ſinne, and become in time pure, yea whiter then ſnow. A figure of this hyſſope was obſerued in Moyſes Law. Num. 19. ſignifying the liuelie heat of Chriſts infinite charitie.\f* whiter then ſnow.
\v 10 To \f q \fr 50.10 \ft When myn affections shal be cleane purged, I shal take ſingular great delight to heare of thee,\f* my hearing thou shalt geue ioy and gladnes, and \f r \fr 50.10 \ft and al my powres of mind and bodie, which are now afflicted, shal reioyce.\f* the bones humbled shal reioyce.
\v 11 \f s \fr 50.11 \ft Leaue of thy cogitation of punishing, to which purpoſe firſt take away myn iniquities, for otherwiſe if they remaine, Gods iuſtice can not but punish them.\f* Turne away thy face from my ſinnes: and wipe away al mine iniquities.
\v 12 \f t \fr 50.12 \ft Create in me new grace, wherby my hart shal be pure. So S. Paul calleth a iuſt ſoul a new creature. \+it Galat. 6. v. 15\+it*.\f* Create a cleane hart in me ô God: and renew a right ſpirit in my \f v \fr 50.12 \ft In my inward thoughtes.\f* bowels.
\v 13 Caſt me not away from thy face: and thy Holie ſpirit \f w \fr 50.13 \ft ſuffer me not ſo to fal againe, that thy grace depart from me.\f* take not from me.
\v 14 Render vnto me \f x \fr 50.14 \ft which I had before my fal, of Chriſt promiſed of my ſeede, and alter not the ſame for my ſinnes. Dauid alſo and other penitents pray here, that God wil reſtore vnto them the ioy, which they had in the ſtate of grace, of eternal ſaluation promiſed;\f* the ioy of thy ſaluation? and \f y \fr 50.14 \ft confirme & conſerue in me hereafter, a ſtrong, conſtant, and willing ſpirite to perſeuere.\f* confirme me with the principal ſpirit,
\v 15 I \f z \fr 50.15 \ft No way can a penitent better shew him ſelf gratful to God, for remiſſion of his ſinnes, then by inſtructing, exhorting, and perſwading other ſinners to repentance, to leaue their former il wayes, and turne to God.\f* wil teach the vniuſt thy waies: and the impious shal be conuerted to thee.
\v 16 Deliuer me \f a \fr 50.16 \ft From the guilt and puniſhment of murder, cauſing Vrias and others with him to be ſlaine. Other penitents pray to be deliuered from what ſinnes ſoeuer they haue committed, by ſheding bloud, or other wrongs and iniuries; promiſing to praiſe Gods iuſtice, in offering and geuing grace, according to his promiſe to ſinners, that they may repent.\f* from bloudes ô God, the God of my ſaluation: and my tongue shal exult [for] thy iuſtice.
\v 17 Lord, thou \f b \fr 50.17 \ft Thou ô God firſt ſtirring me vp, opening my lippes, which of my ſelfe I can not do, then my tongue and mouth wil praiſe thee.\f* wilt open my lippes: & my mouth shal shew forth thy prayſe.
\v 18 Becauſe if thou \f c \fr 50.18 \ft If thou wouldeſt eſpecially legal ſacrifice, I would eaſily haue offered great ſtore:\f* wouldeſt haue had ſacrifice, I had verily giuen it: with holocauſtes thou wilt \f d \fr 50.18 \ft but the beſt of that kind is not ſufficient:\f* not be delighted.
\v 19 A ſacrifice to God is \f e \fr 50.19 \ft true contrition of hart pleaſeth thee farre better.\f* an afflicted ſpirit: a contrite, and humbled hart, ô God thou wilt not deſpiſe.
\v 20 Deale fauorably ô Lord in thy good wil \f f \fr 50.20 \ft After a penitent hath remiſſion of his owne ſinnes, he muſt pray for the whole Church.\f* with Sion: that the walles of Ieruſalem may be built vp.
\v 21 \f g \fr 50.21 \ft The Church proſpering, her faithful children shal offer\f* Then shalt thou accept ſacrifice of \f h \fr 50.21 \ft the ſacrifice of iuſtice, rendering to euerie one that is due;\f* iuſtice, \f i \fr 50.21 \ft alſo free offeringes without obligation,\f* oblations, & \f k \fr 50.21 \ft yea holocauſtes, which is the chiefeſt,\f* holocauſtes: \f l \fr 50.21 \ft calues, and like hoſtes vpon the altar, according to the ſtate of the old law: but in the law of Chriſt, the moſt B. Sacrifice by him inſtituted.\f* then shal they lay calues vpon thyne altar.
\c 51
\cd Holie Dauid inueigheth against wicked Doeg a traitor. 7. prophecieth his ruine. 10. and his owne exaltation. \f - \ft David's invective againſt Doeg. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, vnderſtanding to Dauid,
\v 2 when Doeg \f a \fr 51.2 \ft Of the race of Eſau, half a Iew, but either an Infidel, or fautor of Infidels, a ſpie for Saul, a perſecutor of Dauid, & a murderer of Innocents. 1. Reg. 22. v. 9. 18.\f* the Idumeite came and told Saul: Dauid is come into the houſe of \f b \fr 51.2 \ft High prieſt, ſlaine with 84. more Prieſtes and others, becauſe they were ſuppoſed to fauour Dauid. ibidem.\f* Achimelech. (1. Reg. 22.) \f 1 \fr 51.2 \ft (1. Reg. 22.)\f*
\v 3 WHY doeſt thou \f c \fr 51.3 \ft Thou perſecutor Doeg, why art thou ſo malicious, to abuſe thy credite with king Saul, to the murdering of innocents?\f* glorie in malice, which art mightie in iniquitie?
\v 4 Al the day hath thy tongue thought iniuſtice: as a sharp raſor thou \f d \fr 51.4 \ft playing the part of a ſpie, in betraying to Saul, that I was with Achimelech?\f* haſt done guile.
\v 5 Thou haſt loued malice more then benignitie: \f e \fr 51.5 \ft Though he told a truth, yet it was iniquitie to betray innocents.\f* iniquitie rather then to ſpeake equitie.
\v 6 Thou haſt loued al wordes of precipitation, a deceitful tongue.
\v 7 Therfore Wil God deſtroy thee for euer, he wil \f f \fr 51.7 \ft Thou shalt vtterly be deſtroyed.\f* plucke thee out, & remoue thee out of thy tabernacle: & \f g \fr 51.7 \ft & al thy race.\f* thy roote out of the land of the liuing.
\v 8 The iuſt shal ſee, and feare, and shal laugh at him, and they shal ſay:
\v 9 Behold the man, that hath not put God for his helper. But hath hoped in the multitude of his riches, and hath \f h \fr 51.9 \ft for a short time in this world.\f* preualed in his vanitie.
\v 10 But I as \f i \fr 51.10 \ft Dauid prophecieth his owne exaltation, and conſeruation of his ſeede in the kingdom of Iſrael.\f* a fruitful oliue tree in the houſe of God, haue hoped in the mercie of God for euer: and for euer and euer.
\v 11 I wil \f k \fr 51.11 \ft sing praiſe, and thankes to thee.\f* confeſſe to thee for euer, becauſe thou haſt done it: and I wil expect thy \f l \fr 51.11 \ft thy Goodnes, which agreeth to thy name.\f* name, becauſe it is good in the ſight of thy ſaints:
\c 52
\cd As in the thirtenth Pſalme, Chriſts Incarnation is prophecied, after that ſinne abunded in the world: ſo here is foreshewed that after general wickednes, 5. Christ wil come to iudge the bad, 7. and deliuer the good. \f - \ft The general Iudgement. The 9. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, for \f a \fr 52.1 \ft Weakenes, or mourning,\f* Maeleth; \f b \fr 52.1 \ft S. Auguſtin expoundeth this Pſalme as an inſtruction to thoſe that ſuffer perſecution and iniuries, eſpecially nere the end of the world.\f* vnderſtandings of Dauid. THE foole hath ſaid in his hart: There is no God.
\v 2 They are corrupte, and become abominable in iniquities: there is not that doth good.
\v 3 God hath looked forth from heauen, vpon the children of men: to ſee if there be that vnderſtandeth, or ſeeketh after God.
\v 4 Al haue declined, they are become vnprofitable together: there is not that doth good, no there is not one.
\v 5 Shal they not al know that worke iniquitie, that deuoure my people as food of bread?
\v 6 God they haue not inuocated: there haue they trembled for feare, where no feare was. Becauſe God hath \f c \fr 52.6 \ft God wil ouerthrow al the counſels, and forces\f* diſſipated the bones of them \f d \fr 52.6 \ft of worldlie politiques.\f* that pleaſe men: they are confounded, becauſe God hath deſpiſed them.
\v 7 \f e \fr 52.7 \ft The true Church afflicted deſireth Chriſts coming to deliuer the oppreſſed.\f* Who wil geue out of Sion the ſaluation of Iſrael? when God shal conuert the captiuitie of his people: Iacob shal reioyce, and Iſrael shal be glad.
\c 53
\cd Dauid in diſtreſſe crieth to God for helpe, 6. confidently truſting therin, 8. and promiſing ſacrifice of thankeſgeuing. \f - \ft A prayer in diſtreſs. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 53.1 \ft Though hiſtorically this Pſalme\f* the end, \f b \fr 53.1 \ft was ſong by Dauid the author therof, shewing how he prayd in danger, and rendered thankes for his deliuerie,\f* in ſongs vnderſtanding for Dauid
\v 2 when the Zipheites were come, and ſaid to Saul: \f c \fr 53.2 \ft when vpon notice geuen that he abode in the mountaines, Saul ſtraictly beſeged him, with a great armie, but the Philiſtims inuading the countrie, Saul was forced to leaue Dauid, and to turne his forces againſt them, \+it 1. Reg. 23\+it*. yet it perteyneth alſo literally to al iuſt men in diſtreſſe, eſpecially to the Church of Chriſt, praying in like dangers, and God by his like prouidence, deliuering his ſeruantes in extremities.\f* Is not Dauid hid with vs? (1. Reg. 1. 23. & 26.) \f 1 \fr 53.2 \ft \+it 1. Reg. c. 23. & 26.\+it*\f*
\v 3 O GOD ſaue me \f d \fr 53.3 \ft For the glorie of thy name\f* in thy name: and in thy ſtrength \f e \fr 53.3 \ft for the iuſtnes of my cauſe defend me.\f* iudge me.
\v 4 O God heare my prayer with thine eares: receiue the words of my mouth.
\v 5 Becauſe \f f \fr 53.5 \ft Barbarous highland men haue betraide the place of myne abode to the perſecuters.\f* ſtrangers haue riſen vp againſt me, & the ſtrong haue ſought my ſoule: and they haue not ſet God before their eies.
\v 6 For \f g \fr 53.6 \ft But I feare them not, becauſe I am in Gods protection.\f* behold God helpeth me: and our Lord is the receiuer of my ſoule.
\v 7 \f h \fr 53.7 \ft A iuſt prayer, that God wil turne intended miſchief, vpon the deuiſers heades,\f* Turne away the euils to mine enimies: and in \f i \fr 53.7 \ft according to his promiſe, that he wil defend the innocent.\f* thy truth deſtroy them.
\v 8 \f k \fr 53.8 \ft Offering voluntarie ſacrifice, more then is commanded:\f* I wil voluntarily ſacrifice to thee, and \f l \fr 53.8 \ft and praiſe thee ô God,\f* wil confeſſe to thy name ô Lord, becauſe it is good:
\v 9 \f m \fr 53.9 \ft as I am bond\f* Becauſe thou haſt deliuered me out of al tribulation: and \f n \fr 53.9 \ft I reioyce in thy iuſt iudgements against the wicked.\f* mine eie hath looked downe vpon mine enimies.
\c 54
\cd The prophet (as wel in his owne, as other iuſt mens perſon) deſcribeth great calamities ſuffered, 10. praieth against the wicked, 13. Lamenting eſpecially that thoſe which profeſſe frendshipe, are aduerſaries. 17. and declareth Gods prouidence in protecting the good, and deſtroying the bad. \f - \ft God's providence towards the good and bad. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 54.1 \ft A ſong as wel for king Dauid himſelfe, as others of al times;\f* the end, \f b \fr 54.1 \ft to ſing,\f* in ſonges, \f c \fr 54.1 \ft and conſider Gods prouidence, in ſuffering one man to afflict an other in this life.\f* vnderſtanding to Dauid.
\v 2 HEARE my prayer ô God, deſpiſe not my petition,
\v 3 Attend to me; and heare me.
\v 4 I am made ſorowful in my \f d \fr 54.4 \ft This life is a warefare, and a continual combate.\f* exerciſe: and am trubled at the voice of the enimie: and at the tribulation of the ſinner. Becauſe they haue \f e \fr 54.4 \ft calumniated me,\f* wreſted iniquities vpon me: & \f f \fr 54.4 \ft and perſecuted me in great furie.\f* in anger they were trubleſome to me.
\v 5 My hart is trubled in me: and \f g \fr 54.5 \ft ſo inwardly afflicted, as if death were at hand.\f* the feare of death is falne vpon me.
\v 6 Feare and trembling are come vpon me: and \f h \fr 54.6 \ft I haue ſcarſe ſenſe, or diſcourſe of reaſon, being almoſt ouerwhelmed with trubles.\f* darkenes hath couered me.
\v 7 And I ſaid: \f i \fr 54.7 \ft would God I could flie, that in the ſimplicitie of a doue, I might ſpedely part away from theſe afflictions.\f* Who wil geue me wings as of a doue, and I wil fly: and reſt?
\v 8 \f k \fr 54.8 \ft I haue fled ſo farre as I could from trubles:\f* Loe I haue gone far flying away: and I abode in the wildernes.
\v 9 I \f l \fr 54.9 \ft for the reſt I remitted to Gods wil and good pleaſure,\f* expected him, that \f m \fr 54.9 \ft and he ſuffered me not to be ouerthrowne,\f* ſaued me from \f n \fr 54.9 \ft though I am weake,\f* puſillanimitie of ſpirit, and \f o \fr 54.9 \ft and the tentations are great.\f* tempeſt.
\v 10 \f p \fr 54.10 \ft O God abate the pride of arrogant perſecuters,\f* Precipitate ô Lord, and \f q \fr 54.10 \ft ſuffer them not to agree amongſt them ſelues.\f* diuide their tongues: becauſe I haue ſene \f r \fr 54.10 \ft They are ful of al iniquitie,\f* iniquitie, and \f s \fr 54.10 \ft they haue alſo contentions among themſelues, turne the ſame to our good.\f* contradiction in the citie.
\v 11 Day and night shal iniquitie compaſſe it vpon the walles therof; and \f t \fr 54.11 \ft VVith their continual great iniquitie, they haue their trubles,\f* labour in the middeſt therof, and \f v \fr 54.11 \ft but leaue not their iniuſtice.\f* iniuſtice.
\v 12 And there hath not ceaſed out of the ſtreetes therof \f w \fr 54.12 \ft They are stil vſurers, and deceiptful oppreſſors of the poore.\f* vſurie, and guile.
\v 13 For \f x \fr 54.13 \ft It is a greater griefe to ſuffer iniuries of thoſe that ſeme to be freindes.\f* if myne enimie had ſpoken euil to me, I would verely haue borne it. And if he that hated me had ſpoken great thinges vpon me: I would perhaps haue hid myſelfe from him.
\v 14 But \f y \fr 54.14 \ft A man that was, or ſemed of the ſame mind, faith, and religion,\f* thou a man of the ſame minde: my \f z \fr 54.14 \ft whom I ſo truſted, that I would haue gone, whitherſoeuer he should haue ledde me.\f* guide, and my familiar.
\v 15 Which diddeſt \f a \fr 54.15 \ft Thou that didſt participate the ſame holie ſacraments with me,\f* take ſwete meats together with me, in the \f b \fr 54.15 \ft in the Catholique Church.\f* houſe of God we walked with conſent.
\v 16 Let \f c \fr 54.16 \ft As Core & his complices: ſpoken of iuſt zele, not of deſire to reuenge: verified in thoſe that ſinne wittingly and knowing, for they deſcend, as it were, aliue into hel.\f* death come vpon them: and let them goe downe quicke into hel. Becauſe there is wickednes in their habitations, in \f d \fr 54.16 \ft The whole crew of the wicked conſpire in iniquitie.\f* the middes of them.
\v 17 But I haue cried to God, and our Lord wil ſaue me.
\v 18 In \f e \fr 54.18 \ft The prophet alludeth to three more ſpecially appointed houres of Diuine ſeruice, the daylie ſacrifice at morning, and euening, and other ſacrifices commonly about midday. VVhich alſo are the three principal times of Diuine Seruice in the Church of Chriſt. Mattins, Euenſong, and the Sacrifice of Maſſe. VVhich Eutymius and other Grecians cal Lyturgiam. S. Clement alſo \+it (li 7. c. 25. Apoſt. Inſtit\+it*) teſtifieth that the Apoſtles ordained three ſet houres of common prayer euerie day.\f* the euening, and morning, & at midday, I wil \f 1 \fr 54.18 \ft \+it * or pray\+it*\f* ſpeake, and declare, and he wil heare my voice.
\v 19 He wil redeme my ſoule in peace from them, that approch to me: becauſe among manie \f f \fr 54.19 \ft Manie enimies combined together approched vnto me, to ouerthrow me.\f* they were with me.
\v 20 God wil heare, and he \f g \fr 54.20 \ft Eternal God.\f* which is before the worldes wil humble them.
\v 21 For there is \f h \fr 54.21 \ft They wil neuer repent of their wickednes.\f* no change with them, & they feared not God: he hath ſtreached forth his hand in repaying.
\v 22 They haue contaminated his teſtament, they are \f i \fr 54.22 \ft They harden their hartes againſt his threatned wrath:\f* diuided by the wrath of his countenance; and \f k \fr 54.22 \ft but Gods prouidence illuminateth others to know and teach the truth, whȇ it is impugned, or contemned.\f* his hart hath approched. \f l \fr 54.22 \ft Gods wordes, which in them ſelues are meke and ſwete,\f* His wordes are made ſofter then oile: and \f m \fr 54.22 \ft are hard to the incredulous, & as dartes that wound them. Chriſt ſaid (Ioan. 6.) Vnles one eate my flesh, & drinke my bloud, he shal not haue life in him, which the Capharnaites not vnderſtanding ſaid one to an other: This is a hard ſpeach, who can abide it? which S. Auguſtin here ſaith was the firſt hereſie againſt our Sauiours preaching. It was not hard to S. Peter, who in the name of the reſt, anſwered, that Chriſt had the wordes of eternal life. He yet vnderſtood not the ſecrete of our Lords ſpeach, but he piouſly beleued that the wordes vvere good, vvhich he vnderſtood not.\f* the ſame are dartes.
\v 23 \f n \fr 54.23 \ft Therfore in al doubtes of doctrin, in al diſtreſſes of perſecution, and other difficulties vvhich ſurpaſſe thy vveakenes, caſt thy care vpon our Lord, and he wil nourish thee.\f* Caſt thy care vpon our Lord: and he wil nourish thee: he vvil not geue \f o \fr 54.23 \ft He vvil not ſuffer the iuſt to remaine alvvayes in fluctuation, that is, in doubtful, dangerous, and vvanering thoughtes or perplexities, as vvhen a shippe is toſſed in the vvaues of the ſea, but vvil geue quiet repoſe of mind, as in a ſure hauen vvithout danger of drovvning.\f* fluctuation to the iuſt for euer.
\v 24 But thou ô God wilt bring \f p \fr 54.24 \ft Coatrariwiſe, the wicked and obſtinate shal fal into deſtruction.\f* them downe into the pitte of deſtruction. \f q \fr 54.24 \ft Often or for moſt part, bloud-ſuckers dye before the courſe of nature requireth, as Saul, Abſolon, Achitophel, Achab, Iezabel, and the like.\f* Bloudy and deceitful men shal not liue halfe their daies. but I wil hope in thee ô Lord.
\c 55
\cd Dauid being in danger before Achis king of Geth, confidently imploreth Gods helpe, againſt the great malice and powre of his enimies; 8. foresheweth their ruine, his owne exaltation, 12. and offereth praiſes and thankes. \f - \ft David's prayer in danger. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 55.1 \ft This Pſalme perteyneth alſo to future times,\f* the end, \f b \fr 55.1 \ft for the vſe of anie iuſt perſons, or people, that are againſt their wil ſeperated from the publike diuine Seruice of holie Church :\f* for a people, that is made far from the Sainctes, Dauid in \f c \fr 55.1 \ft moſt worthie to be noted with title, for perpetual memorie,\f* the inſcription of the title, \f d \fr 55.1 \ft made by Dauid when the Philiſtims detected him to their king in Geth.\f* when the foreners held him in Geth. (1. Reg. 12. v. 12.) \x - \xt 1. Reg. 12. v. 12.\x*
\v 2 HAVE mercie on me ô God, becauſe \f e \fr 55.2 \ft Now one ſorte of il diſpoſed men, now an other,\f* man hath troden vpon me: \f f \fr 55.2 \ft neuer ceaſe to ſeeke my deſtruction.\f* al the day impugning he hath afflicted me.
\v 3 Myne enimies haue troden vpon me al the day: becauſe they are \f g \fr 55.3 \ft Saul with his great armie, the Philiſtimes, and other ſtrangers, ſome in manifeſt hoſtilitie, others detecting and betraing me to myn aduerſaries. So al that liue godly in Chriſt haue manie enimies viſible and inuiſible,\f* manie that warre againſt me.
\v 4 From \f h \fr 55.4 \ft Of theſe moſt eminent great dangers I am in deede afeard,\f* the height of the day I shal feare: \f i \fr 55.4 \ft but ſo that my truſt and aſſured confidence is in thee ô God.\f* but I wil truſt in thee.
\v 5 In God I wil praiſe \f k \fr 55.5 \ft Wordes and promiſes made to me; or the good which I ſpeake or do by Gods grace.\f* my wordes, in God haue I hoped: I wil not feare what flesh may do to me.
\v 6 Al the day did they \f l \fr 55.6 \ft They calumniate whatſoeuer I ſay,\f* deteſt my wordes; againſt me, \f m \fr 55.6 \ft wreſting al my wordes to euil ſenſe.\f* al their cogitations are vnto euil.
\v 7 They wil \f n \fr 55.7 \ft They meete together, and ſecretly conſpire to intreppe me or catch me tripping.\f* inhabite and keepe ſecret: they wil obſerue my heele. As they haue expected \f o \fr 55.7 \ft to take my life:\f* my ſoule,
\v 8 \f p \fr 55.8 \ft for this their vaine purpoſe to deſtroy me, thou wilt ſaue them, as they deſerue, that is,\f* for nothing shalt thou ſaue them: \f q \fr 55.8 \ft thou wilt breake them in peeces.\f* in wrath thou wilt breake peoples. O God,
\v 9 I haue shewed my life to thee: thou haſt ſet my teares in thy ſight. As alſo in thy promiſe:
\v 10 then shal mine enemies be turned backward. In what day ſoeuer I shal inuocate thee: loe I haue knowne that thou art my God.
\v 11 In God I wil praiſe \f r \fr 55.11 \ft I wil alwayes gratfully acknowlege thy promiſes, and ſayings, for they are aſſured.\f* the word, in our Lord wil I praiſe thee ſaying, I haue hoped in God, I wil not feare what man can do to me.
\v 12 In me, ô God \f s \fr 55.12 \ft I haue purpoſed and vowed to offer ſacrifice of praiſe, and by thy helpe wil performe it.\f* are thy vowes, which I wil render, praiſes to thee.
\v 13 Becauſe thou haſt deliuered my ſoule from death, and my feete from falling: that I may \f t \fr 55.13 \ft do that pleaſeth God,\f* pleaſe before God, in \f v \fr 55.13 \ft in true faith and pious workes.\f* the light of the liuing.
\c 56
\cd The Prophet prayeth in tribulation, 4. teſtifieth Gods helpe, 6. praiſeth his greatnes: 8. promiſing, and inuiting al nations to praiſe him. \f - \ft Dauids great patience. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 56.1 \ft The heroical facts of Dauid are for examples to al Chriſtians.\f* the end, \f b \fr 56.1 \ft Innocent Dauid hauing opportunitie to kil his vniuſt perſecutor, obeyed the motion of God, ſuggeſting vnto him, not to deſtroy his enimie; contrarie to the counſel of his freindes:\f* deſtroy not, to Dauid in \f c \fr 56.1 \ft a thing moſt worthie to be recorded for perpetual memorie,\f* the inſcription of the title; \f d \fr 56.1 \ft being in ſo great and vniuſt truble, as to lye in the caue of a mountaine, yet ſpared to kil, or hurt him, that driue him into ſuch ſtraictes. See the hiſtorie 1. Reg. 24.\f* when he fled from the face of Saul into the caue. (1.Reg.22.24.) \x - \xt 1. Reg. 22. 24.\x*
\v 2 HAVE mercie on me ô God, haue mercie on me: becauſe my ſoule hath truſted in thee. And I wil hope in the shadow of thy winges, vntil iniquitie paſſe.
\v 3 I wil crie to God the higheſt: God that hath done me good.
\v 4 He ſent \f e \fr 56.4 \ft Extraordinarie diuine helpe, paſſing mans powre.\f* from heauen, and deliuered me: he hath geuen into \f f \fr 56.4 \ft It fel reprochfully to Saul, that Dauid might haue ſlaine him if he would, yet did neither hurt him, nor inſult vpon him, but meekely and piouſly admonished him of his error, and iniurious perſecution.\f* reproche them that trode vpon me. God hath ſent his mercie, and his truth,
\v 5 and hath deliuered my \f g \fr 56.5 \ft my life.\f* ſoule out of the middes of \f h \fr 56.5 \ft from moſt mightie and rauenous perſecuters.\f* Lions whelpes: I ſlept trubled. The ſonnes of men, their \f i \fr 56.5 \ft Though they haue not lions natural teeth, yet they exerciſe crueltie by artificial weapons,\f* teeth are weapons and arrowes: and their \f k \fr 56.5 \ft and with their cruel tongues incite their folowers to the ſame furie. 1. Reg. 22. 7. 16.\f* tongue a sharpe ſword.
\v 6 Be exalted aboue the heauens ô God: and thy glorie vpon al the earth.
\v 7 They prepared a ſnare for my feete: and bowed downe my ſoule. They \f l \fr 56.7 \ft Saul endeuored manie wayes to ouerthrow Dauid, amongſt other meanes prouoked him to ſette vpon the Philiſtims, thincking they should haue ſlaine him, \+it 1. Reg. 18. v. 17\+it*. but the ſame Philiſtims ouerthrew Saul. \+it 1 Reg. 31\+it*.\f* digged a pit before my face: and they are falne into it.
\v 8 My hart is readie ô God, my hart is readie: I wil ſing, and ſay \f 1 \fr 56.8 \ft Pſalmes more in vſe with Chriſtian Gentils, then they were with the Iewes. See page. 12.\f* a Pſalme.
\v 9 Ariſe my glorie, ariſe pſalter and harpe: I wil ariſe early.
\v 10 I wil confeſſe to thee among \f m \fr 56.10 \ft Gods benefites beſtowed vpon Dauid, and vpon faithful Chriſtians prefigured by him, are for euer to be praiſed by al peoples and nations.\f* peoples ô Lord: and I wil ſay a Pſalme to thee among \f n \fr 56.10 \ft See previous note\f* the Gentiles.
\v 11 Becauſe thy mercie is magnified euen to the heauens, and thy truth euen to the cloudes.
\v 12 Be axalted aboue the heauens ô God: and thy glorie vpon al the earth.
\c 57
\cd Holie Dauid inueigheth againſt diſſembling wicked men. 7. deſcribeth their manifold punishment, 11. wherin the iuſt shal be comforted. \f - \ft God's providence in ſuffering evil. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 57.1 \ft This Pſalme was made vpon the ſame occaſion, and to the ſame purpoſe as the former,\f* the end, \f b \fr 57.1 \ft to exhorte the iuſt and innocent to patience,\f* deſtroy not, to Dauid in, \f c \fr 57.1 \ft by Dauids memorable example.\f* the inſcription of the title.
\v 2 IF \f d \fr 57.2 \ft Few are ſo wicked, but they ſpeake and pretend iuſt thinges:\f* in very dede you ſpeake iuſtice: iudge right thinges ye ſonnes of men.
\v 3 For in \f e \fr 57.3 \ft but neither thincke wel,\f* the hart you worke iniquities: in the earth your \f f \fr 57.3 \ft nor do wel, but both contrarie, which feaned ſanctitie is duble iniquitie.\f* handes forge iniuſtice.
\v 4 \f g \fr 57.4 \ft Theſe wicked ſinners that flatter and incite king Saul, ſeme to haue ſpent al their life from their infancie in malice.\f* Sinners are alienated from the matrice, they haue erred from the wombe: they haue ſpoken falſe thinges.
\v 5 They haue furie according to the ſimilitude of \f h \fr 57.5 \ft Their furie is vnquiet, til they may wound the innocent with their poiſonful ſting,\f* a ſerpent: as of \f i \fr 57.5 \ft neither wil they harken to good admonitions, but ſtoppe their eares like an aſpe, that layeth one eare cloſe to the ground, and ſtoppeth the other with his taile.\f* the aſpe that is deafe, and ſtoppeth his eares.
\v 6 Which wil not heare the voice of the inchanters, and of the ſorcerer inchanting wiſely.
\v 7 God shal breake their \f k \fr 57.7 \ft But God wil breake their cruel force,\f* teeth in their mouth, the \f l \fr 57.7 \ft though it ſemeth moſt ſtrong, and inſuperable.\f* cheeke tooth of the lions, our Lord wil breake in peeces.
\v 8 They shal come to nothing as water running downe: he hath \f m \fr 57.8 \ft Gods iuſt determination of punishing the wicked ſtil remaineth beat and readie, though execution be ſome while differred.\f* bent his bow til they be weakened.
\v 9 As \f n \fr 57.9 \ft That force and powre which is now inuincible, hard and ſtrong like a lions ſtrongeſt teeth, shal then be as impotent and ſoft as waxe:\f* waxe that melteth, shal they be taken away: \f o \fr 57.9 \ft Gods wrath, like fire, the moſt forcible element, shal fal vpon them, and they shal be caſt into vtter darkenes, depriued of the ſunne and al comfortable light.\f* fyre hath falne on them, and they haue not ſcene the ſunne.
\v 10 Before your \f p \fr 57.10 \ft Before their malice can bring to effect, the great miſchieffes which they plette and purpoſe, God ſuddainly cutteth them of before they fully vnderſtand of, either ſicknes or death, caſteth them as it were aliue into hel.\f* thornes did vnderſtand the old bryar: as liuing ſo in wrath he swalloweth them.
\v 11 The iuſt \f q \fr 57.11 \ft The iuſt reioyce in the punishment of the wicked for three cauſes, firſt in zele of iuſtice, conforming his wil and mind to Gods iudgement; ſecondly, for that himſelfe through Gods mercie hath eſcaped that terrible damnation; thirdly, for that he is now deliuered from moleſtation, and continual tribulation.\f* shal reioice when he shal ſee reuenge: he shal wash his handes in the bloud of a ſinner.
\v 12 And man shal ſay: If certes there be fruite to the iuſt: \f r \fr 57.12 \ft The iuſt ſeing, or by faith knowing what punishment remaineth for the wicked, is therby aſſured that the good shal reape fruict for his wel doing, and that in the meane time God ruleth and iudgeth on the earth, though as yet it appeareth not ſo euidently.\f* there is a God certes iudging them on the earth.
\c 58
\cd Holie Dauid (being beſieged in his owne houſe by men ſent to kil him) confidently prayeth God to deliuer him, 6. and al faithful nations in like danger: 7. and praiſeth God. \f - \ft Another prayer of David in danger. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, deſtroy not, to Dauid in the inſcription of the title, \f a \fr 58.1 \ft King Saul hauing thriſe attempted in vaine to kil Dauid, (\+it 1. Reg. 18. v. 11.\+it* & c. 19. v. 9) ſent ſome of his guard to fetch him, from his owne houſe, that he might be ſlaine: but God moued the mind of Michol, to admonish him of the danger, and to helpe him away in ſaftie, though Saul thought she would haue bene a ſcandal vnto him (or cauſe of ruine) by the handes of the Philiſtians \+it 1. Reg. 18. v. 21.\+it* Vpon which occaſion Dauid made this Pſalme. As he alſo made others, for perpetual memorie of Gods like benefites, in deliuering him in iminent dangers, when Saul ſent three troupes of ſeriants to kil him, and folowed them himſelfe. 1. Reg. 19. v. 10. likewiſe vvhen he was knowen and bewrayed before Achis king of Geth. 1. Reg. 21. alſo in Ceila, in the deſerts of Ziph, and of Maon. c. 23, in Engaddi, c. 24. in Hachila. c. 26. and againe amongſt the Philiſtians. c. 27. and 30.\f* when Saul ſent, and watched his houſe to kil him. (1. Reg. 19.) \x - \xt 1. Reg. 19.\x*
\v 2 DELIVER me from mine enimies ô my God: and from them that riſe vp againſt me defend me.
\v 3 Deliuer me from them that worke iniquitie: and from bloudy men ſaue me.
\v 4 Becauſe loe they \f b \fr 58.4 \ft They haue ſo ſtraictly beſeged me, that it is now in their handes, to take away my life.\f* haue taken my ſoule: the ſtrong haue falne violently vpon me.
\v 5 Neyther is it mine iniquitie, nor my ſinne ô Lord: \f c \fr 58.5 \ft Of my part I haue committed no fault againſt myn enimies, for which they can haue anie iuſt cauſe to perſecute me.\f* without iniquitie haue I runne, and gone directly.
\v 6 Ryſe vp to meete me, and ſee: and thou ô Lord the God of powres, God of Iſrael attend to viſite \f d \fr 58.6 \ft The prophet foreſeing in ſpirite, that the Catholique Church shal be vniuſtly perſecuted, prayeth, and teacheth others to pray, that God wil mercifully viſite his faithful people of al nations:\f* al nations: haue \f e \fr 58.6 \ft and not ſpare obſtinate perſecuters.\f* no mercie on al that worke iniquitie.
\v 7 They wil returne \f f \fr 58.7 \ft Perſecuters laboring how much, or how long ſoeuer, shal at night, that is, in the end of al their wicked endeuoures be vnſatisfied in their deſires,\f* at euening: and they shal ſuffer famine \f g \fr 58.7 \ft as hungrie dogges that runne hunting al the day, & night alſo, ſtil ſeeking & not finding wherwith to fil their rauenous mouthes and deuouring bellies.\f* as dogges, and shal compaſſe the citie.
\v 8 Behold they \f h \fr 58.8 \ft They threaten and determine to vſe al crueltie,\f* wil ſpeake in their mouth, and a ſworde in their lippes: \f i \fr 58.8 \ft as if there were no God, that heareth, and wil punish it.\f* becauſe who hath heard?
\v 9 And thou ô Lord wilt ſcorne them: thou wilt bring to naught al the nations.
\v 10 I wil keepe my ſtrength to thee: \f k \fr 58.10 \ft Through Gods grace the Church is ſtil ſtrong and the vertuous do perſeuere.\f* becauſe thou art my recciuer:
\v 11 my God, thy mercie shal preuent.
\v 12 God wil shew vnto me concerning mine enimies, kil them not \f l \fr 58.12 \ft God ſuffereth afflictions to fal vpon his ſeruants to kepe them exerciſed, leſt in proſperitie they forgete their duties to him.\f* leſt ſometime my peoples forgete. Diſperſe them in thy ſtrength: and \f m \fr 58.12 \ft Depriue them of powre, that they may not do ſo much euil as they deſire.\f* depoſe them my protector ô Lord.
\v 13 The ſinne of their mouth, the word of their lippes: and let them be taken in their pride. And for \f n \fr 58.13 \ft After that their iniquitie is complete\f* curſing and lying they shal be \f o \fr 58.13 \ft they shal be accuſed and punished for their blaſphemies and lies.\f* talked of
\v 14 in conſummation: in wrath of comſummation and they shal not be. And they shal know that God wil rule ouer Iacob: and ouer the ends of the earth.
\v 15 They \f p \fr 58.15 \ft As. \+it v. 7\+it*.\f* shal be turned at euening, and shal ſuffer famine as dogges: and shal compaſſe the citie.
\v 16 They shal be \f q \fr 58.16 \ft They shal in vaine ſeeke oyle for their lampes with the foolish virgins, repent with Iudas, and finding no helpe,\f* diſperſed to eate: and if they be not filled, they \f r \fr 58.16 \ft ſhal continually blaſpheme in hel.\f* wil murmur alſo.
\v 17 But I wil ſing thy ſtrength: and wil exalt thy mercie in \f s \fr 58.17 \ft In the reſurrection.\f* the morning. Becauſe thou art become my receiuer, and my refuge, in the day of my tribulation.
\v 18 My helper, I wil ſing to thee, becauſe thou art God my receiuer: my God, my mercie.
\c 59
\cd King Dauid after his owne and the peoples manie tribulations, 8. rendereth thankes for their renowmed victories, 11. atchiued by Gods only powre. \f - \ft King Dauids thankes for victories. the 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, for them, \f a \fr 59.1 \ft The change of ſtate from aduerſitie to proſperitie in the people of Iſrael, was a figure of the like change in the Church of Chriſt,\f* that shal be changed, in \f b \fr 59.1 \ft worthie to be remembred,\f* the inſcription of the title \f c \fr 59.1 \ft for the inſtruction,\f* to Dauid him ſelfe, \f d \fr 59.1 \ft of Gods beloued,\f* for doctrine,
\v 2 \f e \fr 59.2 \ft as the ſame are more largely recorded in the bookes of kinges.\f* when he ſet fire on Meſopotamia of Syria, and in Sobal, and Ioab returned, and ſtroke Idumæa in the valley of ſalt-pittes twelue thouſand. (2. Reg. 8. & 10. & 1. Paral. 18.) \x - \xt 2. Reg. 8. & 10. & 1. Paral. 18.\x*
\v 3 O GOD thou \f f \fr 59.3 \ft God ſuffereth his people to be afflicted, as wel for their ſinnes, as for exerciſe in vertue,\f* haſt repelled vs, & haſt deſtroyed vs: thou waſt angrie \f g \fr 59.3 \ft after sheweth his mercie in pardoning, and fauour in aduancing them.\f* and haſt had mercie on vs.
\v 4 Thou haſt moued the earth, and haſt trubled it: heale the breaches thereof, becauſe it is moued.
\v 5 Thou haſt shewed vnto thy people \f h \fr 59.5 \ft by punishīg ſinners.\f* hard thinges: thou haſt made vs drinke the wine of compunction.
\v 6 Thou haſt \f i \fr 59.6 \ft VVarning them to amēd.\f* geuen a ſignification to them that feare thee: they flee from the face of the bow.
\v 7 That thy beloued \f k \fr 59.7 \ft and then reſtoreth them to former good ſtate.\f* may be deliuered, ſaue me with thy right hand; and heare me.
\v 8 God hath ſpoken in \f l \fr 59.8 \ft God alſo as he hath promiſed by his holie oracle,\f* his holie: I shal reioyce, and \f m \fr 59.8 \ft hath aduanced king Dauid, in his temporal kingdom, and doth much more aduance him, and other elect in euerlaſting life.\f* shal diuide Sichem: and shal meſure the valley of tabernacles.
\v 9 Galaad is mine, and Manaſſes is mine: and Ephraim the ſtrength of my heade.
\v 10 Iuda my king: Moab \f n \fr 59.10 \ft As a veſſel for meaner vſes\f* the pot of my hope. Into Edom wil I \f o \fr 59.10 \ft Bring it vnder my dominion.\f* ſtretch out my shoe: the foreners are ſubiect to me.
\v 11 Who shal conduct me into a fenſed citie? who shal conduct me euen into Idumea?
\v 12 Shal not thou ô God, \f p \fr 59.12 \ft As God doth ſometimes punish:\f* that haſt expelled vs: and \f q \fr 59.12 \ft ſo he alſo rewardeth.\f* wilt not thou ô God goe forth in our hoſtes?
\v 13 Giue vs aide from tribulation: becauſe mans ſaluation is vaine.
\v 14 In God we shal doe \f r \fr 59.14 \ft ſtrongly, with fortitude.\f* ſtrength: and he shal bring to nothing them that afflict vs.
\c 60
\cd Faithful people of the whole earth pray, and acknowlege that God mercifully heareth their prayer. 6. expect the eternal kingdom of Chriſt, in which they shal praiſe him for euermore. \f - \ft A confident prayer for Chriſt'ſ Incarnation. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, \f a \fr 60.1 \ft In ſongues of praiſe and thankes to God.\f* in hyms to Dauid.
\v 2 HEARE ô God my petition: attend to my praier.
\v 3 From \f b \fr 60.3 \ft From al coaſtes of the earth faithful people pray to God,\f* the ends of the earth I haue cried to thee: whiles my hart was in anguish, thou \f c \fr 60.3 \ft the Church builded vpon an aſſured fundation, is exalted to great powre and dignitie.\f* didſt exalt me on a rocke.
\v 4 Thou haſt \f d \fr 60.4 \ft God conducteth, defendeth, and deliuereth thoſe that confidently truſt in him.\f* conducted me, becauſe thou art made my hope: a toure of ſtrength from the face of the enimie.
\v 5 I shal inhabite in \f e \fr 60.5 \ft in the Church, a place of aſſured protection.\f* thy tabernacle for euer: I shal be protected in the couert of thy winges.
\v 6 Becauſe thou my God haſt heard my prayer: thou haſt giuen inheritance to thoſe that feare thy name.
\v 7 Thou wilt adde \f f \fr 60.7 \ft Chriſts kingdome the Church perpetual to the end of this world, and eternal after the general Reſurrection.\f* dayes vpon the dayes of the king: his years euen vnto the day of generation and generation.
\v 8 He is permanent for euer in the ſight of God: his \f g \fr 60.8 \ft Who is able to vnderſtand, or explicate how great Chriſts mercie is in redeming vs,\f* mercie and \f h \fr 60.8 \ft and his truth in performing his promiſed rewardes?\f* truth who shal require?
\v 9 So \f i \fr 60.9 \ft For ſo imeſtimable benefites, I wil alwayes praiſe thee with Pſalmes, Canticles, or other thankes in this life:\f* wil I ſay a Pſalme to thy name for euer and euer: that I may render my vowes \f k \fr 60.9 \ft and eternally in the life to come.\f* from day to day.
\c 61
\cd A iuſt man encoregeth his owne ſoule to ſerue God in ſincere humilitie, 9. exhorteth alſo al others to truſt in God, not in falſe and worldlie pollicie, or wealth, becauſe Gods powre and mercie wil render to euerie one as they deſerue. \f - \ft Exhortation to good life, in reſpect of reward or punishment. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, for \f a \fr 61.1 \ft Directed to Idithun, one of the maſters of muſike, to ſing it, or to make tune for it.\f* Iduthun a Pſalme of Dauid.
\v 2 Shal \f b \fr 61.2 \ft The wicked threating to ruinate others, Dauid, or anie iuſt man, feareth them not, becauſe his ſoule is ſubiect to God.\f* not my ſoule be ſubiect to God? for of him is my ſaluation.
\v 3 For he is my God, and my ſauiour: my receiuer \f c \fr 61.3 \ft Therfore I firmely purpoſe neuer to be moued from God.\f* I shal be moued no more.
\v 4 \f d \fr 61.4 \ft In vaine do you myn aduerſaries ſtil aſſault me,\f* How long ſet you violently vpon a man: \f e \fr 61.4 \ft though ye be al confederate to kil me;\f* you al doe kil: as it were vpon \f f \fr 61.4 \ft ſuppoſing me to be like a ruinous, or shaken wal, that is eaſily throwne downe.\f* a wal, that is leaning, and a wal shaken.
\v 5 But \f g \fr 61.5 \ft They thinck ſtil to depriue me of my reward, the price of my laboures and merites,\f* yet they thought to repel my price, \f h \fr 61.5 \ft but I runne ſo much more diligently, as thirſting after righteouſnes in this life, and glorie in the next, to finish my courſe.\f* I ranne in thirſt: they \f i \fr 61.5 \ft A moſt dangerous tentation, when after threates and crueltie, perſecutors endeuour by ſwete wordes, and promiſes to perſwaed the iuſt to fal into ſinne.\f* bleſſed with their mouth, and curſed with their hart.
\v 6 But yet my ſoule be thou ſubiect to God: becauſe my patience is from him.
\v 7 Becauſe he is my God, and my ſauiour: my helper \f k \fr 61.7 \ft I reſolutly purpoſe not to yeld to anie tentations.\f* I shal not remoue.
\v 8 In God is my ſaluation, and my glorie: the God of my helpe, and my hope is in God.
\v 9 Hope in him \f l \fr 61.9 \ft Gods faithful ſeruantes are not only conſtant themſelues, but alſo exhorte and perſwade al others, as much as in them lieth, to ſerue God and truſt in him.\f* al ye the congregation of people: powre out your harts before him, God is our helper for euer.
\v 10 But yet the children of men are vaine, the children of men are \f m \fr 61.10 \ft Vſing falſe weightes they defraud one an other.\f* liers in balances: that they may deceiue by vanitie together.
\v 11 Hope not in iniquitie, and couete not robberies: if riches abound ſet not your hart vpon them.
\v 12 \f n \fr 61.12 \ft God hauing nce ſpoken it is moſt aſſured.\f* Once hath God ſpoken, theſe \f o \fr 61.12 \ft Two eſpecial attributes of God.\f* two things haue I heard:
\v 13 That \f p \fr 61.13 \ft God is Omnipotent, ſo that he can both reward, and punish infinitly;\f* powre is Gods, and \f q \fr 61.13 \ft and Merciful, that he is readie to receiue al ſinners into his fauour, if they wil repent and turne vnto him.\f* mercie ô Lord is to thee: becauſe thou wilt render to euery one according to his workes. \x - \xt Mat. 16. Rom. 2. 1. Cor. 3. Gal. 6.\x*
\c 62
\cd Dauid in banishment with great affection deſireth to vnite himſelfe with God in meditation, 4. purpoſing and promiſing euer to praiſe him : 10. prophicieth the vaine endeuoures, and condemnation of his enimies; and his owne aduancement. \f - \ft David's devotion in banishment. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Pſalme \f a \fr 62.1 \ft Holie Dauid made this deuout meditation when he was in the foreſt of Haret, or deſert of Ziph 1. Reg. 22. & 23. and could not come to the tabernacle of God, nor to Ieruſalem, where he eſpecially deſired to be, in the inheritance of our Lord, which was to him a great affliction. As the like is now to Catholiques, when they are put in cloſe priſon for their faith, or otherwiſe hindered, that they can not be preſent at the moſt holie and daylie Sacrifice. In which caſe we muſt ſupplie as we may, this great loſſe, and comfort ourſelues with this, or like Pſalme, or prayer, ſaying : O God my God, to thee I watch.\f* of Dauid when he was in the deſert of Iuda (1. Reg. 22.) \x - \xt Reg. 22.\x*
\v 2 O GOD my God to thee I watch, \f b \fr 62.2 \ft Euen from the firſt downing of the morning,\f* from the morning light. \f c \fr 62.2 \ft my ſoule thirſteth after thee,\f* My ſoule hath thirſted to thee, \f d \fr 62.2 \ft yea alſo my very flesh, and whole bodie feeleth great paines, by this affliction of mind, and deſireth releeſe and reſt.\f* my flesh to thee very manie wayes.
\v 3 In a deſert land, and inacceſſible, and without water, \f e \fr 62.3 \ft Being now in caſe that I can not ſerue thee, ô God, as I would, yet I exhibite myſelfe preſent in ſpirite, before thy holie place,\f* ſo in the holie haue I appeared to thee, \f f \fr 62.3 \ft meditating thy powre, and thy glorie.\f* that I might ſee thy ſtrength, and thy glorie.
\v 4 Becauſe \f g \fr 62.4 \ft This conſolation in banishment from thy diuine Seruice, is ſweeter to me then manie temporal liues, or anie worldlie proſperitie.\f* thy mercie is better then liues, my lippes shal prayſe thee.
\v 5 \f h \fr 62.5 \ft For as the Paſſions of Chriſt abound in vs (ſaith S. Paul \+it 2. Cor. 1\+it*.) ſo alſo by Chriſt our comfort aboundeth.\f* So wil I bleſſe thee in my life: and in thy name I wil liſte vp my hands.
\v 6 As with \f i \fr 62.6 \ft Replenish my ſoul ô God, with the aboundance of thy grace,\f* marrow and fatnes let my ſoule be filled: \f k \fr 62.6 \ft ſo ſhal I be more able to praiſe thee.\f* and my mouth shal praiſe with lippes of exultation.
\v 7 \f l \fr 62.7 \ft Seing in the night, alſo in my bed I meditate of thee;\f* If I haue bene mindful of thee vpon my bedde, \f m \fr 62.7 \ft I wil more diligently do the ſame in the morning.\f* in the morning I wil meditate on thee:
\v 8 becauſe thou haſt bene my helper.
\v 9 And in the couert of thy winges I wil reioice, my ſoule hath cleaued after thee: thy right hand hath receiued me.
\v 10 But \f n \fr 62.10 \ft My temporal and ſpiritual enimies:\f* they in vaine haue ſought my ſoule, \f o \fr 62.10 \ft and they shal be damned for their ſinnes.\f* they shal enter into the inferiour partes of the earth.
\v 11 They \f p \fr 62.11 \ft It happened literally to Saul, that he was ſlaine in battle, which he made againſt his enimies;\f* shal be deliuered into the handes of the ſworde, they shal be \f q \fr 62.11 \ft and his dead bodie was hung on a wal \+it (1. Reg. 31.)\+it* expoſed to wilde beaſtes, or birdes, though it was afterwards burnt and buried.\f* the portions of foxes.
\v 12 But \f r \fr 62.12 \ft Dauid was preſently after Sauls death exalted to the kingdome, in figure of Chriſt, whoſe name and glorie was exalted, after the deſtruction of the Iewes by Pagane Emperours.\f* the king shal reioice in God, al shal be praiſed that ſweare by him: becauſe the mouth is ſtopped of thoſe that ſpeake wicked thinges.
\c 63
\cd A prayer of the iuſt repoſing their whole truſt in God: 7. and reioycing that the enimies machinations are fruſtrate. \f - \ft A confident prayer in tribulation. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, a Pſalme of Dauid.
\v 2 HEARE ô God my prayer when I make petition: from the feare of my enimie deliuer my ſoule.
\v 3 Thou \f a \fr 63.3 \ft By example of thy former protection,\f* haſt protected me from the \f b \fr 63.3 \ft from the conſpiracie of wicked men, I truſt moſt aſſuredly in thy helpe.\f* aſſemblie of the malignant: from the multitude of them that worke iniquitie.
\v 4 Becauſe they haue sharpned their tongues as a ſworde: they haue bent the bow a bitter thing,
\v 5 that they may shoote in ſecretes at the immaculate.
\v 6 Sodanely they wil shoote at him, and wil not feare: they \f c \fr 63.6 \ft They are reſolued to intrappe me,\f* haue confirmed to themſelues a wicked worde. They haue talked to hide ſnares: they haue ſaide, who shal ſee them?
\v 7 They haue ſearched iniquities: they \f d \fr 63.7 \ft But as they haue failed: ſo they ſhal ſtil faile, and be ouerreached in their bad counſels; as Achitophel. \+it 1. Reg. 17\+it*.\f* haue failed ſearching with ſcrutanie.
\v 8 Man shal come to a deepe hart: and God shal be exalted. \f e \fr 63.8 \ft God hath choſen the weake of this world to confound the ſtrong.\f* Childrens arrowes are made their woundes:
\v 9 and their tongues are weakned againſt them. Al that ſaw them \f f \fr 63.9 \ft much merueled, ſeing the wicked ſo punished.\f* were trubled:
\v 10 and euerie man feared. And they shewed forth the workes of God: and they vnderſtood his doinges.
\v 11 The iuſt shal reioice in our Lord, and shal hope in him, and al the right of hart \f g \fr 63.11 \ft The iuſt shal be praiſed, for rightly ſeruing God.\f* shal be praiſed.
\c 64
\cd God is rightly praiſed in Sion and Ieruſalem (in his Church only) for his benefites beſtowed and promiſed. 8. Vnto which alſo in the time of grace, al nations shal be called. \f - \ft Converſion of Gentiles. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 To the end, a Pſalme of Dauid, \f a \fr 64.1 \ft The ſeuentie Interpreters ſeing Dauid here prophecie of the peoples returne from Babylon, added the names of Ieremie and Ezechiel, who being in that captiuitie, prophecied the ſame more largely. As likewiſe theſe and other Prophetes, foreſaw in ſpirite, and more eſpecially prophecied the going forth of al nations from Babylon, that is, forſaking Idolatrie, and embracing true Religion in the Church of Chriſt, ſo S. Auguſtin, Eutymius, and others.\f* the Canticle of Ieremie, and Ezechiel, to the people of the tranſmigration, when they begane to goe forth.
\v 2 AN hymne ô God becometh thee \f b \fr 64.2 \ft Not in Babylon, nor els where, but only in the Church, praiſes and vowes are gratful to God.\f* in Sion: & a vow shal be rendered to thee in Ieruſalem.
\v 3 Heare my prayer: \f c \fr 64.3 \ft Al nations shal know thee.\f* al flesh shal come to thee.
\v 4 The wordes of the wicked \f d \fr 64.4 \ft The wicked are inſolent in threatning,\f* haue preuailed vpon vs: and thou wilt be \f e \fr 64.4 \ft but thou mercifully pardoning our ſinnes, they ſhal not hurt vs.\f* propitious to our impieties.
\v 5 \f f \fr 64.5 \ft They are happie to whom thou haſt prepared grace, and glorie.\f* Bleſſed is he, whom thou haſt choſen, and taken: he shal dwel in thy courtes.
\v 6 \f g \fr 64.6 \ft The voice of the faithful, reioycing in the hope of eternal glorie. Rom. 5.\f* We shal be replenished in the goods of thy houſe: holie is thy temple, \f h \fr 64.6 \ft nothing polluted shal enter into heauen. Apo. 21.\f* meruelous in equitie. Heare vs ô God our ſauiour, the hope of al the ends of the earth; and in the ſea farre.
\v 7 Preparing \f i \fr 64.7 \ft Thou which art al powreful, as appeareth by the huge montaines,\f* mountanes in thy ſtrength, girded with might:
\v 8 which trubleſt \f k \fr 64.8 \ft ſeas, and other thy workes.\f* the depths of the ſea, the ſound of the waues therof.
\v 9 The Gentiles shal \f l \fr 64.9 \ft Thou wilt by thy omnipotent powre moue the hartes of obdurate men, and ſo conuert innumerable of al nations to thee.\f* be trubled, and they that inhabite the borders shal be affraide of thy ſignes: \f m \fr 64.9 \ft Thou wilt draw manie to thee, with ioy and gladnes, from the vttermoſt coaſtes of the eaſt and weaſt.\f* the outegoinges of the morning and euening thou shalt delight.
\v 10 Thou haſt viſited the earth, and haſt inebriated it: thou haſt multiplied to inrich it. The \f n \fr 64.10 \ft God wrought diuers miracles in waters (\+it Gen. 7. Exo. 7. 14. 15. Ioſue. 3. 4. Reg. 5. 6\+it*. &c.)\f* riuer of God is replenished with waters, thou haſt prepared their \f o \fr 64.10 \ft likewiſe in prouiding meate for his people. (Exo. 16. 3. Reg. 17. 4. Reg. 4. 7. &c.) which were figures of Baptiſme, Euchariſt, and other Sacraments of Chriſt, waſhing from ſinnes, and augmenting grace,\f* meat: becauſe \f p \fr 64.10 \ft ſo replenishing the Church with moſt ſacred Myſteries.\f* ſo is the preparation therof.
\v 11 Inebriate \f q \fr 64.11 \ft Endewing the Apoſtles and other preachers with ſpiritual grace and lerning,\f* her riuers, \f r \fr 64.11 \ft continuing the ſucceſſion of paſtores to watter and feede the faithful people.\f* multiplie her fruites: in her dropps she shal reioyce ſpringing.
\v 12 Thou wilt \f s \fr 64.12 \ft God bleſſeth the whole courſe, or circle of time, of the Church militant in this world,\f* bleſſe the crowne of the yeare of thy goodnes: \f t \fr 64.12 \ft and the crowne, or happie end of euerie iuſt perſons life.\f* and thy fildes shal be replenished with plentie.
\v 13 The \f v \fr 64.13 \ft u Euen thoſe which before had only a ſhew of beautie, but in dede vvere barren, ſhal yelde abundant fruict.\f* beautiful places of the deſert shal be fat: and the \f w \fr 64.13 \ft thoſe which are more eminent ſhal particularly reioyce in their owne, and others ſpiritual progreſſe in vertue.\f* litle hilles shal be girded aboute with exultation.
\v 14 The \f x \fr 64.14 \ft The principal paſtores ſhal in proportion reioyce aboue the reſt, for the grace and glorie of al their flocke.\f* rammes of the shepe are clothed, and \f y \fr 64.14 \ft the ſubiectes alſo and inferior people shal be ſatiate vvith their happie lotte.\f* the valleis shal abounde with corne: \f z \fr 64.14 \ft Al together, prelates and people, higher and lovver ſhal with vniforme voice ſing praiſes to God, and perpetual hymnes.\f* they wil crie, yea they wil ſay an hymne.
\c 65
\cd The prophet inuiteth al men to praiſe God for his meruelous workes, and benefites donne to the Iewes. 7. Who being vngratful, 8. Gentiles are called, 16. and bring forth better fruict. \f - \ft Gentiles ſucceed the Jews. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, a Canticle \f a \fr 65.1 \ft Myſtical reſurrection, Gentiles ſucceding in place of the Iewes.\f* of reſurrection. MAke ye \f b \fr 65.1 \ft Shew your internal ioy by external wordes and deedes.\f* iubilation to God al the earth,
\v 2 ſay a pſalme to his name: geue glorie to his praiſe.
\v 3 Say ye to God: \f c \fr 65.3 \ft In drovvning the vvorld, in confounding the tongues in Babel, in burning Sodom and Gomorrha with brimſton, in plaging the Ægyptians, in drovvning Pharao and his vvhole armie in the read ſea; in deſtroying the Chananites and other infideles, in puniſhing the tenne tribes, and aftervvards the other tvvo by captiuitie, and innumerable other puniſhments, al for ſinnes,\f* How terrible are thy workes ô Lord! in the multitude of thy ſtrength thine enimies \f d \fr 65.3 \ft for vvhich euen the vvicked, though not ſincerly conuerted, yet of ſeruile feare, feaned and falſly promiſed to amend, but performed it not: as Pharao afflicted vvith plagues, vvas forced to promiſe libertie to the children of Iſrael, vvhich he aftervvards denied.\f* shal lie to thee.
\v 4 Let al the earth adore thee, and ſing to thee: let it ſing a pſalme to thy name.
\v 5 Come ye, and ſee the workes of God: terrible in counſels ouer the chidren of men.
\v 6 Who turneth \f e \fr 65.6 \ft The read ſea,\f* the ſea into drie land, in \f f \fr 65.6 \ft when Ioſue brought the people ouer Iordan,\f* the riuer they shal paſſe on foote, \f g \fr 65.6 \ft in remembring and reciting theſe ſingular benefites.\f* there we shal reioyce in him.
\v 7 Who ruleth in his ſtrength for euer his eyes looke vpon the gentiles: \f h \fr 65.7 \ft Gods choſen people the Iewes did often exaſperate God by their ingratitude, murmuring, and other ſinnes: whom the prophet therfore admoniſheth,\f* they that exaſperate him let them \f i \fr 65.7 \ft not to be proud, leſt they be ſaboued, and brought lowe.\f* not be exalted in themſelues.
\v 8 Ye \f k \fr 65.8 \ft By way of inuitation the Pſalmiſt prophecieth the conuerſion of Gentiles.\f* Gentiles bleſſe our God: and make the voice of his prayſe hearde.
\v 9 Who hath put \f l \fr 65.9 \ft The voice of the whole Church: confeſſing Gods prouidence and protection, that she neuer faleth; for the Iewes falling from Chriſt the Gentiles beleued in him; and ſome nations, or countries failing from Religion, others are conuerted.\f* my ſoule in life: and hath not geuen my feete to be moued.
\v 10 Becauſe thou \f m \fr 65.10 \ft God ſuffereth his Church to be perſecuted with al kindes of tribulation, as ſome are here recited.\f* haſt proued vs ô God: by fire thou haſt tried vs, as ſiluer is tried.
\v 11 Thou haſt brought vs into a ſnare, thou haſt laide tribulations on our backe: thou haſt ſet men vpon our heades.
\v 12 \f n \fr 65.12 \ft But through Gods aſſiſtance his ſeruants paſſe through, and ouercome al tentations.\f* We haue paſſed through fire and water: and thou haſt brought vs out into refreshing.
\v 13 I wil goe into thy houſe with \f o \fr 65.13 \ft Sacrifice of thankes,\f* holocauſtes: I wil render thee \f p \fr 65.13 \ft and voluntarie vowes,\f* my vowes:
\v 14 which my lippes haue diſtinguished. And \f q \fr 65.14 \ft that which anie promiſeth to God in tribulation, they muſt performe accordingly.\f* my mouth hath ſpoken in my tribulation.
\v 15 \f r \fr 65.15 \ft Theſe were the beſt external ſacrifices of the old law.\f* Holocauſtes with marrow wil I offer to thee, with incenſe of rammes: I wil offer to thee oxen with bucke goates.
\v 16 \f s \fr 65.16 \ft But both then and now the internal ſacrifices of contrite hart, of iuſtice, and of diuine praiſe beſt pleaſe God.\f* Come ye, heare, and I wil tel al ye that feare God, what great things he hath done for my ſoule.
\v 17 To him haue I cried with my mouth, and haue exulted \f t \fr 65.17 \ft from the hart, which is vnder the tongue, and directeth the tongue what to ſpeake.\f* vnder my tongue.
\v 18 \f v \fr 65.18 \ft Whoſoeuer wil be heard in prayer, muſt repent of his ſinnes.\f* If I haue beheld iniquitie in my hart, our Lord wil not heare.
\v 19 Therfore hath God heard, and hath attended to the voice of my petition.
\v 20 Bleſſed be God who hath not remoued my prayer, and his mercie from me.
\c 66
\cd The prophet prayeth for (and withal foresheweth) the propagation of the Church of Chriſt. \f - \ft Propagation of the Church. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, in hymes, a Pſalme \f a \fr 66.1 \ft This Pſalme beginning to be ſongue by voices, inſtruments were adioyned.\f* of Canticle to Dauid.
\v 2 GOd \f b \fr 66.2 \ft God firſt remitte our ſinnes:\f* haue mercie vpon vs, and \f c \fr 66.2 \ft then geue vs thy manifold graces,\f* bleſſe vs: \f d \fr 66.2 \ft grant faith and repentance,\f* illuminate his countenance vpon vs, and \f e \fr 66.2 \ft and ſo forgeuenes of ſinnes.\f* haue mercie on vs.
\v 3 That we may know thy way vpon earth: in al nations thy ſaluation.
\v 4 Let peoples ô God, confeſſe to thee: \f f \fr 66.4 \ft Al nations shal be conuerted.\f* let al peoples confeſſe to thee.
\v 5 Let nations be glad & reioice: becauſe thou iudgeſt peoples in equitie, and the nations in earth thou doeſt direct.
\v 6 Let peoples ô God confeſſe to thee, let al peoples confeſſe to thee:
\v 7 the earth hath yelded her fruite. \f g \fr 66.7 \ft God the Fater,\f* God, \f h \fr 66.7 \ft God the Sonne,\f* our God bleſſe vs,
\v 8 \f i \fr 66.8 \ft God the Holie Ghoſt, ſaue the peoples of al nations, by Euangelical preaching of thee, the moſt Bleſſed Trinitie.\f* God bleſſe vs: and let al the endes of the earth feare him.
\c 67
\cd Notwithſtanding great perſecutions the Church proſpereth. 10. Eſpecially in the new teſtament, by Apoſtolical function, 18. miniſterie of Angels, Chriſts Aſcenſion, coming of the Holie Ghoſt, 31. confirming the faithful, repreſſing the inſolent, and conuerting manie. 35. For al which the prophet inuiteth al men to praiſe God. \f - \ft The Church ſtill conſerved. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, a Pſalme of Canticle, to Dauid himſelf.
\v 2 LEt \f a \fr 67.2 \ft In maner of praying that God wil vouchſafe to defend the Church, the Pſalmiſt prophecieth that God wil ariſe,\f* God ariſe, and let his enimies be diſperſed, and let them that hate him \f b \fr 67.2 \ft and the enimies ſhal flee away, not daring to abide the combate.\f* flee from his face.
\v 3 As ſmoke vanisheth, let them vanish away: as waxe melteth at the preſence of fire, ſo let ſinners perish at the preſence of God.
\v 4 And \f c \fr 67.4 \ft As God is terrible to the wicked: ſo he is comfortable to the iuſt.\f* let the iuſt make merrie, and reioyce in the ſight of God: and let them be delighted in mirth.
\v 5 Sing to God, ſay a Pſalme to his name: \f d \fr 67.5 \ft Reſiſt not Gods inſpiration, but receiue it with ioy and thankes.\f* make way to him, who \f e \fr 67.5 \ft who triumpheth ouer death\f* mounteth vpon the weſt, \f f \fr 67.5 \ft God is Lord, not only of theſe or thoſe nations, countries or other creatures, but abſolutly and vniuerſally of al.\f* Lord is his name. Reioyce ye in his ſyght, they shal be trubled at the preſence of him:
\v 6 the father of orphanes, and iudge of widowes. God in his \f g \fr 67.6 \ft That is the true holie Church, which hath\f* holie place:
\v 7 God that maketh men to inhabite \f h \fr 67.7 \ft vnitie in doctrin, touching faith and maners. · S.Cypr.ep.76.\f* of one manner in a houſe. That bringeth forth them, \f i \fr 67.7 \ft That be bond in ſinne.\f* that be bound, in ſtrengh, likewiſe them, that \f k \fr 67.7 \ft euen rebellious willes are altered by Gods mercie, and freely embrace his law\f* exaſperate, that dwel \f l \fr 67.7 \ft alſo the dead and drie hartes that cared not for ſpiritual thinges, are ſoftened, and quickned with new grace.\f* in ſepulchers.
\v 8 O God \f m \fr 67.8 \ft The benefites beſtowed on the Iſraelites, are written in the bookes of Moyſes, Ioſue, and Iudges.\f* when thou wenteſt forth in the ſight of thy people, when thou didſt paſſe through the deſert.
\v 9 The earth was moued, and the heauens alſo diſtilled, at the preſence of the God of Sina, at the face of the God of Iſrael.
\v 10 \f n \fr 67.10 \ft Not mans deſeruing, but Gods mere good wil, and free grace cauſed Chriſt to come, and by himſelfe and his Apoſtles to preach the Euangelical doctrin, which watereth the whole world,\f* Voluntarie rayne shalt thou ſeperat ô God to thine inheritance: and it was \f o \fr 67.10 \ft God choſe the weake, but made them ſtrong.\f* weakned, but thou haſt perfited it.
\v 11 Thy \f p \fr 67.11 \ft Thoſe whom thou haſt choſen, and ſo made thyn owne peculiar people, ſhal enioy this grace.\f* liuing creatures shal dwel in it: thou haſt prepared \f q \fr 67.11 \ft Thou gaueſt Manna in the deſert, the B. Sacrament in the new teſtament.\f* in thy ſwetnes for the poore, ô God.
\v 12 Our Lord shal geue \f r \fr 67.12 \ft God geueth to the preacher what to ſpeake,\f* the word to them that euanglize, \f s \fr 67.12 \ft and to ſome he geueth alſo powre to worke miracles, in confirmation of their doctrin. \+it Mar. 16\+it*.\f* with great powre.
\v 13 The \f t \fr 67.13 \ft Some potent king, or ( as it is in the Hebrew ) kinges being beloued, of the beloued of God, the only Sonne of God, ſhal yeld themſelues to the ſame beloued Sonne of God:\f* king of hoaſtes the beloued of the beloued: and \f v \fr 67.13 \ft which ſhal redound to the glorie and beautie of his Church, gayning ſuch ſpiritual prayes from the diuel.\f* to the beautie of the houſe, to diuide the ſpoyles.
\v 14 If ye \f w \fr 67.14 \ft If you be in ſuch danger, that the aduerſaries caſt dice, or lottes for your perſons, and goodes, yet you ſhal be deliuered, as if a doue, with her gliſtering fethers, like ſiluer and gold, flie away into a ſecure place, without loſſe or diminution, but rather with increaſe of vertues.\f* ſleepe among the middeſt of the lottes, the winges of a doue layde ouer with ſiluer, and the hinder parts of her backe in the palenes of gold.
\v 15 Whiles \f x \fr 67.15 \ft VVhen the heauenlie king determineth thus of earthlie kinges,\f* the heauenlie diſcerneth kings ouer her, \f y \fr 67.15 \ft they shal be purged from their ſinnes, and made white like ſnow, that falleth in mount Selmon, which is a ſhadowed hil, thicke with trees, in mount Ephraim nere to Iordan.\f* with ſnow they shal be made white in Selmon.
\v 16 The \f z \fr 67.16 \ft The Church of God is viſible, and durable like to a mountane.\f* mountane of God a fat mountane. A mountane \f a \fr 67.16 \ft Combined, or ioyned together, as when milke is turned into curde, and ſo into cheeſe.\f* crudded as cheeſe, a \f b \fr 67.16 \ft fructful, enriched by ſpiritual giftes of the Holie Ghoſt.\f* fatte mountane:
\v 17 why \f c \fr 67.17 \ft ye that are not of this Church, do in vaine and erroniouſly imagine, that anie other mountaines are vnited.\f* ſuppoſe you crudded mountanes? A mountane, in which it hath wel pleaſed God to dwel therin: for in dede our Lord wil dwel euen to the end.
\v 18 The \f d \fr 67.18 \ft Innumerable Angels miniſters of Gods wil, do continually attend vpon his Diuine Maieſtie, as if he ( who otherwiſe nedeth no ſeruice ) were caried by them, as in a chariotte of infinite magnificence. Dan 7.\f* chariote of God is ten thouſand folde, thouſands of them that reioyce, our Lord in them, \f e \fr 67.18 \ft So God appeared in Maieſtie, when he gaue his law in mount Sinai.\f* in Sina in the holie place.
\v 19 Thou art \f f \fr 67.19 \ft Chriſt aſcended with innumerable Angels attending vpon him,\f* aſcended on hygh, thou \f g \fr 67.19 \ft caried with him the fathers of the old teſtament, that had benne captiue,\f* haſt taken captiuitie: thou \f h \fr 67.19 \ft as man he receiued giftes of God, in and for men, his faithful ſeruantes,\f* haſt receiued gifts in men: for euen thoſe \f i \fr 67.19 \ft yea alſo he receiued for his merite, that innumerable, which before were incredulous, were conuerted, and God dwelt in their ſoules.\f* that do not beleue, our Lord God to inhabite. \x - \xt Ephe. 4.\x*
\v 20 Bleſſed be our Lord day by day: the God of our ſaluations wil make vs a proſperous iourney.
\v 21 Our God is the God of ſauing: and \f k \fr 67.21 \ft Our Lord, I ſay, our Lord, and none but he could ouercome death by dying.\f* the iſſues of death are of our Lord, our Lord.
\v 22 But yet God \f l \fr 67.22 \ft Though Chriſt died to deliuer al men from death. yet he wil geue capital ſentence of eternal death to al that obſtinatly remaine his enimies, and multiplie ſinnes vpon ſinnes, to the end of their temporal life.\f* ſtil breake the heads of his enimies: the hearie croune of them, that walke in their ſinnes.
\v 23 Our Lord Said: Out \f m \fr 67.23 \ft Euen of the rudeſt barbarous nations, manie shal be conuerted to Chriſtiantie\f* of Baſan I wil conuert, I wil conuert \f n \fr 67.23 \ft namely Gods grace is extended into the Ilandes of the Ocean, and other ſeas.\f* into the depth of the ſea.
\v 24 That thy foote \f o \fr 67.24 \ft But ſuch ſeuere ſlaughter shal fal vpon the obſtinate contemners of this grace, that mens feete shal be defiled in their bloud, and dogges ſhal lappe it.\f* may be dipped in bloude: the tongue of thy dogges \it [made redde\it*] with the ſame \it [bloud\it*] of the enimies. \f 1 \fr 67.24 \ft \+it Ex inimicis ab ipſo\+it*.\f*
\v 25 They \f p \fr 67.25 \ft Manie haue ſene, or knowen in general, but the faithful more exactly know how Chriſt came into this world, his conuerſation therin, and his going forth:\f* haue ſeene thy entringes in ô God, the entrings of my God: of my King Who is \f q \fr 67.25 \ft his reigning now in heauen our Mediatour, by whom al other interceſſors haue acceſſe to God.\f* in the holie place.
\v 26 \f r \fr 67.26 \ft The Apoſtles ſowing the firſt ſeede of Euangelical doctrin,\f* Princes came before \f s \fr 67.26 \ft with whom other Apoſtolical men,\f* ioyned with them that ſang, in the middes \f t \fr 67.26 \ft and other ſoules of al nations conuerted by their preaching, moſt ioyfully ſing together in hart, voice, and inſtruments: eſpecially in good workes, ſhew their gratful affections to our Redeemer.\f* of young wemen plaing on tymbrels.
\v 27 In \f v \fr 67.27 \ft And al this in the particular Churches of diuers Kingdomes, and partes of the world:\f* churches bleſſe ye God our Lord, of \f w \fr 67.27 \ft beginning with the Iſraelites in Ieruſalem, and ſo proceding into al Iurie, and Samaria, and to the vtmoſt of the earth. Act. 1.\f* the fountains of Iſrael.
\v 28 There \f x \fr 67.28 \ft S. Paul of Iacobs yongeſt ſonne Beniamin, laſt called to Apoſtleſhip, was chiefly ſent to the Gentiles.\f* Beniamin a youngman, in exceſſe of minde. \f y \fr 67.28 \ft Other Apoſtles of diuers tribes ſent firſt to the Iewes, ſecondarily to Gentiles.\f* The Princes of Iuda: their leaders: the Princes of Zabulon, the Princes of Nephthali.
\v 29 Command thy ſtrength ô God: \f z \fr 67.29 \ft As the Church begane by the omnipotent powre of God; ſo by the ſame only powre it is conſerued.\f* confirme this ô God, which thou haſt wrought in vs.
\v 30 From thy temple in Ieruſalem, kinges shal offer giftes to thee.
\v 31 Rebuke \f a \fr 67.31 \ft Chaſtice therfore ô God, al perſecuters of thy Church, who are but as weake wauering reedes, in compariſon of thy powre:\f* the wilde beaſts of the reede, the congregation \f b \fr 67.31 \ft no better then bulles, with kyne, that is, captaines and popular people,\f* of bulles in the kine of thy peoples: \f c \fr 67.31 \ft endeuoring to alienate the conſtant proued confeſſors from their faith.\f* that they may exclude them, which are tried with ſiluer. Diſſipate the nations that wil warres:
\v 32 Legates shal come \f d \fr 67.32 \ft A prophecie that manie ſhould be conuerted to Chriſt in Ægypt, and Æthiopia: as appeareth by the innumerable multitude of religious Monkes, & Nunnes in thoſe countries, ſhortly after the Apoſtles dayes.\f* out of Ægypt: Æthiopia shal preuent his handes to God.
\v 33 Ye kingdomes \f e \fr 67.33 \ft The like afterwards in al other nations, whom therfore the prophet inuiteth to praiſe God, for ſo ineſtimable benefites in the whole world.\f* of the earth ſing to God: ſing to our Lord:
\v 34 Sing ye to God, that mounteth vpon the heauen of heauen, to the Eaſt. Behold he wil giue to his voice \f f \fr 67.34 \ft Chriſt will come to judge, in terror of voice, and vvith magnificence, accompanied vvith holy Angels and other Saints.\f* the voice of ſtrength,
\v 35 giue you glorie to God vpon Iſrael, his magnificence, and his powre in the cloudes.
\v 36 God is meruelous in his ſaintes, the God of Iſrael he wil giue powre, and ſtrength to his people, God be bleſſed.
\c 68
\cd Chriſt in middes of afflictions (as one in dangerous waters) 5. deſcribing the malice of perſecutors, and his owne true zele, 14. prayeth his heauenlie Father for helpe. 23. By way of iuſt imprecation, forsheweth the ſeuere punishment of his aduerſaries, 30. his owne glorious Reſurrection, and proſperous building of his Church. For which he inuiteth al creatures to praiſe God. \f - \ft Chriſt's afflictions and victory. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 68.1 \ft Perteyning to the new Teſtament,\f* the end, \f b \fr 68.1 \ft for gentiles conuerted to Chriſtianitie, and from vice to vertue,\f* for them that shal be changed, \f c \fr 68.1 \ft prefigured in Dauid.\f* to Dauid.
\v 2 SAve me ô God: becauſe \f d \fr 68.2 \ft Vehement afflictions inuiron my hart.\f* waters are entered into my ſoule.
\v 3 I \f e \fr 68.3 \ft I am as one intangled with quickesand, or quadmyre in the bottom of a great water.\f* ſticke faſt in the myre of the depth: and there is no ſure ſtanding. I am come into the depth of the ſea: and a tempeſt hath ouerwhelmed me.
\v 4 I haue \f f \fr 68.4 \ft Our Lord ſvveat bloud for anguiſh in his prayer, and vvas not deliuered from his Paſſion, neither are his ſeruantes preſently deliuered from tribulations, but as is moſt to Gods honour and their owne good.\f* labored crying, my iawes are made hoarſe: my eies haue failed, whiles I hope in my God.
\v 5 They are multiplied aboue the heares of my head, that hate me without cauſe. \x - \xt Ioan. 15.\x* Mine enimies are made ſtrong, that haue perſecuted me vniuſtly: \f g \fr 68.5 \ft Ioan. 15. · Our Sauiour who had no ſinne, payde the ranſom for al ſinnes.\f* then did I pay the thinges that I tooke not.
\v 6 O God thou knoweſt my \f h \fr 68.6 \ft O God thou knoweſt, that this which ſemeth follie to worldlie men, is true wiſdom;\f* foolishnes: and \f i \fr 68.6 \ft and though men charge me vvith offences, thou knoweſt that I am innocent.\f* mine offences are not hide from thee.
\v 7 \f k \fr 68.7 \ft Suffer not the weake to be ſcandalized in my paſſions.\f* Let them not be ashamed vpon me, which expect thee ô Lord, Lord of hoſtes. Let them not be confounded vpon me that ſeeke thee, ô God of Iſrael.
\v 8 Becauſe for thee haue I ſuſtained reproch, confuſion hath couered my face.
\v 9 I am become a forener to my brethren, and a ſtranger to the ſonnes of my mother.
\v 10 Becauſe \f l \fr 68.10 \ft Ioan. 2. Rom. 15. · The zele of ſeeking Gods honour, in propagating and aduancing his Church, is the cauſe of perſecution. As vve ſee thoſe are leſſe perſecuted, which haue leſſe godlie zele.\f* the zele of thy houſe hath eaten me: and the reproches of them that reproched thee, fel vpon me. \x - \xt Ioan. 2. Rom. 15.\x*
\v 11 And I couered my ſoule in faſting: and iton \f m \fr 68.11 \ft The wicked do reproch thoſe that mortifie themſelues.\f* was made a reproch to me.
\v 12 And I put heare cloth my garment, & I became a parable to them.
\v 13 They ſpake aganſt me \f n \fr 68.13 \ft The great men and iudges, alſo the drunkards and raſcalitie of the people.\f* that ſat in the gate; and they ſoong aganſt me that dranke wine.
\v 14 But I, \f o \fr 68.14 \ft But I direct my prayer to thee.\f* my prayer to thee ô Lord: a time of thy good pleaſure ô God. \f p \fr 68.14 \ft Expecting the time of thy good pleaſure.\f* In the multitude of thy mercie heare me, in the truth of thy ſaluation:
\v 15 Deliuer me out of the myre, that I ſtick not faſt: deliuer me from them that hate me, and from the depthes of \f q \fr 68.15 \ft tribulations.\f* waters.
\v 16 Let not the tempeſt of water drowne me, nor the depth ſwallowe me: \f r \fr 68.16 \ft Though Chriſt died and vvas buried, and in ſoule deſcended into hel, yet he could not be holden in his ſepulchre, nor in limbo, but roſe againe.\f* neyther let the pit shut his mouth vpon me.
\v 17 Heare me ô Lord becauſe thy mercie is benigne: according to the multitude of thy commiſerations haue reſpect to me.
\v 18 And turne not away thy face from thy ſeruant: becauſe I am in tribulation, heare me ſpeedily.
\v 19 Attend to my ſoule and, deliuer it: \f s \fr 68.19 \ft That they may either be conuerted, or confounded, and ſo do no more hurt.\f* becauſe of mine enimies deliuer me.
\v 20 Thou knoweſt my reproch, & my confuſion, & my shame.
\v 21 In thy ſight are al they that afflict me, my hart hath looked for reproch and miſerie. And I expected ſome bodie that would be ſorie together with me, and there \f t \fr 68.21 \ft Not anie that could mitigate our Sauiours affliction, vvould shew compaſſion tovvards him.\f* was none: and that would confort me, and I founde not.
\v 22 And they gaue \f v \fr 68.22 \ft Mat. 27. Ioan. 19. · But contrariwiſe when he complained of thirſt, they gaue him gal and vinegre to drinke.\f* gal for my meate: & in my thirſt they gaue me vinegre to drinke.
\v 23 \f w \fr 68.23 \ft Rom. 11. · A prophecie of the deſtruction of the Iewes, at the time of Paſch, when they ſhould thinke to eate their paſchal lamb with ioy.\f* Let their table be made a ſnare before them, & for retributions, and for a ſcandal. \x - \xt Rom. 11.\x*
\v 24 Let \f x \fr 68.24 \ft They are alſo blinde in hart, that they wil not ſee the truth of Chriſts doctrine:\f* Their eies be darkned that they ſee not: and make \f y \fr 68.24 \ft but bow themſelues to earthlie thinges, and vvorldlie gaines euen to this day.\f* their backe crooked alwaies.
\v 25 Poure out thy wrath vpon them: and let the furie of thy wrath ouertake them.
\v 26 Let their habitation be made deſert: and in their tabernacles let there be none to dwel. \x - \xt Act. 1.\x*
\v 27 Becauſe \f z \fr 68.27 \ft Chriſt, vvhom God of this mercie deſigned to ſuffer death, for redemption of mankind, the Iewes of mere enuie and malice perſecuted to death.\f* whom thou haſt ſtriken, they haue perſecuted: and vpon the ſorrow of my wounds they haue added.
\v 28 Adde thou \f a \fr 68.28 \ft A prophecie that God would ſuffer them, to fal from one iniquitie to an other.\f* iniquitie vpon their iniquitie: and let them not enter into thy iuſtice.
\v 29 \f b \fr 68.29 \ft In the end of their liues, they shal not be found in the booke of life, where they ſuppoſe themſelues to be written.\f* Let them be put out of the booke of the liuing: and with the iuſt let them not be written.
\v 30 \f c \fr 68.30 \ft The voice of Chriſt, humbling himſelfe to death, euen to death of the Croſſe:\f* I am poore and ſorowful: \f d \fr 68.30 \ft from which he roſe againe.\f* thy ſaluation ô God hath receiued me.
\v 31 I wil praiſe the name of God with canticle: and wil magnifie him in prayſe.
\v 32 And \f e \fr 68.32 \ft Deuout praiſe and thankeſgeuing pleaſe God more then ſacrifices of the moſt tender calues, which were othervviſe alſo gratful ſacrifices.\f* it shal pleaſe God more then a young calfe: that bringeth forth hornes and hoofes.
\v 33 Let the poore ſee and reioyce: ſeeke ye God, and your ſoule shal liue.
\v 34 Becauſe our Lord hath heard the poore: and he hath not deſpiſed \f f \fr 68.34 \ft God doth aſſuredly comfort al ſuch, as are impriſoned for profeſſing the truth.\f* his priſoners.
\v 35 Let \f g \fr 68.35 \ft Al the creatures of God.\f* the heauens and earth praiſe him, the ſea, and al the creeping beaſtes in them.
\v 36 \f h \fr 68.36 \ft God wil alwayes eſtablish and protect the Catholique Church:\f* Becauſe God wil ſaue Sion: and \f i \fr 68.36 \ft and particular Churches, members of the vniuerſal ſhal alſo proſper.\f* the cities of Iuda shal be built vp. And they shal inhabite there, & by inheritance they shal get it.
\v 37 And \f k \fr 68.37 \ft Perpetual ſucceſſion of the Catholique Church.\f* the ſeede of his ſeruants shal poſſeſſe it, and they that loue his name shal dwel in it.
\c 69
\cd An other prayer of Dauid, when he was perſecuted by Abſolom : made in a Pſalme after his deliuerie. \f - \ft David's prayer in perſecution. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 69.1 \ft An apt prayer alſo for the afflicted in the new Teſtament,\f* the end, a Pſalme of Dauid, in remembrance, that our \f b \fr 69.1 \ft from the danger of Abſolom. \+it (2. Reg. 18.)\+it* or from anie perſecutor.\f* Lord ſaued him.
\v 2 O God \f c \fr 69.2 \ft Al men at al times nede Gods helpe:\f* intend vnto my helpe: Lord \f d \fr 69.2 \ft but moſt preſent nede, in preſent dangers. The reſt of this Pſalme is conteyned in the \+it 39\+it*. Pſalme, from the \+it 15\+it*. verſe. but there the whole Church prayeth for helpe, the world being almoſt drowned in ſinnes; here Dauid, or other particular perſons, or peoples pray in their ſeueral diſtreſſes,\f* make haſt to helpe me.
\v 3 Let them be confounded, and be ashamed, that ſeeke my ſoule. \x - \xt Pſa. 39.\x*
\v 4 Let them be turned away backeward, and be ashamed that wil me euils. Let them be turned away forthwith ashamed, that ſay to me: Wel, wel.
\v 5 Let al that ſeeke thee reioyce, and be glad in thee, and let them ſay alwayes: Our Lord be magnified: which loue thy ſaluation.
\v 6 But I am needie and poore: ô God helpe me thou art my helper, and deliuerer: ô Lord be not ſlacke.
\c 70
\cd King Dauid, or anie other iuſt perſon, prayeth God to continew his grace, and protection againſt the malice of the enimie. 5. Recounteth Gods mercie in deliuering him from falling into tentations. 6. prayeth for the ſame in old age, or weakenes of bodie and ſpirite, 20. and promiſeth perpetual gratitude and praiſes. \f - \ft A prayer for perſeverance in virtue. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Pſalme \f a \fr 70.1 \ft Though this Pſalme (as alſo diuers others) is intitled to, or for Dauid, it proueth not that ſome other was the author therof, but the Seuentie Interpreters inſinuate hereby, that it perteyneth in more particular ſorte to Dauid, growing old;\f* for Dauid, \f b \fr 70.1 \ft they adde alſo the ſonnes of Ionadab a moſt holie familie,\f* of the ſonnes of Ionadab, and \f c \fr 70.1 \ft who for their ſingular pietie, were ſuffered to remaine in Ieruſalem, in the firſt captiuitie. Ierem. 35.\f* the former captiues. IN thee ô Lord \f d \fr 70.1 \ft The wordes of Dauid, or anie faithful iuſt perſon.\f* I haue hoped, let me not be confounded for euer:
\v 2 in \f e \fr 70.2 \ft God of his iuſtice reuengeth the iniuries done to his ſeruantes.\f* thy iuſtice deliuer me, and receiue me. Incline thine eare to me, and ſaue me.
\v 3 Be vnto me for a God protector, and for a fenſed place: that thou maiſt ſaue me: Becauſe thou art my firmament, and my refuge.
\v 4 My God deliuer me out of the hand of the ſinner, and out of the hand of him that doth aganſt the law, and of the vniuſt.
\v 5 Becauſe thou art \f f \fr 70.5 \ft Through thy grace I haue patience to expect thy pleaſure, when I shal be releeued.\f* my patience ô Lord: ô Lord my hope from my youth.
\v 6 Vpon thee haue I bene confirmed from the wombe: from my mothers bellie thou art my protector. In thee is my ſinging alwaies:
\v 7 I was made to manie as \f g \fr 70.7 \ft It ſemeth ſtrange to moſt men, why the iuſt haue more trubles then the wicked.\f* a wonder: and thou art a ſtrong helper.
\v 8 Let my mouth be filled with praiſe, that I may ſing thy glorie: \f h \fr 70.8 \ft A neceſſarie prayer for perſeuerance, which none can haue without ſpecial and continual helpe of grace.\f* al the day thy greatnes.
\v 9 Reiect me not in the time of olde age: \f i \fr 70.9 \ft Againſt this ſpecial danger, and laſt aſſaultes of the enimie, Chriſt hath inſtituted the Sacrament of Extreme Vnction.\f* when my ſtrength shal faile, forſake me not.
\v 10 Becauſe mine enimies haue ſaid to me: and they that watched my ſoule, conſulted together,
\v 11 Saying: God hath forſaken him, purſew, and take him: becauſe there is none to deliuer.
\v 12 O God be not farre from me: my God haue reſpect to mine ayde.
\v 13 Let them be confounded, and fayle that detract from my ſoule: let them be couered with confuſion, and shame, that ſeeke euils to me.
\v 14 But I wil alwaies hope: and \f k \fr 70.14 \ft The beſt meanes to conſerue vertue, is to exerciſe the ſame, adding good workes towardes our neighbours, and praiſes to God.\f* wil adde vpon al thy praiſe.
\v 15 My mouth shal shew forth thy iuſtice: al the day thy ſaluation. Becauſe \f l \fr 70.15 \ft I profeſſe not exact knowlege of natural thinges;\f* I haue not knowne lerning, \f m \fr 70.15 \ft but I conſider and contemplate the infinite powre of God, which appeareth in his workes,\f* I wil enter into the \f n \fr 70.15 \ft and his iuſt iudgements, as partly haue appeared in puniſhing, and rewarding ſome in this life, but eſpecially wil be manifeſt in the end of this world.\f* powres of our Lord:
\v 16 ô Lord I wil be mindful of thy iuſtice onely.
\v 17 O God thou haſt taught me from my youth: and vntil now I wil pronounce thy meruelous workes.
\v 18 And vnto ancient age, and olde age: ô God forſake me not? Vntil I shew forth thy arme to al the generation, that is to come. Thy might,
\v 19 and thy iuſtice ô God euen to the higheſt, great meruailes which thou haſt done: ô God who may be like to thee?
\v 20 How great tribulations haſt thou shewed me, manie and euil: and turning thou \f o \fr 70.20 \ft After tribulations recreated me.\f* haſt quickened me: and from the depthes of the earth thou haſt brought me backe againe.
\v 21 Thou haſt multiplied thy magnificence: and being turned thou haſt comforted me.
\v 22 For I alſo wil confeſſe to thee in the inſtruments of Pſalme thy truth: ô God I wil ſing to thee on the harpe, holie one of Iſrael.
\v 23 My \f p \fr 70.23 \ft I wil praiſe thee with mouth and hart.\f* lippes shal reioyce when I shal ſing to thee; and my ſoule, which thou haſt redemed.
\v 24 Yea and my tongue al the day shal meditate thy iuſtice: when they shal be confounded and ashamed that ſeeke euils to me.
\c 71
\cd By way of prayer the Pſalmiſt prophecieth Chriſts coming, our King and Iudge: 4. the deliuerer of mankind from the thraldom of the diuel. 8. the greatnes of his ſpiritual kingdom in the Gentiles. 16. and his continual glorie and praiſe. \f - \ft Chriſt our King & Judge. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 \f a \fr 71.1 \ft This Pſalme is of Chriſt, perfigured by Salomon, whoſe kingdom was moſt glorious of al the kinges of Gods people; for of king Salomon himſelfe manie thinges in this Pſalme can not be truly vnderſtood. S. Aug.\f* Concerning Salomon.
\v 2 O God giue thy iudgement to \f b \fr 71.2 \ft O God moſt bleſſed Trinitie, geue powre and authoritie, to the Sonne of Man, God incarnate, King of al kinges,\f* the king: and thy iuſtice to \f c \fr 71.2 \ft the Sonne of king Dauid, to iudge for mankind againſt the diuel.\f* the ſonne of the king. To iudge thy people in \f d \fr 71.2 \ft Chriſt paying ranſom for al mankind, and ſo man renouncing the diuel, and ſeruing God iuſtly, not iniuriouſly deliuered from captiuitie of ſinne, and of the diuel.\f* iuſtice, and thy poore in iudgement.
\v 3 Let \f e \fr 71.3 \ft A prophecie of the Apoſtles receiuing powre to preach Chriſts Goſpel, of peace and reconciliation of men to God by penance;\f* the mountains receiue peace for the people: and \f f \fr 71.3 \ft and of other Apoſtolical men that folow their ſteppes.\f* the litle hilles iuſtice.
\v 4 He \f g \fr 71.4 \ft Salomon in figure of Chriſt was for a time a iuſt and good king.\f* shal iudge the poore of the people, and shal ſaue the children of the poore: and he shal humble the calumniator.
\v 5 And he shal \f h \fr 71.5 \ft But only Chriſt, not Salomon, nor anie other king of that people, contineweth, or reigneth for euer.\f* continew with the ſunne, and before the moone in generation and generation.
\v 6 He \f i \fr 71.6 \ft The maner of Chriſts incarnation moſt ſilent, ſweete, and gratful.\f* shal deſcend as rayne vpon a fleece: and as droppes diſtilling vpon the earth.
\v 7 There shal riſe in his dayes iuſtice, and aboundance of peace, \f k \fr 71.7 \ft Agane the prophet inculcateth that Chriſts kingdom, his Church, shal continew for euer.\f* vntil the moone be taken away.
\v 8 And he shal rule \f l \fr 71.8 \ft The Church is not only vniuerſal in al times, but alſo in places.\f* from ſea vnto ſea: and from the riuer euen to the ends of the round world.
\v 9 Before him shal the Æthiopians fal downe: and his enimies shal lick the earth. \x - \xt Iſa. 60.\x*
\v 10 The \f m \fr 71.10 \ft The three Sages or Kinges, which adored our Sauiour, and offered gold, frankencenſe, and myrrh, were the firſt that fulfilled this prophecie : and afterwards Conſtantin the great, and other Emperors, Kinges, and Princes.\f* kinges of Tharſis, and \f n \fr 71.10 \ft Amongſt other Ilandes great Bryttannie (the greateſt of Europe) was conuerted to Chriſt, according to this prophecie : firſt ſome few in the Apoſtles time (Metaphraſtes apud Surium, Theodoret, epiſt. ad Timoth. Sophronius, Ser. de Nat. Apoſt, & alij.) More in the time of Eleutherius. Laſtly our Engliſe nation by S. Auguſtin and others ſent by S. Gregorie.\f* the Ilands shal offer preſentes: the kings of the Arabians, and of Sabá shal bring giftes. \x - \xt Mat. 2.\x*
\v 11 And al kinges of the earth shal adore him: al nations shal ſerue him.
\v 12 Becauſe he shal deliuer the poore from the mightie: and the poore which had no helper.
\v 13 He shal ſpare the poore and needy: and he shal ſaue the ſoules of the poore.
\v 14 From vſuries and iniquitie he shal redeme their ſoules: and their name shal be honorable before him.
\v 15 And \f 1 \fr 71.15 \ft How is this prophecie verified, except the Church be alwayes viſible?\f* he shal liue, and there shal be giuen him of the gold of Arabia, and they shal adore it alwaies: al the day they shal bleſſe him.
\v 16 And there shal be a firmament in the earth, in the toppes of mountanes, the fruite therof shal be extolled farre aboue Libanus: and they shal florish of the citie, as the graſſe of the earth.
\v 17 Be his name bleſſed for euer: before the ſunne his name is permanent. And al the tribes of the earth shal be bleſſed in him: al nations shal magnifie him.
\v 18 Bleſſed be our Lord the God of Iſrael, \f o \fr 71.18 \ft No miracles can be donne but by Gods powre.\f* who only doth meruelous thinges.
\v 19 And bleſſed be the name of his maieſtie for euer: and al the earth shal be filled with his maieſtie. \f p \fr 71.19 \ft Aboue al other deſires the holie prophet wiſhed Gods glorie, and praiſes in al the earth, as it is in heauen.\f* Be it, be it.
\v 20 \f q \fr 71.20 \ft It ſemeth by this appendix added by Eſdras, that this Pſalme was laſt compoſed, though not put in the laſt place, but as it came to his handes.\f* The praiſes of Dauid, \f r \fr 71.20 \ft Dauid the natural ſonne of Ieſſe, made no more Hymnes, that is, no more muſical praiſes of God after this Pſalme, but the prefigured Dauid Chriſt our Sauiour, the Sonne of God, in his bodie the Church, ſingeth ſtil new praiſes. S. Aug. in enarrat. Pſal. ſequentis. 72.\f* the ſonne of Ieſſe are ended.
\c 72
\cd The royal prophet, firſt profeſſing the vnſpeakable goodnes of God, 2. in the perſon of the weake complaineth, that the wicked proſper, and the iuſt are afflicted. 15. reprehendeth the murmuration, for though we can not comprehend the ſecrete iudgements of God. 18. yet they are moſt iuſt. 25. So with deſire to reſt in God, he promiſeth euer to praiſe him. \f - \ft Affliction in this world is recompenſed in the end. The 9. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Pſalme \f a \fr 72.1 \ft To be ſongue by Aſaph the maſter of muſike, and his companie.\f* to Aſaph. HOw \f b \fr 72.1 \ft God is ſo excellent good, as no tongue can expreſſe, no hart can conceiue.\f* good is God to Iſrael, to them, that are of a right hart! \x - \xt Pſa. 39.\x*
\v 2 But \f c \fr 72.2 \ft The peace and proſperitie of the wicked tempted me, to leaue the right narrow way, and to goe the brode eaſie way with them.\f* my feete were almoſt moued, my ſteppes almoſt ſlipped.
\v 3 Becauſe I haue had zele vpon the wicked, ſeing the peace of ſinners.
\v 4 Becauſe there is \f d \fr 72.4 \ft They neuer thincke of death;\f* no reſpect to their death: and \f e \fr 72.4 \ft and if they ſuffer ſome affliction, they quickly remoue it from them.\f* ſtabilitie in ther plague.
\v 5 In \f f \fr 72.5 \ft They trauel not ſo much as good men do, neither are they ſo often punished.\f* the labours of men they are not, and with men they shal not be ſcourged.
\v 6 Therfore \f g \fr 72.6 \ft Which maketh them proud, and ſo they runne into al ſortes of iniquitie.\f* hath pride helde them, they are couered with their iniquitie and impietie.
\v 7 Their iniquitie hath proceded as it were of fatte: they haue paſſed into \f h \fr 72.7 \ft Whatſoeuer miſchief came in their mind, they feared not to committe it in fact.\f* the affection of the hart.
\v 8 They haue thought, and haue ſpoken wickednes they haue ſpoken iniquitie \f i \fr 72.8 \ft boldly extolling their owne wordes and workes, and deſpiſing others.\f* on high.
\v 9 They haue ſette their mouth vnto heauen: ãd their tongue hath paſſed in the earth.
\v 10 Therfore wil my people \f k \fr 72.10 \ft For this proſperitie of the wicked, ſome of Gods people wil fall from the right way,\f* returne here: and \f l \fr 72.10 \ft and their dayes wil abound with calamities.\f* ful daies shal be found in them.
\v 11 And they haue ſaide: \f m \fr 72.11 \ft The voice of the weake, inclining to diffidence, and doubt of Gods knowlege, and prouidence.\f* How doth God know, and is there knowledge in the higheſt?
\v 12 Loe the ſinners them ſelues, and they that abounde in the world, haue obtained riches.
\v 13 And I ſaide: Then haue I iuſtified my hart without cauſe, and haue washed my handes amongſt innocentes:
\v 14 And haue bene ſcourged al the day, and my chaſtiſing in the morninges.
\v 15 If I ſaide: I wil ſpeake this: behold \f n \fr 72.15 \ft The prophet in his owne perſon anſwereth, that if he ſhould ſay ſuch thinges, he ſhould reproue Gods children, as faultie, vnworthie of his protection, and to be neglected; wheras it is Gods prouidence to chaſtice his children whom he loueth.\f* I reproued the nation of thy children.
\v 16 I \f o \fr 72.16 \ft In this life we can not know the particular cauſes, why the iuſt are afflicted, and manie wicked proſper in this world.\f* thought to know this thing, it is labour before me:
\v 17 Vntil I may enter into the ſanctuarie of God: and may vnderſtand concerning their latter endes.
\v 18 But yet \f p \fr 72.18 \ft But only in general we are taught, that euils are prepared for the guiles, or deceptes,\f* for guiles thou haſt put it to them: thou haſt caſt them downe whiles they \f q \fr 72.18 \ft pride, and other ſinnes of the wicked.\f* were eleuated.
\v 19 How are they brought into deſolation, they haue failed ſodanely: they haue perished for their iniquitie.
\v 20 As the dreame of them that riſe ô Lord, in thy citie thou shalt bring their \f r \fr 72.20 \ft Not real but imaginarie felicitie.\f* image to nothing.
\v 21 Becauſe my hart is \f s \fr 72.21 \ft Almoſt burned vp with afflictions.\f* inflamed, and my reynes are changed:
\v 22 And I am brought to nothing, and knew not.
\v 23 As \f t \fr 72.23 \ft Charged with heauie burdens, without knowlege how long, or to what end,\f* a beaſt am I become with thee: and I \f v \fr 72.23 \ft yet not deſtitute of thy protection.\f* alwaies with thee.
\v 24 Thou haſt helde my right hand: and in thy wil thou haſt conducted me, and with glorie thou haſt receiued me.
\v 25 For what is to me \f w \fr 72.25 \ft Nothing to be deſired in heauen, nor in earth but God.\f* in heauen? and beſides thee what would I vpon the earth?
\v 26 My \f x \fr 72.26 \ft In bodie and mind I thirſt after thee, my true inheritance.\f* flesh hath fainted, and my hart: God of my hart, and God my portion for euer.
\v 27 For behold, they \f y \fr 72.27 \ft Men by their freewil make themſelues enimies to God.\f* that make them ſelues farre from thee, shal perish: thou \f z \fr 72.27 \ft determined their damnation, forſeing their final ſinnes.\f* haſt deſtroyed al, that fornicate from thee.
\v 28 But it is good for me to cleaue to God: to put my hope in our Lord God: That I may shew forth al thy prayſes, in the gates of the daughter of Sion.
\c 73
\cd Faithful people preſſed with perſecution lamentably complayning, beſecheth God to reſpect his owne inheritance, cruelly afflicted; 10. and leift long without helpe, 12. wheras heretofore he releeued his people in like diſtreſſes. 18. And therfore confidently hopeth he wil renenge the blaſphemers of his name, \f - \ft A prayer in long affliction. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnderſtanding \f a \fr 73.1 \ft Inſtruction for the congregation of the faithful in diſtreſſe, not to ſeeke temporal rewardes and proſperitie, but to expect ſpiritual and eternal. S. Aug.\f* to Aſaph. WHy haſt thou ô God \f b \fr 73.1 \ft In long perſecution the weake beginne to feare, or ſuſpect, that God hath vtterly abandoned them.\f* repelled for euer: is thy furie wrath vpon the sheepe of thy paſture?
\v 2 Be \f c \fr 73.2 \ft But the ſtronger pray with confidence,\f* mindful of thy congregation, \f d \fr 73.2 \ft being aſſured that God who hath conſerued his Church hitherto, from the beginning of the world, wil conſerue it ſtil to the end.\f* which thou haſt poſſeſſed from the beginning. Thou haſt \f e \fr 73.2 \ft An other reaſon why God wil conſerue his Church is, becauſe he hath deliuered it often in a ſtrong armie; as from bondage in Ægypt, from perſecution and oppreſſion in the time of Iudges, and kings, and laſtly redemed it from the tyrannie of the diuel by Chriſts death.\f* redemed the rod of thine inheritance: mount Sion, in which thou haſt dwelt.
\v 3 Lift vp thy handes vpon \f f \fr 73.3 \ft Thirdly the pride of the enimies moueth God to reuenge their wickednes, and ſo to deliuer his owne people.\f* their prides for euer: how great things hath the enimie done malignantly in the holy place:
\v 4 And they that hate thee haue gloried: in the middes of \f g \fr 73.4 \ft They pretend to kepe ſolemne feaſtes like to thyne.\f* thy ſolemnitie. They haue ſette \f h \fr 73.4 \ft They ſet vp altares againſt thyn altares, or ſome new deuiſed external shew, againſt the holie Rites of the Church :\f* their ſignes, for ſignes:
\v 5 and haue not knowne, \f i \fr 73.5 \ft and that in publike places, in the endes of high wayes, or portes, and pinnacles.\f* as in the iſſue on high. As \f k \fr 73.5 \ft The prophet deſcribeth the maner how perſecutors deſtroy temples, and al ſacred thinges.\f* in a wood of trees they haue with axes
\v 6 cut out the gates therof together: in hatchet, and chippeaxe they haue caſt it downe.
\v 7 They haue burnt thy ſanctuarie with fire: they haue polluted the tabernacle of thy name in the earth.
\v 8 Their \f l \fr 73.8 \ft The whole crew, or band of perſecutors.\f* kinred together haue ſaide in their hart: Let vs make al the feſtiual daies of God to ceaſe from the earth.
\v 9 Our \f m \fr 73.9 \ft The weake complaine that God ſheweth not wonted ſignes, nor ſendeth ſuccourſe to his people, by raiſing ſome prophet, or other meanes to helpe them.\f* ſignes we haue not ſeene, there is now no prophet: and he wil know vs no more.
\v 10 How long ô God shal the enimie vpbraide: the aduerſarie prouoke thy name for euer?
\v 11 Why doeſt thou turne away thy hand, and thy right hand, out of the middes of thy boſome for euer?
\v 12 But \f n \fr 73.12 \ft The perfect do anſwer, that God both hath, and wil releeue his people.\f* God our king before the worldes: he hath wrought ſaluation in the middes of the earth. \x - \xt Luc. 1. v. 70.\x*
\v 13 Thou in thy ſtrength haſt confirmed \f o \fr 73.13 \ft Made the redde ſea like walles.\f* the ſea: thou haſt \f p \fr 73.13 \ft drowned Pharao and his hoſte.\f* crushed the head of Dragons in the waters.
\v 14 Thou haſt broken the heads of the dragon: thou haſt giuen him for meate to the peoples \f q \fr 73.14 \ft of blacke diuels.\f* of the Æthiopians.
\v 15 Thou haſt \f r \fr 73.15 \ft geuen water out of rockes,\f* broken vp fountaines, and torrentes: thou haſt dried the riuers \f s \fr 73.15 \ft made paſſage ouer Iordan.\f* of Ethan.
\v 16 The day is thine, and the night is thine: thou haſt made the morning and the ſunne.
\v 17 Thou haſt made al the coaſtes of the earth: the ſummer and the ſpring thou haſt formed them.
\v 18 Be mindeful of this, the enimie hath vpbraided our Lord: and a foolish people hath prouoked thy name.
\v 19 Deliuer not to beaſts the ſoules that confeſſe to thee: and the ſoules of thy poore forget not for euer.
\v 20 Haue reſpect vnto thy teſtament: becauſe they that are \f t \fr 73.20 \ft Ignorant, blind, and barbarous infidels, lodge al kindes of iniquitie in their conſciences.\f* obſcure of the earth, are filled with houſes of iniquities.
\v 21 \f v \fr 73.21 \ft And therfore it is vnmeete, that faithful people should be in bondage vnder them.\f* Let not the humble be turned away being confounded: the poore and needy shal praiſe thy name.
\v 22 Ariſe God, iudge thy cauſe: be mindful of thoſe thy reproches, that are \f w \fr 73.22 \ft Theſe foolish audacious men do continually blaſpheme al holie thinges.\f* from the foolish man al the day.
\v 23 \f x \fr 73.23 \ft Omitte not to punish them,\f* Forget not the voices of thine enimies: the \f y \fr 73.23 \ft ſeing they are obſtinate and obdurate in pride, and in hatred.\f* pride of them that hate thee, hath aſcended alwaies.
\c 74
\cd Chriſt (with his Aſſeſſors) wil iudge the whole world, at the laſt day: in the meane time exhorteth ſinners to amend their life; 7. for none shal eſcape iuſt iudgement. 11. The wicked shal be punished, and the good rewarded. \f - \ft General iudgement. The 9. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, \f a \fr 74.1 \ft Either this was the beginning of a ſongue, to the tune wherof this Pſalme was ſong; as ſome Hebrew Rabbins teſtifie; or, as S. Auguſtin and other fathers explicate, the Septuagint do admonish vs, by occaſion of this Pſalme, not to faile in our hope, for that God wil in no caſe violate his promiſe nor purpoſe, but wil render to euerie one in the day of Iudgement, as they deſerue.\f* Corrupt not, a Pſalme of Canticle to Aſaph.
\v 2 We \f b \fr 74.2 \ft Chriſt with his Apoſtles, and other aſſeſſorie iudges, wil praiſe and thanke God, for his admirable good prouidence, and gouernement of this world.\f* wil confeſſe to thee ô God: \f c \fr 74.2 \ft This duplication of the ſame word, confirmeth vs of the aſſured performance of that, which is here prophecied.\f* we wil confeſſe, and wil inuocate thy name. We wil tel thy meruelouſe workes:
\v 3 when \f d \fr 74.3 \ft Chriſt our ſingular, principal, and proper Iudge only, and no mere creature, knoweth the determinate time of general iudgement.\f* I shal take a time, I wil iudge iuſtices.
\v 4 \f e \fr 74.4 \ft The earth at that time ſhal be purged with flaming fire :\f* The earth is melted, and al that dwel in it: \f f \fr 74.4 \ft yet shal not be deſtroyed, but changed in qualities.\f* I haue confirmed the pillers thereof.
\v 5 I ſaid to the wicked: \f g \fr 74.5 \ft An epitome, or briefe ſumme of Chriſts doctrine, to flee from ſinne.\f* doe not wickedly: and to them that offend: \f h \fr 74.5 \ft be not proud, abuſe not the powre you haue, to do what you pleaſe.\f* Exalt not the horne.
\v 6 Exalt not your horne on high: ſpeake not iniquitie aganſt God.
\v 7 For neither from the Eaſt, nor from the Weſt, nor from the deſert mountanes:
\v 8 \f i \fr 74.8 \ft VVhen other Iudges ſitte on tribunales, ſome may be abſent, or eſcape from their ſentences : but al without exception ſhal be brought in perſons, when Chriſt God and Man ſhal iudge.\f* becauſe God is Iudge. \f k \fr 74.8 \ft Some ſhal haue ſentence of eternal paine in hel; ſome of eternal reward in glorie,\f* This man he humbleth, and him he exalteth,
\v 9 \f l \fr 74.9 \ft though (partly in this world, partly in particular iudgement of euerie ſoule) God hath alreadie, and wil before the laſt day puniſh ſinners, yet there reſteth more puniſhment, and no impenitent ſinners ſhal eſcape, but al ſhal drinke of the bitter cuppe of eternal damnation.\f* becauſe there is a cuppe in the hand of our Lord \f m \fr 74.9 \ft ſtrong wine not delayde with water, but mingled with bitter ſharpe thinges, as gal, vinegre, brimſtone &c. and ſo powred into feſtered woundes, ſo they shal ſuffer vntolerable endles paine, mixed with the worme of conſcience, loſſe of glorie, rancor of hart, and eternal deſperation.\f* of mere wine ful of mixture. And he hath powred it out \f n \fr 74.9 \ft Varietie of hellish torments, from one extreme to another : as from ſnowwaters to exceeding heate. Iob. 24.\f* of this into that, but yet the dregges therof are not emptied: al the ſinners of the earth shal drinke.
\v 10 But I wil shewforth for euer: I wil ſing to the God of Iacob.
\v 11 And I wil breake al the hornes of ſinners: and \f o \fr 74.11 \ft The iuſt for wel vſing their powre of freewil, and of al powre geuen them in this life, are rewarded in heauen.\f* the hornes of the iuſt shal be exalted.
\c 75
\cd The royal prophet ſingeth Gods praiſes, for his particular prouidence towards the Iewes: 10. further to be extended to al the meeke of the whole earth. \f - \ft God's providence towards his people. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, in prayſes, a Pſalme \f a \fr 75.1 \ft For the congregation of faithful and godlie people.\f* to Aſaph, a Canticle to \f b \fr 75.1 \ft Amongſt others, one notorious example of Gods prouidence is recorded of an hundred foureſcore & fiue thouſand Aſſirians, ſlaine in one night by an Angel. 4.Reg.19.\f* the Aſſirians.
\v 2 God is knowne \f c \fr 75.2 \ft God was not only knowen in general, as to pagane Philoſophers, and ſome others, but more particularly to the Iewes, the iſſue of Abraham, Iſaac, and Iacob, by his ſpecial benefites towards them.\f* in Iewrie: in Iſrael his name is great.
\v 3 And \f d \fr 75.3 \ft God ſuffering al other nations to erre for their ſinnes, in their fond phanteſies of falſe goddes, reſerued the Iſraelites for his Church,\f* his place is made in peace: and \f e \fr 75.3 \ft eſtablishing the principalitie therof in Sion.\f* his habitation in Sion.
\v 4 \f f \fr 75.4 \ft For obtaining and conſeruing of Sion from wicked Infidels, God ouerthrewe al ſortes of contrarie forces.\f* There he brake the powres of bowes, the shilde, the ſword, and the battle.
\v 5 Thou \f g \fr 75.5 \ft God not only gaue his people temporal victories ouer their enimies, but alſo illuminated their mindes with knowlege of true religion:\f* doeſt illuminate meruelouſely from the eternal mountaynes:
\v 6 al the foolish of hart \f h \fr 75.6 \ft others are often trubled in mind, hearing the truth, but are not conuerted through their obſtinate follie.\f* were trubled. They \f i \fr 75.6 \ft VVorldlie men in ſupine careleſnes as in a ſleepe, paſſe ouer this life, and afterwards find themſelues excluded from heauen, for lacke of merites, and good workes, with the foolish virgins.\f* ſlept their ſleepe: and al the men of riches found nothing in their handes.
\v 7 At \f k \fr 75.7 \ft God vndertaking the defence of his people, and threatning the aduerſe part,\f* thy reprehenſion ô God of Iacob, they \f l \fr 75.7 \ft they failed in courage, as men ouercome with drowſines of ſleepe.\f* haue al ſlumbered that mounted on horſes.
\v 8 Thou art terrible, and who shal reſiſt thee? \f m \fr 75.8 \ft Euen from the firſt notice of thy wil, the aduerſaries were deiected, fearing thy potent wrath.\f* from that time thy wrath.
\v 9 From \f n \fr 75.9 \ft Terrible ſignes from the firmament appearing before the day of iudgement,\f* heauen thou \f 1 \fr 75.9 \ft The Prophetes do often ſpeake in the preter-tence, for the aſſurance of the thinges to come.\f* haſt made thy iudgement hearde: \f o \fr 75.9 \ft perſecutors and others being terrified ſhal be aſtonied and ſilent.\f* the earth trembled and was quiet.
\v 10 When God aroſe vnto iudgement, \f p \fr 75.10 \ft God wil come to iudge the vvorld, more eſpecially for the iuſts ſake.\f* that he might ſaue al the meeke of the earth.
\v 11 Becauſe \f q \fr 75.11 \ft Men that ſhal ſeriouſly thinke and meditate vpon theſe thinges, vvil praiſe and thanke God for them:\f* the cogitation of man shal confeſſe to thee: and \f r \fr 75.11 \ft and the effect, and ſweete repaſt of ſuch meditation, shal make as it vvere a great feſtiual day, in the deuout ſoule ſpiritually ioyned with God.\f* the remanes of the cogitation shal keepe feſtiual day to thee.
\v 12 \f s \fr 75.12 \ft The ſoule thus inflamed with Gods loue, is then apt of gratitude, for his goodnes tovvardes man, to make vowes of thinges wherto vve are not obliged,\f* Vowe ye, and \f t \fr 75.12 \ft but moſt neceſſarie it is to be maturely aduiſed, and not raſhly, nor lightly to vow; for being once vovved, vve are ſtrictly bound to whatſoeuer vve haue lavvfully promiſed. And it is great ſinne to vow vndiſcretly.\f* render to our Lord your God: al ye that round about him bring \f v \fr 75.12 \ft Remembring that for vovves, and al other workes we muſt anſwer to God, who is a terrible iudge, readie to punish in bodie and ſoule, ſparing none for their greatnes, not princes, nor kinges, nor vulgar ſorte, for al are to him alike.\f* giftes.
\v 13 To vthe terrible, and him that taketh away the ſpirite of princes, terrible to the kinges of the earth.
\c 76
\cd Anie faithful deuout perſon meditating Gods benefites, 7. examineth his cõſcience, that nothing be in his ſoule, that may offend God. Who is alwayes readie to forgeue: 11. and therfore he ſtil reneweth his purpoſe to ſerue God ſincerly, 15. particularly remembring the deliuerie of Iſrael from Ægypt. \f - \ft Gods ſpecial protection of the Iewes. the 4. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, for \f a \fr 76.1 \ft For Idithun to ſing, or to make tune for it.\f* Idithun, a pſalme \f b \fr 76.1 \ft For the faithful congregation to conſider Gods benefites.\f* to Aſaph.
\v 2 With \f c \fr 76.2 \ft Hauing heretofore prayed,\f* my voice I haue cried to our Lord: with my voice to God \f d \fr 76.2 \ft I haue obtayned.\f* and he attended to me. \x - \xt Pſal. 141.\x*
\v 3 In the day of my tribulation I ſought God, with my handes \f e \fr 76.3 \ft Eſpecially being in tribulation, and praying vvith hart and handes lifted vp, as vvel in the night as day:\f* in the night before him: and I \f f \fr 76.3 \ft I was not fruſtrate of my prayer.\f* was not deceiued. My \f g \fr 76.3 \ft I was ſometimes in ſuch anguish that nothing ſemed comfortable,\f* ſoule refuſed to be comforted,
\v 4 I \f h \fr 76.4 \ft but I relied vpon God ſo firmly,\f* was mindeful of God, and was delighted, and was exerciſed: and \f i \fr 76.4 \ft that my ſpirite came into an extaſie, or traunce.\f* my ſpirite fainted.
\v 5 Myne eies \f k \fr 76.5 \ft I aroſe early before the ordinarie time of avvayking,\f* preuented the watches: I was \f l \fr 76.5 \ft my hart being attentiue inwardly, I vttered nothing vvith my tongue.\f* trubled, & ſpake not.
\v 6 I thought vpon old daies: and the eternal yeares I had in minde.
\v 7 And I meditated in the night with my hart, and I was exerciſed, and I \f m \fr 76.7 \ft I diligently examined my conſcience.\f* ſwept my ſpirite.
\v 8 Why, \f n \fr 76.8 \ft Aſſuredly God wil not reiect for euer, but he wil be pleaſed with his Church.\f* wil God reiect for euer: or wil he not adde to be better pleaſed as yet?
\v 9 Or wil he cutte of his mercie for euer, from generation vnto generation?
\v 10 Or wil God forget to haue mercie? or wil he in his wrath keepe in his mercies?
\v 11 And I ſayde: \f o \fr 76.11 \ft VVhiles I thus thought, I erred greatly, novv I ſee and confeſſe that God ſuffereth al calamities for the good of his ſeruantes:\f* Now haue I begune: \f p \fr 76.11 \ft and this I knew not by my ſelfe, but by the inſpiration of God, making this change in me by his gracious hand.\f* this is the change of the right hand of the Higheſt.
\v 12 I haue bene mindful of the workes of our Lord: becauſe I wil be mindful, from the beginning of thy meruelous workes.
\v 13 And I wil meditate in al thy workes: and in thy inuentions I wil be exerciſed.
\v 14 O God in the holie is thy way: what God is great as our God?
\v 15 thou art the God that doeſt meruelous thinges. Thou haſt made thy powre knowne among peoples:
\v 16 thou haſt with thine arme redemed thy people, the children of \f q \fr 76.16 \ft The progenie of Iacob, receiued and nourished in Ægypt for Ioſephs ſake, as his adopted children.\f* Iacob, and Ioſeph.
\v 17 The waters \f r \fr 76.17 \ft The read ſea, and Iordan felt thy diuine powre, and obeyed thy wil.\f* ſaw thee ô God, the waters ſaw thee: and they were afrayd, and the depthes were trubled.
\v 18 A \f s \fr 76.18 \ft Noiſe of vvaters meeting after the Iſraelites vvere paſſed, thunders and lightninges alſo hapened, to the terror of the perſecutors, though not mentioned in Exodus.\f* multitude of the ſounding of waters: the cloudes gaue a voice. For in deede arrowes doe paſſe:
\v 19 the voice of thy thunder in a wheele. Thy lightenings shined to the rounde world: the earth was moued and trubled.
\v 20 Thy way in the ſea, and thy pathes in many waters: and thy ſteppes shal not be knowne. \x - \xt Exo. 14.\x*
\v 21 Thou haſt conducted thy people as sheepe: \f t \fr 76.21 \ft Exo. 14 · By the miniſterie of Moyſes and Aaron.\f* in the hand of moyſes and Aaron.
\c 77
\cd The royal prophet exhorting the people to attend, 5. reciteth manie great benefites of God towards their forefathers (whoſe ingratitude, often rebellion, and chatiſement he ſtil noteth) 9. 11 in their entrance into the land of Chanaan: 12. alſo before the ſame in Ægypt, and in the deſert. 42. How God plagued the Ægyptians: 52. protected, and conducted his people into the promiſed land. 56. where likewise they often offended, were punished: 65. yet were ſtil conſerued: 69. and the tribe of Iuda exalted in king Dauid. \f - \ft God'ſ great Benefits beſtowed upon the Jews, and their ingratitude. The 4. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnderſtanding \f a \fr 77.1 \ft Commended to Aſaph a chiefe muſitian, that the people might vnderſtand and conſider Gods benefites.\f* to Aſaph. MY people attend ye to \f b \fr 77.1 \ft Neither the law, nor the people vvas Dauids, but preſenting Gods perſon, he ſpeaketh in his name or authoritie, with whoſe inſpiration he was repleniſhed. S. Greg. Præfat. in Iob. c. 2.\f* my law: incline your eare vnto the wordes of my mouth.
\v 2 I wil open my mouth in \f c \fr 77.2 \ft Albeit the prophet reciteth hiſtorically thinges donne, yet the ſame were parables, ſimilitudes, and figures of other thinges:\f* parables: I wil ſpeake \f d \fr 77.2 \ft yea of ſecret hidden Myſteries, obſcurly ſignified in the old Teſtament, and reueled in the new.\f* propoſitions from the beginning.
\v 3 How great thinges haue we heard and \f e \fr 77.3 \ft Which partly we know by written holie Sriptures:\f* haue knowne them, and \f f \fr 77.3 \ft partly by Traditions.\f* our fathers haue told vs.
\v 4 They were not hid from their children, in an other generation. Telling the prayſes of our Lord, and his powers, and his meruelous workes which he hath done.
\v 5 And he \f g \fr 77.5 \ft God of his mercie without precedent merite, raiſed vp a peculiar people of Abraham, Iſaac, and Iacob,\f* raiſed vp a teſtimonie in Iacob: and \f h \fr 77.5 \ft and gaue them a particular law, firſt of Circunciſion, & more largely by Moyſes.\f* made a law in Iſrael. How great thinges he commanded our fathers, \f i \fr 77.5 \ft So Abraham inſtructed his children and his houſe after him, Gen. 18.\f* to make
\v 6 the ſame knowne to their children: that \f k \fr 77.6 \ft in like ſorte others taught their children.\f* an other generation may know. The children that shal be borne, and shal riſe vp, and shal tel their children.
\v 7 That they may \f l \fr 77.7 \ft For three cauſes God gaue his law, that his people may haue confidence in him, he shewing his care to inſtruct and gouerne them;\f* put their hope in God, and may \f m \fr 77.7 \ft that they remember his benefites;\f* not forget the workes of God: and may \f n \fr 77.7 \ft and kepe his commandmentes.\f* ſeeke after his commandmentes.
\v 8 That they become not as their fathers: \f o \fr 77.8 \ft The Iewish nation very often, and in great numbers murmured, rebelled, and committed other great ſinnes, and therfore Dauid exhorted the people of his time, not to do the like. And this exhortation perteyneth more eſpecially to Chriſtianes. as S. Paul teacheth. 1. Cor. 10.\f* a peruerſe generation and exaſperating. A generation, that hath not directed their hart, their ſpirit hath not bene faithful towards God. \x - \xt 1. Par. 7. v. 21.\x*
\v 9 The \f p \fr 77.9 \ft They firſt (truſting in their owne ſtrength) without Gods commandment (Num. 14.) went forth to batle and were ouerthrowne. \+it 1. Par 7. v. 11\+it*.\f* children of Ephrem bending, and shooting with bow: were turned in the day of battel.
\v 10 They kept not the teſtament of God: and in his law they would not walke.
\v 11 And they forgate his benefites, and his meruelous workes, which he shewed them.
\v 12 Before their fathers he did meruelous thinges in the land of Ægypt, in the filde \f q \fr 77.12 \ft Tanis the principal citie in Ægypt nere the riuer Nilus, where Moyſes wrought his great miracles.\f* of Tanis.
\v 13 He diuided the ſea & brought them through: and he made the waters to ſtand as in a bottle. \x - \xt Exo. 14.\x*
\v 14 And he \f r \fr 77.14 \ft This cloude shadowed them from the heate of the ſunne in the day, and the fire ſhined in the night, al the time that they were in the deſert.\f* conducted them in a cloude by day: and al the night by light of fire.
\v 15 He ſtroke the rocke in \f s \fr 77.15 \ft In mount Horeb: and there was continual water in al the campe, which occupied nere foure miles in length and breadth.\f* the deſert: and gaue them water to drinke as in a great depth.
\v 16 And he brought forth water out of the rocke: and made waters runne downe as riuers.
\v 17 And they added as yet to ſinne vnto him: they prouoked the Higheſt to wrath in the place \f t \fr 77.17 \ft Which naturally wanted water: but by miracle had abundance.\f* without water.
\v 18 And they tempted God in their hartes: ſo that they asked \f v \fr 77.18 \ft Not content with Manna, they demanded to haue flesh.\f* meats for their liues.
\v 19 And they ſpake euil of God: they ſaide: Can God prepare a table in the deſert?
\v 20 Becauſe he ſtroke the rocke and waters ranne, & torrentes flowed: \f w \fr 77.20 \ft Stil incredulous, not beleuing Gods omnipotencie, they thought that, albeit he had geuen them manna, and water, yet he could not geue them fleſh.\f* Can he alſo giue \f x \fr 77.20 \ft By bread in general is vnderſtood al competent meate vſual for a table.\f* bread, or prepare a table for his people?
\v 21 Therfore our Lord heard, and \f y \fr 77.21 \ft For this incredulitie, murmuring, and other ſinnes God kept the children of Iſrael fourtie yares in the deſert, til al that were of age, when they came from Ægypt, were dead, except only Ioſue and Caleb.\f* made delay: and \f z \fr 77.21 \ft In the meane time amongſt other puniſhments, manie murmurers were burnt to death with ſtrange fire. Num. 11.\f* fire was kindled in Iacob, and wrath aſcended vpon Iſrael.
\v 22 Becauſe they beleued not in God, nor hoped in his ſaluation.
\v 23 And he commanded the cloudes from aboue, and opened the gates of heauen.
\v 24 And he rayned them Manna to eate, and bread of heauen he gaue to them.
\v 25 Bread \f a \fr 77.25 \ft Manna made by Angels.\f* of Angels did man eate: he ſent them victuals in abundance.
\v 26 He \f b \fr 77.26 \ft God ſo changed the wind, that it brought abundance of quailes and other birdes, into their campe. \+it Exo. 16. Nu. 11\+it*.\f* tranſported the Southwinde from heauen: and in his powre he brought in the Southweſt winde.
\v 27 And he rayned vpon them flesh as duſt: and as the ſand of the ſea fethered fowles.
\v 28 And they fel in the middes of their campe: about their tabernacles.
\v 29 And they did eate and were filled excedingly, and their deſire he brought to them:
\v 30 They were not defrauded of their deſire. \f c \fr 77.30 \ft Immediatly after a moneth (for ſo long they had abundance of theſe birdes ibid. v. 20,) they were ſtriken with a plague, and manie died, for their concupiſcence.\f* As yet their meats were in their mouth:
\v 31 And the wrath of God aſcended vpon them. And he killed their fat ones, and \f d \fr 77.31 \ft The moſt fresh ſtrong men died, and ſo were hindered from poſſeſſing the promiſed land of Chanaan.\f* the choſen of Iſrael he hindered.
\v 32 In al theſe things they ſinned as yet: and they beleued not in his meruelous workes.
\v 33 And their daies failed in vanitie: and their years \f e \fr 77.33 \ft In fourtie yeares aboue ſix hundred thouſand died.\f* in haſt.
\v 34 When he ſlew them, they ſought him: and they returned, and \f f \fr 77.34 \ft They offered morning ſacrifice.\f* early they came to him.
\v 35 And they remembred that God is their helper: and the high God is their redemer.
\v 36 And they loued him \f g \fr 77.36 \ft But were not ſincere in their hartes.\f* with their mouth, and with their tongue they did lie to him.
\v 37 But their hart was not right with him: neither were they counted faithful in his teſtament.
\v 38 \f h \fr 77.38 \ft Howſoeuer multitudes of people committe great ſinnes, and are ſeuerely puniſhed, yet Gods mercie preſerueth ſome by his effectual grace, and neuer ſuffereth the whole Church to faile, nor to be deſtroyed.\f* But he is merciful, and wil be propicious to their ſinnes: and he wil not deſtroy them. And he abunded to turne away his wrath: and he kindled not al his wrath.
\v 39 And he remembred that they are flesh: \f i \fr 77.39 \ft Mans life is like the winde, that ſtil paſſeth, and the ſame returneth not. As Ariſtotel teacheth. Here the Hebrewes note the middes of the Pſalter, in 1263. verſes. and ſo manie in the reſt.\f* ſpirit going, and not returning.
\v 40 How \f k \fr 77.40 \ft The people of Iſrael murmured ſo often in the deſert, that it was not eaſie to tel how often. See the Annotation.\f* often haue they exaſperated him in the deſert: prouoked him to wrath in the place without water.
\v 41 And \f l \fr 77.41 \ft For efſoones repenting they offended God againe and againe.\f* they returned, and tempted God: and the holie one of Iſrael they exaſperaed.
\v 42 They did not remember his hand: in the day that he redemed them from the hand of the afflicter.
\v 43 As he put \f m \fr 77.43 \ft The firſt ſigne was in turning a rodde into a ſerpent, which was a miracle, but no plague, the other ſignes were alſo plagnes to the Ægyptians.\f* his ſignes in Ægypt, and his wonders in the filde of Tanis.
\v 44 And he turned \f n \fr 77.44 \ft The firſt plague.\f* their riuers into bloude, & their \f o \fr 77.44 \ft Pooles, lakes, and al ſortes of water, yea showers, or raine water, which ſeldome happeneth in Ægypt.\f* showers that they might not drinke.
\v 45 He ſent vpon them \f p \fr 77.45 \ft The fourth plague, in order as they are recited in Exodus.\f* a ſwarme of flies, and it eate them: and \f q \fr 77.45 \ft The ſecond plague.\f* the frogge, and it deſtroyed them. \f 1 \fr 77.45 \ft \+it * Coenomyiam.\+it*\f*
\v 46 And he gaue their fruites to \f r \fr 77.46 \ft This was ∷ leſſe plague, not mentioned in with the greater.\f* the blaſt, and their labors to \f s \fr 77.46 \ft The eight plague.\f* the locuſte.
\v 47 And he killed their vineyeardes with \f t \fr 77.47 \ft The ſeuenth plague.\f* haile: and their mulberie trees with \f v \fr 77.47 \ft This alſo is omitted in. · Exodus.\f* horefroſt.
\v 48 And he deliuered \f w \fr 77.48 \ft Not only al trees, and plantes, but alſo beaſtes were ſubiect to the haile.\f* their beaſt to haile: and their poſſeſſion \f x \fr 77.48 \ft and to firie leghtnings.\f* to fire.
\v 49 He ſent vpon them \f y \fr 77.49 \ft In theſe general termes, of wrath, indignation, and tribulation, the Prophet comprehendeth al the other plagues, to witte, the third of ſcinies, the fifth of peſtilence, the ſixt of boyles in men and beaſtes, the ninth of darknes three dayes together.\f* the wrath of his indignation: indignation, & wrath, and tribulation: immiſſions \f z \fr 77.49 \ft Al which God ſent by the miniſterie of diuels, euil angels.\f* by euil angels.
\v 50 He made a way to the path of his wrath, he ſpared not their liues from death: and their cattel he shut vp in death.
\v 51 And \f a \fr 77.51 \ft a The tenth and greateſt plague, \+it 11. v. 5. & c. 12. v. 29.\+it*\f* he ſtroke al the firſtborne in the land of Ægypt: the firſt fruites of al their labors in the tabernacles \f b \fr 77.51 \ft Egyptians alſo deſcended from cham, by his ſonne Meſraim. \+it Gen. 10. v. 6.\+it*\f* of Cham.
\v 52 And he \f c \fr 77.52 \ft After that Ægypt was thus plagued, God brought Iſrael out of their ſeruitude, as a shepheard leddeth his sheepe, and defendeth them.\f* tooke away his people as sheepe: and led them as a flock in the deſert.
\v 53 And he brought them forth in hope, and they feared not: and the ſea couered their enemies.
\v 54 And he brought them into \f d \fr 77.54 \ft Iudea a hillie countrie.\f* the mount of his \f e \fr 77.54 \ft Into that countrie which God choſe, and endewed with manie bleſſinges.\f* ſanctification, the mount, which his right hand purchaſed. And he caſt \f f \fr 77.54 \ft As is written in Ioſue.\f* out the gentiles from their face: and by lot he diuided the land of them in a corde of diſtribution:
\v 55 And he made the tribes of Iſrael to dwel in their tabernacles.
\v 56 And \f g \fr 77.56 \ft After the conqueſt and quiet poſſeſſion, the Iſraelites often fel into groſſe ſinnes, eſpecially in the time of Iudges.\f* they tempted, and exaſperated God the higheſt, and they kept not his teſtimonies.
\v 57 And they turned away themſelues, & kept not the couenant: euen as their fathers, they were turned as a \f h \fr 77.57 \ft A croked bow deceiueth the archer, ſo this people failed to ſerue God, and deceiued themſelues.\f* crooked bow.
\v 58 They incenſed him to wrath in their \f i \fr 77.58 \ft In their altares erected in hilles to Idoles.\f* hilles: and in their \f 1 \fr 77.58 \ft \+it * Sculptilibus.\+it*\f* grauens they prouoked him \f k \fr 77.58 \ft By grauen imagies of Idoles, they prouoked God to indignation.\f* to emulation.
\v 59 God heard, and contemned: and he brought Iſrael to nothing \f l \fr 77.59 \ft Not abſolutely to nothing, but punished them exceedingly, til they repented, and then ſpared and deliuered them from tribulation, as appeareth in the booke of Iudges.\f* excedingly.
\v 60 And he reiected the tabernacle \f m \fr 77.60 \ft The Arke of God ſometime kept in Silo, Ioſue \+it 18.\+it* in the tribe of Ephraim, was taken by the Philiſtims. 1. Keg. 4. and neuer returned thither agane.\f* of Silo, his tabernacle, \f n \fr 77.60 \ft but wherſoeuer the Arke was, there God more eſpecially heard their petitions, and gaue anſwers.\f* where he dwelt among men.
\v 61 And he deliuered \f o \fr 77.61 \ft For their ſinnes God ſuffered the Arke to be taken.\f* their force into captiuitie: and their beautie into the hands of the enemie.
\v 62 And he \f p \fr 77.62 \ft And the Iſraelites to be ſore afflicted by their enimies.\f* shut vp his people in the ſword: and he diſpiſed his inheritance.
\v 63 \f q \fr 77.63 \ft The zele, and iuſt wrath of God ſuffered theſe calamities to happen.\f* Fyre deuoured their young men: and their virgins were not lamented.
\v 64 Their \f r \fr 77.64 \ft Ophni and Phinees the ſonnes of Heli ſlaine and Heli himſelfe hearing that the Arke was taken fel from his ſtoole and broke his neck. \+it 1. Reg 4\+it*.\f* Prieſtes fel by the ſworde: and their widowes were not wept for.
\v 65 And \f s \fr 77.65 \ft Neuertheles God plagued the infidels, and conſerued his Church. \+it 1. Reg. 5\+it*.\f* our Lord was raiſed vp as one that ſleepeth: as a mightie man hauing ſurfited of wine.
\v 66 And he ſtroke his enimies on the hinder partes: an euerlaſting reproch he gaue to them.
\v 67 And he reiected the tabernacle \f t \fr 77.67 \ft As before. \+it v. 60\+it*.\f* of Ioſeph: and the tribe of Ephraim he choſe not.
\v 68 But he choſe the tribe \f v \fr 77.68 \ft After a time the Arke was brought into the tribe of Iuda.\f* of Iuda, mount Sion which he loued.
\v 69 And he built \f w \fr 77.69 \ft The Church was firme, and euer preſerued in the old teſtament til Chriſt, and from Chriſts time to the end of the world.\f* his ſanctuarie as of vnicornes in the land, which he hath founded for euer.
\v 70 And \f x \fr 77.70 \ft Gods particular grace in chooſing, and exalting Dauid, was a ſpecial benefite to the Iſraelites.\f* he choſe Dauid his ſeruant, and tooke him from the flockes of sheepe: from after the ewes with yong he tooke him.
\v 71 To \f y \fr 77.71 \ft To rule and gouerne the people of Iſrael.\f* feede Iacob his ſeruant, and Iſrael his inheritance.
\v 72 And he fedde them in the innocencie of his hart: and in the vnderſtandings of \f z \fr 77.72 \ft Prudently vſing his powre and authoritie.\f* his hands he conducted them.
\c 78
\cd The Prophet, in perſon of the Church, lamenteth the crueltie of perſecutors (both in the old and new teſtament) 5. prayeth for releaſe, with iuſt reuenge againſt Gods enimies, that blaſpheme his name, 3. and promiſeth to be gratful in diuine praiſes. \f - \ft The Church ſuffereth very great perſecutions. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Pſalme \f a \fr 78.1 \ft to be ſungue by Aſaph and his companie, in the behalfe of people vnited in faith.\f* to Aſaph. O God \f b \fr 78.1 \ft Cruel infidels haue inuaded thoſe thinges, which perteyne to thy Church:\f* the Gentiles are come into thine inheritance, they \f c \fr 78.1 \ft euen poſſeſſed and prophaned the holie temple of the Iewes, and Churches of Catholique Chriſtians. Fulfilled by Antiochus, in Ieruſalem; by the Vandals in Afrike, by Proteſtants, and Puritanes, in diuers partes of Europe, and wil be more vniuerſally by Antichriſt in al Chriſtendom.\f* haue polluted thy holie temple: they haue made Ieruſalem \f d \fr 78.1 \ft In ſteed of great and faire Churches, Gods ſeruantes are fane to vſe meane houſes, yea poore cotages.\f* as a watch toure of fruits.
\v 2 They haue made \f e \fr 78.2 \ft Some perſecutors ſuffer not Martyrs bodies to be buried, but hang them on polles, and pinnacles, where birdes may eate them.\f* the carcaſes of thy ſeruants, meats for the foules of the aire: the flesh of thy ſainctes for the beaſtes of the land.
\v 3 They haue poured out their bloude as water round about Ieruſalem: and there was none to burie them.
\v 4 We are \f f \fr 78.4 \ft Chriſtianes Gallileans, Catholiques Homouſians, and now Papiſtes, in ſpite and reproch.\f* become a reproch to our neighboures: a ſcorne and mocke to them that are round about vs.
\v 5 How long ô Lord wilt thou \f g \fr 78.5 \ft Sinnes prouoking Gods wrath, are one cauſe why he ſuffereth his people to be perſecuted.\f* be angrie for euer: shal thy zele be kindled as a fire?
\v 6 Poure out thy wrath vpon the Gentiles, \f h \fr 78.6 \ft Though the faithful committe ſome ſinnes, yet in reſpect that they beleue righly, haue zele in Gods cauſe, and denie not God, they are nerer to grace and fauour.\f* that haue not knowne thee: & vpon the kingdomes, that haue not inuocated thy name.
\v 7 Becauſe they haue deuoured Iacob: and his place they haue made deſolate.
\v 8 \f i \fr 78.8 \ft Eſpecially when they repent, and confeſſe their ſinnes, then thoſe that neither know nor wil know God; but obſtinatly impugne and reſiſt the truth,\f* Remember not our old iniquities, let thy mercies quickly preuent vs: becauſe we are become exceding \f k \fr 78.8 \ft ſo much afflicted, and deſpiſed, that none wil regard the truth which we profeſſe.\f* poore.
\v 9 Help vs ô God our ſauiour: and for the glorie of thy name ô Lord deliuer vs: and be propicious to our ſinnes for thy names ſake.
\v 10 Leſt they ſay perhaps amongſt the Gentiles: \f l \fr 78.10 \ft Albeit ô Lord, thou ſuffer vs to be puniſhed, yet ſuffer not thyn enimies to inſult, as though thou were not our God; and conſequently thou haddeſt no people at al in the world.\f* where is their God? and \f m \fr 78.10 \ft Therfore we pray, that the reuenge of thy ſeruantes bloud may be ſo notified, that it can not be denied, nor doubted of. Which is alſo here propheſied, that it wil ſo come to paſſe in the end.\f* be notified in the nations before our eies. The reuenge of the bloud of thy ſeruants, which hath benne shed:
\v 11 let the groning of the fettered enter in thy ſight. According to the greatnes of thine arme, poſſeſſe thou \f n \fr 78.11 \ft Preſerue alſo, ô God, the reliques of thy ſeruantes, the ſucceſſors of thy Martyrs.\f* the children of them that are put to death.
\v 12 And \f o \fr 78.12 \ft This alſo is a propheſie, that God wil moſt ſeuerely reuenge the blaſphemie of perſecutors.\f* tender to our neighboures ſeuenfold in their boſome: their reproch wherewith they haue reproched thee ô Lord.
\v 13 But \f p \fr 78.13 \ft Gods people deliuered from perſecution, and perpetually conſerued, wil alwayes praiſe God for the ſame.\f* we thy people, and the sheepe of thy paſture, wil confeſſe to thee for euer: Vnto generation and generation we wil shew forth thy praiſe.
\c 79
\cd The Prophet prayeth for the releaſe of Iſrael in great tribulation. 15. forsheweth the coming of Chriſt to redeme man from ſinne, and from thraldome of the diuel. \f - \ft Chriſt our Redeemer from ſin and captivity. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 79.1 \ft Perteyning to the new Teſtament,\f* the end, for \f b \fr 79.1 \ft eſpecially to Gentiles, that shal be conuerted to Chriſt.\f* them, that shal be changed, teſtimonie to \f c \fr 79.1 \ft for perpetual memorie to the congregation of faithful.\f* Aſaph, a Pſalme.
\v 2 Thou that ruleſt Iſrael, attend: that \f d \fr 79.2 \ft By Ioſeph the prophet vnderſtandeth al Iſrael; becauſe the firſt birthright being taken from Ruben was geuen to him, to witte duble portion of enheritance, two tribes of twelue.\f* conducteſt Ioſeph as a sheepe. Which ſitteſt vpon the cherubs \f e \fr 79.2 \ft Appeare, and shew thy mightie hand before thy people:\f* be manifeſt \f f \fr 79.2 \ft wherof Ephraim, Beniamin, and Manaſſes folowing the Arke, when they marched or camped, ſhould moſt directly ſee what appeared therin, the other nine tribes being placed before it, and on both ſides. Num. 2.\f*
\v 3 before Ephraim, Beniamin, and Manaſses. Raiſe vp \f g \fr 79.3 \ft Thou which alwayes can helpe vs, now vſe thy powre in deliuering vs from this temporal miſerie.\f* thy might, and come, to ſaue vs.
\v 4 O God \f h \fr 79.4 \ft Set vs in a better ſtate.\f* conuert vs: and shew \f i \fr 79.4 \ft Shew thy benigne countenance and fauoure, Myſtically, ſend the promiſed Meſſias, Chriſt, the eſſential Image of God. 1. Cor. 4. v. 4. Collos. 1. v. 15.\f* thy face, & we shal be ſaued.
\v 5 O Lord the God of hoſtes, how long wilt thou be angrie \f k \fr 79.5 \ft How long wilt thou differre to heare our prayer?\f* vpon the prayer of thy ſeruant?
\v 6 Thou wilt feede vs with the bread of teares: and giue vs drinke with teares \f l \fr 79.6 \ft Thou doſt iuſtly punish vs, but thou tempereſt the ſame with meaſure, that it exceede not to our ruines.\f* in meſure.
\v 7 Thou haſt made vs to be a contradiction to our neighboures and our enimies haue ſcorned vs.
\v 8 O God of hoſts \f m \fr 79.8 \ft God firſt preuenting with his grace,\f* conuert vs: and shew thy face, and we \f n \fr 79.8 \ft man may cooperate therwith to his iuſtification, and ſaluation.\f* shal be ſaued.
\v 9 Thou didſt tranſport \f o \fr 79.9 \ft Thy Church and people,\f* the vineyeard out of Ægypt: thou didſt caſt out \f p \fr 79.9 \ft the Chananites.\f* the Gentiles, and plantedſt it.
\v 10 Thou waſt the guide of the way \f q \fr 79.10 \ft The cloude, and piller of fire were viſible guides.\f* in the ſight therof: thou didſt plant the rootes therof, and it \f r \fr 79.10 \ft and it multiplied mightely.\f* filled the earth.
\v 11 \f s \fr 79.11 \ft Hyperbolical ſpeach to ſignifie the great multiplication of the Iſraelites.\f* The shadow of it couered the mountanes: and the bowghes of it the ceders of God.
\v 12 It extended her branches euen to \f t \fr 79.12 \ft from the mediterranean ſea of Paleſtin,\f* the ſea; and her boughes vnto \f v \fr 79.12 \ft to the riuer Euphrates. Exo. \+it 23. v. 31.\+it* Deut. \+it 11. v. 24.\+it*\f* the riuer.
\v 13 Why haſt thou deſtroyed the hedge therof: and al that paſſe by the way doe plucke it.
\v 14 The \f w \fr 79.14 \ft cruel enimies more like to moſt cruel beaſtes then to men.\f* boare of the wood hath deſtroyed it: and \f x \fr 79.14 \ft yea ſo cruel, as none els in the world are like vnto them.\f* the ſingular wilde beeſt hath eaten it.
\v 15 O God of hoaſts returne: regard \f y \fr 79.15 \ft The Prophet now prayeth for the coming of Chriſt, which he ſaw in ſpirite.\f* from heauen, and ſee, and viſite this vineyeard.
\v 16 And \f z \fr 79.16 \ft The Church of the old teſtament, in her beſt ſtate, wanted the perfection, which the Church of Chriſt hath.\f* perfite it, which thy right hand hath planted: and vpon \f a \fr 79.16 \ft Chriſt our Meſſias moſt commonly calleth by this title: The Sonne of man.\f* the ſonne of man whom thou haſt confirmed to thee.
\v 17 \f b \fr 79.17 \ft So thy vinyard can not indure if it be ſtil afflicted, and trodden downe.\f* Thinges burnt with fyre, and digged doune at the increpation of thy countenance shal perish.
\v 18 Let \f c \fr 79.18 \ft Chriſt working by Gods powre redreſſeth al miſeries.\f* thy hand be vpon the man of thy right hand: and vpon the ſonne of man, whom thou haſt confirmed to thee.
\v 19 And we depart not from thee, thou wilt quicken vs: and we wil inuocate thy name.
\v 20 \f d \fr 79.20 \ft The ſame is the \+it 8\+it*. and the \+it 15\+it* verſe (with litle alteration) and here repeted the third time, In which we alſo pray for three thinges firſt to be purged, and conuerted from ſinne, ſecond to be illuminated by Chriſt, the Image of God, Thirdly to be ſanctified, and ſaued in eternal glorie to haue the fruition of the moſt bleſſed Trinitie.\f* O Lord the God of hoaſtes conuert vs: and shew thy face, and we shal be ſaued.
\c 80
\cd Al men are inuited to celebrate feſtiual dayes, 6. which were inſtituted in memorie of benefites receiued. 9. the deuont shal be protected, and the negligent leift in diſtreſſe. \f - \ft Invitation to celebrate feſtival days devoutly. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 80.1 \ft This Pſalme perteyneth not only to the old teſtament, but alſo to the new,\f* the end, for \f b \fr 80.1 \ft Gods ſeruants oppreſſed with tribulations,\f* wine preſſes, a Pſalme to \f c \fr 80.1 \ft to be ſongue by Aſaph a chief maſter of muſike.\f* Aſaph him ſelfe.
\v 2 Reioice to God our helper: make Iubilation to the God of Iacob.
\v 3 Take ye Pſalme, and \f d \fr 80.3 \ft Make readie al theſe muſical inſtruments.\f* giue timbrel: pleaſant Pſalter with the harpe.
\v 4 Sound ye with trumpet in \f e \fr 80.4 \ft In the Calendes, or firſt day of euerie moneth, in remembrance of Gods prouidence, and perpetual gouernment of al creatures:\f* the \f 1 \fr 80.4 \ft The feaſt of Neomenia.\f* new moone, in \f f \fr 80.4 \ft and moſt eſpecial ſolemnitie in the firſt day of the ſeuenth moneth, in memorie of Iſaac conſerued from death, in whom God promiſed Abraham, to multiplie his ſeede, and to bleſſe al nations. Gen. \+it 17. v. 21. c. 21. v. 12. c. 22. v. 18\+it*.\f* the \f 2 \fr 80.4 \ft Feaſt of trumpetes.\f* notable day of your ſolemnitie.
\v 5 Becauſe it is a precept in Iſrael, and iudgement to the God of Iacob.
\v 6 He put it for a teſtimonie in \f g \fr 80.6 \ft The people of Iſrael ſignified by Ioſeph, as Pſal. \+it 79. v. 2\+it*.\f* Ioſeph, when he came out of the Land of Ægypt: he heard a tongue which he knew not.
\v 7 He \f 1 \fr 80.7 \ft In memorie of this benefite Paſch was inſtituted.\f* turned away his backe \f h \fr 80.7 \ft God deliuered the ſame people from their vntolerable bondage of carying burdens in baskettes, from geathering ſtraw, making brickes, and other ſeruitude. Exo. \+it 1. v. 14. c. 5. v. 7\+it*.\f* from burdens: his handes ſerued in baskettes.
\v 8 In \f i \fr 80.8 \ft The admonition of God to the people:\f* tribulation thou dideſt inuocate me, and I heard thee: I heard thee in the ſecret of the tempeſt: I \f k \fr 80.8 \ft yet after ſo great benefites thou dideſt murmure, and contradict me. Exo. \+it 17\+it*. Num. \+it 20\+it*.\f* proued thee at the water of contradiction.
\v 9 Heare ô my people: and I \f l \fr 80.9 \ft Seriouſly admonish thee.\f* wil conteſt thee: Iſrael \f m \fr 80.9 \ft Man by free wil may chooſe whether he wil obey or no.\f* if thou wilt heare me.
\v 10 \f 1 \fr 80.10 \ft The feaſt of Pentecoſt in memorie of the lawe.\f* There shal be \f n \fr 80.10 \ft This was an other great benefite, to geue an expreſſe law, for their inſtruction.\f* no new God in thee, neither shalt thou adore a ſtrange God.
\v 11 For I am the Lord thy God, which brought thee out of the land of Ægypt: \f o \fr 80.11 \ft Obſeruing my commandments, aske what thou wilt, and I wil geue it thee.\f* dilate thy mouth and I wil fil it.
\v 12 And my People heard not my voice: and Iſrael attended not to me.
\v 13 And I let them alone, according to the deſires of their hart, they shal goe in their owne inuentions. \x - \xt Rom. 1. v. 24.\x*
\v 14 If my people had heard me: if Iſrael had walked in my wayes:
\v 15 I had \f p \fr 80.15 \ft Very eaſely.\f* for nothing, \f q \fr 80.15 \ft of my freewil and liberalitie, without neceſſitie or obligation.\f* perhaps humbled their enimies: and had laide my hand vpon thoſe that afflict them.
\v 16 The enimies of our Lord haue lied to him: and \f r \fr 80.16 \ft The wicked that promiſe to ſerue God, and do it not shal be in eternal torments:\f* their time shal be for euer.
\v 17 And \f s \fr 80.17 \ft In the meane time God beſtowed theſe benefites vpon them, for the iuſts ſake.\f* he fedde them of the fatte of corne: and out of the rocke, with honie he filled them.
\c 81
\cd The Prophet declareth, 2. that God reprehendeth vniuſt Iudges and Magiſtrates: premonisheth them of his ſeuere & eternal punishment: 8. Wherto the prophet conforming his wil prayeth for the ſame. \f - \ft Admonition to Magiſtrates. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Pſalme to Aſaph. God ſtood in the aſſemblie \f a \fr 81.1 \ft The wordes of the prophet, admoniſhing al magiſtrates, that when they ſitte in iudgement, or determine anie cauſe, God who is there, and euerie where preſent,\f* of goddes, and in the middes he \f b \fr 81.1 \ft attendeth their proceſſe: and therfore it behoueth them to be aduiſed what they doe: euen as if they heard God ſpeaking as here foloweth:\f* iudgeth goddes.
\v 2 \f c \fr 81.2 \ft The wordes of God, though not vttered ſenſibly, yet in effect intimated by his law, according:· wherto he wil procede in iudgement againſt vniuſt Iudges.\f* How long iudge ye iniquitie: and accept ye the perſons of ſinners.
\v 3 Iudge ye for the needie and the pupil: iuſtifie ye the humble, and the poore.
\v 4 Take away the poore: and deliuer the needie from the hand of the ſinner.
\v 5 They \f d \fr 81.5 \ft Such iudges procede in groſſe ignorance, not caring to vnderſtand, but content to walke in darknes.\f* knew not, neither did they vnderſtand, they walke in darkeneſſe: \f e \fr 81.5 \ft Ye are ſo euil diſpoſed, that you would turne al vpſide downe, iuſtifying the wicked, and condemning the iuſt.\f* al the fundations of the earth shal be moued.
\v 6 I ſaide: Yow are \f f \fr 81.6 \ft For your office which you participate of me, you are certaine goddes vpon earth:\f* goddes, and the ſonnes of the higheſt al.
\v 7 \f g \fr 81.7 \ft But when you die you shal find that you are men, ſubiect to Gods iudgement.\f* But you shal die as men: and fal \f h \fr 81.7 \ft yea your punishment wil be greuous, and importable: for the mightie shal mightely ſuffer torments. sap.\f* as one of the princes. \x - \xt Sap. 6.\x*
\v 8 \f i \fr 81.8 \ft Againe the wordes of the prophet praying God,\f* Ariſe ô God, iudge the earth: \f k \fr 81.8 \ft that eing he is Lord of al he wil iudge al.\f* becauſe thou shalt inherite in al the Gentiles.
\c 82
\cd The Church impugned by al ſortes of enimies 10. prayeth God to confound them, as she hath donne diuers the like, 17. wherby ſome shal be conuerted. \f - \ft Perſecuters of the Church confounded or converted. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Canticle of Pſalme to Aſaph.
\v 2 O God \f a \fr 82.2 \ft Seing none is like to thee ô God,\f* who shal be like to thee? \f b \fr 82.2 \ft shew thy powre and maieſtie,\f* hold not thy peace, \f c \fr 82.2 \ft be not ſilent.\f* neither be thou appeaſed ô God:
\v 3 For behold \f d \fr 82.3 \ft The cruel perſecuters are moſt inſolent,\f* thine enimies haue made a ſound: they that hate thee, \f e \fr 82.3 \ft and proud.\f* haue lifted vp the head.
\v 4 They haue taken malignant counſel vpon thy people: and they haue deuiſed againſt thy ſaincts.
\v 5 They haue ſaide: Come, and let vs deſtroy them out of \f f \fr 82.5 \ft That there be no more anie faithful people,\f* the nation: and let \f g \fr 82.5 \ft anie Catholiques left aliue.\f* the name of Iſrael be remembred no more.
\v 6 Becauſe they haue deuiſed with one conſent: they haue together made a couenant againſt thee.
\v 7 The tabernacles of \f h \fr 82.7 \ft The progenie of Eſau,\f* the Idumeians, and \f i \fr 82.7 \ft the ſeede of Iſmael.\f* the Iſmahelites \f k \fr 82.7 \ft deſcending from the elder ſonne of Lot.\f* Moab, and \f l \fr 82.7 \ft the iſſue of Abraham by Agar, who falſly cal themſelues Saraſcens, as if they were of Sara.\f* the Agarens.
\v 8 \f m \fr 82.8 \ft People of Gebal à citie of Syria,\f* Gebal, and \f n \fr 82.8 \ft of the other ſonne of Lot.\f* Ammon, and \f o \fr 82.8 \ft thoſe that firſt oppugned the Iſraelites, after they were parted from Ægypt. \+it Exo. 17.\+it*\f* Amalec: \f p \fr 82.8 \ft The Philiſtians,\f* the foreners, with \f q \fr 82.8 \ft and Tyrians, al nere neighboures, and ſome of them nere akine to the Iſraelites, were their great enimies.\f* the inhabitantes of Tyre.
\v 9 Yea and \f r \fr 82.9 \ft Others alſo coming further of, ioyned againſt Gods people, in figure that al heretikes, and other infidels conſpire together againſt Catholiques.\f* Aſſur alſo is come with them: they are made an aide to the children of Lot.
\v 10 Doe to them as \f s \fr 82.10 \ft The Pſalmiſt therfore prayeth (and withal prophecieth) that God wil at laſt deſtroy them, as he did Madian. Num \+it 31.\+it* Iudic. 6. & 7.\f* to Madian, and \f t \fr 82.10 \ft Siſara captaine general,\f* Siſara: as to \f v \fr 82.10 \ft for Iabin king of Aſor nere Ciſſon. Iudic. \+it 4. v. 7.\+it* & \+it 23.\+it*\f* Iabin in the torrent Ciſſon.
\v 11 They perished \f w \fr 82.11 \ft Within the territorie of Manaſſes \+it (Ios. 17.)\+it* which they inuaded.\f* in Endor: they were made as \f x \fr 82.11 \ft ſlaine and not buried.\f* the dung of the earth.
\v 12 Put their princes as \f y \fr 82.12 \ft Theſe foure princes of the Madianites were ſlaine by Gedeons forces Iudic. \+it 7. & 8\+it*.\f* Oreb, and Zeb, and Zebee, and Salmana.
\v 13 Al their princes: which haue ſaide: Let vs poſſeſſe the Sanctuarie of God for an inheritance.
\v 14 My God put them \f z \fr 82.14 \ft By foure ſimilitudes the prophet deſcribeth the punishment that shal fal vpon perſecuters.\f* as a wheele: and as ſtubble before the face of the winde.
\v 15 Euen as fire that burneth a wood: & as a flame that burneth the mountaines:
\v 16 So shalt thou purſew them in thy tempeſt: and in thy wrath thou shalt truble them.
\v 17 Fil their faces with ignominie: and \f a \fr 82.17 \ft God by punishing ſeeketh the conuerſion of ſinners, not their eternal death.\f* they wil ſeeke thy name, ô Lord.
\v 18 Let \f b \fr 82.18 \ft But ſuch as be ſtil obſtinate, and finally impenitent do perish for euer.\f* them be ashamed, and trubled for euer and euer: and let them be confounded, and perish.
\v 19 And let them know that \f c \fr 82.19 \ft God only the creator of heauen and earth is properly called LORD: VVhoſe eſſential, and incommunicable name is VVHICH IS. Exo. 3. v. 14. & 6. v. 3.\f* Lord is thy name, thou onlie the Higheſt in al the earth.
\c 83
\cd Deuout perſons feruently deſire eternal glorie, 6. accounting it, in the meane time, a happie ſtate to be in the militant Church, 12. where God firſt geuing grace, wil geue glorie in the triumphant. \f - \ft Eternal glory. The 10. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, \f a \fr 83.1 \ft For men afflicted in this vale of miſerie.\f* for wine preſſes, \f b \fr 83.1 \ft By the children of Core not being muſitians (but porters in the temple, \+it 1. Par. 26\+it*.) S. Auguſtin here and in other titles of Pſalmes, vnderſtandeth the faithful children of Chriſt.\f* the children of Core, a Pſalme.
\v 2 How beloued are \f c \fr 83.2 \ft The glorious manſions in heauen, which God hath prepared for the iuſt.\f* thy tabernacles ô Lord of hoaſtes!
\v 3 My ſoule coueteth, and \f d \fr 83.3 \ft Vehement deſires do ſometimes depriue vs of external ſenſe.\f* fainteth vnto the courtes of our Lord. My hart, and \f e \fr 83.3 \ft The mind reioycing in hope, the bodie is alſo recreated, releeued, and reuiued, which before was dulle and heauie.\f* my flesh haue reioyced toward the liuing God.
\v 4 For \f f \fr 83.4 \ft As ſparowes by natural inſtinct ſeeking habitations, finde houſes to dwel in,\f* the ſparow alſo hath found her an houſe: and \f g \fr 83.4 \ft and turtles haue neſtes, wherin to lay their young ones: ſo faithful ſoules ſeeke to dwel in heauen, and in the meane time to lay vp good workes within the Catholique Church; out of which (ſayth S. Auguſtin in this place) how good ſoeuer workes do ſeme (as when paganes, and heretikes feede the hungrie, cloth the naked, receiue ſtrangers into their houſes, viſite the ſicke, comforte priſoners) being not laid in the neſt, conculcabuntur, & conterentur: non ſeruabuntur, non cuſtodientur: they ſhal be trodde vnder foote, they ſhal be bruiſed in peeces: they shal not be conſerued, they ſhal not be kept: but (that ſuch workes may be profitable and be conſerued) they muſt be donne in true faith, in the Catholique faith, in ſocietie of the vnitie of the Church.\f* the turtledoue a neſt for her ſelfe, where she may lay her young ones. Thine \f h \fr 83.4 \ft VVorkes are good, and rightly laide vp when they are donne in vnitie, and participation of Gods Altares, the moſt proper places of Diuine Seruice, of external Sacrifice in this life, and ſpiritual ſacrifice of perfect praiſes in eternal glorie; where al Sainctes without ceaſing ſing: Holie, holie, holie, Lord God of hoaſtes. Iſaiæ \+it 6\+it*. Apoc. \+it 4\+it*.\f* altars ô Lord of hoaſtes: my King, and my God.
\v 5 Bleſſed are they, that dwel in \f i \fr 83.5 \ft The Catholique Church.\f* thy houſe ô Lord: for euer and euer they shal praiſe thee.
\v 6 Bleſſed is the man, whoſe helpe is from thee: he \f k \fr 83.6 \ft The iuſt by Gods grace and helpe, may reſolue to aſcend by ſteppes and degrees, from vertue to vertue \+it (v. 8.)\+it* euen to heauen,\f* hath diſpoſed aſcenſion in his hart,
\v 7 in \f l \fr 83.7 \ft though he be now in this vaile of teares, by reaſon of mans ſinne, who otherwiſe was before ſinne in paradiſe, a place of delight.\f* the vaile of teares, in the place, which he hath appointed.
\v 8 For \f m \fr 83.8 \ft Chriſt our lawgeuer,\f* the lawgeuer shal geue \f n \fr 83.8 \ft geueth abundance of graces,\f* bleſſing, they shal goe \f o \fr 83.8 \ft with continual increaſe.\f* from vertue into vertue: \f p \fr 83.8 \ft but our only omnipotent God, is to be ſenne by this effect of his grace, in the Church and not elswhere.\f* the God of goddes shal be ſeene in Sion.
\v 9 Lord God of hoaſtes heare my prayer: receiue with thine eare ô God of Iacob.
\v 10 Behold ô God our protectour: and \f q \fr 83.10 \ft Agreably to this the Church maketh al her petitions, concluding al prayers; By Chriſt our Lord.\f* looke vpon the face of thy Chriſt.
\v 11 Becauſe \f r \fr 83.11 \ft In reſpect of the future retribution, which euerie one shal receiue according to their deſertes, one day in Gods Church is better then thouſands out of it.\f* better is one day in thy courtes, aboue thouſands. I haue choſen to be \f s \fr 83.11 \ft And better to be in the pooreſt ſtate of Catholique Chriſtians,\f* an abiect in the houſe of my God: rather then to dwel \f t \fr 83.11 \ft then in greateſt palaces or hiegheſt dignities amongſt ſinners.\f* in the tabernacles of ſinners.
\v 12 Becauſe God \f v \fr 83.12 \ft The Diuine wiſdom ſo vſeth mercie, and veritie, that neither may preiudice the other:\f* loueth mercie and truth: our Lord wil geue \f w \fr 83.12 \ft and ſo geueth grace in this life,\f* grace, \f x \fr 83.12 \ft and glorie in the next.\f* and glorie.
\v 13 He wil not depriue them of good thinges, that \f y \fr 83.13 \ft Beſides innocencie conſerued without ſinne, there is alſo innocencie after remiſſion of ſinne, of which the prophet here ſpeaketh.\f* walke in innocencie: ô Lord of hoaſtes bleſſed is the man, that hopeth in thee.
\c 84
\cd With commemoration of Gods former benefites, 5. Chriſts Incarnation is prophecied, 9. bringing peace and ſaluation, 11. mercie and iuſtice concurring together. \f - \ft Incarnation of Chriſt. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, to the children of Core, a Pſalme.
\v 2 O Lord thou \f a \fr 84.2 \ft God beſtowed manie great benefites vpon the people of Iſrael:\f* haſt bleſſed thy land: thou \f b \fr 84.2 \ft he brought them out of the bondage of Ægypt.\f* haſt turned away the captiuitie of Iacob.
\v 3 Thou \f c \fr 84.3 \ft Remitted their manifold ſinnes:\f* haſt forgeuen the iniquitie of thy people: thou \f d \fr 84.3 \ft pardoned alſo a great part of due punishment.\f* haſt couered al their ſinnes.
\v 4 Thou haſt mitigated al thy wrath: thou haſt turned away from the wrath of thine indignation.
\v 5 \f e \fr 84.5 \ft As thou haſt ſpared thy peculiar people, ſo we beſech thee ô God creator and general Sauiour of al mankind,\f* Conuert vs ô God our ſauiour: and \f f \fr 84.5 \ft mitigate thy wrath towards vs al.\f* auert thy wrath from vs.
\v 6 Wilt thou be wrath with vs for euer? or wilt thou extend thy wrath from generation vnto generation?
\v 7 O God thou \f g \fr 84.7 \ft Til God firſt shew his mercie, ſinners lye dead in guilt of ſinne, but by his grace they are ſturred vp, and quickned:\f* being turned shalt quicken vs: and thy people \f h \fr 84.7 \ft and ioyfully retutne to God.\f* shal reioyce in thee.
\v 8 Shew vs ô Lord thy mercie and giue vs thy ſaluation.
\v 9 I wil \f i \fr 84.9 \ft The wordes of the prophet,\f* heare what our Lord God wil ſpeake in me: \f k \fr 84.9 \ft ſignifying that God had reueled vnto him the redemption of mankind.\f* becauſe he wil ſpeake peace vpon his people. And vpon \f l \fr 84.9 \ft Not al men are iuſtified, and ſaued, but thoſe that are hartely and ſincerely conuerted.\f* his ſainctes: and vpon them, that are conuerted to the hart.
\v 10 But yet his ſaluation is nigh to them that \f m \fr 84.10 \ft Though al be not ſaued (becauſe manie wil not cooperate to Gods grace) yet very manie hauing the feare of God, which is the beginning of godlie wiſdom, freely accept of Gods mercie, and ſo the Church is gloriouſly propagated.\f* feare him: that glorie may inhabite in our land.
\v 11 \f n \fr 84.11 \ft VVheras Gods mercie would ſaue al, and his truth, or iuſtice requireth that ſinnes be duly punished, by Chriſts Paſſion and death, ſufficient ſatisfaction is offered for al ſinnes, and thoſe that wil be partakers by penance, and conformitie to Gods law, may haue remiſſion,\f* Mercie and truth haue met each other: \f o \fr 84.11 \ft and ſo iuſtice is obſerued, and peace made betwen God and his ſubiects.\f* iuſtice and peace haue kiſſed.
\v 12 \f p \fr 84.12 \ft Integritie of conſcience reigneth in good men,\f* Truth is riſen out of the earth: \f q \fr 84.12 \ft God ſending iuſt meanes from heauen to ſaue them.\f* and iuſtice hath looked downe from heauen.
\v 13 For \f r \fr 84.13 \ft God geueth grace,\f* our Lord certes wil geue benignitie: and \f s \fr 84.13 \ft and ſo men yeld fruict.\f* our land shal giue her fruite.
\v 14 \f t \fr 84.14 \ft Yea they walke in iuſtice, and right path of Gods law.\f* Iuſtice shal walke before him: and shal ſet his ſteppes in the way.
\c 85
\cd In conſideration of his owne imperfections, the royal prophet, or other faithful perſon prayeth God, 5. according to his mercie and goodnes, 9. shewed in conuerting Gentiles, 13. and in deliuering the ſupplicant him ſelf from the ſtate of damnation, 16. that he wil ſtil direct and defend him againſt al enimies. \f - \ft A prayer for continual grace. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A praier \f a \fr 85.1 \ft A forme of prayer for king Dauid, and for anie faithful perſon.\f* to Dauid him ſelfe. Incline thine eare ô Lord, and \f b \fr 85.1 \ft There be ſundrie iuſt cauſes which moue God to heare our prayers:\f* heare me: \f c \fr 85.1 \ft firſt our neceſſitie requireth Gods helpe.\f* becauſe I am needie and poore.
\v 2 Keepe my ſoule, becauſe \f d \fr 85.2 \ft Secondly becauſe we profeſſe, and promiſe to lead a holie life.\f* I am holie: ſaue thy ſeruant my God, that \f e \fr 85.2 \ft Thirdly, becauſe we truſt and hope in God.\f* hopeth in thee.
\v 3 Haue mercie on me ô Lord, becauſe I haue cried to thee \f f \fr 85.3 \ft Fourtly, becauſe we perſeuere in prayer.\f* al the day:
\v 4 make ioyful the ſoule of thy ſeruant, becauſe to thee ô Lord haue I \f g \fr 85.4 \ft Fiftly, if we pray with attention of mind.\f* lifted vp my ſoule.
\v 5 Becauſe thou ô Lord art \f h \fr 85.5 \ft Sixtly becauſe God of his owne nature is benigne, readie to beſtow benefites.\f* ſwete, and \f i \fr 85.5 \ft Seuently, he is meeke to remitte offences.\f* milde: and \f k \fr 85.5 \ft Eighily, he is merciful to mitigate the puniſhment, to thoſe that make recourſe vnto him.\f* of ſo much mercie to al that inuocate thee. \f l \fr 85.5 \ft For theſe cauſes we pray as foloweth.\f*
\v 6 Receiue ô Lord my praier with thine eares: and attend to the voice of my petition.
\v 7 In the day of my tribulation I haue called to thee: becauſe thou haſt heard me.
\v 8 There is not the like to thee amongſt goddes ô Lord: and there is not according to thy workes.
\v 9 \f m \fr 85.9 \ft Vocation of Gentiles.\f* Al nations whatſoeuer thou haſt made, shal \f n \fr 85.9 \ft They shal come by faith.\f* come, and shal adore before thee ô Lord: and they shal \f o \fr 85.9 \ft and glorifie God by good workes. Mat. 5.v.17.\f* glorifie thy name.
\v 10 Becauſe thou art great and doing meruelous thinges: thou onlie art God.
\v 11 Conduct me ô Lord in thy way, and I wil walke in thy truth: let my hart reioyce that it may feare thy name.
\v 12 I wil \f p \fr 85.12 \ft Cofeſſion of praiſe.\f* confeſſe to thee ô Lord my God with al my hart, and wil glorifie thy name for euer:
\v 13 Becauſe thy mercie is great vpon me: and thou haſt deliuered my ſoule out of \f q \fr 85.13 \ft From the ſtate of eternal damnation.\f* the lower hel.
\v 14 O God the wicked are riſen vp vpon me, and the ſynagogue of the mightie haue ſought my ſoule: and they haue not ſet thee in their ſight.
\v 15 And thou ô Lord the God of compaſſion and merciful, patient, and of much mercie, and \f r \fr 85.15 \ft In performing al promiſes.\f* true.
\v 16 Haue reſpect to me, and haue mercie on me, \f s \fr 85.16 \ft A digreſſion (vſual to prophetes) of Chriſts Empyre and Kingdom the Church, geuen to him, being the ſonne of an immaculate virgin, the handmaide of God.\f* giue thine empire to thy ſeruant: and ſaue the ſonne of thy handmaide.
\v 17 Make with me \f t \fr 85.17 \ft The chief and principal ſigne of Chriſts, and his Churches glorie, is his Reſurrection, præfigured in Ionas.\f* a ſigne vnto God, that they may ſee which hate me, and may be confounded: \f v \fr 85.17 \ft VVhereby al enimies are confounded, either to their conuerſion, or to eternal damnation. See 6. Auguſtin.\f* becauſe thou ô Lord haſt holpen me.
\c 86
\cd The Church of Chriſt beginning in Ieruſalem, 3. is extended to al Nations, 5. glorious, 6. and permanent, 7. in holie ioy. \f - \ft A prayer for continual grace. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 To the children of Core, a Pſalme of Canticle. THE fundations thereof in the holie \f a \fr 86.1 \ft Chriſts Church was firſt founded in Ieruſalem on whitſunday, \+it Act 2\+it* in mount Sion, which hath two coppes, in one of which the Temple ſtood, in the other Dauids towre or palace.\f* mountaynes:
\v 2 our Lord loueth the gates of Sion, aboue al the tabernacles of Iacob.
\v 3 Glorious thinges are ſaide of thee, ô cittie of God.
\v 4 \f b \fr 86.4 \ft The Prophet in the perſon of Chriſt, ſaith he wil commend vnto his Apoſtles, and other Apoſtolical men, that they conuert al nations (as our Sauiour gaue expreſſe commiſſion and commandment. Math. \+it 28\+it*. Luc. \+it 24. v. 47\+it*. Act. \+it 1. v. 8\+it*.)\f* I wil be mindeful of \f c \fr 86.4 \ft naming here Raab (which is Ægypt) and Babylon:\f* Raab, and Babylon knowing me. Behold \f d \fr 86.4 \ft The Philiſtims, Tyrians, Æthiopians,\f* the foreners, and Tyre, & the people of the Æthiopians, theſe \f e \fr 86.4 \ft & the reſt, ſhal be regenerate in this Church, which for the aſſured certaintie therof (after the prophets maner of ſpeaking) is affirmed in the pretertence, as if it were then donne.\f* were there.
\v 5 Shal it not be ſaid of Sion \f f \fr 86.5 \ft It shal be reported, or one ſhal ſay to an other: Loe this and that man, al theſe and al theſe men are regenerate by Baptiſme in the Church of Chriſt.\f* Man and man, is borne in her; and \f g \fr 86.5 \ft God himſelfe, Chriſt God and Man, founded this Church.\f* the Higheſt himſelfe founded her?
\v 6 Our Lord wil declare \f h \fr 86.6 \ft The multitude of the elect is ſo great, that only God knoweth the number,\f* in ſcriptures of peoples, and \f i \fr 86.6 \ft and the qualities of al ſortes, of Princes, Prelates, and Peoples.\f* of princes: of thoſe that haue bene in her.
\v 7 The \f k \fr 86.7 \ft Great ſpiritual ioy with peace of conſcience, is in true Chriſtian Catholiques in the militant Church; but the bleſſed haue the moſt abſolute & ſecure ioy of al, in the Church triumphant.\f* habitation in thee, is as it were of al reioycing.
\c 87
\cd A faithful perſon ſore and long afflicted, lamentably complaineth, praying God, 15. not ſtil to repel him, being leift deſolate, 19. without al conſolation of freindes. \f - \ft A prayer in long affliction. The 7 key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Canticle of a Pſalme, to the children of Core, vnto the end, for \f a \fr 87.1 \ft An inſtrument of muſike apt for lamentable ſongues. Not expreſſed in the title of anie other Pſalme. Perhaps becauſe this Pſalme mixeth not anie conſolation with mourning, as other Pſalmes do, which are alſo prayers in affliction. As the 30. 33. 63. 73.\f* Maheleth \f b \fr 87.1 \ft Neither is this word in anie other title. It is added here to admonish vs, that as this Pſalme, and ſome others were ſongue by two quires, one anſwering the other, ſo we muſt in anſwer and imitation of Chriſt, ſuffer long and great afflictions with patience, ſeeing he in his paſſion was leift without ordinarie côſolation.\f* to anſwer, \f c \fr 87.1 \ft By ſome interpreted [his bretheren] For Chriſt (ſaith S. Auguſtin) vouchſaſſeth to make them his bretheren, which vnderſtand the myſterie of his Croſſe, and not only are not aſhamed therof, but alſo faithfully glorie therin.\f* of vnderſtanding to Eman the Ezrahite.
\v 2 O Lord the God of my ſaluation: \f d \fr 87.2 \ft I haue cried to thee very often both by day, and by night.\f* in the day haue I cried, and in the night before thee.
\v 3 Let my prayer enter in thy ſight: incline thine eare to my petition.
\v 4 Becauſe my ſoule is replenished with euils: and \f e \fr 87.4 \ft I am almoſt dead.\f* my life hath approched to hel.
\v 5 I \f f \fr 87.5 \ft Accounted as dead, and readie to be buried.\f* am accounted with them that deſcend into the lake. I am become as a man without helpe,
\v 6 \f g \fr 87.6 \ft If I were dead, I ſhould be free from theſe afflictions. Eſpecially it agreeth to Chriſt, who was free, yea of infinite powre amongſt the dead.\f* free among the dead, as the wounded ſleeping in the ſepulchres, of whom thou art mindeful no more: and they are caſt of from thy hand.
\v 7 They haue put me in \f h \fr 87.7 \ft Myn enimies haue endeuored not only to bereue me of temporal life, wherby I ſhould goe into limbus, but alſo to kil my ſoule ſpiritually, wherby I should deſcend into the lower hel of the damned.\f* the lower lake: in the darke places, and in the shadowe of death.
\v 8 Thy \f i \fr 87.8 \ft Thy iuſt wrath alſo ô God hath excedingly afflicted me.\f* furie is confirmed vpon me: and al thy waues thou haſt brought in vpon me.
\v 9 Thou haſt made my familiars far from me: they haue put me abomination to themſelues. I was deliuered and came not forth:
\v 10 myne eies languished for pouertie. I cried to thee ô Lord al the day: I ſtretched out my handes to thee.
\v 11 Wilt thou \f k \fr 87.11 \ft O God deliuer me whiles I am yet liuing, for I may not looke for extraordinarie, and miraculous helpe, as to be raiſed againe after death:\f* doe meruels to the dead: or shal phiſicians raiſe to life, and they confeſſe to thee?
\v 12 Shal any \f l \fr 87.12 \ft when I shal be dead & buried, I can not denounce thy praiſes as now I can to mortal men.\f* in the ſepulchre declare thy mercie, and thy truth in perdition?
\v 13 Shal thy meruelous workes be knowne \f m \fr 87.13 \ft Much leſſe shal the damned praiſe thee in eternal perdition.\f* in darkenes: and thy iuſtice in the land of obliuion.
\v 14 And I ô Lord haue cried to thee: and in the morning shal my praier preuent thee.
\v 15 Why doeſt thou o Lord reiect my prayer: turneſt away thy face from me?
\v 16 I am poore, and in labours \f n \fr 87.16 \ft As wel young,\f* from my youth: and being \f o \fr 87.16 \ft as waxing elder I haue bene ſtil afflicted.\f* exalted, humbled and troubled.
\v 17 Thy wrathes haue paſſed vpon me: and thy terrouts haue trubled me.
\v 18 They haue compaſſed me as water al the day: they compaſſed me together.
\v 19 Thou \f p \fr 87.19 \ft My miſerable eſtate hath alienated al freindes, neighbours, & acquantances from me.\f* haſt made frend, and neighboure far from me: and my familiars becauſe of miſerie.
\c 88
\cd Gods mercie and truth, with his great promiſes to Dauid, 6. his powre in the whole world, and iuſt iudgements, are the true ioy of his ſeruantes. 20. Chriſts kingdom shal remaine for euer: 31. yea manie offending yet al shal not perish, 39. but after great affliction, 47. God wil reſpect mans infirmitie, 50. his owne promiſe, and the enimies reproching his ſeruantes and himſelfe: 53. who is bleſſed for euer. \f - \ft The Church of Chriſt never faileth. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Of vnderſtanding \f a \fr 88.1 \ft Otherwiſe called Idithun. \+it 1. Paral. 25\+it*. or rather Ethan, who was very wiſe, mentioned with others, to whom Salomon is preferred for wiſdom. \+it 3. Reg. 4. v. 31\+it*, and ſignifieth ſtrong, applied here to thoſe that are ſtrong in aſſured hope of Chriſts promiſes, notwithſtanding it ſemeth ſometimes to the weake, that his promiſes are not performed.\f* to Ethan the Ezrahite.
\v 2 THE mercies of our Lord I wil ſing for euer. In generation \f b \fr 88.2 \ft In al generations.\f* and generation I wil shewe forth thy truth, in my mouth.
\v 3 Becauſe thou ſaidſt: Mercie shal be built vp for euer \f c \fr 88.3 \ft The heauens ſhal rather fal, then Gods truth faile. Myſtically, in the Apoſtles, and by their preaching, the Church of Chriſt is built for euer.\f* in the heauens: thy truth shal be prepared in them.
\v 4 I haue ordained a teſtament with mine elect, I haue ſworne to Dauid my ſeruant:
\v 5 \f d \fr 88.5 \ft Dauids ſeede conſerued til Chriſt was borne of his virgin mother: and in his ſpiritual ſeede, his kingdom the Church is for euer conſerued. Otherwiſe not verified of Dauids temporal kingdom, which decayed in the captiuitie of Babylon, and is now wholly deſtroyed.\f* for ever wil I prepare thy ſeede. And I wil build thy ſeat vnto generation and generation.
\v 6 The heauens shal confeſſe thy meruelous workes ô Lord: yea and thy truth in the church of ſaintes.
\v 7 For who in the cloudes shal be equal to our Lord: shal be like to God among \f e \fr 88.7 \ft The Angels.\f* the ſonnes of God?
\v 8 God, who is glorified in the counſel of ſaintes: great and terrible ouer al that are round about him.
\v 9 O Lord God of hoaſtes who is like to thee? thou art mightie ô Lord, and thy truth round about thee.
\v 10 Thou ruleſt ouer the powre of the ſea: and the mouing of the waues therof thou doeſt mitigate.
\v 11 Thou \f f \fr 88.11 \ft The prophet aludeth to the plagues and miracles in Ægypt, and in other enimes.\f* humbledſt the proud one, as one wounded: in the arme of thy ſtrength thou haſt diſperſed thine enimies.
\v 12 The heauens are thine, and the earth is thine, the round earth, and the fulnes therof thou haſt founded:
\v 13 the north, and the ſea thou haſt created. \f g \fr 88.13 \ft Conuerſion of Gentiles.\f* Thabor and Hermon shal reioice in thy name:
\v 14 thy arme is with might. Let \f h \fr 88.14 \ft VVhether God punish, as with the left hand,\f* thy hand be confirmed, and thy \f i \fr 88.14 \ft or beſtow benefites, as with the right hand, al is to his glorie, and according to mercie, and truth.\f* righthand exalted:
\v 15 iuſtice and iudgement is the preparation of thy ſeat. Mercie and truth shal goe before thy face:
\v 16 \f k \fr 88.16 \ft They are ſpiritually happie, that do thus conſider of Gods meruelous procedings, praiſe the ſame, and reioyce therin.\f* bleſſed is the people that knoweth iubilation. Lord they shal walke in the light of thy countinance,
\v 17 and in thy name they shal reioyce al the day: and in thy iuſtice they shal be exalted.
\v 18 Becauſe thou art the glorie of their ſtreingth: and in thy good pleaſure shal our \f l \fr 88.18 \ft powre, and kingdom.\f* horne be exalted.
\v 19 Becauſe our protection is of our Lord: and of the holie one of Iſrael our king.
\v 20 Then didſt thou ſpeake in viſion to thy ſaintes, and ſaidſt \f 1 \fr 88.20 \ft Thus God promiſed to eſtabliſh the kingdom of the Iewes in Dauid and his familie. \+it 1. Reg. 16. 2. Reg. 5\+it*. and other places. which was performed as in a figure but more fully in Chriſt. \+it Act. 13. v. 22\+it*.\f* I haue put helpe on the mightie one: and haue exalted an elect one of my people.
\v 21 I haue found Dauid my ſeruant: with myne holie oyle haue I anointed him.
\v 22 For mine hand shal helpe him: and myne arme shal ſtrengthen him.
\v 23 The enimie shal nothing preuale in him: and the ſonne of iniquitie shal not adde to hurt him.
\v 24 And I wil cut downe his enimies before his face: and them that hate him I wil put to flight.
\v 25 And my truth, and my mercie with him: and in my name shal his horne be exalted.
\v 26 And I wil put his hand in the ſea: and his righthand in the riuers.
\v 27 He shal inuocate me: Thou art my Father: my God, and the protector of my ſaluation.
\v 28 And I wil put him the firſtbegotten, high aboue the kings of the earth.
\v 29 I wil kepe my mercie vnto him for euer: and my teſtament faithful to him.
\v 30 I wil put his ſeed for euer and euer: and his throne as the daies of heauen.
\v 31 But if his children shal forſake my lawe: and wil not walke in my Iudgementes:
\v 32 If they shal profane my iuſtices: and not kepe my commandmentes:
\v 33 I wil viſite their iniquities with a rod: and their ſinnes with ſtripes:
\v 34 But, my mercie I wil not take away from him: neither wil I hurt in my truth:
\v 35 Neither wil I profane my teſtament: and the words that procede from my mouth I wil not make fruſtrate.
\v 36 Once I haue ſworne in my holie, if I lie to Dauid:
\v 37 his ſeede shal continewe for euer. \f 1 \fr 88.37 \ft \+it I wil not lie\+it*.\f*
\v 38 And \f m \fr 88.38 \ft Chriſtian iuſt ſoules as the ſunne,\f* his throne as the Sunne in my ſight, and as the \f n \fr 88.38 \ft and as the perfect, or full moone. See the firſt Tome. page \+it 716\+it*. S. Auguſtin alſo expoundeth this verſe in the Anagogical ſenſe, of the iuſt after the Reſurrection in glorie, where the ſoule shal be like the ſunne, and the bodie, which now is mutable, ſhal be like the moone, not as now alwayes changing, but as the ful moone, alwayes perfect.\f* Moone perfect for euer: and a faithful witneſſe in heauen.
\v 39 But \f 1 \fr 88.39 \ft God hauing promiſed al the aforſaide the prophet in the perſon of the weake, lamenteth that the contrarie shal happen as wel in the temporal kingdom oppreſſed by the Aſſirians Babylonians, Perſians, Grecians, and Romanes: as in the Church impugned by innumerable ſortes of Heretikes and other Infidels.\f* thou haſt repelled and diſpiſed: thou \f o \fr 88.39 \ft Amongſt manie penſiue thinges, this one word doth comforte vs, thy promiſe remaineth, thou haſt not denied to ſend Chriſt, but differred him,\f* haſt differred thy Chriſt.
\v 40 Thou haſt ouerthrowne the teſtament of thy ſeruant: thou haſt profaned his ſanctuarie on the earth.
\v 41 Thou haſt deſtroyed al the hedges therof: thou haſt made the firmament therof feare.
\v 42 Al that paſſe by the way haue ſpoiled him: he is become a reproch to his neighbours.
\v 43 Thou haſt exalted the righthand of them that oppreſſe him: thou haſt made al his enimies ioyful.
\v 44 Thou haſt turned away the helpe of his ſword: and haſt not holpen him in battel.
\v 45 Thou haſt deſtroied him from \f p \fr 88.45 \ft From the vſe of Sacrifice and Sacraments, wherby ſinners were wount to be cleanſed.\f* emundation: and his ſeat thou haſt broken downe to the ground.
\v 46 Thou haſt leſſened the daies of his time: thou haſt ouerwhelmed him with confuſion.
\v 47 \f 1 \fr 88.47 \ft The Pſalmiſt prayeth and prophecieth that God wil reſpect the weaknes of man, maintaine his Church in manie natiôs, & ſaue manie ſoules.\f* How long ô Lord doeſt thou turne away for euer: shal thy wrath burne as a fire?
\v 48 Remember what my ſubſtance is: for haſt thou made al the children of men in vaine?
\v 49 Who is the man that shal liue, and shal not ſee death: shal deliuer his ſoule from the hand of hel?
\v 50 Where are thyne old mercies ô Lord, as thou ſwareſt to Dauid in thy truth?
\v 51 Be mindeful ô Lord of the aeproch of thy ſeruantes (which I haue held in my boſome) of manie nations.
\v 52 Which thine enimies haue reproched ô Lord, which they haue reproched \f q \fr 88.52 \ft As though Chriſt were changed and turned from vs.\f* the commutation of thy Chriſt.
\v 53 Bleſſed be our Lord for euer: \f r \fr 88.53 \ft So we wish and pray that al may bleſſe and praiſe thee. Amen.\f* Be it, be it.
\c 89
\cd Vnder the forme of prayer, the Pſalmiſt deſcribeth the shortnes of mans life and other calamites. 7. Gods ſtrict iudgement. 13. but firſt his comfortable mercie: 16. and perpetual regard of his owne worke. \f - \ft Man rightly created fell by ſin into miſeries. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A prayer \f a \fr 89.1 \ft Some Expoſitors thincke Moyſes was the author of this Pſalme, and of the tenne next folowing. But others hold that Dauid vvas author of al, and that Moyſes his name is here put in the title by Eſdras, becauſe this Pſalme is like to the prayer of Moyſes, when the people prouoked Gods wrath by their ſinnes in the deſert. And becauſe mans creation, fal, punishmẽt, and Gods mercie towards him, are here deſcribed: which Moyſes firſt writte, as going before the vvritten law. And that Moyſes made not this Pſalme is probably gethered by the \+it 10\+it*. verſe, where the ordinarie age of men is deſcribed to be (in ſtreingth and vigore) ſeuentie yeares, or of ſome fourſcore: and the greater part (of the one or the other) is in labour and ſorow. And it is euident ( Deut. \+it 34\+it*. ) that Moyſes liued in al an hundred and tvventie yeares, and his eye was not dimme, neither were his teeth moued. So Aaron, Ioſue, and others commonly liued longer then is here mentioned. But Dauid was old and impotent at ſeuentie yeares. \+it 3. Reg. 1\+it*. S. Hilarion, liuing neere ſeuentie yeares in his heremitage, S. Remigius gouerning the Church of Rhemes, ſeuentie yeares, and the like are accounted to haue bene ful of dayes, and ſuch as liued longer are reputed extraordinarie. Agane it is more euidenly proued that Moyſes vvas not author of the \+it 94\+it*. and \+it 95\+it*. Pſalmes.\f* of Moyſes the man of God. Lord, thou art made a refuge for vs: \f b \fr 89.1 \ft Alwayes from the beginning of the world to the end.\f* from generation vnto generation.
\v 2 Before \f c \fr 89.2 \ft The Prophet sheweth that the world was created in and with time, not eternal.\f* the mountaines were made, or the earth and the world formed: \f d \fr 89.2 \ft And that only God is eternal.\f* from euerlaſting euen vnto euerlaſting thou art God.
\v 3 \f e \fr 89.3 \ft God hath often ſaide, that he vvould not the death of ſinners, but rather that they be conuerted and liue for euer.\f* Turne not away man into humiliation: thou ſaidſt: Be conuerted ye children of men.
\v 4 Becauſe \f f \fr 89.4 \ft Though ſome liued long ( none for al that did reach to a thouſand yeares ) yet it is nothing before God, and in reſpect of eternitie.\f* a thouſand years before thine eies, are as yeſterday, that is paſt. And as a watch in the night,
\v 5 thinges that are counted nothing shal their years be.
\v 6 In \f g \fr 89.6 \ft The youth of man quickly paſſeth:\f* the morning as an herbe he shal paſſe, in the morning he shal florish, and paſſe: in \f h \fr 89.6 \ft old age can not laſt long: wherof cometh our English prouerb: A young man may dye ſowne, an old man can not liue long.\f* the euening he shal fal, be hardened, and withered.
\v 7 Becauſe we haue faynted in thy wrath, and in thy furie we are trubled.
\v 8 Thou haſt put \f i \fr 89.8 \ft Sinne the cauſe of shortnes of mans life.\f* our iniquities in thy ſight: our age in the light of thy countinance. \f 1 \fr 89.8 \ft \+it Sæculum.\+it*\f*
\v 9 Becauſe al our daies haue failed: and in thy wrath we haue failed. Our yeares shal be conſidered \f k \fr 89.9 \ft ſpider. Man's life as brittle as a ſpider's web: or man's life vvaſteth continually, as a ſpider waſteth herſelf by ſpinning, and conſuming her own ſubſtance.\f* as a ſpyder:
\v 10 the daies of our yeares in them, are \f l \fr 89.10 \ft Theſe numbers literarly shew the ſhortnes of the longer ſorte of mens liues. Myſtically, ſeuen ſignifie the reſt after laboures of this world, and perteyn to the old teſtament: eight ſignifie the revvard in the reſurrection, perteyning to the nevv teſtament. VVhich multiplied by tenne, a perfect number, make ſeuentie and eightie. VVhich ioyned together make an hundred and fiftie. The number of al theſe Pſalmes.\f* ſeuentie yeares. And if in ſtrong ones \f m \fr 89.10 \ft See previous note\f* eightie years: and the more of them, labour and ſorrow. Becauſe \f n \fr 89.10 \ft It is of Gods milde prouidence, that mans life is ſhort, for that manie if they vvere ſure, or had probabilitie to liue long, vvould preſume to ſinne more.\f* mildnes is come vpon vs: and we shal be chaſtiſed.
\v 11 \f o \fr 89.11 \ft Seing God of his iuſtice puniſhed al mankind, for one ſinne of our firſt parente, his wrath muſt nedes be very great to euerie ſinner, for his ovvne proper ſinnes.\f* Who knoweth the powre of thy wrath: and for feare
\v 12 to number thy wrath? So make thy righthand knowne: and men learned in hart, in wiſedome.
\v 13 Turne ô Lord, how long? and be intreated for thy ſeruants.
\v 14 \f p \fr 89.14 \ft The hope of glorious reſurrection turneth our calamities into ſpiritual ioy.\f* We are replenished in the morning with thy mercie: and we haue reioyced, and are delighted al our daies.
\v 15 \f q \fr 89.15 \ft Yea the more we ſuffer in this life for the truth, the greater is our comforth in hope of reward.\f* We haue reioyced for the daies wherin thou haſt humbled vs: the yeares, wherin we haue ſeene euils.
\v 16 Looke vpon \f r \fr 89.16 \ft Not only in that we are thy creatures, but alſo in that we are thy ſeruants, we are thy proper worke, therfore in both theſe reſpectes, ô God looke vpon vs with clemencie.\f* thy ſeruants, and vpon thy workes: and \f s \fr 89.16 \ft lead alſo our poſteritie into the right way, and make them thy ſeruantes.\f* direct their children.
\v 17 And \f t \fr 89.17 \ft O God illuminate our vnderſtanding,\f* let the brightnes of our Lord God be vpon vs, and \f v \fr 89.17 \ft make our actions by thy grace profitable to vs:\f* direct thou the workes of our handes ouer vs: and \f w \fr 89.17 \ft and make perfect in vs the worke of charitie. In which one worke, al good workes are included and to which al other are directed. For then workes are right ( ſayth S. Auguſtin ) when they are directed to this one end.\f* the worke of our handes doe thou direct.
\c 90
\cd Whoſoeuer faithfully and firmly truſteth in Gods prouidence, is ſecure from al dangers of ſecrete, ſutle, and open enimies. 7. his aduerſaries shal come to ruine. 11. Angels shal defend him. 13. no kind of ſerpent, nor beaſt shal hurt him. 14. God himſelf aſſureth him of his protection, and of eternal ſaluation. \f - \ft God's providence. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 \f a \fr 90.1 \ft Praiſe of Gods prouidence, with thankes,\f* Prayſe of a \f b \fr 90.1 \ft Which Dauid ſonge with voice.\f* Canticle to Dauid. He \f c \fr 90.1 \ft He that firmely relieth and reſteth vpon Gods prouidence, is aſſuredly protected by him.\f* that dwelleth in the helpe of the Higheſt, shal abide in the protection of the God of heauen.
\v 2 He shal ſay to our Lord: Thou art my protectour, and my refuge: my God I wil hope in him.
\v 3 Becauſe he hath deliuered me from \f d \fr 90.3 \ft Al ſecret and ſutle machinations :\f* the ſnare of the hunters, and from \f e \fr 90.3 \ft and from al crueltie of tyrants.\f* the sharpe word.
\v 4 With his shoulders shal he ouershadowe thee: and vnder his winges thou shalt hope.
\v 5 With shilde shal his truth compaſſe thee: thou shalt not be afrayed \f f \fr 90.5 \ft Terrors obſcurly ſuggeſted by euil men or ſpirites, with erronious conceipte that men are not bond in time of temporal dangers, to confeſſe the truth.\f* of the feare in the night.
\v 6 Of \f g \fr 90.6 \ft Open perſecution threatning preſent death, except men denie the truth which they know.\f* the arrow flying in the day, \f h \fr 90.6 \ft circumuention of craftie enimies by ſutle arguing, and drawing men into error, and ſo to decline from Catholique Religion,\f* of buſines walking in darkenes: \f i \fr 90.6 \ft long torments, euen to death, except Gods ſeruants wil relent, and denie the truth, which they aſſuredly beleue, and know in their conſcience, that they are bond to profeſſe it.\f* of inuaſion, and the midday diuel.
\v 7 A \f k \fr 90.7 \ft On thy left ſide, in aduerſitie manie fal from God,\f* thouſand shal fal on thy ſyde, & \f l \fr 90.7 \ft & on thy right ſide, in proſperitie manie more forgete, and forſake God.\f* ten thouſand: on thy righthand: but to thee it shal not approch.
\v 8 But thou shalt conſider with thine eies: and shalt ſee the retribution of ſinners.
\v 9 Becauſe \f m \fr 90.9 \ft In ſincerely ſaying thou art my hope: thou makeſt God thy refuge.\f* thou ô Lord art my hope: thou haſt made the Higheſt thy refuge.
\v 10 There shal no euil come to thee: and ſcourge shal not approch to thy tabernacle.
\v 11 Becauſe he hath geuen \f n \fr 90.11 \ft Angels haue protection of men by Gods ordinance .\f* his Angels charge of thee: \f o \fr 90.11 \ft The diuel corruptly alleageth this ſcripture (Mat. 4) omitting the latter part of this verſe: which ſheweth when Angels protect iuſt men, to witte, when they walke in a right path, obſeruing ordinarie courſe in their actions, not in geuing themſelues headlong into needles danger, as the ſame diuel propoſed to our Sauiour, to caſt himſelf downe from the pinnacle of the temple. Such falling is not the way of the iuſt, but of Lucifer, that fel from heauen. So S. Bernard noteth Ser. 15. in hunc Pſal.\f* that they keepe thee in al thy waies.
\v 12 In their handes they shal beare thee: leſt perhaps thou knocke thy foote againſt a ſtone.
\v 13 Vpon the aſpe, and the baſiliſcus thou shalt walke: & thou shalt tread vpon the lion, and the dragon.
\v 14 \f p \fr 90.14 \ft God ſpeaketh the reſt that foloweth in this Pſalme.\f* Becauſe he hath hoped in me, I wil deliuer him: I wil protect him, becauſe he hath knowne my name.
\v 15 He shal crie to me, and I wil heare him: with him I am in tribulation: I wil deliuer him, and \f q \fr 90.15 \ft In eternal ſaluation.\f* wil glorifie him.
\v 16 With length of daies I wil replenish him: and I wil shew him my ſaluation.
\c 91
\cd God is by al maner of voices and inſtruments to be praiſed in his admirable workes. 7. which the foolish not doing are punished; 11. and the wiſe are rewarded. \f - \ft The works of God admirable. The 2. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Pſalme \f a \fr 91.1 \ft Voices beginning inſtrumentes proſecute this ſong:\f* of Canticle, \f b \fr 91.1 \ft when we reſt from worke then eſpecially vve ought to thincke vpon Gods vvorkes, praiſe and thanke him for the ſame.\f* in the ſabbath day.
\v 2 IT is good \f c \fr 91.2 \ft To geue thankes.\f* to confeſſe to our Lord; and to ſing to thy name ô Higheſt.
\v 3 To shewforth thy mercie in \f d \fr 91.3 \ft In proſperitie,\f* the morning: and thy truth in \f e \fr 91.3 \ft in aduerſitie.\f* the night.
\v 4 In \f f \fr 91.4 \ft On euerie inſtrument of tenne ſtringes, ſignifying the obſeruation of the tenne commandments:\f* the inſtrument of tenſtrings, in \f g \fr 91.4 \ft namely on the Pſalter.\f* Pſalter: with Canticle, on \f h \fr 91.4 \ft alſo on the harpe, which ſignifieth mortification.\f* the Harpe.
\v 5 Becauſe thou haſt delighted me ô Lord in thy workemanship: and in the workes of thy handes I wil reioyce.
\v 6 How are thy workes magnified ô Lord! thy cogitations are made very profounde.
\v 7 The \f i \fr 91.7 \ft Carnal and ſenſual man,\f* vnwiſe man wil not know; and the \f k \fr 91.7 \ft he that thinketh only of preſent thinges, not of future.\f* foule wil not vnderſtand theſe thinges.
\v 8 When ſinners shal ſpring vp as graſſe: and al that worke iniquitie shal appeare. That they may perish for euer:
\v 9 but thou the Higheſt for euer ô Lord.
\v 10 Becauſe loe thine enimies ô Lord, becauſe loe thine enimies shal perish: and al that worke iniquitie shal be diſperſed.
\v 11 And \f l \fr 91.11 \ft The iuſt in confidence of a good conſcience, expect exaltation of their powre:\f* my horne shal be exalted, as the vnicorns: \f m \fr 91.11 \ft and great conſolation in the end of their life.\f* and my old age in plentiful mercie.
\v 12 And \f n \fr 91.12 \ft Then shal the iuſt ſee their enimies depreſſed: and themſelues florish, like the palme, and ceder trees as folovveth.\f* mine eie hath looked vpon mine enimies: and the malignant ryſing vp againſt me, mine ears shal heare.
\v 13 The iuſt, shal florish as a palme tree: as the ceder of Libanus shal he be multiplied.
\v 14 They that are planted in \f o \fr 91.14 \ft Militant Church:\f* the houſe of our Lord, shal florish in \f p \fr 91.14 \ft triumphant.\f* the courtes of the houſe of our God.
\v 15 As yet shal they be multiplied in plentiful old age: and they shal be wel affected,
\v 16 that they \f q \fr 91.16 \ft Publikly profeſſe Gods praiſes, as in the wordes folowing:\f* may shewforth: That the Lord our God is righteous, and there is no iniquitie in him.
\c 92
\cd Chriſt reigneth for euer in his Church: 3. notwithſtanding manie and great perſecution againſt the faithful. \f - \ft Perpetuitie of the Church. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Prayſe \f a \fr 92.1 \ft Praiſe to be ſongue vvith voice:\f* of Canticle \f b \fr 92.1 \ft compoſed by Dauid:\f* to Dauid himſelfe, in \f c \fr 92.1 \ft the ſixth day of the weeke, vvhich is our friday,\f* the day before the ſabbath, \f d \fr 92.1 \ft in vvhich day the Church of Chriſt vvas founded, by his bloud ſhed on the croſſe.\f* when the earth was founded. OVr Lord \f e \fr 92.1 \ft Our Sauiour founding his Church by his death begane then to reigne therin:\f* hath reigned, he \f f \fr 92.1 \ft glorioſly deſcending a ſoule into limbus, and in bodie to his graue:\f* hath put on beutie: our Lord hath \f g \fr 92.1 \ft he then put on al armour of ſtrength to reforme the world, and to inlarge his kingdom, according to his owne prediction where he ſaide: If I be exalted from the earth, I wil draw al thinges vnto myſelfe. Ioan. \+it 12. v. 31\+it*.\f* put on ſtrength, and hath girded him ſelfe. For he hath eſtablished \f h \fr 92.1 \ft Not only in Iurie and Samaria, but the whole earth:\f* the round world, which \f i \fr 92.1 \ft and the ſame Church ſhal not be deſtroyed.\f* shal not be moued.
\v 2 Thy ſeat is prepared from that time: thou \f k \fr 92.2 \ft Chriſt being eternal, hath an euerlaſting Church.\f* art from euerlaſting.
\v 3 The \f l \fr 92.3 \ft Al ſortes of perſecuters, the High prieſtes (who ſometimes vvatered the ſpiritual land, like riuers) vvith Scribes, Phariſes, and other incredulous Ievves, alſo Paganes, Turkes, and Heretikes haue oppugned the Church.\f* riuers ô Lord haue lifted vp: the riuers haue lifted vp their voice. The riuers haue lifted vp their waues,
\v 4 \f m \fr 92.4 \ft With more force then anie perſecutions in the old Teſtament.\f* aboue the voices of manie waters. The ſurges of the ſea are meruelous; \f n \fr 92.4 \ft but though al theſe aſſaultes be great, and meruelous, yet Chriſt in protecting his Church, is more meruelous.\f* meruelous is our Lord on high.
\v 5 Thy teſtimonies are made \f o \fr 92.5 \ft Articles of faith are not euidently apparent to knovvlege, but euident to credibilitie, to thoſe that are diſpoſed by Gods grace, illuminating their vnderſtanding, and mouing their free vvil, to geue conſent of beleefe if they vvil\f* credible exceedingly: \f p \fr 92.5 \ft It behoueth therfore al members of the Church to conuerſe piouſly, and religiouſly in this life, ſeing she hath ſo excellent a ſpouſe, protector, and inſtructor,\f* holines becometh thy houſe ô Lord \f q \fr 92.5 \ft euen to the end of the vvorld.\f* for length of daies.
\c 93
\cd The faithful ſeruant of God aſſuredly profeſſeth, that al the pride, 5. crueltie, 7. foolish imaginations, and ſecret thoughts of the wicked are manifeſt to God, 12. acknowlegeth himſelf happie, that he is better inſtructed of God: wheras he had otherwiſe bene damned. 20. sharply reprehendeth thoſe that conſider not of Gods iudgements: concluding that the iuſt shal be glorified, and the wicked damned. \f - \ft Eternal ſalvation and damnation. The 10. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 To \f a \fr 93.1 \ft The Hebrevv letter Lamed, vvhich ordinarily is prefixed to the datiue caſe, or ſignifieth to, being ſet before proper names, is a ſigne of the genetiue caſe. Yet the Septuagint, expreſſe it by the datiue, and ſo doth the latin, \+it ipſi\+it* Dauid, and conſequently our Engliſh hath, to Dauid himſelfe, to shevv a difference betvven ſacred and profane vvriters. For in humane bookes the vvriter and auctor is al one; but in diuine, the Holie Ghoſt is the proper auctor, and a man is the vvriter. To ſignifie therfore the principal auctor, Dauid is ſometimes named as the inſtrumental cauſe, to vvhom the Holie Ghoſt inſpired this, and other Pſalmes, and by vvhom they vvere vvritten. And vvhen the titles expreſſe othervviſe: A Pſalme of Dauid, yet it is ſo to be vnderſtood, that the Holie Ghoſt is alvvayes the principal auctor, and Dauid the inſtrumental, miniſterial, or ſecondarie auctor. But vvhen other names are expreſſed, either in the genetiue or datiue caſe, or hovvſoeuer, it proueth not that thoſe men vvere the vvriters of the ſame Pſalmes, but importeth ſome other thing, as by S. Auguſtins iudgement, vve noted in the proemial Annotations page 3. & 4. vvherby is proued that this Pſalme vvas not written, nor compoſed by Moyſes, as Hebrevv Rabbins ſuppoſe, but by the Royal Pſalmiſt Dauid.\f* Dauid himſelfe, in \f b \fr 93.1 \ft Made and ordinarily ſongue in the fourth day of the vveke, our vveneſday, in vvhich day Iudas the traitor ſold our Sauiour Chriſt to his enimies. The reuenge of vvhich vvickednes, and of al other ſinnes, is here prophecied.\f* the fourth of the ſabbath. OVr Lord God of reuenges: the God \f c \fr 93.1 \ft God more commonly called the God of mercie (vvhich vertue in him is aboue al his vvorkes, Pſal. 144) is alſo the God of reuenges, according to his iuſtice.\f* of reuenges hath done \f d \fr 93.1 \ft He proceedeth in iudgement reſolutly, not depending, nor fearing, not reſpecting anie perſon, povvre, dignitie, vviſdome or other like qualitie, but their iuſt merites.\f* freely.
\v 2 Be \f 1 \fr 93.2 \ft A prayer of iuſt zele,\f* exalted thou that iudgeſt the earth: render retribution to the proude.
\v 3 How long shal ſinners ô Lord: how long shal ſinners glorie?
\v 4 Shal they vtter, and ſpeake iniquitie? shal al they ſpeake, \f e \fr 93.4 \ft Shal moſt wicked men ſtil be ſuffered to ſpeake ſo inſolently?\f* that worke iniuſtice?
\v 5 Thy \f 1 \fr 93.5 \ft A deſcription of heathnish and heretical crueltie.\f* people ô Lord they haue humbled: and thine inheritance they haue vexed.
\v 6 The widow, and the ſtranger they haue ſlaine: and the pupilles they haue killed.
\v 7 And \f 1 \fr 93.7 \ft Scarſe anie Atheiſtes are ſo blind, as thus to thinke but manie ſinners ſo behaue themſelues, as if God ſaw not, knew nor, or at leaſt cared not vvhat they do.\f* they haue ſaide: The Lord shal not ſee, neither shal the God of Iacob vnderſtand.
\v 8 Vnderſtand ye foolish in the people: and ye fooles be wiſe at ſometime.
\v 9 He that planted the eare, shal he not heare? Or he that made the eie doth he not conſider?
\v 10 He that chaſtiſeth nations, shal he not rebuke: he that teacheth man knowledge?
\v 11 Our Lord knoweth \f f \fr 93.11 \ft So vnpoſſible is it that God should be ignorant, or careles vvhat men do, that he alſo knovveth and obſerueth moſt ſecret thoughtes.\f* the cogitations of men: that they be vaine.
\v 12 Bleſſed is the man, whom thou shalt inſtruct ô Lord: and shalt teach out of thy lawe.
\v 13 That thou maiſt \f g \fr 93.13 \ft Mitigate and temper his afflictions, that by patience and fortitude, the iuſt may perſeuere, and not be ouervvhelmed.\f* geue him quietnes from the euil daies: til a pitte be digged for the ſinner.
\v 14 Becauſe our Lord \f h \fr 93.14 \ft The whole Church shal neuer be reiected, nor forſaken.\f* wil not reiect his people: and his inheritance he wil not forſake.
\v 15 Vntil iuſtice \f i \fr 93.15 \ft Iuſtice is conuerted into iudgement, vvhen iuſt meaning is put in vvorke and practiſe, that it may appeare in iudgement. Alſo God vvho doth & ſuffereth al iuſtly, vvil conſerue his inheritance the Church, euen vnto the day of iudgement.\f* be turned into iudgement: and \f k \fr 93.15 \ft The ſenſe is eaſie by tranſpoſing the vvordes: al that are right of hart, are nere it, that is, ſhal like and approue Gods iuſtice, vvhen the vvicked shal repine, and blaſpheme it.\f* they who are neere it, are al that are right of hart.
\v 16 Who shal riſe for me againſt the malignant? or who shal ſtand with me againſt them that worke iniquitie?
\v 17 But that our Lord hath holpen me: within very litle my ſoule had dwelt in hel.
\v 18 If I ſaid: \f l \fr 93.18 \ft When I felt, and complained that I was in danger, thou didſt aſſiſt me.\f* My foote is moued: thy mercie ô Lord did help me
\v 19 According to the multitude of my ſorrowes in my hart: thy conſolations haue made my ſoule ioyful.
\v 20 Doth the ſeat of iniquitie cleaue to thee: which makeſt \f m \fr 93.20 \ft Onlie faith ſufficeth not, but careful laboure, in keping Gods commandmnts is required.\f* labour in precept?
\v 21 They wil hunt after the ſoule of the iuſt: and wil condemne innocent bloud.
\v 22 And our Lord became my refuge: and my God the helpe \f n \fr 93.22 \ft The iuſt do hope for eternal ſaluation, to which God wil bring them.\f* of my hope.
\v 23 And he \f o \fr 93.23 \ft And God, the reuenger of vvronges, vvil at laſt caſt the wicked into eternal torments.\f* wil repay them their iniquitie: and in their malice he wil deſtroy them: the Lord our God wil deſtroy them.
\c 94
\cd An inuitation to ſerue and adore Chriſt our Lord and Meſſias, 3. aswel for the benefites of creating al thinges, 7. as for his Incarnation, and not to harden our hartes as the Iewes did. \f - \ft Chriſt our Lord and King. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Praiſe \f a \fr 94.1 \ft Praiſe ſongue with voices:\f* of Canticle, \f b \fr 94.1 \ft inſpired to Dauid, & written by him.\f* to Dauid him ſelfe. COME, \f 1 \fr 94.1 \ft This Inuitation is moſt fitly ordayned by the Church for the proeme or beginning of Mattins.\f* let vs \f c \fr 94.1 \ft VVith great and ſolemne exultation:\f* reioyce to our Lord: let vs make iubilation to God \f d \fr 94.1 \ft God our Creator, is alſo our Protector & Sauiour.\f* our ſauiour.
\v 2 Let vs \f e \fr 94.2 \ft Let vs be more diligent, and preuent our accuſtomed time. For no man can preuent Gods grace with anie good worke, who firſt preuenteth vs; els we can neither doe, nor thincke anie good thing.\f* preuent his face in confeſſion: and \f f \fr 94.2 \ft not only in ſinging his praiſe with voice, but alſo with muſical inſtruments.\f* in Pſalmes let vs make iubilation to him.
\v 3 Becauſe our Lord is a great God: and a great King aboue al goddes.
\v 4 Becauſe in his hand are the endes of the earth: and the heightes of the mountaines be his.
\v 5 Becauſe the ſea is his, and he made it: and his handes formed the drie land.
\v 6 Come let vs adore, \f g \fr 94.6 \ft So alſo Iſaias \+it (c. 45. v. 23\+it*.) and S. Paul \+it (Philip. 2\+it*.) teach that kneeling or bowing the knees, as an external religious ceremonie, is acceptable to God.\f* and fal downe: and wepe before our Lord, that made vs.
\v 7 Becauſe he is the Lord \f h \fr 94.7 \ft It is moſt iuſt and neceſſarie that we adore God, becauſe he made vs, and al this world for vs, hath alſo redemed vs, and made vs his people, as ſhepe of his paſture, and as a Paſtor feedeth and gouerneth vs.\f* our God; and we the people of his paſture, and the shepe \f i \fr 94.7 \ft of his making.\f* of his hand.
\v 8 \f k \fr 94.8 \ft Though ſome haue often repelled, and reſiſted Gods grace, yet if they receiue it being offered againe, it wil auaile them to remiſſion of ſinnes.\f* To day if ye shal heare his voice, harden not your hartes;
\v 9 As in the prouocation according to the day \f l \fr 94.9 \ft The Iſraelites in the deſert tempted God, by deſiring water, and flesh, of voluptuous concupiſcence without neceſſitie. For Manna did both extinguiſh their thirſt, and taſted vnto them, whatſoeuer they deſired: Exo. \+it 16\+it*. That alſo which was left vngathered when the ſunne waxed hotte, melted \+it (v. 21\+it*.) and ſerued their cattel for drincke. So this tentation was a figure of thoſe, which require to communicate vnder both kindes, as if one did not conteine as much as both.\f* of the tentation in the deſert: where your fathers tempted me, proued me, and ſaw my workes.
\v 10 \f m \fr 94.10 \ft By this mention of the offence of fourtie yeares, as long before paſſed, is conuinced that Moyſes writte not this Pſalme, who died in the very fourtith yeare of their abode in the deſert. And S. Paul citing the wordes of this Pſalme. (Heb. \+it 4\+it*.) manifeſtly acknowlegeth Dauid the writer therof, and that it was written long after Moyſes time in theſe wordes: \+it (v. 7\+it*.) Againe he limiteth a certaine day; To day, in Dauid ſaying after ſo long time, s is aboue ſaide. To day if you shal heare his voice, do not obdurate your hartes. For if Ieſus (that is Ioſue) had geuen them reſt, he would neuer ſpeake of an other day afterward.\f* Fourtie years was I \f n \fr 94.10 \ft Being greatly offended, I approched nere vnto them, in puniſhing the offenders.\f* offended with that generation, and ſaid: Theſe alwaies erre in hart.
\v 11 And theſe haue not knowne my waies: as I ſware in my wrath: \f o \fr 94.11 \ft Thoſe that murmured died in the deſert, and entered not into the promiſed land, euen ſo thoſe that finally offend Chriſt, ſhal not enter into euerlaſting reſt: Heb. \+it 3\+it*. & \+it 4\+it*.\f* if they shal enter into my reſt.
\c 95
\cd Al peoples & nations are inuited to praiſe the bleſſed Trinitie, 3. for Chriſts Incarnation, and ſpiritual kingdom in al the world. 10. euen ſenſles creatures acknowledging his maieſtie, 13. and iudicial powre. \f - \ft Chriſt's divine power. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Canticle \f a \fr 95.1 \ft Inſpired to Dauid, and written by him:\f* to Dauid \x - \xt 1. Par. 16. v. 23.\x* himſelfe, \f b \fr 95.1 \ft prophecying the reſtauration of the temple, after the future captiuitie. And that in figure of the vniuerſal redemption of mankind by Chriſt, from the captiuitie of the diuel.\f* when the houſe was built after the captiuitie. SING ye to our Lord \f c \fr 95.1 \ft For a new benefite, farre greater then the deliuerie of Iſrael from Ægypt.\f* a new ſong: ſing to our Lord al the earth.
\v 2 \f d \fr 95.2 \ft The ſame wordes [Sing to our Lord] thriſe repeted, ſignifie the Bleſſed Trinitie, as ſome Fathers note. Likewiſe \+it v. 7\+it*. and \+it 8\+it*. Bring ye to our Lord, &c. in both places concluding in the ſingular number, bleſſe his name, bring to his name, importing one God.\f* Sing ye to our Lord, and bleſſe his name: shewforth his ſaluation from day to day.
\v 3 Shewforth his glorie among the Gentiles, his meruelous workes in al peoples.
\v 4 Becauſe our Lord is great, and exceeding laudable: he is terrible aboue al goddes.
\v 5 Becauſe al the goddes of the Gentiles are \f e \fr 95.5 \ft VVhat creatures ſoeuer ſpiritual or corporal, viſible or inuiſible the paganes ſerue for goddes, ſtil they be diuels that deceiue them, and diuers wayes vſurpe diuine honour, making ſuch idolaters to thinke, that there is diuine powre, where none is.\f* diuels, but our Lord \f f \fr 95.5 \ft He only is true God, who is Creator of heauen, and of al creatures. For no creature can create anie thing at al, that is, make anie thing of nothing but only God.\f* made the heauens.
\v 6 Confeſſion, and beauty in his ſight: holineſſe, and magnificence in his ſanctification.
\v 7 Bring to our Lord ye families of Gentiles, bring ye to our Lord glorie and honour:
\v 8 bring to our Lord glorie vnto his name. Take vp hoaſtes, and enter into his courtes:
\v 9 adore ye our Lord in his holie court. Let al the earth be moued before his face:
\v 10 ſay ye among the Gentiles that our Lord \f g \fr 95.10 \ft Diuers ancient Doctors read more in this place: Our Lord hath reigned from the wood, to witte, Chriſt by his death on the croſſe conquered the diuel, ſinne, and death, and thence begane to reigne. S. Iuſtinus Martyr, dialogo aduers. Triphonem. Tertullian li. aduers. Iudæos. c. 9. & 13 & aduerſ Marcionem. li 1. c. 19. & 21. S. Auguſtin in this place, according to the old Roman Pſalter Before him Arnobius, and after him Caſſiadorus and others, wherby it is probable, that it was ſometimes in the Hebrew text, and blotted out by the Iewes.\f* hath reigned. For he hath corrected the round world which shal not be moued: he wil iudge peoples in equitie.
\v 11 \f h \fr 95.11 \ft The Pſalmiſt in abundance of ſpirite inuiteth al creatures to praiſe God, as Daniel in his Canticle. \+it c. 3\+it*.\f* Let the heauens be glad, and the earth reioyce, the ſea be moued, and the fulneſſe therof:
\v 12 the fieldes shal be glad, and al things, that are in them. Then shal the trees of the woodes reioyce
\v 13 before the face of our Lord, becauſe he cometh: becauſe he cometh to iudge the earth. He \f i \fr 95.13 \ft Chriſt iudgeth now in the world by his miniſters diſcerning and deciding cauſes, rewarding and puniſhing, but eſpecially he wil iudge al in the laſt day.\f* wil iudge the round world in equitie, and peoples in his truth.
\c 96
\cd Al the earth is inuited to reioyce in Chriſts kingdom, 3. with deſcription of the ſignes coming before the day of Iudgement. 7. Idolaters shal be confounded. 8. Holie Angels and iuſt men shal adore Chriſt, and reioyce. \f - \ft The laſt iudgement, The 9. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 To \f a \fr 96.1 \ft In figure of Chriſt,\f* this Dauid, \f b \fr 96.1 \ft whoſe bodie roſe the third day after his death: to whom manie returned beleuing in him after his reſurrection, which fel from him in his paſſion: and to whom al thinges shal be ſubdued, as to their true Lord, in the day of iudgement.\f* when his land was reſtored agane to him. OVr Lord hath reigned, let the earth reioyce: let \f c \fr 96.1 \ft Holie Dauid, and other Prophetes hauing great ioy to ſee long before in ſpirite only, Chriſts kingdom extended in the whole earth, yea to the Ilandes, we Ilanders haue great cauſe to be gladde, that God hath not only ſo bleſſed vs long ſince, but as yet conſerueth ſeede, wherby we truſt the whole Iland ſhal be againe reſtored vnto him.\f* manie Ilands be glad.
\v 2 \f d \fr 96.2 \ft As in a cloud with terror God gaue his law to the Iewes: ſo in a cloud with greater terror and maieſtie, he wil iudge the world;\f* Cloude, and miſt round about him: iuſtice, and iudgement \f e \fr 96.2 \ft not as manie corrupted ſeates of iudgement, in this world, but as a corrected tribunal, where iuſtice and right iudgement ſhal be practiſed.\f* the correction of his ſeat.
\v 3 Fire shal goe before him, and shal inflame his enimies round about.
\v 4 His lightninges \f 1 \fr 96.4 \ft Theſe thinges are denounced as if they were alredy donne, for the aſſured certaintie therof.\f* shined to the round world, the earth ſawe, and was moued,
\v 5 The mountaines melted as waxe, before the face of our Lord: before the face of our Lord al the earth.
\v 6 The heauens haue shewed forth his iuſtice: and al peoples haue ſeene his glorie.
\v 7 Let them al be confounded, that adore \f f \fr 96.7 \ft As wel the worſhippers of grauen, or painted images of Iupiter, Mars, Bacchus and the like, as the worſhippers of the ſame imagined falſe goddes shal be confounded.\f* ſculptils: and that glorie in their idoles. Adore him al ye his Angels:
\v 8 \f g \fr 96.8 \ft The Catholique Church.\f* Sion heard, and was glad. And \f h \fr 96.8 \ft And al particular Churches, members of the vniuerſal.\f* the daughters of Iuda reioyced, becauſe of thy iudgements ô Lord.
\v 9 Becauſe thou Lord moſt high ouer al the earth: thou art exalted excedingly aboue al goddes.
\v 10 You that loue our Lord, hate ye euil: our Lord keepeth the ſoules of his ſaintes, out of the hand of the ſinner he wil deliuer them.
\v 11 Light is riſen to the iuſt, and ioy to the right of hart.
\v 12 Be glad ye iuſt in our Lord: and confeſſe ye to the memorie \f i \fr 96.12 \ft Praiſe our Lord Chriſt who is ſanctitie it ſelfe, and ſanctifieth others.\f* of his ſanctification.
\c 97
\cd Al men are againe inuited ioyfully to celebrate the meruelous conqueſt of Chriſt in al nations, 4. with hart, voice, and inſtruments. 8. al creatures acknowledging his coming to iudge the world. \f - \ft The Church in al nations. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A pſalme \f a \fr 97.1 \ft Prefiguring Chriſt, who hath made his ſaluation knowen in al nations.\f* to Dauid himſelfe. SING ye to our Lord \f b \fr 97.1 \ft A new benefite of grace, making men new in ſpirite, requireth a new ſongue of gratitude.\f* a new ſong: becauſe he hath done meruelous thinges. His righthand hath wrought ſaluation \f c \fr 97.1 \ft Raiſed vp himſelfe from death.\f* to himſelfe: and his arme is holie.
\v 2 Our Lord hath \f d \fr 97.2 \ft Made his grace effectually knowen by raiſing men from ſinne, and deliuering them from the powre of the diuel.\f* made knowne his ſaluation: in the ſight of the Gentiles he hath reueled his iuſtice.
\v 3 He hath remembred his mercie, and his truth to \f e \fr 97.3 \ft Some of the Iewes conuerted to Chriſtianitie. Rom. \+it 11\+it*.\f* the houſe of Iſrael. Al the ends of the earth haue ſeene the ſaluation of our God.
\v 4 Make ye iubilation to God al the earth: \f f \fr 97.4 \ft In voice.\f* chaunt, and \f g \fr 97.4 \ft In hart.\f* reioyce, and \f h \fr 97.4 \ft In inſtruments.\f* ſing. \f 1 \fr 97.4 \ft \+it Cantat, exultate, pſallite.\+it*\f*
\v 5 Sing to our Lord on harpe, on harpe and voice of pſalme:
\v 6 on long drawen trumpets, and voice of cornet of horne. Make iubilation in the ſight of the king our Lord:
\v 7 let the ſea be moued, and the fulnes therof: the round world, and they that dwel therin:
\v 8 The riuers shal clappe with hand, the mountaynes together shal reioyce
\v 9 at the ſight of our Lord: \f i \fr 97.9 \ft Chriſt directeth and diſpoſeth al thinges rightly in this world.\f* becauſe he cometh to iudge the earth. \f k \fr 97.9 \ft And wil accordingly geue iuſt ſentence in the end.\f* He wil iudge the round earth in iuſtice, and the peoples in equitie.
\c 98
\cd Chriſt reigneth, notwithſtanding his enimies repine, is adored (5. alſo his footeſtoole) 6. whom ancient Prophetes did inuocate. \f - \ft Chriſt our Meſſias. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Pſalme to Dauid himſelfe. OVr Lord hath reigned, \f a \fr 98.1 \ft Though manie enimies do rage, and impugne Chriſt:\f* let peoples be angrie: he that ſitteth vpon the Cherubs, let \f b \fr 98.1 \ft though the whole earth be trubled thervvith, yet Chriſt vvho ſitteth Lord ouer the higheſt Angels, Cherubins and Seraphins, obteyneth the victorie, reigneth, and doth his vvil in al the earth.\f* the earth be moued.
\v 2 Our Lord great in Sion: and high aboue al peoples.
\v 3 Let them confeſſe to thy great name: becauſe it is terrible, and holie.
\v 4 And the honour of the king \f c \fr 98.4 \ft Requireth diſcretion.\f* loueth iudgement. Thou haſt prepared directions: thou haſt done iudgement and iuſtice \f d \fr 98.4 \ft in fauour of thyn elect people.\f* in Iacob.
\v 5 Exalt ye the Lord our God, and adore \f e \fr 98.5 \ft Hebrevv Doctors expound this of the Arke in the old teſtament, but the Doctors of the Church vnderſtand Chriſts humanitie, in the holie Euchariſt.\f* his footſtoole: becauſe it is holie.
\v 6 Moyſes, and Aaron in his \f f \fr 98.6 \ft Here it is euident (and S. Auguſtin ſayth this place taketh avvay al doubt) that Moyſes vvas a Prieſt, againſt thoſe that for maintaining the hereſie of Laicheadshippe, denie it.\f* prieſtes: and Samuel among them, that inuocate his name: They inuocated our Lord \f g \fr 98.6 \ft By example of their praying and obtaining, the Pſalmiſt confirmeth his prophecie, that Prieſtes of the new Teſtament ſhal pray, and obtaine mercie of Chriſt for the Church.\f* and he heard them:
\v 7 in a piller of a cloud he ſpake to them. They kept his teſtimonies, & the precept which he gaue them.
\v 8 O Lord our God thou heardeſt them: God thou waſt propitious to them, and taking vengeance vpon al \f h \fr 98.8 \ft God reuenged the machinations made againſt them, punishing the rebellion of chore, Dathan and Abyron. Num. 16.\f* their inuentions.
\v 9 Exalt ye the Lord our God, and adore ye in his holie mount: becauſe the Lord our God is holie.
\c 99
\cd Al are inuited to reioyce in God, Creator of al. \f - \ft One Creator of all things. The 1. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Pſalme \f a \fr 99.1 \ft of praiſe.\f* in confeſſion.
\v 2 MAKE ye iubilation to God \f b \fr 99.2 \ft Not only Iewes, but alſo al Gentiles.\f* al the earth: ſerue ye our Lord in gladneſſe. Enter ye in \f c \fr 99.2 \ft God eueryvvhere preſent, yet more peculiarly heareth his ſuppliants, praying in the temple, or place dedicated to his ſeruice.\f* before his ſight, in exulration.
\v 3 Know ye that our Lord \f d \fr 99.3 \ft He only whom we ſerue as our Lord is the only God, and there is no other.\f* he is God: he made vs, and not we ourſelues. His people, and the sheepe of his paſture:
\v 4 enter ye into \f e \fr 99.4 \ft Peculiar dedicated place, as \+it v. 2\+it*.\f* his gates in confeſſion, his courtes in hymnes: confeſſe ye to him. Praiſe ye his name:
\v 5 becauſe our Lord is ſweete, his \f f \fr 99.5 \ft As God is alwayes merciful in geuing, and promiſing:\f* mercie for euer, and \f g \fr 99.5 \ft ſo he is euer faithful in performing.\f* his truth euen vnto generation and generation.
\c 100
\cd King Dauid gratfully celebrateth the two general diuine vertues, Mercie and Iuſtice: 2. by his owne example exhorteth al, eſpecially Superiors, to direct their wayes in ſinceritie, 4. and to ſeperate the wicked from conuerſation of the good. \f - \ft Inſtruction to gouerne. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Pſalme to Dauid himſelfe. MERCIE \f a \fr 100.1 \ft Theſe two capital diuine vertues are euer ioyned in al Gods vvorkes, for both vvhich experienced tovvards him ſelfe, the Pſalmiſt rendereth thankes and praiſes.\f* and iudgement I wil ſing to thee ô Lord: I wil ſing,
\v 2 and I \f b \fr 100.2 \ft I wil do myn endeuoure to know the immaculate vvay,\f* shal vnderſtand in the immaculate way, \f c \fr 100.2 \ft vvhich I can not do, but by thy grace coming vnto me. For by helpe therof I did as followeth.\f* when thou shalt come to me. I walked through in the inocencie of my hart, in the middes of my houſe.
\v 3 I did not propoſe before mine eies any vniuſt thing: I hated them that do preuarication.
\v 4 A peruerſe hart hath not cleaued to me: the malignant declining from me I knew not.
\v 5 \f d \fr 100.5 \ft That is, al and euerie one thus wickedly diſpoſed I abhorred.\f* One ſecretly detracting from his neighbour, him did I perſecute. One of a proud eye, and vnſatiable hart, with him I did not eate.
\v 6 Mine eies are towards the faithful of the earth that they may ſit with me. A man that walketh in the immaculate way, \f e \fr 100.6 \ft I kept ſuch vnder, as a ſeruant or ſlaue.\f* he did miniſter to me.
\v 7 He that doth proudly shal not dwel in the middes of my houſe: he that ſpeaketh vniuſt thinges, hath \f f \fr 100.7 \ft Proſpered not, gotte no benefite by me.\f* not directed in the ſight of mine eies.
\v 8 In \f g \fr 100.8 \ft Speedely and without delay I cutte of al diſordered people:\f* the morning did I kil al the ſinners of the earth: that I might deſtroy \f h \fr 100.8 \ft that others might not be corrupted by them.\f* out of the citie of our Lord, al thoſe that worke iniquitie.
\c 101
\cd A ſinner in affliction of mind prayeth God to deliuer him, 10. deſolate of al other helpe. 13. conceiueth comforth in Gods eternal goodnes, and ſingular mercie, in redeming mankind, and propagating the Church. 24. Prayeth to be made mature in vertue before he dye, that he may liue with God: 26. who only and wholly being immutable, eſtablisheth his ſeruantes for euer. \f - \ft The fifth penitential Pſalm. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 The prayer of the poore, when he shal be anxious, and shal make his petition before our Lord.
\v 2 LORD heare my \f a \fr 101.2 \ft Euerie petition is a prayer,\f* prayer: and let my \f b \fr 101.2 \ft and that which proceedeth from more feruent affection, is called a crie, though it burſt not out into clamoure, nor perhaps into anie voice at al. For God ſaide to Moyſes, praying in mere ſilence, but with vehemencie of ſpirite \+it (Exod. 14\+it*) VVhy crieſt thou to me?\f* crie come to thee.
\v 3 Turne \f c \fr 101.3 \ft Though ſinne prouoke Gods wrath, becauſe we by ſinning turne from him, and not he firſt from vs: yet we pray God not ſo to leaue vs, but to geue vs new grace, that by humilitie, and penance we may returne to him, and not dye in ſinne.\f* not away thy face from me: in what day ſoeuer I am in tribulation, incline thine eare to me. In what day ſoeuer I shal inuocate thee, heare me ſpeedely.
\v 4 Becauſe my dayes haue vanished as \f d \fr 101.4 \ft Mans dayes, and al his workes are nothing worth, but vaniſh like ſmoke, ſo long as he is in mortal ſinne:\f* ſmoke: and my \f e \fr 101.4 \ft yea his beſt workes, as if he geue almoſe, faſt, pray, and dye for the truth, yet al thoſe auaile nothing (\+it 1. Cor. 13.\+it*) but are\f* bones are withered as \f f \fr 101.4 \ft like dryed ſtickes, or chippes, ſitte to kindle the fire.\f* a drie burnt firebrand.
\v 5 I \f g \fr 101.5 \ft My ſoule ſeparated by ſinne from God, withereth as graſſe, that is cutte from the roote:\f* am ſtriken as graſſe, and my hart is withered: becauſe \f h \fr 101.5 \ft becauſe I haue loſt al ſauour and appetite to ſpiritual meate.\f* I haue forgotten to eate my bread.
\v 6 For \f i \fr 101.6 \ft In this miſerable ſtate.\f* the voyce of my groning, my \f k \fr 101.6 \ft I am as bones and flesh cleauing together without moyſture, or radical humour.\f* bone hath cleaued to my flesh.
\v 7 I am become like \f l \fr 101.7 \ft I fled from conuerſation of men for ſorow, and shame of my ſinnes:\f* a pellicane of the wildernes: I am become as \f m \fr 101.7 \ft as a crow that only flieth by night: or as an owle, or batte.\f* a nightcrow in the houſe.
\v 8 I haue watched, and am become as \f n \fr 101.8 \ft Alſo as a ſparow hauing loſt her mate, remaineth mourning and ſolitarie in the accuſtomed neſt, or mere vnto it,\f* a ſparow ſolitarie in the houſetoppe.
\v 9 Al the day did mine enemies vpbrayde me: and they \f o \fr 101.9 \ft Thoſe that were wont to praiſe or flatter me, now are as ſworne enimies againſt me.\f* that praiſed me, ſware againſt me.
\v 10 Becauſe I did \f p \fr 101.10 \ft Bread ſauoured to me no better then ashes.\f* eate ashes as bread, & mingled \f q \fr 101.10 \ft and drinke gaue me no comfort, but ſtil I wept.\f* my drinke with weeping.
\v 11 At \f r \fr 101.11 \ft I am moſt eſpecially afflicted, becauſe thou art angrie.\f* the face of thy wrath and indignation: becauſe \f s \fr 101.11 \ft In that thou didſt ſometime aduance me in proſperitie,\f* lifting me vp thou haſt throwne me \f t \fr 101.11 \ft my fall is ſo much greater, and more grieuous.\f* downe.
\v 12 My daies haue declined \f v \fr 101.12 \ft As a ſhadow declineth to nothing, and al becometh darknes, when the ſunne, and other light departeth, euen ſo I, that am but a ſhadow, decline to mere darknes, when thy fauoure parteth from me.\f* as a ſhadow: and I am withered \f w \fr 101.12 \ft and I loſe my beautie, as graſſe cutte from the ground withereth.\f* as graſſe.
\v 13 \f x \fr 101.13 \ft But I am meruelouſly comforted, conſidering that thou our Meſſias, the Sonne of God, art immutable for euer:\f* But thou ô Lord endureſt for euer: and \f y \fr 101.13 \ft and thy memorable promiſe of redeeming mankind, wil haue effect in al generations.\f* thy memorial in generation and generation.
\v 14 Thou \f z \fr 101.14 \ft Thou riſing to helpe, who ſemedſt to haue forgote, wilt protect the Church, and euerie faithful ſoule.\f* ryſing vp shal haue mercie on Sion: becauſe it is \f a \fr 101.14 \ft becauſe thou haſt differred long:\f* time to haue mercie on it, becauſe \f b \fr 101.14 \ft and becauſe the time by thee deſigned ſemeth to approch\f* the time cometh.
\v 15 Becauſe \f c \fr 101.15 \ft Men that ſhal heare thyn Apoſtles preach, shal proue good and fitte matter, for the building of thy Church:\f* the ſtones therof haue pleaſed thy ſeruantes: and they shal haue pittie on \f d \fr 101.15 \ft and the ſimpleſt poore people, as it were, the earth or duſt;\f* the earth \f e \fr 101.15 \ft shal participate of this mercie.\f* therof.
\v 16 And \f f \fr 101.16 \ft Beſides thoſe Iewes that ſhal beleue in Chriſt, much more the Gentiles ſhal feare and ſerue him.\f* the Gentiles shal feare thy name ô Lord, and al \f g \fr 101.16 \ft His glorie is ſo euident that al kinges know it, though al be not conuerted.\f* the kinges of the earth thy glorie.
\v 17 Becauſe our Lord hath built Sion: and he shal be ſeene in his glorie.
\v 18 He hath had reſpect to the prayer \f h \fr 101.18 \ft Of holie Patriarches, Prieſtes, Prophetes, and of al true penitents,\f* of the humble: and he hath not deſpiſed their petition.
\v 19 Let theſe thinges be written vnto an other generation: and the people, that shal \f i \fr 101.19 \ft That shal be made a new creature in Chriſt.\f* be created, shal praiſe our Lord.
\v 20 Becauſe he hath lookedforth from his high holie place: our Lord from heauen hath looked vpon the earth.
\v 21 That he might heare the gronings of the fettered: that he might looſe the children of them that are ſlayne:
\v 22 That they may \f k \fr 101.22 \ft The faithful people of the Church, according to their habilitie endeuour to ſerue Chriſt.\f* shewforth the name of our Lord in Sion: and his praiſe in Ieruſalem.
\v 23 In the aſſembling of the people together in one, and kinges to ſerue our Lord.
\v 24 He anſwered him in the way of his ſtrength: Shew me the fewnes of my daies.
\v 25 Cal me not backe \f l \fr 101.25 \ft Grant me time and meanes to be mature in vertue in this life.\f* in the halfe of my daies: thy yeares are vnto generation and generation.
\v 26 In the beginning ô Lord thou didſt found the earth: and the heauens are the workes of thy hands.
\v 27 They \f m \fr 101.27 \ft Be changed in qualitie.\f* shal perish, but thou art permanent: and they shal al waxe old as a garment. \x - \xt Heb. 1.\x* And as a veſture thou shalt change them, and they shal be changed:
\v 28 but thou art the ſelfe ſame, and thy yeares shal not faile.
\v 29 The children of thy ſeruantes shal inhabite: and \f n \fr 101.29 \ft The Church of Chriſt perpetual.\f* their ſeede shal be directed for euer.
\c 102
\cd Thankes to God for priuate, 6. and publike benefites. 17. His mercie, iuſtice, and other proprieties are immutable. 20. Angels, and al other creatures are inuited to praiſe him. \f - \ft Gratitude for God's benefits. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 To \f a \fr 102.1 \ft Inſpired to Dauid, and written by him.\f* Dauid himſelf. MY ſoule \f b \fr 102.1 \ft Shew forth praiſes, and thankes:\f* bleſſe thou our Lord: and \f c \fr 102.1 \ft al my cogitations, affections, ſenſes, and powres.\f* al thinges, that are within me, his holie name.
\v 2 My ſoule bleſſe thou our Lord: and forget not al his retributions.
\v 3 Who is \f d \fr 102.3 \ft The firſt benefite of grace is remiſſion of ſinnes:\f* propitious to al thine iniquities: who \f e \fr 102.3 \ft the ſecond, is curing euil habites, or diſpoſitions.\f* healeth al thine infirmities.
\v 4 Who \f f \fr 102.4 \ft The third, to conſerue from falling againe:\f* redemeth thy life from deadly falling: who \f g \fr 102.4 \ft the fourth, to geue victorie and reward in abundant meaſure.\f* crowneth thee in mercie and commiſerations.
\v 5 Who \f h \fr 102.5 \ft The fifth, to grant al lawful petitions temporal and ſpiritual, which are good for the ſoule,\f* replenisheth thy deſire in good thinges: thy youth \f i \fr 102.5 \ft the ſixth, reſurrection of flesh in glorie.\f* shal be rewed as the eagles.
\v 6 Our Lord \f k \fr 102.6 \ft Our Lord, whoſe ſpecial propertie is to shew mercie,\f* doth mercies: and \f l \fr 102.6 \ft when the ſame is neglected, he reuengeth the wrongs, deliuering the oppreſſed, and puniſhing the oppreſſors.\f* iudgement to al that ſuffer wrong.
\v 7 He made his waies \f m \fr 102.7 \ft By geuing them a written law.\f* knowne to Moyſes, his willes to the children of Iſrael.
\v 8 Our Lord is \f n \fr 102.8 \ft God is naturally pittiful, to releeue the afflicted.\f* pitieful, and \f o \fr 102.8 \ft merciful towards ſinners.\f* merciful: \f p \fr 102.8 \ft loath to be angrie, or to punish.\f* long ſuffering, and very merciful.
\v 9 He wil \f q \fr 102.9 \ft God puniſheth not penitents with eternal paine, but with temporal: for though (as in the next verſe) our ſinnes of their owne nature, deſerue eternal puniſhment, yet Chriſt paying our ranſome, true penitents are only punished temporally.\f* not be angrie alwayes: neither wil he threaten for euer.
\v 10 He hath not done to vs according to our ſinnes: neither according to our iniquities hath he rewarded vs.
\v 11 For according to the height of heauen from the earth: hath he ſtrengthned his mercie vpon them that feare him.
\v 12 As far \f r \fr 102.12 \ft See here the perfect washing away, and ſeparation of ſinnes.\f* as the Eaſt is diſtant from the Weſt: hath he made our iniquities far from vs.
\v 13 As a father hath compaſſion of his children, ſo hath our Lord compaſſion on them that feare him:
\v 14 becauſe he hath knowen \f s \fr 102.14 \ft In reguard of our frailtie he hath compaſſion: yet his mercie only auaileth to them that feare him: as in the next wordes before, and \+it v. 17\+it*.\f* our making. He remembred that we are duſt:
\v 15 man, his daies are as graſſe, as the floure of the filde ſo shal he florish.
\v 16 Becauſe the ſpirit shal paſſe in him, and he shal not ſtand: and he shal know his place no more.
\v 17 But the mercie of our Lord from euerlaſting, and vnto euerlaſting vpon them that feare him. And his iuſtice is vpon the childrens children, to them that keepe his teſtament.
\v 18 And are mindful of his commandmentes, to \f t \fr 102.18 \ft A iuſt man not only knoweth, and remembreth, but alſo doth the commandments.\f* doe them.
\v 19 Our Lord hath prepared his ſeate in heauen: and his kindom shal haue dominion ouer al.
\v 20 Bleſſe our Lord al ye his Angels: mightie in powre, doing his word, that feare the voice of his wordes.
\v 21 Bleſſe our Lord al ye his hoaſtes: you his miniſters, that doe his wil.
\v 22 Bleſſe ye our Lord \f v \fr 102.22 \ft Al creatures, though naturally ſenſeles, yet praiſe God, becauſe they are his worke,\f* al his workes: in euerie place \f w \fr 102.22 \ft and becauſe they are in his dominion.\f* of his dominion, my ſoule bleſſe thou our Lord.
\c 103
\cd The Pſalmiſt inuiteth himſelf and others to praiſe God, for his meruelous workes in the heauens, 5. the earth, and water, 9. limiting their bondes, producing al thinges neceſſarie for al liuing creatures, in conuenient ſeaſons. 27. with continual prouidence of al. \f - \ft God's works marvelous. The 2. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 To Dauid him ſelf. MY ſoule bleſſe thou our Lord: ô Lord my God thou art magnified excedingly.
\v 2 Thou haſt put on \f a \fr 103.2 \ft Thou poſſeſſeſt al maieſtie, and matter of praiſe.\f* confeſſion and beautie: being clothed with light as with a garment: Stretching out the heauen as a skinne:
\v 3 which couereſt the higher partes therof \f b \fr 103.3 \ft Thou haſt compaſſed the ſphere of the fixed ſtarres, with a ſphere of chriſtalline ſubſtance which is as water congeled.\f* with waters. Which \f c \fr 103.3 \ft According to our capacitie the prophet deſcribeth the ſpeedie coming, or vvorking of God as if he came in a ſwift cloud, or vvith winges of the vvind, to ſignifie that he vvorketh wel and vvhen he pleaſeth vvithout delay: He ſaide, and thinges were made: he commanded and they vvere created, \+it Pſal. 32\+it*.\f* makeſt the cloude for thee to aſcend on: which walkeſt vpon the winges of windes.
\v 4 Which makeſt ſpirites thine \f d \fr 103.4 \ft Thy meſſengers to execute thy vvil:\f* Angels: and thy \f e \fr 103.4 \ft & the ſame Angels are as a burning fire in operation yea they diſpatch more eaſily, and more ſpedely then vve can conceiue,\f* miniſters a burning fyre.
\v 5 Which haſt founded the earth vpon \f f \fr 103.5 \ft Moſt firmly eſtablished by natural weight, in the center of the world:\f* the ſtabilitie therof: it shal not be inclined for euer and euer
\v 6 The \f g \fr 103.6 \ft The water hi\f* depth, \f h \fr 103.6 \ft should naturally couer al the earth:\f* as a garment, is his clothing: vpon the mountaines shal waters ſtand.
\v 7 At \f i \fr 103.7 \ft But by thy commandment the waters are conteined in their limited places.\f* thy reprehention they shal flee: at the voice of thy thunder they shal feare.
\v 8 The \f k \fr 103.8 \ft The waters being conteyned in their appointed chanels, both hilles and fieldes appeare which otherwiſe would be couered.\f* mountaines aſcend: and the plaine fildes deſcend into the place, which thou haſt founded for them.
\v 9 Thou haſt ſet a bound, which \f l \fr 103.9 \ft The wate…\f* they shal not paſſe ouer: neither shal they returne to couer the earth.
\v 10 Which ſendeſtforth fontaines in the valles: betwen the middeſt of mountaines shal waters paſſe.
\v 11 Al the beaſtes of the filde shal drinke: the wilde aſſes shal \f m \fr 103.11 \ft hope for and receiue\f* expect in their thirſt.
\v 12 Ouer them shal the foules of the ayre inhabite: out of the middes of rockes they shal geueforth voices.
\v 13 Watering the mountaines from his higher places: of the fruite of thy worke shal the earth be filled:
\v 14 Bringing forth graſſe for beaſtes, and herbe for the ſeruice of men. That thou mayſt bring forth \f n \fr 103.14 \ft By theſe three principal kindes of foode, breade, wine, and oyl, al ſortes of nutriment are vnderſtood.\f* bread out of the earth:
\v 15 and \f o \fr 103.15 \ft See previous note\f* wine may make the hart of man ioyful: That he may make the face chereful with \f p \fr 103.15 \ft See previous note\f* oile: and bread may confirme the hart of man.
\v 16 The trees of the filde shal be filled, and the ceders of Libanus, which he hath planted:
\v 17 there ſparowes shal make their neſt. The houſe of the hearne is the leader of them:
\v 18 the high mountaines for hartes: the rocke a refuge for the Irchins.
\v 19 He made the moone for ſeaſons: the ſunne knoweth his going downe.
\v 20 Thou didſt appoint darkenes, and night was made: in it shal al the beaſtes of the wood paſſe.
\v 21 The whelpes of lions roaring, to rauen, and to ſeeke of God meate for themſelues.
\v 22 The ſunne is riſen, and they are gathered together: and in their couches they shal be placed.
\v 23 Man shal goe forth to his worke: and to his working vntil euening.
\v 24 How magnified are thy workes ô Lord! thou haſt made al thinges in wiſedom: the earth is filled with \f q \fr 103.24 \ft With thy creatures.\f* thy poſſeſſion.
\v 25 This great ſea, and very large, there are \f r \fr 103.25 \ft Fiſhes, ſerpents, wormes, and al liuing creatures that lacke feete.\f* creeping beaſtes, wherof \f s \fr 103.25 \ft No kind of liuing creatures multiplieth ſo much as fishes Ariſtotel \+it li. 9\+it*. Animal. \+it c. 17\+it*.\f* there is no number. Litle beaſtes with great:
\v 26 there shippes shal paſſe. This \f t \fr 103.26 \ft A moſt huge fish called Leuiathan. Iob. \+it 40. v. 20\+it*.\f* dragon, whom thou madeſt to \f v \fr 103.26 \ft Albeit in the water he paſſeth mans ſtreingth, yet depriued of water he is not able to defend himſelfe. \+it v. 19\+it*.\f* delude:
\v 27 al expect of thee that thou geue them meate in ſeaſon.
\v 28 Thou geuing vnto them, they shal gather it: thou opening thy hand, al shal be filled with bountie.
\v 29 But thou turning away the face, they shal be trubled: thou shalt take away their ſpirite, and they shal faile, and shal returne into their duſt.
\v 30 Thou shalt ſendforth thy ſpirit, and they shal be created: and thou shalt renewe the face of the earth.
\v 31 Be the glorie of our Lord for euer: our Lord wil reioice in his workes:
\v 32 Who looketh vpon the earth, & maketh it to tremble: who toucheth the mountaines, and they ſmoke.
\v 33 I wil chaunte to our Lord in my life: I wil ſing to my God as long as I am.
\v 34 Let my ſpeach be acceptable to him: but I wil take delight in our Lord.
\v 35 \f w \fr 103.35 \ft A prediction that impenitent ſinners ſhal be damned, wherin the Prophet conforming his wil to Gods, vttereth it in forme of a prayer.\f* Let ſinners faile from the earth, and the vniuſt, ſo that they be not: my ſoule bleſſe thou our Lord.
\c 104
\cd The Iſraelites are exhorted to ſing praiſes to God, 5. for his meruelous benefites towards Abraham, Iſaac, and Iacob. 11. whoſe particular familie, being then ſmal, went from Chanaan into Ægypt (17. Whither Ioſeph by Gods prouidence was caried before) there increaſed in number, was perſecuted, 26. deliuered by Moyſes and Aaron, working manie great miracles, 36. protected, and fedde in the deſert, 44. and finally poſſeſſed Chanaan. \f - \ft God's ſpecial benefits towards the Jews. The 4. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 \f a \fr 104.1 \ft Alleluia ſignifieth more then Laudate Dominum, Praiſe ye our Lord. For by theſe two hebrew wordes, Allelu a, the Prophet inuiteth al men to praiſe God, with gladnes, and iubilation, with hart, voice, and geſture, with inſtruments, and howſoeuer we are able. And therfore S Ierom, S. Auguſtin, and al Catholique writers kepe the ſame worde, and tranſlate it not, neither in the titles of Pſalmes, not ordinarily in anie place of holie Scripture. This is the firſt Pſalme thus titled, and is the ſame Pſalme in ſenſe, and in good part of the wordes, which the royal Prophet made, and cauſed to be ſongue, when he brought the Arke of God from the houſe of Obededom into his owne houſe. 1. Par. 16. v. 8.\f* Alleluia. CONFESSE ye to our Lord, and inuocate his name: \f b \fr 104.1 \ft How much more gratful is it now to God, that we celebrate the greater myſteries of the new Teſtament.\f* shew forth his workes among the Gentiles. \f 1 \fr 104.1 \ft For an expoſition of this Pſalme read the places quoted in the inner margen.\f*
\v 2 Chaunt to him, and ſing to him: tel ye al his meruelous workes.
\v 3 Prayſe ye him in his holie name: let the hart of them reioice that ſeeke our Lord.
\v 4 Seeke ye our Lord, and be confirmed: ſeeke \f c \fr 104.4 \ft His preſent helpe.\f* his face alwayes.
\v 5 Remember ye his meruelous workes, which he hath done: his wonders, and the iudgments of his mouth.
\v 6 The ſeede of Abraham, his ſeruantes: the children of Iacob his elect.
\v 7 He is the Lord our God: in \f d \fr 104.7 \ft not only in Iſrael, but in al the world.\f* al the earth are his iudgementes.
\v 8 He hath bene mindeful for euer of his teſtament: of the word, which he commanded \f e \fr 104.8 \ft For euer, to the end of the world.\f* vnto a thouſand generations. \x - \xt Gen. 12. v. 7. Gen. 17. v. 4. Gen. 26. v. 3. Gen. 28. v. 13. Gen. 46. v. 26. 27.\x*
\v 9 Which he diſpoſed to Abraham: and of his oath to Iſaac.
\v 10 And he appointed it to Iacob for a precept: and to Iſrael for an eternal teſtament.
\v 11 Saying: To thee wil I geue the land of Chanaan, the corde of your inheritance.
\v 12 When they were \f f \fr 104.12 \ft But 70. perſons.\f* of ſmal number, very few and ſeiourners therof:
\v 13 And they paſſed from nation into nation, & from kingdom to an other people.
\v 14 He leift not a man to hurt them: and he rebuked kings for their ſake.
\v 15 Touch not my annointed, and toward my prophetes be not malignant.
\v 16 And \f g \fr 104.16 \ft By his prouidence ſuffered.\f* he called a famine vpon the land: and he deſtroyed al the ſtrength of bread. \x - \xt Gen. 41. v. 54.\x*
\v 17 He ſent a man before them: Ioſeph was ſold to be a ſeruant.
\v 18 They humbled his feete in fetters, yron paſſed through his ſoule, \x - \xt Gen. 37. v. 28. Gen. 39. & ſeq.\x*
\v 19 vntil his word came. The word of our Lord inflamed him:
\v 20 the king ſent, and looſed him; the prince of the people, and releaſed him.
\v 21 He appointed him lord of his houſe: and prince of al his poſſeſſion.
\v 22 That he might inſtruct his princes as himſelfe: and might teach his ancientes wiſedom.
\v 23 And Iſrael entered into Ægypt, and Iacob was a ſeiourner in the land \f h \fr 104.23 \ft Ægypt, poſſeſſed by Meſraim Chams ſecond ſonne. Gen. \+it 10. v. 13\+it*.\f* of Cham. \f 1 \fr 104.23 \ft Gen 46.\f*
\v 24 And he increaſed his people excedingly: and ſtrengthned them ouer their enemies. \x - \xt Exo. 1. v. 7.\x*
\v 25 He \f i \fr 104.25 \ft May it be vnderſtood, or beleued (ſaith S. Auguſtin) that God turneth the hart of man to committe ſinnes? Or is it no ſinne, or is it a ſmal ſinne, to hate the people of God? Or to worke guile towards his ſeruants? VVho wil ſay this? VVhat then, is God author of theſe ſo greeuous ſinnes, who is not to be ſuppoſed the author of a moſt ſmal ſinne? This lerned Father therfore anſwereth, that God peruerted not a right hart, but turned that was of it ſelfe peruerſe, to the hatred of his people, where he might vſe that euil wel, not by making them euil, but by beſtowing vpon his owne people good thinges, which the euil might eaſily enuie. VVhich hatred of theirs how God vſed both to the exerciſe of his people (which is profitable to vs) & to the glorie of his owne name, the thinges that folow do teach vs, which are here remembred to his praiſe.\f* turned their hart, that they hated his people: and to worke guile toward his ſeruantes.
\v 26 He ſent Moyſes his ſeruant: Aaron, \f k \fr 104.26 \ft In whom God eſtablished the Prieſthood of Moyſes law.\f* him ſelfe whom he choſe. \x - \xt Exo. 3. 4. 7. 8. 9. 10. 12.\x*
\v 27 He did put in them the wordes of his ſignes, and of his wonders in the Land of Cham.
\v 28 He ſent \f l \fr 104.28 \ft The ninth plague of the Ægyptians.\f* darkenes, and obſcured: and did \f m \fr 104.28 \ft God willingly, not as one loath or vnwilling, performed al that he threatned.\f* not exaſperate his wordes.
\v 29 He turned their \f n \fr 104.29 \ft The firſt plague.\f* waters into bloud: and killed their fishes.
\v 30 Their land broughtforth \f o \fr 104.30 \ft The ſecond plague.\f* frogges in \f 1 \fr 104.30 \ft Dauid knew this by reuelation, or by tradition for it is not in Exodus.\f* the inner chambers of their kinges.
\v 31 He ſayd, and \f p \fr 104.31 \ft The fourth plague.\f* the \f 1 \fr 104.31 \ft p \+it A ſvvarme of flies\+it*.\f* cænomyia came: and the \f q \fr 104.31 \ft The third plague.\f* ciniſes in al their coaſtes.
\v 32 He made theyr raynes \f r \fr 104.32 \ft The ſeuenth plague.\f* haile: fire burning in their land.
\v 33 And he ſtroke their vines, and their figtrees: and he deſtroyed the wood of their coaſtes.
\v 34 He ſaid, & \f s \fr 104.34 \ft The eight plague.\f* the locuſt came, and the \f t \fr 104.34 \ft A worme that ſpoyleth corne, graſſe, and fruict.\f* bruchus wherof there was no number.
\v 35 And it did eate al the graſſe in their land: and it did eate al the fruicte of their land.
\v 36 And he ſtroke euerie \f v \fr 104.36 \ft The tenth plague. · The fifth & ſixt of peſtilence and boyles are omitted.\f* firſt begotten in their land: the firſt fruictes of al their labour.
\v 37 And he brought them forth with gold and ſiluer, and there was not in their tribes a feeble perſon.
\v 38 Ægypt was glad at their departure: becauſe the feare of them lay vpon them.
\v 39 He ſpred a cloude for their protection, and fire to shine vnto them by night. \x - \xt Exod. 13. v. 21. Exod. 16. v. 13. Exod. 17. v. 6. Gen. 12.\x*
\v 40 They made petition, and the quaile came: and he filled them with the bread of heauen.
\v 41 He diuided the rocke, and waters flowed: riuers ranne in the drie ground.
\v 42 Becauſe he was mindful of his holie word, which he had vttered to Abraham his ſeruant.
\v 43 And he broughtforth his people in exultation, and his elect in ioy.
\v 44 And he gaue them the countries of the Nations: and they poſſeſſed the labours of peoples: \x - \xt Ioſue 6. & ſeq.\x*
\v 45 That they might keepe his iuſtifications, and ſeeke after his lawe.
\c 105
\cd The prophet exhorteth the people to render thankes and praiſes to God, 6. for remitting their manifold ſinnes, in the deſert. 34. and in the conquered land : 38. foreshewing like ſinnes to come, Gods wrath and punishment for the ſame. 44. and that he wil geue grace of repentance : to ſome 47. for which he prayeth, and praiſeth God. \f - \ft The Iſraelites often ſinned, and were mercifully punished. The 4. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. CONFESSE ye to our Lord \f a \fr 105.1 \ft God is of himſelfe and eſſentially good, al other goodnes is participated of him.\f* becauſe he is good: becauſe his mercie is for euer. \f 1 \fr 105.1 \ft Iudith. v. 21.\f*
\v 2 \f b \fr 105.2 \ft No creature can fully expreſſe Gods perfections.\f* Who shal ſpeake the powers of our Lord, shal make al his praiſes to be heard?
\v 3 \f c \fr 105.3 \ft It is a happie ſtate in this life, either to kepe Gods law :\f* Bleſſed are they, that keepe iudgement, and \f d \fr 105.3 \ft or to repent and do worthie penance for tranſgreſſing.\f* doe iuſtice at al time.
\v 4 Remember vs ô Lord \f e \fr 105.4 \ft According to thy wonted beneuolence, promiſed to thy people.\f* in the good pleaſure of thy people: viſite vs in thy ſaluation:
\v 5 To \f f \fr 105.5 \ft That we may ſee, and enioy the benefites promiſed to thyne elect.\f* ſee in the goodnes of thyne elect, to reioyce in the ioy of thy nation: that thou maiſt be praiſed with thine inheritance.
\v 6 We haue \f g \fr 105.6 \ft We haue erred and hurt ourſelues :\f* ſinned with our fathers: we haue \f h \fr 105.6 \ft we haue wronged our neighboures :\f* dealt vniuſtly, we haue \f i \fr 105.6 \ft we haue offended againſt God.\f* done iniquitie.
\v 7 Our fathers in Ægypt did not vnderſtand thy meruelous workes: they were not mindeful of the multitude of thy mercie. And they prouoked thee to wrath going vp vnto the ſea, the Read ſea.
\v 8 And he ſaued them \f k \fr 105.8 \ft Albeit the people by their murmuring deſerued more punishment, yet God for the glorie of his owne name ſaued them from vtter deſtruction.\f* for his name ſake; that he might make his power knowen. \x - \xt Exod. 14. v. 11.\x*
\v 9 And he rebuked the Read ſea, and it was made drie: and he led them in the depths as in a deſert.
\v 10 And he ſaued them from the hand of them that hated them: and he redemed them out of the hand of the enemie.
\v 11 And water ouerwhelmed thoſe that afflicted them: there did not one of them remaine.
\v 12 And they beleued his wordes: and they ſang his praiſe.
\v 13 They had \f l \fr 105.13 \ft They perſeuered not long in their dutie towards God, ſeing his omnipotent powre by his meruelous workes :\f* quickly donne, they forgot his workes: and they \f m \fr 105.13 \ft nor were content with his prouidence, but carnally coueted thinges, not neceſſarie.\f* expected not his counſel.
\v 14 And they coueted concupiſcence in the deſert: and tempted God in the place without water. \x - \xt Exo. 16. & 17.\x*
\v 15 He gaue them their petition: and ſent ſaturitie into their \f n \fr 105.15 \ft According to their carnal deſires.\f* ſoules. \x - \xt Nu. 10.\x*
\v 16 And they prouoked Moyſes in the campe: Aaron the \f o \fr 105.16 \ft Holie by his function.\f* holie of our Lord.
\v 17 The earth was opened, and ſwalowed Dathan: and ouerwhelmed the congregation of Abiron.
\v 18 And a fire flamed vp in their ſinagogue: the flame burnt the ſinners.
\v 19 And they made a calfe in Horeb: and they adored \f p \fr 105.19 \ft They adored the image that repreſented a calf, not God.\f* the ſculptil. \x - \xt Exo. 23.\x*
\v 20 And they \f q \fr 105.20 \ft God being their true glorie, they changed him, for a falſe god of the Ægyptians (who eſpecially honoured a calfe called Apis) making an image therof, and attributed their deliuerie from Ægypt to this imagined god. Exo. \+it 32. v. 4. 8\+it*. Of which and the like foolish, and abominable idolatrie S. Paul writeth, Rom. \+it 1. v. 23\+it*. They changed the glorie of the incorruptible God, into a ſimilitude of the image of a corruptible man, and of foules, and of foure footed beaſtes, and of them that crepe : where we ſee what maner of imagies holie Scriptures condemne, and not the imagies of Chriſt and his Sainctes.\f* changed their glorie into the ſimilitude of a calfe that eateth graſſe.
\v 21 They forgat God, which ſaued them, which did great thinges in Ægypt,
\v 22 meruelous thinges in the land of Cham, terrible thinges in the Read ſea.
\v 23 And \f r \fr 105.23 \ft He ſayd, he would deſtroy them, but for Moyſes prayer ſpared them.\f* he ſayd to deſtroy them: if Moyſes his elect had not ſtood in the way before him: \x - \xt Exo. 32.\x*To turne away his wrath that he should not deſtroy them:
\v 24 and they eſtemed for naught the land that was to be deſired. \x - \xt Nu. 14.\x* They did not beleue his word,
\v 25 and they murmured in their tabernacles: they heard not the voice of our Lord.
\v 26 And he lifted vp his hand ouer them: to ouerthrowe them in the deſert: \x - \xt Num. 14. v. 21. 22.\x*
\v 27 And to caſt doune their ſeede among the Nations: and to diſperſe them in the countries.
\v 28 And they were profeſſed to \f s \fr 105.28 \ft The Idol of Moabites, and Madianitees,\f* Beelphegor: and they did eate the ſacrifices \f t \fr 105.28 \ft As God is in deede the liuing God, that liueth of himſelfe, and geueth life to others : ſo falſe goddes are called dead goddes, that can not geue life to anie, but doe kil al that ſerue them at leaſt ſpiritually, and often corporally.\f* of the dead. \x - \xt Num. 25. v. 3.\x*
\v 29 And they prouoked him in their inuentions: and ruine was multiplied on them.
\v 30 And Phinees ſtood, and \f v \fr 105.30 \ft Phinees moued by the zele of God (as the holie text witneſſeth. Nu. \+it 25. v. 11.\+it*) in killing the adulterers pleaſed God, and merited reward.\f* pacified: and the ſlaughter ceaſed.
\v 31 And it was reputed to him vnto iuſtice, in generation and generation euen for euer.
\v 32 And they prouoked him at the waters of contradiction: and \f w \fr 105.32 \ft Moyſes afflicted in ſpirite, by the enormious murmuring of the people, doubted whether God would geue them water out of the rocke or no, not doubting of his powre, but of his wil : and ſo when he ſhould haue ſpoken to the rocke, Num. \+it 20. v. 8\+it*. he ſpoke to the incredulous people. \+it v. 10\+it*. and therin offended God. for which he was temporally puniſhed. \+it v. 12\+it*. Deut. \+it 1. v. 37\+it*. & c. \+it 3. v. 26. c. 4. v. 21\+it*.\f* Moyſes was vexed for them: \x - \xt Nu. 10. v. 2. 12.\x*
\v 33 becauſe they exaſperated his ſpirit. And he plainly affirmed in his lips:
\v 34 they deſtroyed not the nations, of which our Lord ſpake to them.
\v 35 And they were mingled among the nations, and learned their workes: \x - \xt Deut. 2. v. 2. & 12. v. 2. & 3. Iudic. 2. v. 11. 12. Iudic. 3. v. 5. 6. &c. Iere. 19. v. 5.\x*
\v 36 and they ſerued their ſculptils: and it became a ſcandal to them.
\v 37 And they immolated their ſonnes, and their daughters to diuels.
\v 38 And \f x \fr 105.38 \ft Some Iewes offered theſe moſt cruel, vnnatural, and abominable ſacrifices ; perhaps in the times of Iudges, when they were mingled with idolatrous people, and ſerued their goddes ; Iud. \+it 2. v. 11. c. 3. v. 6\+it*. But it is more expreſſe after Dauids time, wherof he here prophecieth, and was verified by Achaz. \+it 4. Reg. 16 v. 3\+it*. and by Manaſſes. \+it 4. Reg. 21. v. 6\+it*. VVhich with other idolatrie king Ioſias deſtroyed. \+it 4. Reg. 23. v. 10\+it*.\f* they shed innocent bloud: the bloud of their ſonnes and of their daughters, which they ſacrificed to the ſculptils of Chanaan. And the land was infected with bloud,
\v 39 and was contaminated in their workes: and they did fornicat in their inuentions.
\v 40 And our Lord was wrath with furie vpon his people: and he abhorred his inheritance.
\v 41 And he deliuered them into the handes of the nations: and they that hated them, had the dominion of them.
\v 42 And their enimies afflicted them: and they were humbled vnder their handes:
\v 43 he did often deliuer them. But they exaſperated him in their counſel: and they were humbled in their iniquities.
\v 44 And he \f y \fr 105.44 \ft God reſpected them with his merciful eye, and gaue them grace to repent.\f* ſaw when they were afflicted: and he heard their prayer.
\v 45 And he was mindeful of his teſtament: and it repented him according to the multitude of his mercie.
\v 46 And he gaue them into mercies in the ſight of al, that had taken them. \f z \fr 105.46 \ft Here the Pſalmiſt concludeth both the hiſtorie and prophecie of this Pſalme, with prayer and praiſe, as foloweth :\f*
\v 47 \f 1 \fr 105.47 \ft A verie fitte prayer in time of ſchiſme.\f* Saue vs ô Lord our God: and geather vs out of the Nations: That we may confeſſe to thy holie name: & may glorie in thy prayſe.
\v 48 Bleſſed be our Lord the God of Iſrael from euerlaſting vnto euerlaſting: and al the people shal ſay: Be it, be it.
\c 106
\cd Againe the Pſalmiſt inuiteth al men to render thankes to God for their deliuerie from dangers, or euils in general : 4. particularly from dangers in iorney, 10. in priſon, or captiuitie, 17. in ſicknes ſpiritual and corporal, 23. in nauigation, 33. deſcribing the changeable course of thinges in this world, 38. eſpecially of mens ſtates, 42. for al which the iuſt wil praiſe God. \f - \ft God'ſ perpetual providence towards all men.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. CONFESSE \f a \fr 106.1 \ft Praiſe God by confeſſing his mercie, prouidence, and goodnes.\f* ye to our Lord becauſe he is good: becauſe his mercie is for euer. \x - \xt 1. Par. 16. v. 34.\x*
\v 2 Let them ſay that are \f b \fr 106.2 \ft God of his mercie promiſed the Redemer of mankind ſtreight after Adams fal.\f* redemed of our Lord, whom he redemed out of the hand of the enemie: and out of the countries he gathered them:
\v 3 From \f c \fr 106.3 \ft VVhich redemption was intended for al, and faileth not of Gods part in anie, but of mens owne wilful refuſing to be duly penitent, and to kepe Gods precepts.\f* the riſing of the ſunne, and the going downe: from the north, and the ſea.
\v 4 They \f d \fr 106.4 \ft Literally of ſuch as wander in this world, hauing no ſetled place to dwel in : ſpiritually of al mankind after his fal.\f* wandered in the wildernes, in a place without water: the way of citie for habitation they found not.
\v 5 Hungrie, and thirſtie: their ſoule fainted in them.
\v 6 And \f e \fr 106.6 \ft VVhenſoeuer they cal vpon God, he helpeth them, as is beſt for their ſpiritual health.\f* they cried to our Lord when they were in tribulation: and he deliuered them out of their neceſſities.
\v 7 And he conducted them into the right way: to goe into a citie of habitation.
\v 8 Let \f f \fr 106.8 \ft Al Gods benefites, which are of his mercie, not of mans deſerte, are iuſt matter of praiſing God.\f* the mercies of our Lord confeſſe to him: and his meruelous workes to the children of men.
\v 9 Becauſe he hath filled the emptie ſoule: and the hungrie ſoule he hath filled with good thinges.
\v 10 Them that ſate in darkenes, and in the shadow of death: bound in needines, and yron.
\v 11 Becauſe they \f g \fr 106.11 \ft Calamities in this world are commonly inflicted for ſinnes.\f* exaſperated the wordes of God: and they prouoked the counſel of the Higheſt.
\v 12 And their hart was humbled in labours: they were weakened, neither was there anie to helpe.
\v 13 And they cried to our Lord when they were in tribulation: and he deliuered them out of their neceſſities.
\v 14 And he brought them out of darkenes, and the shadow of death: and brake their bondes aſunder.
\v 15 Let the mercies of our Lord confeſſe to him: and his meruelous workes to the children of men.
\v 16 Becauſe he hath deſtroyed the gates of braſſe: and the barres of yron he hath broken.
\v 17 He hath receiued them out of the way of their iniquitie: for they were humbled for their iniuſtices.
\v 18 Their ſoule did abhorre al meate: & they approched euen to the gates of death.
\v 19 And they cried to our Lord when they were in tribulation: and he deliuered them out of their neceſſities.
\v 20 He ſent his word, and healed them: and deliuered them out of their deſtructions.
\v 21 Let the mercies of our Lord confeſſe to him: and his meruelous workes to the children of men.
\v 22 And let them ſacrifice the ſacrifice of praiſe: and shew forth his workes in exulation.
\v 23 They that goe downe into the ſea in shippes, making trafike in the great waters.
\v 24 They haue ſene the workes of our Lord, and his meruelous thinges in the depth.
\v 25 He ſayd, and the blaſt of the ſtorme ſtood: and the waues therof were exalted.
\v 26 They aſcend euen to the heauens, and they deſcend euen to the depthes: their ſoule pyned away in euils.
\v 27 They were trubled, and were moued as a drunken man: and al their wiſedom was deuoured.
\v 28 And \f h \fr 106.28 \ft As before in the \+it 6, 13\+it*, and \+it 19\+it*, verſes,\f* they cried to our Lord when they were in tribulation, and he brought them out of their neceſſities.
\v 29 And he turned his ſtorme into calme: and the waues therof were quiet.
\v 30 And they reioyced becauſe they were quiet: and he conducted them into the hauen of their wil.
\v 31 Let \f i \fr 106.31 \ft This verſe alſo is foure times in this Pſalme. \+it v. 8. 15. 21\+it*. and \+it 31\+it*. to admonish vs, that as there is one meanes to eſcape from al dangers by crying to God, as \+it v. 6. 13. 19\+it*. and \+it 28\+it* : with mourning and penance : ſo there is one cauſe of praiſe and thankes for our deliuerie, which is Gods mercie and grace.\f* the mercies of our Lord confeſſe to him: and his meruelous workes to the children of men.
\v 32 And let them exalt him in the church of the people: and the chayre of the ancientes let them praiſe him.
\v 33 \f k \fr 106.33 \ft God to shew ſometimes his powre, alſo to benefite ſome, and to puniſh others, changeth the accuſtomed courſe of thinges, and ſtates of men, at his diuine pleaſure : as here the Royal prophet reciteth ſome examples. And ſome others are recorded in diuers times and places.\f* He turned \f l \fr 106.33 \ft No doubt much change was made in the earth by Noes flood. And manie thincke that the land of Chanaan, was made more fruictful in the time of the Iewes inhabiting, and now is more barrane againe.\f* the riuers into a deſert: and the iſſues of waters into drineſſe.
\v 34 The \f m \fr 106.34 \ft He alludeth to the countrie about Sodome, and Gomorre, which was moſt fruictful, and moſt pleaſant, Gen. \+it 13. v. 10\+it*, but shortly after vvas burnt vvith fire and brimſton, Gen. \+it 19, v. 24\+it*, ſubuerted, and turned into a dead and ſalt ſea.\f* fruiteful land into a ſalt ground, for the malice of them that inhabite it.
\v 35 He turned the deſert into pooles of waters: and the land without water into iſſues of waters.
\v 36 And he placed the hungrie there, and they built a citie of habitation.
\v 37 And they ſowed fildes, and planted vineyardes: and they made fruicte \f n \fr 106.37 \ft Made abundance of fruict to grow.\f* of natiuitie.
\v 38 And he bleſſed them, and they were multiplied excedingly: and their beaſtes he leſſened not.
\v 39 And \f o \fr 106.39 \ft Againe ſome countries punished for ſinnes.\f* they were made fewe: and were vexed by the tribulation of euiles, and with ſorow.
\v 40 Contempt was powred out vpon princes: and he made them wander where was no way, and not in the way.
\v 41 And he \f p \fr 106.41 \ft An other change in releeuing the poore being humbled.\f* did helpe the poore out of pouertie: and made families as sheepe.
\v 42 The iuſt shal ſee, and shal reioyce: & al iniquitie shal ſtoppe her mouth.
\v 43 Who is wiſe and wil keepe theſe thinges? and wil vnderſtand the mercies of our Lord?
\c 107
\cd The royal prophet promiſeth, 5. and rendereth praiſes to God, 7. for his deliuerie from trubles, and aduancement in the kingdom, 13. praying God ſtil to helpe mans infirmitie. \f - \ft David ſingeth praiſes for benefits received. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Canticle \f a \fr 107.1 \ft This Pſalme was ſongue with inſtruments beginning the muſike, and voices folowing.\f* of Pſalme, to Dauid himſelfe.
\v 2 MY HART is readie ô God, my hart is readie: \x - \xt Pſa. 56.\x* I wil chaunte, and wil ſing in my glorie. \f 2 \fr 107.2 \ft The former part of this Pſalme to the 7. verſe, is the ſame in ſenſe, and almoſt in wordes, with the latter part of the 56 from the 8. verſe.\f*
\v 3 Ariſe my glorie, ariſe pſalter, and harpe: I wil ariſe early.
\v 4 I \f b \fr 107.4 \ft King Dauid ſubdued not only ſome partes of Chanaan, not ſubiect to the Iewes before ( 2. Reg. 5. 1. Par. 11. ) but alſo brought the Philiſtims, Moabites, Ammonites, Idumeans, Amalechites, the kinges of Soba, Syria, and Emath, to pay tribute, \+it 2. Reg. 8. 1. Par. 18.\+it*\f* wil confeſſe to thee in peoples ô Lord: and I wil ſing to thee \f c \fr 107.4 \ft Yet al theſe victories and conqueſtes were but a figure of Chriſts powre and dominion in al nations. And therfore, the reſt of this Pſalme, by S. Auguſtin, and other fathers iudgement, was rather prophetically vttered by Dauid, in the perſon of Chriſt, and more perfectly performed by Chriſt in his Church, then hiſtorically ſuffered of Dauid himſelfe.\f* in the Nations.
\v 5 Becauſe thy mercie is great aboue the heauens: and thy truth euen to the cloudes.
\v 6 Be exalted aboue the heauens ô God, and thy glorie ouer al the earth: \x - \xt Pſa. 59. v. 7.\x*
\v 7 \f 1 \fr 107.7 \ft The reſt of this Pſalme is the ſame with the latter part of the 59 from the 7. verſe.\f* that thy beloued may be deliuered. Saue with thy righthand; and heare me:
\v 8 God ſpake in his holie: I wil reioyce, and wil diuide Sichem; and I wil meſure the vale of tabernacles.
\v 9 Galaad is mine, and Manaſſes is mine: and Ephraim the protection of my head.
\v 10 Iuda is my king: Moab the potte of my hope. Vpon Idumea I wil extend my shoe: the ſtrangers are made my freindes.
\v 11 Who wil conduct me into a fenſed citie? who wil conduct me into Idumea?
\v 12 Wilt not thou ô God, which haſt repelled vs, and wilt not thou goeforth ô God in our hoaſtes?
\v 13 Geue vs helpe out of tribulation: becauſe mans ſaluation is vayne.
\v 14 In God we shal doe ſtrength: and he wil bring our enemies to nothing.
\c 108
\cd Chriſt ( by the mouth of Dauid ) requeſteth of God to be iuſtly declared innocent, and his enimies punished, 6. particularly deſcribing Iudas the traitors malice, 21. and his owne temporal afflictions, 26. prayeth, 30. and praiſeth God for his deliuerie. \f - \ft Chriſt perſecuted & his enemies punished. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, a Pſalme of Dauid.
\v 2 O \f a \fr 108.2 \ft The wordes of Chriſt.\f* God conceale not my prayſe: becauſe the mouth of the ſinner, and the mouth of \f b \fr 108.2 \ft The Phariſees and Herodians ( \+it Mat. 22\+it*. ) with their mouth acknowleged Chriſt a true ſpeaker, and a teacher of the way of God in truth, therby to draw him into danger, and to ſheede his bloud.\f* the deceitful man is open vpon me.
\v 3 They \f c \fr 108.3 \ft At other times they accuſed him of great crimes, laſtly of treaſon againſt Cæſar.\f* haue ſpoken againſt me with deceitful tongue, and with wordes of hatred they haue compaſſed me: and they haue impugned me without cauſe.
\v 4 For that they should loue me, they backbited me: but I prayed.
\v 5 And they ſet againſt me euil thinges for good: and hatred for my loue.
\v 6 Appoint \f d \fr 108.6 \ft A prediction that Iudas would not make recourſe to anie good counſeller, but complaine of his miſerable tormented conſcience to the wicked, who gaue him no comfort at al,\f* a ſinner ouer him: and \f e \fr 108.6 \ft and ſo deſparing, the diuel perſwaded him to hang himſelfe.\f* let the diuel ſtand on his righthand.
\v 7 When he is iudged, let him comeforth condemned: and let his prayer be turned into ſinne.
\v 8 Let his dayes be made fewe: and let an other take his \f f \fr 108.8 \ft The office of Apoſtleshippe.\f* bishopricke. \x - \xt Act. 1. v. 16.\x*
\v 9 Let \f g \fr 108.9 \ft The poſte title, or ſucceſſors of wicked perſecuters proſper not long in this world.\f* his children be made orphans: and his wife a widow.
\v 10 Let his children be tranſported wandering, and let them begge: and let them be caſt out of their habitations.
\v 11 Let the vſurer ſearch al his ſubſtance: and let ſtrangers ſpoile his labours.
\v 12 Let there be none to helpe him: neither let there be anie to haue pittie on his pupilles.
\v 13 Let his children come to deſtruction: in \f h \fr 108.13 \ft Arch heretikes that deuiſe newe opinions, are shortly forſaken, their followers ſtil coining new hereſies of their owne, differing from their falſe maſters.\f* one generation let his name be cleane put out.
\v 14 Let the iniquitie of his fathers returne to memorie in the ſight of our Lord: and let not the ſinne of his mother be blotted out.
\v 15 Let them be before our Lord alwayes, and let the memorie of them perish out of the earth:
\v 16 For that he remembred not to doe mercie.
\v 17 And he perſecuted the poore, and needie man, and the compunct in hart to kil him.
\v 18 And he \f i \fr 108.18 \ft Let them obſerue this, that vſe more ſwearing and blaſpheming, then praying or meditating.\f* loued curſing, and it shal come to him: and he would not bleſſing, and it shal be far from him. And he put on curſing as a garment, and it entred as water into his inner partes, and as oile in his bones.
\v 19 Be it to him as a garment, wherwith he is couered: and as a girdle, wherwith he is alwayes girded.
\v 20 This is the worke of them, that detract from me before our Lord: and that ſpeake euils againſt my ſoule.
\v 21 And thou Lord, Lord, doe with me for thy names ſake: becauſe thy mercie is ſwete.
\v 22 Deliuer me becauſe I am needie, and poore: and \f k \fr 108.22 \ft Chriſts ſoule was penſiue when he prayed in the garden, and he did workes of penance for our ſinnes al his life.\f* my hart is trubled within me.
\v 23 As a shadow when it declineth, am I taken away: and I am shaken as locuſtes.
\v 24 My knees are weakened with faſting: and my flesh is changed by reaſon of oile.
\v 25 And I am made a reproch to them: they ſaw me, and wagged their heades.
\v 26 Helpe me ô Lord my God: \f l \fr 108.26 \ft He prayed alſo for his reſurrection, and glorification.\f* ſaue me according to thy mercie.
\v 27 And let them know that this is thy hand: and thou ô Lord haſt done it.
\v 28 They wil curſe, and thou shalt bleſſe: let them that riſe vp againſt me, be confounded: but thy ſeruant shal reioyce.
\v 29 Let them that detract from me, be clothed with shame: and let them be couered with their confuſion, as with a \f m \fr 108.29 \ft With al poſſible confuſion.\f* duble patched cloke.
\v 30 I wil confeſſe to our Lord excedingly with my mouth: and in the middes of manie I wil prayſe him:
\v 31 Becauſe he hath ſtood on the righthand of the poore, that he might ſaue my ſoule from the perſecutors.
\c 109
\cd Chriſt riſing and aſcending into heauen ſitteth on the right hand of God: 2. beginning in Ieruſalem reigneth in the Church of the whole earth. 4. vſeth the Prieſthood of Melchiſedechs order to the end of the world. 6. and shal iudge the world. \f - \ft Chriſt's exaltation. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A pſalme of Dauid. OVR \f a \fr 109.1 \ft God the Father.\f* Lord ſayd \f b \fr 109.1 \ft To God the Sonne, the Lord of Dauid; and of al mankind, yet the ſonne of Dauid, according to his humanitie.\f* to my Lord: Sitte on my right hand: \f c \fr 109.1 \ft He limiteth not the time, but excludeth al time, wherin the enimie might imagine, that Chriſts kingdom should ceaſe : ſignifying that Chriſt shal reigne, til al his enimies be ſubdued, much more afterwards in al eternitie.\f* til I make thine enemies, thy footeſtoole of thy feete. \x - \xt Matt. 22. Act. 2. 1. Cor. 15. Heb. 1. & 10.\x*
\v 2 Our Lord wil ſendforth the rod of thy ſtrength \f d \fr 109.2 \ft The Church of Chriſt beginning in Ieruſalem on whitſunday, the fiftith day from his Reſurrection, continueth euer more.\f* from Sion: rule thou in the middes of thine enemies.
\v 3 With thee \f e \fr 109.3 \ft Thou shalt haue principalitie,\f* the beginning in the day \f f \fr 109.3 \ft in the day of thy powrful conqueſt, and riſing from death.\f* of thy ſtrength, in \f g \fr 109.3 \ft in excellencie of al holie ſpiritual myſteries and graces :\f* the brightnes of holie thinges: from the wombe \f h \fr 109.3 \ft becauſe, I God the Father of my ſubſtance begate thee, God the Sonne in eternitie. The ſame which Micheas ſaith ( c. 5. v. 2. ) His comingforth from the beginning, from the dayes of eternitie.\f* before the day ſtarre I begat thee.
\v 4 Our Lord \f i \fr 109.4 \ft God moſt firmly, and vnchangeably affirmed : that thou ( Chriſt our Meſſias ) art not only a King, but alſo a Prieſt :\f* ſware, and it shal not repent him: Thou art a Prieſt \f k \fr 109.4 \ft not for a time, as Aaron was, but for euer,\f* for euer according to \f l \fr 109.4 \ft neither of Aarons order, but according to the Order of Melchiſedec.\f* the order of Melchiſedech. \x - \xt Heb. 5. v. 7.\x*
\v 5 Our Lord on thy righthand, \f m \fr 109.5 \ft Kinges that ſometimes perſecute Chriſtians, are ſubdued with other people to Chriſt.\f* hath broken kinges in the day of his wrath.
\v 6 He \f n \fr 109.6 \ft He shal iudge and punish the incredulous people,\f* shal iudge in nations, he \f o \fr 109.6 \ft make great ſlaghters amongſt thoſe that reſiſt :\f* shal fil ruines, he \f p \fr 109.6 \ft and bring princes with their populous kingdoms to nothing.\f* shal crush the heads in the land of manie.
\v 7 \f q \fr 109.7 \ft He shal in the meane time (and alſo his beſt ſeruants) ſuffer much tribulation in this life :\f* Of the torrent in the way he shal drinke: \f r \fr 109.7 \ft and for the ſame be highly exalted in life euerlaſting.\f* therfore shal he exalt the head.
\c 110
\cd Praiſe of God for benefites, 4. eſpecially for the B. Sacrament of the Euchariſt, 6. with other graces imperted to the Catholique Church. \f - \ft Graces given to the Church. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. I wil confeſſe to thee ô Lord with al my hart: in \f a \fr 110.1 \ft I wil praiſe God both in ſecret for diſcharge of myn owne conſcience;\f* the counſel of the iuſt, and \f b \fr 110.1 \ft and in publique for edification of others.\f* the congregation. \f 1 \fr 110.1 \ft This Pſalme in the Hebrew is compoſed with euerie verſe and midle of verſe, beginning with a diſtinct letter, in order of the Alphabet.\f*
\v 2 The workes of our Lord are great: exquiſite according \f c \fr 110.2 \ft Gods wil is the whole cauſe of al his workes.\f* to al his willes.
\v 3 \f d \fr 110.3 \ft Euerie worke of his is praiſe worthie, and magnifical.\f* Confeſſion and magnificence his worke: and his iuſtice continueth for euer and euer.
\v 4 He hath made \f e \fr 110.4 \ft God hath leift one moſt ſpecial and beneficial memorie of al other benefites, his owne bodie and bloud, in memorie of his Paſſion, and our redemption,\f* a memorie of his meruelous workes; merciful and pittiful Lord:
\v 5 he hath geuen \f f \fr 110.5 \ft the ſpiritual foode and ſuſtinance of al the ſoules that rightly feare him.\f* meate to them that feare him. He wil be mindful for euer \f g \fr 110.5 \ft Of his promiſe to conſerue his Church perpetually.\f* of his teſtament:
\v 6 \f h \fr 110.6 \ft the powrable operation of his death, and of al his myſteries.\f* the force of his workes he wil shew forth to his people:
\v 7 To geue them the inheritance of the gentiles: the workes of his handes truth, and iudgement.
\v 8 Al his commandmentes \f i \fr 110.8 \ft Gods commandments do iuſtifie al that kepe them.\f* are faithful: confirmed for euer and euer, made in truth and equitie.
\v 9 He ſent \f k \fr 110.9 \ft He alſo of his mercie redemed man; that he might be able to kepe his precepts.\f* redemption to his people: he commanded his teſtament for euer. Holie, and terrible is his name:
\v 10 \f l \fr 110.10 \ft Begingning with feare of God, bringeth at laſt by other degrees to true wiſdom, which two are the firſt and laſt of the ſeuen giftes of the Holie Ghoſt.\f* the feare of our Lord is the beginning of wiſedom. Vnderſtanding is good to al that doe it: his prayſe remaineth for euer and euer.
\c 111
\cd True happines conſiſteth in fearing God, keping his commandments, 5. and in doing workes of mercie. 10. The contrarie bringeth to miſerie. \f - \ft The meanes to be happie. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia, \f a \fr 111.1 \ft The Septuagint Interpreters added this mention of Aggeus and Zacharie, returning from captiuitie, to ſignifie that this Pſalme was very proper, & meete to be comended to the people at that time, wherby they might lerne, that their ſinnes were the cauſe of their captiuitie, and of al their miſeries: and if they deſired temporal, or ſpiritual proſperitie, they muſt obſerue the meanes here preſcribed to obtayne the ſame.\f* Of the returne of Aggeus, and Zacharie. \x - \xt Pſal. 1.\x* BLESSED is the man that feareth our Lord: \f b \fr 111.1 \ft He that ſincerly feareth God, wil take great delight in keping his commandments.\f* he shal haue great delight in his commandmentes. \f 2 \fr 111.1 \ft This Pſalme is alſo compoſed by the Alphabet, as the next before.\f*
\v 2 \f c \fr 111.2 \ft So doing he and his shal proſper.\f* His ſeede shal be mightie in the earth: the generation of the righteous shal be bleſſed,
\v 3 Glorie, and riches in his houſe: and \f d \fr 111.3 \ft The iuſt shal not only proſper in this world but alſo in the next.\f* his iuſtice abideth for euer and euer.
\v 4 \f e \fr 111.4 \ft God wil alſo comfort the iuſt in tribulations.\f* Light is riſen vp in darkenes to the righteous: he is merciful, and pitiful, and iuſt.
\v 5 Acceptable is the man, that is merciful and lendeth, that \f f \fr 111.5 \ft That shal geue diſcrete and wholſome counſel to the afflicted.\f* shal diſpoſe his wordes in iudgement:
\v 6 Becauſe he shal not be moued for euer.
\v 7 The iuſt shal be in eternal memorie: he shal not feare at the hearing of euil.
\v 8 His hart is readie to hope in our Lord, his hart is confirmed:
\v 9 he shal not be moued til he looke ouer his enemies.
\v 10 He diſtributed, he gaue to the poore: \f g \fr 111.10 \ft Workes of mercie are alſo called iuſtice, becauſe they concurre to mans iuſtification,\f* his iuſtice remaineth for euer and euer \f h \fr 111.10 \ft and to his ſaluation.\f* his horne shal be exalted in glorie. \x - \xt 2. Cor. 9.\x*
\v 11 The ſinner shal ſee, and wil be angrie, he shal gnash his teeth and pine away: the deſire of ſinners shal perish.
\c 112
\cd God is to be praiſed, who being hiegh regardeth, and prouideth for the needie in this world. \f - \ft God'ſ providence. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. PRAYSE our Lord ye \f a \fr 112.1 \ft Al Gods ſeruants.\f* children: praiſe ye the name of our Lord.
\v 2 Be the name of our Lord bleſſed, from henceforth now and for euer.
\v 3 From the riſing of the ſunne vnto the going downe, the name of our Lord is laudable.
\v 4 Our Lord is high aboue al nations, and his glorie aboue the heauens.
\v 5 Who is as the Lord our God, that dwelleth on high,
\v 6 and beholdeth the low thinges \f b \fr 112.6 \ft In reſpect of God al creatures are low, though they be in heauen.\f* in heauen and in earth?
\v 7 Raiſing vp the needie from the earth, and lifting vp the poore out of the dung:
\v 8 \f c \fr 112.8 \ft See the example of Ioſeph ſo aduanced\f* To place him with princes, with the princes of his people.
\v 9 Who maketh the \f d \fr 112.9 \ft of Sara, Rebecca, Rachael, and other wemen made fruitful.\f* barren woman to dwel in a houſe, a ioyful mother of children.
\c 113
\cd For the meruelous paſſage of Iſrael out of Ægypt, 3. the red ſea, the riuer of Iordan, 7. and the hilles geuing them place, 8. the rockes yelding them water, 9. God, not themſelues, is to be praiſed. 12. Idoles and Idolaters are vaine, and shal be confounded: 17. the faithful truſt in God, 20. are bleſſed, and for euer praiſe God. \f - \ft The marvelous paſſage of Iſrael from Ægypt. The 4. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Allelu ia. IN the comming forth of Iſrael out of Ægypt, of the houſe of Iacob from \f a \fr 113.1 \ft People of falſe religion counted barbarous, eſpecially ſuch as alſo perſecute the true Religion; for otherwiſe the Ægyptians were both ciuil in maners, and lerned in manie ſciences.\f* the barbarous people.
\v 2 \f b \fr 113.2 \ft The people of Iewes were more notoriouſly renowmed in the world from the time of their deliuerie out of Ægypt, for the peculiar people, whom God ſanctified, and in whom, as in his elected enheritance or dominion, he dwelled and reigned.\f* Iewrie was made his ſanctification, Iſrael his dominion.
\v 3 The \f c \fr 113.3 \ft The Pſalmiſt writing in verſe doth often deſcribe thinges in poetical maner, but more truly then prophane poetes: for that in very dede, al creatures otherwiſe ſenſles, as the ſea, do in a ſorte feele the powre of their Creator, & obey his wil,\f* ſea ſaw, and \f d \fr 113.3 \ft VVhen the Iſraelites went forth of Ægypt,\f* fled: Iordan \f e \fr 113.3 \ft when they entred into Chanaan,\f* was turned backeward. \x - \xt Exo. 14. Ioſue 3.\x*
\v 4 \f f \fr 113.4 \ft Either there was an earthquake, or ſome other mouing of hilles not mentioned by Moyſes, or els the Pſalmiſt ſpeaketh of the rockes of the torrentes which bowed, that the Iſraelites might reſt in Ar, and lie in the borders of the Moabites, Num, \+it 21, v, 15\+it*,\f* The mountaines leaped as rammes: and the litle hilles as the lambes of sheepe.
\v 5 \f g \fr 113.5 \ft By the figure Apoſtrophe he ſpeaketh to the ſea, riuer, and hilles : vſing alſo Proſopopœia: as if ſenſles thinges, vnderſtood, and should anſwer.\f* What ayleth thee ô ſea that thou didſt flee: and thou ô Iordan, that thou waſt turned backeward?
\v 6 Ye mountaines leaped as rammes, and ye litle hilles as the lambes of shepe.
\v 7 At the face of our Lord was the earth moued, at the face of the God of Iacob.
\v 8 Who turned \f h \fr 113.8 \ft An other miraculous benefite, that the rocke yelded them water in their neceſſitie.\f* the rocke into pooles of waters, and ſtonie hil into fountaines of waters.
\v 9 \f 1 \fr 113.9 \ft Here ſome Hebrewe Rabbins beginne an other Pſalme, but by the coherence of the matter, S. Auguſtin proueth that it is but one Pſalme, where is shewed that the true inuiſible God is knowen by ſuch workes as are here recited: and contrariwiſe, that the Gentils idoles are not goddes, becauſe they are made of ſiluer, gold, or other matter, by mens handes, hauing reſemblance of liuing thinges are altogether ſenſles.\f* \sc NOT TO VS O LORD, NOT\sc* TO VS: but to thy name geue the glorie.
\v 10 For \f i \fr 113.10 \ft Thou didſt al this ô God, of mere mercie towards thy people :\f* thy mercie, and \f k \fr 113.10 \ft for thy truths ſake, ſeing thou didſt promiſe to protect them :\f* thy truth: leſt at any time \f l \fr 113.10 \ft that the Gentiles should not take occaſion to blaſpheme.\f* the Gentiles ſay: Where is their God?
\v 11 But our God is in heauen: he hath done al thinges whatſoeuer he would.
\v 12 The idols of the gentiles are ſiluer, and gold, the workes of mens handes.
\v 13 They haue mouth, and shal not ſpeake: they haue eies, and shal not ſee.
\v 14 They haue eares, and shal not heare: they haue noſthrels and shal not ſmel.
\v 15 They haue handes, and shal not handle: they haue feete, and shal not walke: they shal not crie in their throte.
\v 16 \f m \fr 113.16 \ft This is a iuſt prayer of the zelous, conforming their deſires to Gods wil But if God geue idolaters grace to amend, then al the iuſt wil alſo reioice in their conuerſion.\f* Let them that make them become like to them: and al that haue confidence in them.
\v 17 \f n \fr 113.17 \ft Though manie Iewes fel to idolatrie, yet there alwayes remained ſo manie in Gods true ſeruice, that it might ſtil be truly ſaide : The houſe of Iſrael hath hoped in our Lord, as is here auerred.\f* The houſe of Iſrael hath hoped in our Lord: he is their helper and their protector.
\v 18 The houſe of Aaron hath hoped in our Lord: he is their helper and their protector.
\v 19 They that feare our Lord, haue hoped in our Lord: he is their helper and their protector.
\v 20 Our Lord hath beene mindful of vs: and hath bleſſed vs: He hath bleſſed the houſe of Iſrael: he hath bleſſed the houſe of Aaron.
\v 21 He hath bleſſed al, that feare our Lord, the litle with the great.
\v 22 Our Lord adde vpon you: vpon you, & vpon your children.
\v 23 Bleſſed be you of our Lord, which made heauen, and earth.
\v 24 \f o \fr 113.24 \ft This in effect al worldlie politikes ſay in their hartes : as it were quitting their intereſt of heauen to God,\f* The heauen of heauen is to our Lord: but \f p \fr 113.24 \ft and contenting themſelues with earthlie poſſeſſions.\f* the earth he hath geuen to the children of men.
\v 25 \f q \fr 113.25 \ft But when ſuch prophane men are dead, they make no ſhew at al of praiſing God :\f* The dead shal not prayſe thee ô Lord: nor al they, \f r \fr 113.25 \ft for parting from the earth, they deſcend into hel, and there eternally blaſpheme God.\f* that goe downe hel.
\v 26 But we \f s \fr 113.26 \ft Contrarivviſe the iuſt, aſpiring to heauen, vvhich is the proper kingdom of God, & vſing this world as they ought to do, for a meanes to aſcend into heauen, ſhal bleſſe and praiſe God for euermore.\f* that liue, doe bleſſe our Lord, from this time, and for euer.
\c 114
\cd The prayer of a iuſt man in tribulation, with confidence and gratitude towards God. \f - \ft A prayer, with praiſe of God. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Allelu ia. I \f a \fr 114.1 \ft I am induced to loue God,\f* haue loued, becauſe our Lord \f b \fr 114.1 \ft becauſe he alwayes heareth my prayers.\f* wil heare the voice of my prayer.
\v 2 Becauſe he hath inclined his eare to me: and \f c \fr 114.2 \ft So long as I shal liue.\f* in my daies I wil inuocate.
\v 3 The ſorowes \f d \fr 114.3 \ft VVhen I erred like a ſtray sheepe from thee, the paines of death,\f* of death haue compaſſed me: and \f e \fr 114.3 \ft and the danger of hel-torments, both due for ſinne, inuironed me, and I was not ware therof:\f* the perils of hel haue found me. \f f \fr 114.3 \ft But by tribulation falling vpon me, I came to knowe my dangerous eſtate :\f* I haue found tribulation and ſorow:
\v 4 and I \f g \fr 114.4 \ft turned to thee, and prayed as foloweth :\f* inuocated the name of our Lord. O Lord deliuer my ſoule:
\v 5 our Lord is merciful, and iuſt, and our God doth mercie.
\v 6 Our Lord kepeth litle ones: \f h \fr 114.6 \ft Afflicted with tribulations.\f* I was humbled, and he hath deliuered me.
\v 7 Turne ô my ſoule into thy reſt: becauſe our Lord hath done good to thee.
\v 8 Becauſe he hath deliuered my ſoule from death: mine eies from teares, my feete from ſliding.
\v 9 I \f i \fr 114.9 \ft I wil endeuoure to pleaſe God, in the congregation of thoſe that liue here in grace, and in heauen in glorie.\f* wil pleaſe our Lord in the countrie of the liuing.
\c 115
\cd A iuſt man acknowledging that ſpiritual life beginneth by faith, and by publique profeſſion therof, 4. gratfully accepteth of Chriſts Redemption, 5. dedicateth his life, and al he hath to Gods ſeruice. \f - \ft Thanks-giving for our Redeemer. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. I \f a \fr 115.1 \ft I beleued that God would helpe me:\f* beleued, \f b \fr 115.1 \ft therfore I freely profeſſed that I truſted in him. For then in dede faith is perfect, when we confeſſe with mouth, that which we beleue in hart:\f* for which cauſe I ſpake: but I \f c \fr 115.1 \ft I was vehemently afflicted in tribulations.\f* was humbled excedingly. \f 1 \fr 115.1 \ft This in the Hebrew is ioyned to the next Pſalme before.\f*
\v 2 I ſaid \f d \fr 115.2 \ft In the middes of my great affliction I profeſſed, that al mans helpe is vaine, falſe, deceipful, and defectiue, and therfore our truſt muſt be in God only.\f* in mine exceſſe: Euerie man is a lyer.
\v 3 What \f e \fr 115.3 \ft Conſidering that God hath not only geuen, and beſtowed manie great benefites vpon me, and al mankind, but alſo hath rendered good for euil, mercie for our ſinnes, we hauing rendered euil for good : what now ſhal I render, ſayth a true penitent, for al that he hath thus rendered to me, deſeruing ſo euil?\f* shal I render to our Lord, for al thinges that he \f f \fr 115.3 \ft See previous note\f* hath rendred to me?
\v 4 I \f g \fr 115.4 \ft Seing I am not able to render anie thing worthie of Gods fauoure to me, yet I wil do that I can : I wil gratfully accept his great benefite. the cuppe of Chriſts paſſion, which he druunke for mankind,\f* wil take the chalice of ſaluation: and \f h \fr 115.4 \ft and wil praiſe, and cal vpon his name.\f* I wil inuocat the name of our Lord.
\v 5 I wil \f i \fr 115.5 \ft I wil pay voluntarie vowes,\f* render my vowes to our Lord \f k \fr 115.5 \ft for Gods glorie, and edification of others.\f* before al his people:
\v 6 \f l \fr 115.6 \ft yea I wil offer my life, and ſuffer death, when Gods glorie shal require it, in whoſe ſight, the death of Sainctes is precious, and moſt highly eſteemed.\f* precious in the ſight of our Lord is the death of his Sainctes.
\v 7 O Lord becauſe \f m \fr 115.7 \ft Alvvayes vnderſtood, that ſuch as ſuffer perſecution, be in good ſtate of their ſoules, the true ſeruantes of God,\f* I am thy ſeruant: I am thy ſeruant, and \f n \fr 115.7 \ft the children of the Church his handmaide.\f* the ſonne of thy handmaide. Thou haſt \f o \fr 115.7 \ft Deliuered me from captiuitie of ſinne.\f* broken my bondes:
\v 8 I wil ſacrifice to thee the hoaſt of praiſe, and I wil inuocate the name of our Lord.
\v 9 I wil render my vowes to our Lord in the ſight of al his people:
\v 10 in the courtes of the houſe of our Lord, in the middes of \f p \fr 115.10 \ft In the Church of the faithful.\f* thee ô Ieruſalem.
\c 116
\cd Gods mercie is largely extended to al Gentiles by Chriſt, and his promiſe withal is performed to the Iewes. \f - \ft The Church of Chriſt in al nations. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Allelu ia. PRAYSE our Lord \f a \fr 116.1 \ft Not only ſome, but al nations of the Gentiles :\f* al ye Gentiles: prayſe him \f b \fr 116.1 \ft and al Ievves, (Chriſts Redemption being abundantly ſufficient for al) are inuited to praiſe God.\f* al ye peoples. \x - \xt Rom. 15. v. 11.\x*
\v 2 Becauſe \f c \fr 116.2 \ft Becauſe he hath multiplied his mercie to vs Gentiles, to vvhom he made no promiſe :\f* his mercie is confirmed vpon vs: and his \f d \fr 116.2 \ft and moſt truly performed his promiſe made to the Ievves,\f* truth remaineth for euer.
\c 117
\cd Faithful people collected in the Church of Chriſt, exhorte each other to render thankes to God, for their deliuerie from ſpiritual and temporal tribulations. 16. The Laitie demand participation of Chriſts Myſteries, promiſing to ſerue him duly: 25. Which the Paſtors freely impert, and together with the people, ſolemnely celebrate Gods praiſe. \f - \ft Chriſt beneficial Myſteries are celebrated by his Church. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Allelu ia. CONFESSE ye to our Lord becauſe he is \f a \fr 117.1 \ft Let vs praiſe God, for his goodnes, in making vs of nothing, geuing vs manie benefites,\f* good: becauſe \f b \fr 117.1 \ft and remitting our ſinnes.\f* his mercie is for euer. \x - \xt Pſal. 105. 106. 135.\x*
\v 2 Let Iſrael \f c \fr 117.2 \ft Let the Church of the new teſtament eſpecially confeſſe his goodnes, which hath receiued more mercie and grace.\f* now ſay that he is good: that his mercie is for euer.
\v 3 Let the houſe of Aaron \f d \fr 117.3 \ft Let al the Clergie praiſe God now in the time of more grace, and of greater ſpiritual functions.\f* now ſay: that his mercie is for euer.
\v 4 Let them now ſay \f e \fr 117.4 \ft Yea let the whole bodie of the Church, al that feare, and ſerue God praiſe his mercie.\f* which feare our Lord: that his mercie is for euer.
\v 5 From \f f \fr 117.5 \ft As wel ſpiritual as temporal.\f* tribulation I inuocated our Lord: and our Lord heard me in largenes.
\v 6 Our Lord is my helper: I wil not feare what man can doe to me.
\v 7 Our Lord is my helper: and I wil looke ouer mine enemies.
\v 8 It is good to hope in our Lord, rather then to hope in man.
\v 9 It is good to hope in our Lord, rather then to hope in princes.
\v 10 \f g \fr 117.10 \ft Though innumerable oppoſe, and endeuour to hurt me (ſaith the Church, or anie iuſt perſon)\f* Al nations haue compaſſed me: and in the name of our Lord am I \f h \fr 117.10 \ft yet by Gods povvre, not by myn ovvne, I am defended, and they puniſhed, and ſo the iuſt hath the victorie, and triumpheth,\f* reuenged on them.
\v 11 Compaſſing they haue compaſſed me: and in the name of our Lord I was reuenged on them.
\v 12 They compaſſed me as \f i \fr 117.12 \ft In great trooper, and furie,\f* bees, and were inflamed as \f k \fr 117.12 \ft vvith ſharpe though ſhorte force, and vvith ſpecial noiſe, to terrifie me, but in God I ouercame al.\f* fyre in thornes: and in the name of our Lord I was reuenged on them.
\v 13 Being thruſt \f l \fr 117.13 \ft I was ſometimes by vehemencie of tentation, declining to ſinne :\f* I was ouerturned to fal: and our Lord \f m \fr 117.13 \ft but Gods grace aſſiſted and ſtrengthned me.\f* receiued me.
\v 14 Our Lord is my ſtrength, and my prayſe: and he is made my ſaluation.
\v 15 The voice of exultation, and of ſaluation in the tabernacles of the iuſt.
\v 16 The right hand of our Lord hath wrought ſtrength: the right hand of our Lord hath exalted me, \f n \fr 117.16 \ft The ſame vvord [ right hand ] thriſe mentioned, ſignifieth the B. Trinitie. Alſo [ Our Lord] ſignifying Chriſt in his humanitie the chief inſtrument of God, is here often repeted, to ſignifie the ſingular efficacie therof.\f* the right hand of our Lord hath wrought ſtrength.
\v 17 I shal not die, but shal liue: and I wil tel the workes of our Lord.
\v 18 Our Lord \f o \fr 117.18 \ft God chaſtiſeth his children,\f* chaſtiſing hath chaſtiſed me: and \f p \fr 117.18 \ft becauſe he would not that they ſhould dye eternally. So he puniſheth as a father, not as an enimie.\f* to death he hath not deliuered me.
\v 19 \f q \fr 117.19 \ft The Prophet now ſpeaketh in the perſon of iuſt ſoules, requiring ſpiritual doctrin, and foode,\f* Open ye the gates of iuſtice to me, being entred into them I wil confeſſe to our Lord:
\v 20 \f r \fr 117.20 \ft and promiſing to ſerue God.\f* this is the gate of our Lord, the iuſt shal enter into it.
\v 21 I wil confeſſe to thee becauſe thou haſt heard me: and art become my ſaluation.
\v 22 \f s \fr 117.22 \ft An euident prophecie of Chriſt vttered by the Royal Pſalmiſt, and now confeſſed by euerie Chriſtian, that our Sauiour reiected by the Ievves, is neuertheles the builder of his Church, by ioyning the tvvo peoples of Iewes and Gentiles, as two vvalles into one houſe.\f* The ſtone, which the builders reiected: the ſame is made into the head of the corner.
\v 23 This was done by our Lord: and it is meruelous in our eies.
\v 24 This \f t \fr 117.24 \ft God ordayned this acceptable time of grace. · Mat. 21. Luc. 10. Act. 4. Rom. 9. 1. Pet. 2.\f* is the day, which our Lord made: let vs reioice, and be glad therein.
\v 25 \f v \fr 117.25 \ft The tongue of the Hebrew children, when Chriſt entered Ieruſalem with palmes of triumph, and acclamations.\f* O Lord ſaue me, ô Lord geue good ſucceſſe: Bleſſed be he that cometh in the name of our Lord. \x - \xt Matt. 21. Lu. 20. Act. 4. Rom. 9. 1. Pet. 2.\x*
\v 26 \f w \fr 117.26 \ft The voice of Chriſt, and his Apoſtles and other Clergie bleſſing the people as they deſire.\f* We haue bleſſed you of the houſe of our Lord:
\v 27 Our Lord is God, and he hath geuen light to vs. Appoint a ſolemne day with \f x \fr 117.27 \ft This was fulfilled when Chriſt was brought with bowes of palme, and other ſignes of triumph, from Bethania,\f* thicke bowes, euen to \f y \fr 117.27 \ft through the whole citie, euen into the Temple and vnto the Altar : Mat. \+it 21.\+it*\f* the horne of the altar.
\v 28 Thou art my God, and I wil confeſſe to thee: thou art my God, and I wil exalt thee. I wil confeſſe to thee becauſe thou haſt heard me: and art become my ſaluation.
\v 29 \f z \fr 117.29 \ft Our firſt, chiefe, and final dutie is to praiſe God. \+it v. 1\+it*. & vlt.\f* Confeſſe ye to our Lord becauſe he is good: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\c 118
\cd A perpetual recommendation of the ſingular excellencie, abſolute neceſſitie, and eternal heauenlie profite of Gods law : with frequent aſpirations to perfection, hatred of ſinne, loue of vertue, and feruent deſire to reſt in God. \f - \ft Perfect juſtice is in keeping God's law. The 7. key.\f*
\s1 GENERAL ANNOTATIONS UPON THIS CXVIII. PSALM.
\ip As this Psalm is the longest in the whole Psalter, so it seemeth to the ancient Fathers most profound in sense. And so much the harder to be understood, because also the very hardness \f - \ft The obscurity of this profound Psalm appeareth not to the vulgar reader.\f* thereof lieth hidden, which in diverse other Psalms and parts of holy Scripture, easily appeareth to the reader. But here the words being clear, and the sense also plain and easy in some points of doctrine, yet the more diligence is employed, the more difficulty is found in searching the whole sense and meaning of every word and sentence, with the manner observed in composing it, and the frequent repetition of the same or like words. All which maturely considered caused that great Clerk, and light of the Church Saint Augustin, to omit this Psalm, when he explicated all the \f - \ft S. Augustin deferred the explication of this Psalm. Omitted to discuss one difficulty.\f* rest. And when at last he added also this, he wittingly omitted one special difficulty, which he doubted not to be contained in the manner of composing it, not only by order of the Hebrew Alphabet, as diverse more Psalms, and some other parts of holy Scripture, but more artificially than any other, the first eight verses all beginning with the first letter Aleph; the next eight, with the second letter Beth: and so to the last of the two and twenty letters. Of which omission he yieldeth this only reason, because he found nothing (as he humbly affirmeth) that might properly pertain thereunto. Confessing also expressly, that whensoever he applied his cogitations to expound the text it-self, it always exceeded his hability. But finally to satisfy the often and earnest request of his brethren and friends, trusting (as always) in God's special help, he largely expoundeth it, in thirty two distinct Sermons. \f - \ft At last made 32. sermons in explication thereof.\f*
\ip S. Ambrose, also moved with like piety, made two and twenty Sermons in exposition of this \f - \ft S. Ambrose writ 22. sermons upon this Psalm.\f* Psalm. Affirming in his Prologue, that amongst other Psalms, especially this sheweth how great a master King David was of moral good life. For all Moral doctrine, being of his own nature sweet, yet most delighteth the ears, and gently toucheth the mind, being uttered, as here it is, with pleasantness of verse, and sweetness of song. Again whereas this Royal Prophet in many places of this book poureth out sentences of moral Psalms or songs, as bright stars, that shine and \f - \ft King David a great master of moral doctrine.\f* glister to all the world, here most excellently he produceth a more singular mirror, as the sun, of full light, burning with meridian heat. And for the profit of all, the better to draw our \f - \ft Why this Psalm was composed in order of the Alphabet.\f* attentions, to learn that we may, though we cannot attain to all that we would, he disposed this Psalm through all the Alphabet: that as children beginning with the first letters, make entrance to further knowledge; so by the same beginnings we should lay the first foundation, and thereupon proceed in our spiritual building, towards perfection in good life, the true service of God. Which is yet further insinuated (as the same Doctor teacheth) by the eight verses continually beginning with the same letter, and so other eight in order through the whole Alphabet, to signify that \f - \ft Why eight verses are begun with every letter.\f* after seven days travail in this temporal life, we may come to that unity, which we expected in the eighth day of resurrection, when we hope to rise revived in our Lord Jesus, in newness of eternal life.
\ip Likewise Saint Basil in the Argument of this Psalm admonisheth, that whereas holy David, \f - \ft Saint Basil's judgement that this Psalm containeth the argument of many Psalms.\f* according to diverse states, which he passed, writ diverse Psalms: as when he fled from his enemies, when he lamented his distresses, mourned in pensiveness, enjoyed peace and comfort, ran a right course of virtue, fell from God by sin, & again returning observed God's laws; in this one Psalm he comprehendeth all his prayers made to God at sundry times, & here proposeth the same, a certain profitable moral doctrine, to all sorts and states of men. Neither doth he pretermit doctrinal points of faith, but interposeth them also with moral documents, in such sort, that this one Psalm may suffice to teach the well disposed, how to attain to perfection in virtue, to stir up the slothful unto diligent care of their souls, to recreate the desolate with spiritual consolations, & briefly it administereth all kind of medicine to the diverse passions of mortal men.
\ip For the like judgements of other Fathers we remit the learned reader, to Saint Hilary, \f - \ft Other expositors of this Psalm.\f* Theodoret, Prosper, Arnobius, Cassiodorus, Beda, Euthymius, and others, but cannot well omit a brief instruction of S. Jerome. Who in his Epistle to Paula Urbica: not only sheweth the interpretation of the two and twenty letters, but also explicateth their sense in this place, by connecting them into certain short sentences, in this manner: \f - \ft S. Jerome's interpretation, and explication of the Hebrew Alphabet.\f*
\ip Which is the first connection, signifying that the doctrine of the house, that is the Church of God, is found in the fullness of divine books.
\ip The second connection is:
\ip For what other life can there be without knowledge of Scriptures? Whereby also Christ is known, who is the life of them that believe in him.
\ip The third connection is: \f - \ft \+it idem prooem. lamen.\+it*\f*
\ip Albeit we now could know all things which are written, yet we know but in part, and in part we prophesy: for we see now by a glass, in a dark sort, but when we shall be worthy to be with \f - \ft \+it 1. Cor. 13.\+it*\f* Christ, and shall be like to Angels, then doctrine of books shall cease, & then we shall see face to face the Good Beginning, even as he is. \f - \ft \+it God in himself.\+it*\f*
\ip The fourth connection is: \f - \ft Most of these letters have also other significations. And are diversely explicated by S. Ambrose, S. Beda, and others. Whereby we may learn (though we understand no more) that holy Scriptures are full of mysteries (as S. Jerome calleth this) & hard to be understood.\f*
\ip The hands are understood in work, heart and discipline are understood in sense or meaning, because we cannot rightly do any thing, unless we first know what things are to be done. The fifth connection is:
\ip This needeth not explication, for it is manifest as the light, that from Scriptures are eternal helps. The sixth connection is:
\ip According to that which we have expounded in the fourth connection: that deeds & intention must concur.
\ip The seventh connection which is last, in which number of seven is also mystical understanding:
\ip Distinct voice is produced by the teeth, & in these signs we come to the Head of all which is Christ, by whom we have access to the everlasting Kingdom.
\ip Or thus (not transposing the words) By vocation of Christ the Head, through distinct voice of signs (for words are signs shewing the mind) we are conducted to the eternal Kingdom, the happiness which all men desire.
\ip What I pray thee (saith this holy Doctor) is more sacred than this mystery, what more pleasant than this delight? What meat, & what honey are sweeter, than to know God's wisdom; to enter into his secret closet; to behold the sense of our Creator; and to teach the words of thy Lord God, full of spiritual wisdom, which are derided by the wise of this world.
\ip We must also advertise the reader of the like discourses of ancient Fathers (over long to be here recited) concerning the manifold high praises of God's Law, contained in this Psalm, with \f - \ft God's law escpecially commended in this Psalm.\f* frequent repetition of certain Synonyma words signifying the same thing, in all fourteen, to wit: The Law of God, his Ways, Testimonies, Commandments, Precepts, Statutes, Justifications, \f - \ft 14. Synonyma signifying the law of God.\f* Judgements, Justice, Equity, Verity, Words, Speeches, & Sermons: of which there is commonly one, in every verse, & sometimes two or three in the same verse. But our English tongue hardly sufficing rightly, to distinguish the three last, which in Latin are Verba, Eloquia, Sermones, we translate WORDS only, adding in the margin, Eloquia, and Sermones, when they occur.
\ip Leaving therefore larger commentaries to others; we shall prosecute our wonted manner of brief glosses. Only here premonishing the diligent readers, especially Clergyman (ourselves and our brethren) who every day sing or read this whole Psalm in the Canonical hours, to observe two particular points of Christian doctrine, evidently proved by many places of this Psalm. The one against the Pelagians' heresy, denying the necessity of God's special grace in meritorious works. For the Psalmist often here inculcateth man's insufficiency, that of himself, and by natural \f - \ft God's grace necessary in every good work.\f* forces, he cannot keep the commandments of God, but needeth always the particular grace of God, as well to believe in him, to repent for sins, and to begin good works; as to proceed, and persevere in good state to the end. The other against the heresy of our time, denying merit by grace & freewill. For here it is also manifest, that God's grace maketh man able, to keep his \f - \ft It enableth freewill to merit.\f* commandments, and by keeping them to become just in this life, and so to merit eternal glory. Sundry other principal Articles of Christian Catholic Religion are likewise comprised in this one Psalm: but especially Moral doctrine.
\p
\v 1 \f 1 \fr 118.1 \ft This title vvas added by the Septuagint, to admoniſh vs that this Pſalme conteyneth that ſingular maner of praiſing God, ſignified by the two Hebrew vvordes Alleluia, as before. Pſal 104.\f* Allelu ia. BLESSED \f a \fr 118.1 \ft Whereas al, without exception, deſire to be happie and bleſſed ;\f* are \f b \fr 118.1 \ft they are in dede happie (according to the perfecteſt happines of this life) that are immaculate :\f* the immaculate in the vvay: which \f c \fr 118.1 \ft and they are immaculate, that walke in the law of God. Where the holie Pſalmiſt preſuppoſeth, that ſome can and do kepe the law of God, and ſo are immaculate, and bleſſed in the way of this life.\f* walke in the law of our Lord.
\v 2 Bleſſed are they, \f d \fr 118.2 \ft Thoſe that are immaculate, are againe bleſſed, by ſearching Gods teſtimonies, that is, his lavv teſtifying that the good ſhal be revvarded, and the vvicked punished, but ſearching theſe teſtimonies, vvhiles one is contaminate vvith ſinnes againſt Gods law, maketh not bleſſed :\f* that ſearch his teſtimonies: that \f e \fr 118.2 \ft neither doth euerie ſuperficial careles ſearch bring this bleſſing, but ſearching with true affection of the hart.\f* ſeeke after him with al their hart.
\v 3 For \f f \fr 118.3 \ft Contrariwiſe they that vvorke iniquitie are not bleſſed ;\f* they that worke iniquitie, haue not walked in his \f g \fr 118.3 \ft becauſe they haue not walked in the vvayes of God, towitte, not kept his commamdments and law, which are the vvay to happines.\f* waies.
\v 4 Thou haſt \f h \fr 118.4 \ft For mans owne good, that he may come to true happines, God hath moſt ſeriouſly commanded vs to kepe his commandments, that is, to obſerue his Law commanded by moſt ſouueraine diuine authoritie.\f* very much commanded thy commandmentes to be kept.
\v 5 Would God my waies \f i \fr 118.5 \ft Therfore the faithful ſeruant of God, knowing his owne inſufficiencie, deſireth that God by his grace vvil direct and ſtreingthen him,\f* might be directed, to keepe thy \f k \fr 118.5 \ft to kepe his lavv, called Iuſtifications, becauſe therby man is made iuſt.\f* iuſtifications.
\v 6 Then shal I \f l \fr 118.6 \ft They shal be ſafe from eternal confuſion, when they ſhal kepe not only part, but al thy commandments, becauſe breach of anie bringeth confuſion.\f* not be confounded, when I shal looke throughly in al thy commandmentes.
\v 7 \f m \fr 118.7 \ft So shal I praiſe thee, and render thankes,\f* I wil confeſſe to thee \f n \fr 118.7 \ft with ſincere not fayned affection,\f* in direction of hart: in that I haue lerned the \f o \fr 118.7 \ft for this great benefite, that I haue lerned, that thy law is according to moſt iuſt iudgement.\f* iudgements of thy iuſtice.
\v 8 \f p \fr 118.8 \ft I haue therfore a firme purpoſe, & do faithfully promiſe to kepe thy law, which maketh the keper therof iuſt.\f* I wil keepe thy iuſtifications: \f q \fr 118.8 \ft Albeit thou ſuffer me ſometimes to be in tribulation, or in tentation, yet forſake me not wholy. The Pſalmiſt knew wel (ſaith S. Gregorie) that he might be profitably leiſt a while, who prayed, that he should not be wholy forſaken.\f* forſake me not wholy.
\v 9 Wherein \f a \fr 118.9 \ft In this ſecond Octonarie, as alſo in al the reſt, the Holie Ghoſt by the prophets penne teacheth the meanes how to come to perfection & happines. Here by way of interrogation, as it were demanding how a youngman, that is euerie man prone to worldlie pleaſure, & ſlow in Gods ſeruice, ſhal beginne to correct his courſe ?\f* doth a yongman correct his way? \f b \fr 118.9 \ft Wherto the ſame Holie Ghoſt anſwereth, that he muſt kepe Gods law, called here his wordes. For al the wordes which God vttereth, are lawes to his ſeruants.\f* in keeping thy \f 1 \fr 118.9 \ft \+it ſermones\+it*.\f* wordes.
\v 10 \f c \fr 118.10 \ft The Pſalmiſt now ſpeaketh in the perſon of perfect iuſt men, or of the whole Church in general. VVhoſe common ſpirite ſeeketh God intyrely.\f* With my whole hart I haue ſought after thee: \f d \fr 118.10 \ft And conſidering that this perfect good wil is the gift of God, prayeth that he wil conſerue the ſame, and not ſuffer it to be altered, or to erre from his commandments.\f* repel me not from thy commandmentes.
\v 11 \f e \fr 118.11 \ft An other ſincere profeſſion of a reſolute good purpoſe not to ſinne.\f* In my hart I haue hid thy \f 1 \fr 118.11 \ft \+it eloquia\+it*.\f* wordes: that I may not ſinne to thee.
\v 12 \f f \fr 118.12 \ft A gratful aſpiration praiſing God.\f* Bleſſed art thou o Lord: \f g \fr 118.12 \ft Againe the iuſt prayeth to be more and more inſtructed in iuſtifications : that which S. Iohn exhorteth vnto : He that is iuſt, let him yet be iuſtified. Apoc. \+it 22\+it*.\f* teach me thy iuſtifications.
\v 13 In my lippes, I haue pronounced al the \f h \fr 118.13 \ft Gods law is alſo called his Iudgements, becauſe ſitting in iudgement he geueth ſentence according to his Law.\f* iudgementes of thy mouth.
\v 14 I am \f i \fr 118.14 \ft As the iuſt profeſſeth by mouth, ſo he delighteth in hart.\f* delighted in the way of thy teſtimonies, as in al riches.
\v 15 I \f k \fr 118.15 \ft practiſeth in worke :\f* wil be exerciſed in thy commandmentes: and I wil conſider thy waies.
\v 16 I \f l \fr 118.16 \ft and diligently meditateth Gods law.\f* wil meditate in thy iuſtifications: I wil not forget thy \f 1 \fr 118.16 \ft \+it ſermones\+it*.\f* wordes.
\v 17 Render \f a \fr 118.17 \ft O Lord liberally geue me that which I here craue,\f* to thy ſeruant, \f b \fr 118.17 \ft quicken me with ſpiritual life, thy grace.\f* quicken me: \f c \fr 118.17 \ft ſo I shal kepe thy law, which otherwiſe I can not.\f* and I shal keepe thy \f 1 \fr 118.17 \ft \+it ſermones\+it*.\f* wordes.
\v 18 \f d \fr 118.18 \ft Illuminate myn vnderſtanding, by thy grace,\f* Reuele mine eies: \f e \fr 118.18 \ft that I may be able to ſee the meruelous great and iuſt reaſons of thy law, inſtructing al, threatning the peruerſe, encoreging the wel diſpoſed, punishing the wicked, rewarding the good, doing right to al.\f* and I shal conſider the meruelous thinges of thy law.
\v 19 \f f \fr 118.19 \ft I that haue but a ſmal time in this world,\f* I am a ſeiourner in the land, \f g \fr 118.19 \ft deſire to be inſtructed in thy law, what is therein commanded.\f* hide not thy commandmentes from me.
\v 20 My ſoule hath coueted to deſire thy iuſtifications, at al time.
\v 21 Thou haſt \f h \fr 118.21 \ft I conſider that thou o God, doſt sharply reproue the prowd contemners of thy commandments :\f* rebuked the prowde: \f i \fr 118.21 \ft laying curſes vpon them for declining from thyn obedience.\f* curſed are they that decline from thy commandmentes.
\v 22 Take from me reproch, and contempt: becauſe I haue ſought after thy teſtimonies.
\v 23 For \f k \fr 118.23 \ft Though perſecutors were very potent,\f* princes ſate, and they ſpake againſt me: but \f l \fr 118.23 \ft yet the faithful ſeruant of God perſeuered in his ſeruice.\f* thy ſeruant was exerciſed in thy iuſtifications.
\v 24 For both \f m \fr 118.24 \ft In time of perſecution and tentation we muſt thincke and meditate, that Gods law teſtifieth eternal revvard, or punishment,\f* thy teſtimonies are my meditation: and \f n \fr 118.24 \ft and in our deliberation or conſultation, we muſt conſider that keping Gods law maketh iuſt : and conſequently meriteth reward.\f* thy iuſtifications my counſel.
\v 25 My ſoule \f a \fr 118.25 \ft This alſo is vttered in the perſon of the iuſt, who is often brought to great diſtreſſe : as it were, euen nere to death,\f* hath cleaued to the pauement: \f b \fr 118.25 \ft in which caſe he confidently prayeth to be relieued, according to Gods word, law, and promiſe.\f* quicken me according to thy word.
\v 26 I haue vttered my wayes, and thou haſt heard me: teach me thy iuſtifications.
\v 27 Inſtruct me the way of thy iuſtifications: and I shal be exerciſed in thy meruelous workes.
\v 28 My ſoule \f c \fr 118.28 \ft Being in ſo great anxietie that my minde is almoſt diſtracted, or ouercome,\f* hath ſlumbered for tediouſnes: \f d \fr 118.28 \ft I cal to thee o God, that thou wilt conſerue me, that I ſtil kepe thy law, vttered by thy wordes.\f* confirme me in thy wordes.
\v 29 \f e \fr 118.29 \ft Protect me that I fal not to iniquitie.\f* Remoue from me the way of iniquitie: and according to thy law, \f f \fr 118.29 \ft And of thy mercie conſerue me in ſtate of grace.\f* haue mercie on me.
\v 30 I haue choſen the way of truth: I haue not forgotten thy iudgements.
\v 31 I haue cleaued to thy teſtimonies o Lord: \f g \fr 118.31 \ft Suffer me not to be confounded.\f* do not confound me.
\v 32 \f h \fr 118.32 \ft Man is able, and doth runne in the right vvay of Gods commandments,\f* I ranne the way of thy commandments: \f i \fr 118.32 \ft yet not of himſelfe, but when God replenisheth his hart with grace.\f* when thou didſt dilate my hart.
\v 33 \f a \fr 118.33 \ft Impreſſe o God thy law in myn affection, make me to loue it, and to deſire to be iuſtified,\f* Set me a law o Lord the way of thy iuſtifications: \f b \fr 118.33 \ft ſo shal I hartely and alwayes ſeeke it.\f* and I wil ſeeke after it alwayes.
\v 34 \f c \fr 118.34 \ft After thou haſt geuen me a deſire to kepe thy lavv, geue me alſo vnderſtanding.\f* Geue me vnderſtanding, \f d \fr 118.34 \ft then ſhal I fruictfully ſearch it. For this is the right order (as before in the firſt and ſecond verſes) firſt to loue Gods lavve, to be iuſtified, and to become immaculate ; and then to ſearch to knovv the lawe, and ſo it is more eaſily lerned.\f* and I wil ſearch thy law: and I wil keepe it with my whole hart.
\v 35 \f e \fr 118.35 \ft Gods grace firſt draweth and leadeth,\f* Conduct me into the path of thy commandments: \f f \fr 118.35 \ft then freewil inflamed with deſire effectually concurreth.\f* becauſe I would it.
\v 36 \f g \fr 118.36 \ft Stil the Prophet inculcateth the neceſſitie of Gods grace, as wel to make vs deſire that is good,\f* Incline my hart into thy teſtimonies: and \f h \fr 118.36 \ft as to flee from euil.\f* not into auarice.
\v 37 \f i \fr 118.37 \ft It is neceſſarie alſo to pray that God wil take away occaſions, which might moue to ſinne :\f* Turne away mine eies that they ſee not vanitie: \f k \fr 118.37 \ft and ſtil to grant his helping grace in progreſſe of vertue.\f* in thy way quicken me.
\v 38 \f l \fr 118.38 \ft Againe the iuſt prayeth for confirmation in grace, to be eſtablished in the feare of God.\f* Eſtablish thy \f 1 \fr 118.38 \ft \+it eloquium\+it*.\f* word to thy ſeruant, in thy feare.
\v 39 \f m \fr 118.39 \ft To be deliuered alſo from al the effectes of former ſinnes,\f* Take away my reproch, which I haue feared: \f n \fr 118.39 \ft for ſinne is therfore reprochful and odious, becauſe it is contrarie to Gods law, and true iudgements, vvhich are moſt pleaſant.\f* becauſe thy iudgements are pleaſant.
\v 40 Behold I haue coueted thy commandments: in thine equitie \f o \fr 118.40 \ft Being thus affected with deſire to kepe the commandments, the ſoule prayeth to be ſtil quickned, more and more vvith good ſpirite, and ſo to perſeuere to the end.\f* quicken me.
\v 41 And \f a \fr 118.41 \ft Againe conſidering that without Gods grace preuenting, man can not do anie good thing, the prophet renevveth his prayer, requeſting Gods mercie,\f* let thy mercie come vpon me o Lord: \f b \fr 118.41 \ft and his helpe freely promiſed to al that aske it.\f* thy ſaluation according to thy \f 1 \fr 118.41 \ft \+it eloquium\+it*.\f* word.
\v 42 And \f c \fr 118.42 \ft VVhervvith being aſſiſted and ſtreingthned, he that before vvas vveake wil boldly anſvver al calumniators ; that reprochfully ſay : God wil not helpe him :\f* I shal anſwer a word to them that vpbrayde me: \f d \fr 118.42 \ft that in dede he hath not in vaine truſted in Gods promiſed helpe.\f* becauſe I haue hoped in thy \f 1 \fr 118.42 \ft \+it ſermonibus\+it*.\f* wordes.
\v 43 And \f e \fr 118.43 \ft He alſo prayeth, though he be ſometimes fearful, that God vvil not ſuffer him vvholly to omitte manifeſt profeſſion of faith and true religion,\f* take not away out of my mouth the word of truth vtterly: \f f \fr 118.43 \ft ſeing by thy former grace I haue already repoſed my truſt in thy promiſes, made to them that are reſolued to kepe thy law.\f* becauſe I haue much hoped in thy iudgementes.
\v 44 And \f g \fr 118.44 \ft For I do firmly purpoſe euer and alwayes to kepe thy law.\f* I wil keepe thy law alwayes: for euer, and for euer and euer.
\v 45 And \f h \fr 118.45 \ft In this I haue had great ioy and comforte of mind :\f* I walked in largeneſſe: \f i \fr 118.45 \ft becauſe I did in dede ſeeke after thy commandements, which is ſpecially vttered (as alſo the three next verſes) in the perſon of thoſe, that are in trial of perſecution for their faith :\f* becauſe I haue ſought after thy commandments.
\v 46 And \f k \fr 118.46 \ft Who boldly in time of perſecution, euen before perſecuting Kinges and Emperors, profeſſe Chriſts true Religion. Verified in innumerable glorious Martyrs, yea alſo of the fraile ſexe, in S Catharin, S. Cecilie, S. Lucie, S. Margaret, S. VVenefrede, S. Vrſula, and her felovves, and manie more, moſt conſtantly anſwering al vvordes of reproch obiected, as if it vvere a baſe or contemtible thing to be Chriſtians, to be Catholiques, to be Papiſtes No, al theſe and the like, are honorable and glorious titles ; importing the true ſeruice of Chriſt ; in vnitie of the Catholique Church ; and ſpiritual participation with the viſible head therof, Chriſts Vicar in earth.\f* I ſpake of thy teſtimonies in the ſight of kinges: and was not confounded.
\v 47 And \f l \fr 118.47 \ft Such confeſſors as yet mortal, reioyce in that they haue meditated in Gods commandments, which they haue feruently loued.\f* I meditated in thy commandments, which I loued.
\v 48 And \f m \fr 118.48 \ft Alſo ſhevved the ſame in external worke, not diſſembling by ſilence, by word, nor fact.\f* I haue lifted vp my handes to thy commandments, which I loued: and \f n \fr 118.48 \ft euerie way exerciſing Gods lavv, which maketh the obſeruers iuſt.\f* I was exerciſed in thy iuſtifications.
\v 49 Be \f a \fr 118.49 \ft That which God hath decreed, and promiſed, being in itſelfe moſt certaine and aſſured, yet includeth the meanes, vvherby it ſhal be put in execution : and therfore the iuſt, his elect, do pray for the performance of his vvil.\f* mindeful of thy word to thy ſeruant, wherein thou haſt geuen me hope.
\v 50 This hath comforted me in my humiliation: becauſe \f b \fr 118.50 \ft Expectation of thy promiſe hath geuen me corege.\f* thy \f 1 \fr 118.50 \ft \+it eloquium\+it*.\f* word hath quickened me.
\v 51 The \f c \fr 118.51 \ft Provvde contemners of Gods lavv, haue euerie vvay moleſted me, by detracting, deriding, calumniating, and violently perſecuting me.\f* prowd did vniuſtly exceedingly: \f d \fr 118.51 \ft Al which I haue borne patiently, and not declined from thy law.\f* but I declined not from thy law.
\v 52 I \f e \fr 118.52 \ft I remembred and conſidered thy iuſt punishments inflicted vpon the impious,\f* haue bene mindful of thy iudgements from \f f \fr 118.52 \ft euen from the beginning of the world (both vpon the diuels, and wicked men) and that thou wilt exerciſe the like hereafter,\f* euerlaſting o Lord: \f g \fr 118.52 \ft which conſideration of thy iuſtice comforted me.\f* and was comforted.
\v 53 \f h \fr 118.53 \ft Otherwiſe if I had not ſene thy iuſtice, my zele againſt contemners of thy law, would haue killed me.\f* Faynting poſſeſſed me, becauſe of ſinners forſaking thy law.
\v 54 \f i \fr 118.54 \ft In this place of my perigrination from heauen, I am comforted by remembring, celebrating, and ſinging thy iuſt commandments and lawe, vvhich make thy ſeruant iuſt.\f* Thy iuſtifications were ſongue by me, in the place of my peregrination.
\v 55 I haue bene mindful in \f k \fr 118.55 \ft In perſecution, and in al tribulation, I kept thy law becauſe I would not dishonour thy name.\f* the night of thy name o Lord: and haue kept thy law.
\v 56 This was done to me: \f l \fr 118.56 \ft And my tribulation eſpecially fel vpon me, becauſe I ſought to be iuſtified by keping thy law.\f* becauſe I ſought after thy iuſtifications.
\v 57 My \f a \fr 118.57 \ft The Prophet procedeth ſpeaking in the perſon of the iuſt tending to perfection, and ſaying : This is my happie choiſe, that I deſire no other enheritance, nor poſſeſſion, but to kepe Gods Law.\f* portion o Lord, I ſayd to keepe thy law.
\v 58 \f b \fr 118.58 \ft And ſeeing this excedeth my proper ſtreingth, I prayed God of his mercie to make me able to kepe it.\f* I beſought thy face, with al my hart: haue mercie on me according to thy \f 1 \fr 118.58 \ft \+it eloquium\+it*.\f* word.
\v 59 I \f c \fr 118.59 \ft Pondering my former actions, I turned my pathes to obſerue more perfectly the Law, which God hath teſtified to be the right way.\f* thought vpon my wayes: and conuerted my feete vnto thy teſtimonies.
\v 60 I \f d \fr 118.60 \ft With prompnes of mind, and without heſitation I reſolued to kepe the commandments.\f* am prepared, and am not trubled: to keepe thy commandments.
\v 61 The \f e \fr 118.61 \ft The wicked laide cordes, nettes, or ſnares to intrappe, and hinder me,\f* cordes of ſinners haue wrapped me round about: and \f f \fr 118.61 \ft but I kept thy law fresh in memorie.\f* I haue not forgotten thy law.
\v 62 At \f g \fr 118.62 \ft That this is not vnderſtood only myſtically in time of affliction, but alſo literally and prophetically, that ſome ſpecial ſeruantes of God, ſhould obſerue a godlie profeſſion of praying at midnight, the vvord [I roſe] maketh it probable. S. Paul & Silas, either of a holie cuſtome, or at leaſt vpon ſpecial occaſion (and ſuch occaſions were to them, and others frequent) prayed, and praiſed God at midnight. And novv in the Church of Chriſt ſome religions men pray, and praiſe God continually at midnight ; beſides other houres, mentioned more diſtinctly v. 164.\f* midnight I roſe to confeſſe to thee; for the iudgements of thy iuſtification.
\v 63 \f h \fr 118.63 \ft A great benefite, and a ſingular conſolation, that al true liuing members of Chriſt, are partakers of al the prayers, good workes, and merites, of the whole Church militant and triumphant. VVhich in our Crede is called, The Communion of Sainctes.\f* I am partaker of al that feare thee: and that keepe thy commandments.
\v 64 The \f i \fr 118.64 \ft So great is the mercie of God, extended, communicated, and multiplied in the whole earth :\f* earth o Lord is ful of thy mercie: \f k \fr 118.64 \ft Inſtruct me, and direct me therfore o God, that I may lerne and obſerue thy law, and ſo be iuſtified, and made participant of ſo great mercie.\f* teach me thy iuſtifications.
\v 65 Thou haſt \f a \fr 118.65 \ft Dealt very bountifully\f* done bountie with thy ſeruant o Lord: \f b \fr 118.65 \ft as thou didſt promiſe.\f* according to thy word.
\v 66 \f c \fr 118.66 \ft He that hath bountifully receiued grace at Gods hand, prayeth for more grace, that he may be beneficial to others in releuing the needie ;\f* Teach me goodneſſe, \f d \fr 118.66 \ft in inſtructing the ignorant,\f* and diſcipline, and \f e \fr 118.66 \ft in perſwading to kepe the law of God :\f* knowledge: \f f \fr 118.66 \ft becauſe he hath lerned and beleueth the commandments, by which he is bond to loue, and haue care of his neighbour.\f* becauſe I haue beleued thy commandments.
\v 67 Before I \f g \fr 118.67 \ft Before I was afflicted, I often fel into ſinne :\f* was humbled I offended: \f h \fr 118.67 \ft but vexation gaue me vnderſtanding, therfore now I kepe thy law.\f* therfore haue I kept thy \f 1 \fr 118.67 \ft \+it eloquium\+it*.\f* word.
\v 68 Thou art good: and in thy goodneſſe teach me thy iuſtifications.
\v 69 The iniquitie of \f i \fr 118.69 \ft Contemners of thy law haue endeuored to intangle me,\f* the prowd is multiplied vpon me: \f k \fr 118.69 \ft but I perſiſt in keping thy commandments. As before. \+it v. 51. 61.\+it*\f* but I in al my hart wil ſearch thy commandments.
\v 70 Their hart is \f l \fr 118.70 \ft Though the wicked combine themſelues together againſt me,\f* crudded together as milke: \f m \fr 118.70 \ft yet I conſider, that it is neceſſarie to perſeuer in thy law.\f* but I haue meditated thy law.
\v 71 It is good for me that thou haſt humbled me: that I may learne thy iuſtifications.
\v 72 The law of thy mouth is \f n \fr 118.72 \ft A clere compariſon, that it is better to kepe Gods law, which bringeth life euerlaſting, then to haue al the riches & kingdoms of this world.\f* good vnto me, aboue thouſands of gold, and ſiluer.
\v 73 Thy handes haue made me, and formed me: \f a \fr 118.73 \ft God being our Creator, we may with confidence pray him to illuminate our mindes, that we may lerne what is his pleaſure, and ſo endeuour to fulfil it.\f* geue me vnderſtanding, and I wil learne thy commandmentes.
\v 74 They that feare thee \f b \fr 118.74 \ft Others that loue God wil be gladde to ſee me alſo ſerue him.\f* shal ſee me, & shal reioyce: becauſe I haue much hoped in thy wordes.
\v 75 \f c \fr 118.75 \ft The iuſt being afflicted, and not ſeing the particular cauſe therof, yet knoweth and confeſſeth, that God doth it for moſt iuſt cauſe.\f* I know o Lord that thy iudgements are equitie: and in thy truth thou haſt humbled me. \f d \fr 118.75 \ft And therfore with patience prayeth for comforth, as foloweth :\f*
\v 76 Let thy mercie be done to comfort me, according to thy \f 1 \fr 118.76 \ft \+it eloquium\+it*.\f* word vnto thy ſeruant.
\v 77 Let thy commiſerations come to me, and I \f e \fr 118.77 \ft who am almoſt dead in tribulation.\f* shal liue: becauſe thy law is my meditation.
\v 78 Let the prowde \f f \fr 118.78 \ft The iuſt alſo prayeth that the wicked may be ashamed, and conuerted, for ſo the hebrew word here ſignifieth, though it is alſo lawful to deſire tho iuſt punishment of obſtinate ſinners.\f* be confounded, becauſe they haue done vniuſtly toward me: but I wil be exerciſed in thy commandments.
\v 79 Let them \f g \fr 118.79 \ft He prayeth againe for conuerſion of the wicked, and to haue peace with them.\f* be conuerted to me that feare thee: and that know thy teſtimonies.
\v 80 Let my hart be made immaculate in thy iuſtifications, that I be not confounded.
\v 81 My ſoule hath fainted for \f a \fr 118.81 \ft Manie iuſt of the old teſtament moſt feruently deſired the coming of Chriſt our Sauiour, as our Lord himſelfe teſtifieth, Mat. \+it 13. v. 17\+it*. And now the iuſt deſire his coming in glorie, \+it 2. Tim. 4. v. 8\+it*.\f* thy ſaluation: and I haue much hoped in thy word.
\v 82 Myne eies haue fayled for thy \f 1 \fr 118.82 \ft \+it eloquium\+it*.\f* word, ſaying: \f b \fr 118.82 \ft Delayed hope afflicteth.\f* When wilt thou comfort me?
\v 83 Becauſe I am made \f c \fr 118.83 \ft As a leather bottel made of a beaſts skinne, congeled with the froſt, and after partched in ſmoke, ſo is the bodie of the iuſt mortified by diuers ſortes of afflictions, made a new bottel fitte to receiue new wine, that is, perfect doctrin of Chriſtian life, as of faſting, and other auſteritie, wherof our Sauiour ſpeaketh, \+it Mat. 9. v. 17\+it*.\f* as a bottel in the hoare froſt: I haue not forgotten thy iuſtifications.
\v 84 How manie are \f d \fr 118.84 \ft Such is mans infirmitie, yea of the iuſt, that he apprehendeth tribulations to be very long, and therfore deſireth conſummation ; and that without ſinne, ſo he ſtil ſubmitte his wil to Gods wil.\f* the daies of thy ſeruant: when wilt thou doe iudgement on them that perſecute me?
\v 85 The vniuſt haue told me \f e \fr 118.85 \ft Friuolous idle tailes, which are not according to Gods law.\f* fables: but not as thy law.
\v 86 Al thy commandmentes are truth: they haue vniuſtly perſecuted me, helpe me.
\v 87 They haue welnere \f f \fr 118.87 \ft I was in great danger, but am not ouerthrowne.\f* made an end of me in the earth: but I haue not forſaken thy commandments.
\v 88 According to \f g \fr 118.88 \ft And by thy merciful grace shal perſiſt.\f* thy mercie quicken me: and I shal keepe the teſtimonies of thy mouth.
\v 89 For euer Lord \f a \fr 118.89 \ft The praiſe of Gods workes ; which are firme and parmanent in the order, wherin he ſet them.\f* thy word is permanent in heauen.
\v 90 Thy truth in generation and generation: thou haſt founded the earth, and it is permanent.
\v 91 By thy ordinance the day contineweth: becauſe \f b \fr 118.91 \ft Al thinges of this world, man excepted, do Gods wil.\f* al thinges ſerue thee.
\v 92 \f c \fr 118.92 \ft Man, except he meditate Gods law, and therby be holden vp, is in danger,\f* But that thy law is my meditation: I had then \f d \fr 118.92 \ft perhaps in eternal tentation to perish eternally. For he can neuer riſe out of mortal ſinne, by his owne powre, and al should perish, if Gods mercie did not ſpare ſome, and geue them new effectual grace to repent.\f* perhaps perished in my humiliation.
\v 93 I wil not forget thy iuſtifications for euer: becauſe in them thou haſt quickned me.
\v 94 I am thine, ſaue me: becauſe \f e \fr 118.94 \ft Alwayes vnderſtood, that Gods grace preuented, els no man can ſeeke to obſerue the commandments.\f* I haue ſought out thy iuſtifications.
\v 95 Sinners haue expected me to deſtroy me: I vnderſtood thy teſtimonies.
\v 96 \f f \fr 118.96 \ft Al worldlie thinges haue their conſummation and end :\f* Of al conſummation I haue ſene the end: \f g \fr 118.96 \ft Gods commandment continueth euer. For we are perpetually bond, to loue and ſerue God : to loue our neighboures, yea and enimies. The reward alſo for keping Gods commandments, & punishment for breaking them, are eternal without end.\f* thy commandment is exceding large.
\v 97 \f a \fr 118.97 \ft It is meruel to a perfect iuſt man, that he hath ſo much loued, and obſerued Gods law. By acknowleging wherof, he yeldeth praiſe and thankes to God, whoſe gift it is.\f* How haue I loued thy law ô Lord! al the day it is my meditation.
\v 98 \f b \fr 118.98 \ft The fruictes of obſeruing Gods law are manie and great. Amongſt others, it maketh the obſeruer, wiſer then his enimies.\f* Aboue mine enemies thou haſt made me wiſe by thy commaundment: becauſe it is to me for euer.
\v 99 Aboue \f c \fr 118.99 \ft It maketh the obſeruer wiſer, then his temporal maiſters, that taught him, towit, then thoſe that teach wel, and do not performe the ſame.\f* al that taught me haue I vnderſtood: becauſe thy teſtimonies are my meditation.
\v 100 Aboue \f d \fr 118.100 \ft Yonger in yeares that kepe Gods commandments, are wiſer then the more ancient that kepe them not.\f* ancientes haue I vnderſtood: becauſe I haue ſought thy commandments.
\v 101 I haue ſtaied my feete from al euil way: that I may keepe thy wordes.
\v 102 I haue not declined from thy iudgements: becauſe thou haſt ſet me a law.
\v 103 \f e \fr 118.103 \ft An other fruict is the ſwetnes, which the iuſt feeleth in his owne ſoule.\f* How ſweete are thy \f 1 \fr 118.103 \ft \+it eloquia\+it*.\f* wordes to my iawes, more then honie to my mouth!
\v 104 By thy commandments I haue vnderſtood: therfore haue I \f f \fr 118.104 \ft It brideth alſo iuſt hatred to ſinne.\f* hated al the way of iniquitie.
\v 105 \f a \fr 118.105 \ft The word or law of God declared by Prophets, Paſtors, or other Preachers is the ordinarie meanes for others to lerne, how to direct their wayes, and actions.\f* Thy word is a lampe to my feete, and a light to my pathes.
\v 106 I \f b \fr 118.106 \ft Such profeſſion Gods people made in the old law, in Circumciſion, or at other times : Chriſtians make it in Baptiſme.\f* ſware, and haue determined to keepe the iudgements of thy iuſtice.
\v 107 I am \f c \fr 118.107 \ft Al that wil liue godly in Chriſt Ieſus, shal ſuffer perſecution. \+it 2. Tim. 3. v. 12\+it*.\f* humbled exceedingly ô Lord: quicken me according to thy word.
\v 108 The \f d \fr 118.108 \ft Beſides the commandments, the iuſt alſo offer voluntarie workes of ſupererogation, acceptable to God.\f* voluntaries of my mouth make acceptable ô Lord: and teach me thy iudgementes.
\v 109 My \f e \fr 118.109 \ft By this Hebrew prouerb is ſignified, that a iuſt mans temporal life is in continual danger, as the thing that is in ones hand, is readie to be laide by, put out of his hand, or to be by and by diſpoſed of, or may forthwith fal from his hand.\f* ſoule is in my handes alwaies: and I haue not forgotten thy law.
\v 110 Sinners laid a ſnare for me: and I haue not erred from thy commandments.
\v 111 For inheritance I haue purchaſed thy teſtimonies for euer: becauſe they are the ioy of my hart.
\v 112 I haue inclined my hart to doe thy iuſtifications for euer, \f f \fr 118.112 \ft A moſt euident place, that the keping of Gods commandments merite reward, and are rightly obſerued in reſpect of reward.\f* for reward.
\v 113 I haue \f a \fr 118.113 \ft Not anie mortal man is to be hated, in his perſon, but his iniquitie, by which he is an enimie to Gods law,\f* hated the vniuſt: and I haue \f b \fr 118.113 \ft vvhich euerie iuſt man loueth.\f* loued thy law.
\v 114 Thou art my helper, and protectour: and vpon thy word I haue much hoped.
\v 115 \f c \fr 118.115 \ft Whoſoeuer wil ſeriouſly and ſecurely ſearch the law of God, muſt auoide the conuerſation of euil men.\f* Depart from me ye malignant: and I wil ſearch the commandmentes of my God.
\v 116 \f d \fr 118.116 \ft A general and very fitte prayer, when we addict ourſelues by a firme reſolution to ſerue God, beſeching him to receiue vs into his protection :\f* Recciue me according to thy \f 1 \fr 118.116 \ft \+it eloquium\+it*.\f* word, \f e \fr 118.116 \ft wherby ſpiritual life is conſerued :\f* and I shal liue: and \f f \fr 118.116 \ft which we pray vvith great confidence, becauſe he hath promiſed to heare thoſe that ſeeke, aske, and knocke at the dore of his grace.\f* confound me not of myn expectation.
\v 117 \f g \fr 118.117 \ft VVe muſt pray alſo that he ſuffer vs not to be confounded, or made fruſtrate of the reward which we expect ; for hope confoundeth not, if charitie be povvred in our hartes, by the Holie Ghoſt, which is geuen vs. \+it Rom. 5. v. 5\+it*.\f* Helpe me, and I shal be ſaued: and I wil meditate in thy iuſtifications alwayes.
\v 118 Thou haſt \f h \fr 118.118 \ft God reiecteth ſinners :\f* deſpiſed al that reuolt from thy iudgementes: \f i \fr 118.118 \ft ſo long as they thincke peruerſly : that is, vntil by his grace, ſome returne to a better mind, which of themſelues they can neuer do.\f* becauſe their cogitation is vniuſt.
\v 119 Al the ſinners of the earth I \f k \fr 118.119 \ft As God accounteth of ſinners, ſo the iuſt alſo eſtemeth them, conforming his iudgement to Gods.\f* haue reputed preuaricatours: therfore haue I loued thy teſtimonies.
\v 120 \f l \fr 118.120 \ft Seruil feare is profitable as this place maketh euident : though perfect charitie afterwards ſucceding, expelleth that feare, and moueth to do vvel for the loue of God, not for feare of punishment. \+it 1. Ioan. 4\+it*.\f* Pearſe my flesh with thy feare: for I am afrayd of thy iudgementes.
\v 121 I \f a \fr 118.121 \ft The iuſt againe in feruent zele, not arrogantly, but confidently profeſſing his innocencie,\f* haue done iudgement and iuſtice: \f b \fr 118.121 \ft prayeth to be defended from calumniators.\f* deliuer me not to them that calumniate me.
\v 122 Recciue thy ſeruant \f c \fr 118.122 \ft Grant therfore o God the good, and lawful requeſt which I demand.\f* vnto good: let not the prowde calumniate me.
\v 123 Mine eies haue fayled. \f d \fr 118.123 \ft By long expecting to be deliuered, and ſaued from tribulation.\f* after thy ſaluation: and for the \f 1 \fr 118.123 \ft \+it eloquium\+it*.\f* word of thy iuſtice.
\v 124 Doe with thy ſeruant according to thy mercie: and teache me thy iuſtifications.
\v 125 I am thy ſeruant: geue me vnderſtanding, that I may know thy teſtimonies.
\v 126 \f e \fr 118.126 \ft It is time, and hiegh time, ſaith feruent zele of the iuſt man, that God deliuer the innocent :\f* It is time to doe ô Lord: \f f \fr 118.126 \ft vvhen the vvicked haue not only perſecuted the good, but haue alſo contemptuouſly made hauooke of Gods lavv, and true religion.\f* they haue diſſipated thy law.
\v 127 \f g \fr 118.127 \ft For this zele of Gods law ſo deſpiſed, and diſputed, the iuſt more and more loueth, that vvhich the vvicked ſo deadly hate.\f* Therfore haue I loued thy commandementes, aboue gold and topazius:
\v 128 \f h \fr 118.128 \ft Euen by the mortal hate of the vvicked I ſaw, that Gods law is moſt excellent, and therfore addicted myſelfe ſo much the more to loue it,\f* Therfore was I directed to al thy commandementes: \f i \fr 118.128 \ft and to hate al wicked wayes.\f* al wicked way I haue hated.
\v 129 Thy \f a \fr 118.129 \ft Gods meruelous povvre and vviſdom, teſtified by his workes and commandments,\f* teſtimonies are meruelous: \f b \fr 118.129 \ft worthely inuite iuſt ſoules, to meditate and contemplate the ſame.\f* therfore hath my ſoule ſearched them.
\v 130 The \f c \fr 118.130 \ft Firſt entrance into knowlege of holie Scripture, illuminateth the vnderſtanding of the humble, wherby they procede to know more.\f* declaration of thy \f 1 \fr 118.130 \ft \+it ſermonum\+it*.\f* wordes doth illuminate: and geueth vnderſtanding to litle ones.
\v 131 I \f d \fr 118.131 \ft By this Metaphor, of gaping, or vvide opening the mouth, and drawing breath, the Prophet deſcribeth the great deſire of the iuſt, to know and kepe Gods commandments.\f* opened my mouth, and drew breath: becauſe I deſired thy commandments.
\v 132 Looke vpon me, and haue mercie on me, according to \f e \fr 118.132 \ft According to thy accuſtomed equitie, in shewing mercie to them that loue thy name.\f* the iudgement of them that loue thy name.
\v 133 Direct my ſteppes according to thy \f 1 \fr 118.133 \ft \+it eloquium\+it*.\f* word: and let not anie iniuſtice haue domination ouer me.
\v 134 Redeme me from the calumnies of men: that I may kepe thy commondmentes.
\v 135 Illuminate \f f \fr 118.135 \ft Let thy diuine Maieſtie looke vpon me with fauorable countenance.\f* thy face vpon thy ſeruant: and teach me thy iuſtifications.
\v 136 \f g \fr 118.136 \ft True repentance conſiſteth not only in purpoſe to auoide ſinne hereafter, which in dede is firſt required, but alſo in ſorow and lamentation for ſinnes paſt.\f* Mine eies haue gushed forth iſſues of waters: becauſe they haue not kept thy law.
\v 137 Thou art iuſt ô Lord: and \f a \fr 118.137 \ft God being eſſentially iuſt of himſelfe, maketh men iuſt according to right iudgement, by geuing them grace of mercie, wherwith they cooperating, are iuſt by iuſtice in dede inherent in their ſoules, not by imputation only : for it were not right iudgement to impute, or account man iuſt, vvho is not ſo in dede.\f* thy iudgement is right.
\v 138 Thou haſt commanded \f b \fr 118.138 \ft The ſame is more confirmed, by theſe three ſynonyma, Iuſtice, Teſtimonies, Veritie, ſignifying the law of God, moſt earneſtly commanded.\f* iuſtice thy teſtimonies: and thy veritie excedingly.
\v 139 My Zele hath made me to pine away: becauſe mine enimies haue forgotten thy wordes.
\v 140 Thy \f 1 \fr 118.140 \ft \+it eloquia\+it*.\f* word is \f c \fr 118.140 \ft Gods law is as pure as anie thing purged by fire.\f* fired excedingly: and thy ſeruant hath loued it.
\v 141 I am \f d \fr 118.141 \ft A iuſt man is often iudged ignorant, immature, vnexperienced, by the worldlie wiſe ;\f* a yongman, and contemned: I \f e \fr 118.141 \ft but in dede is wiſe, in that he forgetteth not to kepe the law, which maketh him iuſt.\f* haue not forgotten thy iuſtifications.
\v 142 Thy iuſtice, is iuſtice for euer: and thy law is veritie.
\v 143 Tribulation, and diſtreſſe haue found me: thy commandments are my meditation.
\v 144 Thy teſtimonies are equitie for euer \f f \fr 118.144 \ft Hauing profeſſed the neceſſitie of perfect iuſtice, he concludeth this Octonarie, praying to be illuminated in his vnderſtanding, that ſo he may attaine iuſtice, and liue therby.\f* geue me vnderſtanding, and I shal liue.
\v 145 I \f a \fr 118.145 \ft Moſt precious and feruent inuocation of God for his grace, is neceſſarie, to the fulfilling of his law.\f* haue cried in my whole hart, heare me ô Lord: I wil ſeeke after thy iuſtifications.
\v 146 I haue cried to thee, ſaue me: that I may keepe thy commandmentes.
\v 147 I haue preuented in \f b \fr 118.147 \ft I haue preuented the mature, and ordinarie time of the night, and haue prayed\f* maturitie, and \f c \fr 118.147 \ft very attentiuely.\f* haue cried: becauſe I hoped much in thy wordes.
\v 148 Mine eies \f d \fr 118.148 \ft Againe in the morning I haue preuented the accuſtomed time of prayer.\f* haue preuented early vnto thee: that I might meditate thy \f 1 \fr 118.148 \ft \+it eloquia\+it*.\f* wordes.
\v 149 Heare my voice according to thy mercie ô Lord: and according to \f e \fr 118.149 \ft According to thy accuſtomed maner of shewing mercie, ſhew it me, that therby I may liue.\f* thy iudgement quicken me.
\v 150 They that perſecute me haue approched to iniquitie: but from thy law they are made far of.
\v 151 Thou art \f f \fr 118.151 \ft God is alwayes readie to heare al that ſincerly inuocate him.\f* nigh ô Lord: and al thy wayes are truth.
\v 152 \f g \fr 118.152 \ft Gods law is the ſame in ſubſtance from the beginning of the world, and wil be for euer.\f* From the beginning I knewe of thy teſtimonies: that thou haſt founded them for euer.
\v 153 See \f a \fr 118.153 \ft An other prayer of the iuſt in affliction.\f* my humiliation, and deliuer me: becauſe I haue not forgotten thy law.
\v 154 Iudge my iudgement, & redeme me: for thy \f 1 \fr 118.154 \ft \+it eloquium\+it*.\f* word \f b \fr 118.154 \ft Conſerue me in thy grace.\f* quicken thou me.
\v 155 Saluation is far from ſinners: becauſe they haue not ſought after thy iuſtifications.
\v 156 Thy mercies are manie ô Lord: \f c \fr 118.156 \ft As before \+it v. 149\+it*.\f* according to thy iudgement quicken me.
\v 157 There are manie that perſecute me, and afflict me: I haue not declined from thy teſtimonies.
\v 158 I ſaw the preuaricatours, and I pyned away: becauſe they kept not thy \f 1 \fr 118.158 \ft \+it eloquia\+it*.\f* wordes.
\v 159 \f d \fr 118.159 \ft Sincere profeſſion of innocencie is no arrogancy.\f* See that I haue loued thy commandmentes ô Lord: in thy mercie quicken me.
\v 160 The \f e \fr 118.160 \ft Gods eſſencial veritie is the beginning from vvhence, as from the fountaine al other truthes are deriued :\f* beginning of thy wordes is truth: \f f \fr 118.160 \ft And al commandments proceding from this firſt truth, are for euer immutable.\f* al the iudgementes of thy iuſtice are for euer.
\v 161 Princes haue perſecuted me \f a \fr 118.161 \ft Potent wicked men perſecute the godlie without cauſe, that is, vvithout anie iuſt reaſon mouing them ; & without the effect intended by them, which is to drawe Gods ſeruants from truth, and equitie ;\f* without cauſe: and my \f b \fr 118.161 \ft vvhoſe hart being poſſeſſed vvith the true feare of God, they perſiſt in keping Gods commandments.\f* hart hath bene afrayd of thy wordes.
\v 162 I \f c \fr 118.162 \ft Yea they alſo reioyce in keping the commandments, vvith ſuch difficultie, as thoſe doe, that gaining the victorie ouer their enimies, carie away great and rich ſpoyles.\f* wil reioyce at thy \f 1 \fr 118.162 \ft \+it eloquia\+it*.\f* wordes: as he that findeth manie ſpoyles.
\v 163 I haue hated iniquitie, and abhorred it: but thy law I haue loued.
\v 164 \f d \fr 118.164 \ft Euerie day the iuſt praiſe God often, ſignified by the number of ſeuen. From hence alſo the Church of Chriſt tooke example to inſtitute the ſeuen Canonical Houres, which is the ordinarie Eccleſiaſtical Office ; conſiſting, as S. Iſidorus, and manie other Fathers teſtifie, of Hymnes, Pſalmes, Canticles, Antiphones, Leſſons, Reſponſories, & other Prayers & Praiſes, diſtributed into diſtinct times, beginning in the night, wherof that part is called the Nocturne (one or three according to the diuerſitie of the Office) and perteineth to one or more of the foure Vigiles, into vvhich ſouldiars diuide the vvhole night. Whereto alſo the Laudes are added. Then Prime, in the morning. Aftervvards, the Third houre, Sixt, Ninth ; and in the euening, Euenſongue, and Compline. Againſt vvhich moſt ancient and religious Conſtitution, eſpecially againſt the part called Vigiles, or Nocturnes, certaine Heretikes repined, and calumniated the Churches cuſtome, as ſuperfluous and vnfructful to ſpiritual worke, violating of Gods ordinance, who made the night for reſt, and the day for laboure. For which cauſe they were called Nyctazontes, Somnicoloſi, Drowſy heretikes, As the ſame S. Iſidorus teſtifieth li. 1. c. 22. de Offic. Eccles. S. Ierom Epiſt. ad Riparium, noteth the ſame hereſie in Vigilantius, calling him Dormitantius, becauſe he reprehended holie Vigiles, as if it were better to ſleepe, then wake in time of Diuine ſeruice. VViclif alſo raiſed vp the ſame hereſie, as witneſſeth Thomas VValdenſis. To. 3. Tit. 3. c. 21. Laſtly Luther and al his broode. But the holie obſeruation of Canonical Houres is proued, by manie ancient Fathers to be altogether agreable to the holie Scriptures, both of the old and new Teſtament. So S. Beda. in 18. Luc. & li. 4. c. 7. Hiſt. Angl. S. Gregorie the Great li. 3. Dialogi. c. 14. S. Auguſtin (Ser. 55. de temp.) exhorting the people to riſe early to the Vigiles (or Nocturnes) and in aniewiſe to come to the Third houre, Sixt, and Ninth. Let none (ſaith he) withdraw himſelfe from the holie worke, but whom either ſicknes, or publique vtilitie, or perhaps ſome great neceſſitie holdeth backe. S. Ierom. Epiſt. 22. ad Euſtoch. & in Epitaph. Paule. c. 10. maketh expreſſe mention of the Third houre. Sixt, Ninth, Morning, and Euening ; alſo of Midnight, adding that no Religious is ignorant that ſometimes they muſt riſe to Diuine Seruice, twiſe, yea thriſe in the night. S. Baſil, in Regulis fuſius diſput. ad Interrog 37. & de Inſtit. Monachorum. firſt. sheweth this ordinance to be agreable to the holie Scriptures, and namely to this place of the Pſalmiſt. S. Cyprian in fine expoſit. Orat. Domini. affirmeth that beſides the three houres in which Daniel and his felovves prayed, the Church of Chriſt hath added more. And (as manie ſuppoſe) S. Clement li. 8. Conſtitut. Apoſtol c. 40. ſhevveth the ſette Houres of prayers, and the reaſons therof : Make your prayers Early in the morning, at the Third houre ; Sixt, Ninth ; Euening ; and at the Time of cocke crovving. Early geuing thankes becauſe our Lord hath illuminated vs, the night being paſſed, & the day coming in ; the Third, becauſe that houre our Lord receiued Pilats ſentence ; the Sixt houre, becauſe then he was Crucified ; the Ninth, becauſe al thinges were moued, when our Lord was crucified, abhorring the audacitie of the wicked, & not bearing the ignominie of our Lord ; at Euening, geuing thankes, for that God hath geuen vs the night for reſt of dayes labours : at the Cocke crowing, becauſe at that time the coming of the day is denounced, to exerciſe the workes of light, thus S. Clement. Touching the diſtinct and ſette times of publique prayer, the continual practiſe by tradition teacheth, that Mattines with Laudes were ſaid in the night, about the firſt Cockcrovving. Prime early in the morning. The other partes in the day time : At euening Euenſongue, and laſt of al Compline. And touching the place : If for the infidels (ſaith the ſame holie Father) there be not acceſſe to the Church, the Biſhop muſt geather the Aſſemblie at home, that the godlie may not enter into the Church of the wicked : for the place doth not ſanctifie man, but man the place. VVherfore if the vvicked occupie the place, that place is to be ſhunned, becauſe it is prophaned by them : for as Prieſtes do ſanctifie holie thinges, ſo the vvicked do contaminate them. If neither at home, nor in the Church Aſſemblies can be celebrated, let euerie one by himſelfe ſing, read, pray, or twoo or three be geathered together. For where twoo or three are geathered in my name (ſaith Chriſt) there am I, in the middes of them. Let not the godlie pray with an heretike, no not at home. For what ſocietie is there of light vvith darknes ?\f* Seuentimes in the day. I haue ſayd praiſe to thee, for the iudgements of thy iuſtice.
\v 165 There is \f e \fr 118.165 \ft Amongſt other benefites, it is a ſpecial commoditie, that thoſe which perfectly loue Gods law, haue alwayes peace in their owne conſcience :\f* much peace to them that loue thy law: & \f f \fr 118.165 \ft and are neuer ſcandalized, that is, do not fal nor committe ſinne, by anie occaſion whatſoeuer geuen them by others. For it is a general aſſured doctrine, that the perfect are not ſcandalized, becauſe they are conſtant in vertue, and not moued by anie example, perſwaſion, prouocation, or other meanes to offend God : but only the weake and vnperfect are moued, and drawne to ſinne by occaſions geuen them, who otherwiſe would not haue ſinned. Neither is he excuſed that falleth by ſuch occaſions, becauſe he ought to be conſtant.\f* there is no ſcandal to them.
\v 166 I expected thy ſaluation ô Lord: and haue loued thy commandmentes.
\v 167 My ſoule hath kept thy teſtimonies: and \f g \fr 118.167 \ft Not of ſeruile feare, but of true charitie & filial loue.\f* hath loued them excedindgly.
\v 168 I haue kept thy commandmentes, and thy teſtimonies: \f h \fr 118.168 \ft Becauſe whatſoeuer I do is in thy ſight, whom i wil in no caſe offend.\f* becauſe al my waies are in thy ſight.
\v 169 \f a \fr 118.169 \ft In this laſt Octonarie, and concluſion of this Pſalme, the faithful ſeruant of God prayeth,\f* Let my petition approch in thy ſight ô Lord: according to thy word giue me \f b \fr 118.169 \ft not for humane knowlege, or other temporal thinges, but to vnderſtand Gods law.\f* vnderſtanding.
\v 170 Let my requeſt enter in thy ſight: \f c \fr 118.170 \ft Thou that haſt promiſed to heare al that inuocate thee,\f* according to thy word \f d \fr 118.170 \ft voutſaffe to deliuer me in time of tribulations and tentations.\f* deliuer me.
\v 171 My lippes shal vtter an hymne, when thou shalt teach me thy iuſtifications.
\v 172 My tongue shal pronounce thy \f 1 \fr 118.172 \ft Why the 15 Pſalms following are called Gradual Canticles. Here follow in order fifteen Pſalms entitle Gradual Canticles.\f* word: \f e \fr 118.172 \ft God geuing grace, it behoueth his ſeruants thankefully to ſerue, and praiſe him.\f* becauſe al thy commandmentes are equitie.
\v 173 \f f \fr 118.173 \ft Though man be indued with grace, yet he nedeth more grace, that by Gods hand and powre, not by his owne, he may reſiſt tentations.\f* Let thy hand be to ſaue me: becauſe I haue choſen thy commandmentes.
\v 174 I haue coueted thy ſaluation ô Lord: and thy law is my meditation.
\v 175 \f g \fr 118.175 \ft By this aſſiſtance of grace, the ſoule continueth in ſpiritual life, and praiſeth God.\f* My ſoule shal liue, and shal prayſe thee: and thy iudgementes shal helpe me.
\v 176 \f h \fr 118.176 \ft Al mankind, and vniuerſally euerie one hath bene as a loſt sheepe,\f* I haue ſtrayed, as a sheepe, that is loſt: \f i \fr 118.176 \ft and Chriſt came into this world to ſeeke and ſaue al :\f* ſeeke thy ſeruant, \f k \fr 118.176 \ft but effectually findeth and ſaueth thoſe only, that forgete not to kepe his commandments.\f* becauſe I haue not forgotten thy commandmentes.
\c 119
\cd The Iewes in captiuitie of Babylon, Chriſtians in perſecution, or other great tribulation, pray with confidence to be deliuered from danger, and ſlander of wicked tongues, 5. lamenting their long indurance. \f - \ft Prayer in tribulation. The 7. key.\f*
\s1 A BRIEF NOTE CONCERNING the Gradual Psalms.
\ip Here follow in order fifteen Psalms entitle Gradual Canticles. The Hebrew word Mahaloth \f - \ft Why the 15 Psalms following are called Gradual Canticles.\f* signifieth Steps, or Ascensions. The reason whereof Aadias, and some other Rabbins yield, for that they were sung with highest elevated notes, that can be in Music. The Talmud saith they are so called, because they were sung in the fifteen steps going up into the Temple. But S. Augustin, S. Basil, and other Christian Fathers expound them according to the history, and immediate prophetical sense, of the delivery of the Jews from captivity of Babylon, ascending into \f - \ft The historical sense.\f* Jerusalem: which is so situated on mountains, that the way from all parts was by ascending unto it. According to the Mystical sense, of ascending spiritually by virtues to perfection, and to \f - \ft The mystical sense.\f* eternal felicity. For the way tending to virtue (saith S. Basil) is like to certain steps (or degrees) by little and little bringing the man that loveth wisdom unto heaven. These Canticles therefore are prayers, mixed with consolations, for the joyful delivery of God's people, from that great captivity in Babylon, which the Psalmist King David saw in prophetical spirit, and which his posterity felt, and sometime endured. Which again as a figure signifieth the return and ascending of mankind from sin to grace, and from the miserable state of this world into heaven. Whereupon S. Augustine interpreteth this prophecy of the ascension, or elevation of the heart, from the vale of tears. In \f - \ft These Psalms are consolatory prayers and prophecies.\f* the mean time, whiles we are in this world, these Psalms are consolatory prayers, and prophetical assurance, that God's people, Catholic Christians, shall be delivered from the thraldom and persecution of Pagans, Turks, and Heretics. As partly we see by the delivery from the Roman persecuting Emperors, from the Vandals, Goths, and Huns: & therefore with assured confidence we hope and expect the like delivery from Turks, and all Heretics of Luther's brood.
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. WHEN I was in tribulation I cried to our Lord: and \f a \fr 119.1 \ft Former experience of Gods mercie in hearing the prayers of thoſe that inuocated him, geueth hope that he wil heare in like caſe.\f* he heard me.
\v 2 O Lord deliuer my ſoule from \f b \fr 119.2 \ft Nothing is more dangerous then vntrue and deceiptful tongues, nothing more damage then to diminish, and detract from the good fame of the iuſt : and therfore this is a moſt neceſſarie prayer, that God wil deliuer vs, from the wicked tongues of Turkes, Heretikes, and other wicked men.\f* vniuſt lippes, and from a deceiptful tongue.
\v 3 \f c \fr 119.3 \ft What punishment is great ynough for wicked tongues?\f* What may be geuen thee, or what may be added vnto thee, to a deceiptful tongue?
\v 4 \f d \fr 119.4 \ft Surely the malice of wicked tongues deſerueth sharpeſt punishments to be afflicted by ſtrong handes.\f* The sharpe arrowes of the mightie, with coales of deſolation.
\v 5 \f e \fr 119.5 \ft Iewes lamented their long abſence from the holie land, eſpecially from Ieruſalem, and the Temple. Chriſtians mourne for their reſtraint from Gods Seruice, and long perigrination from heauen.\f* Woe is to me, that my ſeiourning is prolonged: I haue dwelte with the \f f \fr 119.5 \ft Of Cedar the ſonne of Iſmael, came Mahomet, the Turkes falſe prophet, whoſe tyrannie is great and long. The name Cedar is interpreted, blackenes, and obſcuritie, which ſignifieth darkenes of error, and ſinne.\f* inhabitantes of Cedar:
\v 6 My ſoule hath bene long a ſeiourner.
\v 7 With them, that hated peace, I was peaceable: when I ſpake to them, they impugned me \f g \fr 119.7 \ft The wicked afflict thoſe that geue no cauſe of offence.\f* without cauſe.
\c 120
\cd The iuſt reioyce that their prayer is heard, and that God continually protecteth them. \f - \ft God's protection. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. I HAVE lifted vp mine eies vnto \f a \fr 120.1 \ft Towardes Ieruſalem, and towards heauen.\f* the mountaines, \f b \fr 120.1 \ft Al helpe cometh from heauen, that is, from God, who of his diuine ordinance eſpecially heareth prayers made in holie places. \+it 3. Reg. 7\+it*.\f* from whence helpe shal come to me.
\v 2 My helpe is from our Lord, who made heauen and earth.
\v 3 Geue he not \f c \fr 120.3 \ft The iuſt ſpeaketh and wisheth wel to his owne ſoule. Eſpecially the whole Church reioyceth in Gods aſſured protection.\f* thy foote to be moued: neither doe he ſlumber that keepeth thee.
\v 4 Loe he shal not ſlumber nor ſleepe, that kepeth \f d \fr 120.4 \ft The militant Church.\f* Iſrael.
\v 5 Our lord kepeth thee, our Lord is thy protection, vpon thy right hand.
\v 6 By day \f e \fr 120.6 \ft Proſperitie,\f* the ſunne shal not burne thee: nor \f f \fr 120.6 \ft nor aduerſitie can ouerthrow the Church.\f* the moone by night.
\v 7 Our Lord doth kepe thee from al euil: our Lord kepe \f g \fr 120.7 \ft ſpiritual life.\f* thy ſoule.
\v 8 Our Lord kepe thy coming in, and thy going out: from hence forth now, and for euer.
\c 121
\cd Vnder the figure of returne to Ieruſalem, vehemently deſired by the Iewes in captiuitie, the Pſalmiſt deſcribeth the deſire, and hope of the iuſt to poſſeſſe heauen. \f - \ft Ioyes of heauen promiſed to the iuſt. The 10. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. I REIOYCED in theſe thinges, which \f a \fr 121.1 \ft Diuers Prophetes told the Iewes in captiuitie, that they ſhould returne to Ieruſalem. Al Prophetes, Chriſt alſo, and his Apoſtles, and Prieſtes preach the entrance, and ioyes of heauen to the iuſt. Al vvhich the Pſalmiſt ſaw in propheticall ſpirite, and reioyced.\f* were ſayd to me: We shal goe into the houſe of our Lord.
\v 2 \f b \fr 121.2 \ft The Iewes conſider that ſometimes they vvere ioyful in the Temple of Ieruſalem : Chriſtians reioyce in the comforte they haue in the militant Church.\f* Our feete were ſtanding, in thy courtes ô Ieruſalem
\v 3 Ieruſalem, which is built as a citie: whoſe \f c \fr 121.3 \ft Communitie and participation of ſpirituall graces, is a great ioy to Catholiques, wherof the ſame Prophet ſpeaketh, \+it Pſal 118. v. 6\+it*, and often els where.\f* participation is together in it ſelf.
\v 4 For \f d \fr 121.4 \ft Al the twelue Tribes frequented Ieruſalem,\f* thither did the tribes aſcend, \f e \fr 121.4 \ft and al nations of the world do come to the Catholique Church.\f* the tribes of our Lord: the teſtimonie of Iſrael to confeſſe vnto the name of our Lord.
\v 5 Becauſe ſeates \f f \fr 121.5 \ft Seates of Iudgement vvere placed in Ieruſalem,\f* ſate there in iudgement, \f g \fr 121.5 \ft and ſeates of Iudgement in the Catholique Church\f* ſeates vpon the houſe \f h \fr 121.5 \ft of Chriſt.\f* of Dauid.
\v 6 \f i \fr 121.6 \ft Chriſt exhorteth to aske,\f* Aske ye the thinges that are for the peace of Ieruſalem: and \f k \fr 121.6 \ft and promiſeth to geue that is rightly asked ; Chriſtians alſo inuite each other to pray for the Church.\f* abundance to them that loue thee.
\v 7 Peace be made in thy ſtrength: and abundance in thy towers.
\v 8 \f l \fr 121.8 \ft Chriſt prayeth for his Church,\f* For my bretheren, and my neigbours ſakes, \f m \fr 121.8 \ft and gaue his peace to the Apoſtles, and in them to their ſucceſſors.\f* I ſpake peace of thee:
\v 9 For the houſe of our Lord God, I haue ſought \f n \fr 121.9 \ft For in heauenlie Ieruſalem, al good thinges are prepared, & are geuen to Sainctes reigning there for euer.\f* good thinges to thee.
\c 122
\cd A feruent and attentiue prayer, to be deliuered from captiuitie, or anie other affliction. \f - \ft A prayer in affliction. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. TO thee haue I lifted vp mine eies, which dwelleſt in the heauens.
\v 2 Behold as the eies \f a \fr 122.2 \ft Seruantes expecting neceſſaries at their maſters handes, are commonly very attentiue to receiue that vvhich they hope for : ſo muſt the faithful praying God, be very attentiue, and not diſtracted in their prayers.\f* of ſeruantes, are on the handes of their maſters, As the eies \f b \fr 122.2 \ft An other example of handmaides, who generally are more diligent then men.\f* of the handmaide on the handes of her miſtreſſe: ſo are our eies to our Lord God vntil he haue mercie on vs.
\v 3 Haue mercie on vs ô Lord, haue mercie on vs: becauſe we are \f c \fr 122.3 \ft Though God ſuffer his ſeruantes to ſuſtaine ſome affliction and reproch, yet he heareth and deliuereth them before they be oppreſſed.\f* much replenished with contempt.
\v 4 Becauſe our ſoule is \f d \fr 122.4 \ft Seing perſecution ſtil increaſe, or continew long, the faithful are then ſpecially to conceiue hope of ſpeedie reliefe.\f* much replenished: reproch to them that abund, and contempt to the prowde.
\c 123
\cd The whole Church, and euerie iuſt perſon, gratfully confeſſeth, that by Gods protection they are ſecure, without which none could eſcape ruine. \f - \ft Gods protection. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. BVt that our Lord \f a \fr 123.1 \ft If God had not ſent his helpe, and defence to vs,\f* was in vs, let Iſrael now ſay:
\v 2 but that our Lord was in vs, \f b \fr 123.2 \ft we could not haue eſcaped the force of our enimies.\f* When men roſe vp againſt vs,
\v 3 \f c \fr 123.3 \ft The word [perhaps] doth not here import a doubt, or vncertaintie, but in modeſt maner of aſſeueration, leaueth the iudgement of the euent that should hapen (if God did not protect his ſeruantes) to their ovvne conſideration : which is an vſual phraſe in moſt langnages.\f* perhaps they had ſwalowed vs \f d \fr 123.3 \ft So ſudainly ſhould the weake, without Gods protection, be deſtroyed, as men are deuoured & ſwalowed vp by rauening wild beaſtes, euen before they be throughly dead. So vvas Ionas ſwalowed into the vvhales bellie.\f* aliue: When their furie was angrie againſt vs,
\v 4 perhaps \f e \fr 123.4 \ft Furious perſecution.\f* water had ſwalowed vs.
\v 5 Our ſoule hath paſſed through \f f \fr 123.5 \ft Suddaine great trubles.\f* a torrent: perhaps our ſoule had paſſed through an intolerable water.
\v 6 Bleſſed be our Lord which hath not geuen vs, for a pray to their teeth.
\v 7 Our ſoule as a ſparow is deliuered from the ſnare of the fowlers: The ſnare is \f g \fr 123.7 \ft Mans ſubtelue often deceiueth an other man, but there is no counſel able to circumuent God.\f* broken, and we are deliuered.
\v 8 Our helpe is in the name of our Lord, who made heauen and earth.
\c 124
\cd The Church, and holie members therof, are alwayes protected by God: 5. The wicked being ſuffered for a while, are at laſt punished. \f - \ft God's protection. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. THEY \f a \fr 124.1 \ft Thoſe that confidently truſt in God, are in ſuch ſecuritie as is mount Sion, which is a hil, defended alſo with other hilles round about.\f* that truſt in our Lord, as mount Sion: he shal not be moued for euer, \f b \fr 124.1 \ft An other thing required to this ſecuritie, is to dwel vvithin Ieruſalem, not the terreſtrial citie, for (as S. Auguſtin obſerueth) the Ievves that dwelt therin are deſtroyed or made captiues, and hitherto reiected of God, but in the Catholique Church.\f* that dwelleth in Ieruſalem.
\v 2 Mountaines round about it: and our Lord round about his people, from hence forth now and for euer.
\v 3 Becauſe our Lord \f c \fr 124.3 \ft God wil not alwayes, nor finally leaue his ſeruants in tribulation, but only a while for their good.\f* wil not leaue the rod of ſinners vpon the lote of the iuſt: that the iuſt reach not their handes to iniquitie.
\v 4 \f d \fr 124.4 \ft This prayer is alſo an aſſeueration, for it is certaine that God wil defend, and reward the good, and right of hart:\f* Doe wel ô Lord to the good, and right of hart.
\v 5 But \f e \fr 124.5 \ft And no leſſe aſſuredly God wil punish not only the notorious wicked, and principal auctors of wickednes, but alſo al thoſe that for feare or for commoditie, or for anie other cauſe, decline into obligations, bondes, couenants, or anie way conſent, in external shew with the wicked againſt God, as in outward profeſſion of Hereſie, or Schiſme, though ſuch temporizers do not thinke in their hart, that the pretended religion is true, wherto they are drawe to yeld external conformitie. For, as the Pſalmiſt here teacheth, our Lord wil bring al ſuch acceſſarie offenders, to the ſame iudgement and puniſhment, vvith the principal vvorkers of iniquitie.\f* thoſe that decline into obligations, our Lord wil bring with them that worke iniquitie: \f f \fr 124.5 \ft Al vvhich being puniſhed, then Ieruſalem, the Catholique Church, shal haue peace.\f* peace vpon Iſrael.
\c 125
\cd Iſralites releaſed from captiuitie of Babylon, much more the bleſſed both of the old and new teſtament deliuered by Chriſt from captiuitie of ſinne, do reioyce: 5. Who neuertheles muſt paſſe through tribulation to eternal felicitie. \f - \ft The deliuered from captiuitie reioyce. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. WHEN our Lord turned the captiuitie of Sion: we were made as \f a \fr 125.1 \ft The Ievves releaſed from captiuitie, vvere exceedingly, and almoſt incredibly comforted, as men for great, and vnexpected ſundaine ioy thinke it rather a dreame, then a truth that they are deliuered from miſeries. So S. Peter when he vvas deliuered out of priſon by an Angel. Act. 12. thought it rather a viſion, then a true deliuerie. Such ſpiritual ioy deuoute ſoules haue vvhen they are deliuered from ſinne.\f* men comforted.
\v 2 Then was our mouth replenished with ioy: and our tongue with exultation. Then \f b \fr 125.2 \ft VVheras in the tvvo former verſes (and very commonly) the Prophet ſpeaketh in the preter tenſe, for the aſſurance of that he foresheweth, as if it vvere already donne, yet here he vttereth his prophecie in the future tenſe, that the Gentiles wil confeſſe that God dealeth magnifically vvith his people.\f* shal they ſay among the Gentiles: Our Lord hath done magnifically vvith them.
\v 3 \f c \fr 125.3 \ft The people alſo themſelues gratfully confeſſe that God dealeth magnifically with them.\f* Our Lord hath done magnifically with vs: we are made ioyful.
\v 4 \f d \fr 125.4 \ft The Prophet forſeing al this in ſpirit, prayeth for the performance hereof.\f* Turne our captiuitie ô Lord, \f e \fr 125.4 \ft And that it may ſpedely be done, as a torrent that runneth in the ſouth part of the world. is commonly very great, much deſired, but ſcarſe expected.\f* as a torrent in the South.
\v 5 \f f \fr 125.5 \ft This is the ordinarie diſpoſition of God, that his ſeruants shal make their ſeeding, vvhich is, doe good vvorkes (ſaith S. Auguſtin) vvith teares, in tribulation vpon earth:\f* They that ſow in teares, \f g \fr 125.5 \ft and reape a plentiful harueſt, the revvard of their ſuffering and vvel vvorking, in the next life. In aſſured hope vvherof the Pſalmiſt, and the vvhole Church ioyfully conclude this Pſalme with the tvvo verſes folovving.\f* shal reape in ioyfulneſſe.
\v 6 Going they went and wept, caſting their ſeedes.
\v 7 But coming they shal come with exultation, carying their sheaues.
\c 126
\cd Neither houſe nor citie can be built, or kept without Gods ſpecial prouidence and helpe, 3. thoſe that truſt in him shal proſper in their endeuours. \f - \ft God's help in all good works. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle \f a \fr 126.1 \ft God not permitting Dauid to build the Temple, promiſed that his ſonne ſhould build it, and therfore beſides other good admonitions, geuen to his ſonne Salomon, he directeth this Pſalme to him, to be ſongue with others in the dedication of the Temple. 3. Reg. 8. 1. Par. 5.\f* of Salomon: VNLESSE \f b \fr 126.1 \ft Vnleſſe God be the principal Agent, no worke can proſper.\f* our Lord build the houſe, they haue laboured in vayne that build it. Vnleſſe our Lord kepe the citie, he watcheth in vayne that kepeth it.
\v 2 It is vaine for you to riſe \f c \fr 126.2 \ft It is vaine to atempt anie thing without Gods grace aſſiſting:\f* before light: \f d \fr 126.2 \ft they that ſo doing, & thincking they haue done ſomething, reſt\f* riſe ye after ye haue ſitten, which eate \f e \fr 126.2 \ft after their painful trauel, muſt riſe againe & beginne anew, because that which they ſeme to haue done wel, is nothing worth, nor ſhal haue reward.\f* the bread of ſorow.
\v 3 \f f \fr 126.3 \ft Contrariwiſe vvhere God geuing grace, thoſe that truly loue him, do good vvorkes,\f* When he shal giue \f g \fr 126.3 \ft with great eaſe, and dilight, as they take their ſleepe,\f* ſleepe to his beloued: behold \f h \fr 126.3 \ft they merite inheritance in heauen,\f* the inheritance of our Lord \f i \fr 126.3 \ft for their good vvorkes.\f* are children: \f k \fr 126.3 \ft the revvard is promiſed to the true children of God, borne to him in the wombe of the Catholique Church.\f* the reward, the fruite of the wombe.
\v 4 As \f l \fr 126.4 \ft Moreouer as a ſtrong archer ſtriketh deepe with his arrowes; ſo they that patiently ſuffer much in this world,\f* arrowes in the hand of the mightie: ſo are the \f m \fr 126.4 \ft do multiplie good workes.\f* children of them that are shaken.
\v 5 \f n \fr 126.5 \ft Such shal be very happie,\f* Bleſſed is the man that hath filled his deſire of them: he shal \f o \fr 126.5 \ft & very eaſily anſvver al that can be obiected againſt them\f* not be confounded when he shal ſpeake to his enemies \f p \fr 126.5 \ft in the day of Iudgement.\f* in the gate.
\c 127
\cd Happines both of this life and of the next, is obtayned by fearing, and ſincerely ſeruing God. \f - \ft Feare of God the way to happines. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. BLESSED are al that feare our Lord, that walke in his wayes.
\v 2 Becauſe thou shalt eate the labours of thy handes: bleſſed art thou, and it shal be wel with thee.
\v 3 Thy \f a \fr 127.3 \ft If ſuch ſeruants of God be maried, they shal ordinarily haue iſſue, and ſucceſſion in their familie: but eſpecially the ſoules of ſuch ſhal bring forth manie meritorious workes,\f* wife as a fruitful vine, in \f b \fr 127.3 \ft in the boſome of the Catholique Church, vvhich vvas founded in Chriſts ſide.\f* the ſides of thy houſe. \f c \fr 127.3 \ft Children alſo ſignifie good workes.\f* Thy children as young plantes of oliuetrees, round about thy table.
\v 4 Behold ſo shal the man be bleſſed, that feareth our Lord.
\v 5 Our Lord out of Sion bleſſe thee: and that thou mayſt ſee the good thinges of Ieruſalem al the dayes of thy life.
\v 6 And that thou mayſt ſee thy \f d \fr 127.6 \ft Reward in heauen for good workes in earth.\f* childrens children, peace vpon Iſrael.
\c 128
\cd The Church often (3. and much) impugned, is not ouercome. 4. Her perſecuters are deſtroyed, 6. contemned, 8. and curſed. \f - \ft The Church ſtill firm in perſecution. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. \f a \fr 128.1 \ft Iſrael which is the Church of God, reioycing ſaith: that enimies haue often,\f* OFTEN haue they impugned me \f b \fr 128.1 \ft euen from the beginning of the world, perſecuted me, as when Cain perſecuted Abel, other wicked perſecuted Seth, Enoch, Noe: the Chaldeis perſecuted Abraham: the Ægyptians perſecuted the Iſraelites: and ſo in other generations.\f* from my youth, let Iſrael now ſay:
\v 2 Often haue they impugned me from my youth: \f c \fr 128.2 \ft But they haue neuer ouercome me. So the Pſalmiſt teſtifieth for al times paſt, & prophecieth the ſame for times to come.\f* but they haue not preuailed againſt me.
\v 3 Sinners \f d \fr 128.3 \ft Perſecuters not being able to ouerthrow, or ſuppreſſe the Church, haue laide great weightie burdens of tribulations vpon her backe, which ſhe hath patiently and ſtrongly borne,\f* haue builded vpon my backe: they haue \f e \fr 128.3 \ft they haue ſtil perſiſted, one ſorte after an other, but with longanimitie the Church hath ſtood faſt, and conſtantly paſſed through al diſtreſſes. In moral ſenſe ſinners build iniquitie vpon the back of the Church, yea and vpon Gods back, when they preſume to ſinne, truſting in the end to be abſolued by vertue of holie Sacraments leift in the Church. Likewiſe when they excuſe their ſinnes, imputing the cauſe to other creatures of God, vvherby they are allured. vvhich is in effect ( ſaith S. Auguſtin) to accuſe God, and to build iniquities on Gods back, vvho made thoſe creatures.\f* prolonged their iniquitie.
\v 4 \f f \fr 128.4 \ft God therfore who is iuſt, vvil at laſt caſt ſuch preſumptuous ſinners from his back, and breake their ſtiffe neckes:\f* Our iuſt Lord wil cut the neckes of ſinners:
\v 5 let them al \f g \fr 128.5 \ft then shal they be confounded,\f* be confounded and \f h \fr 128.5 \ft ſeparated eternally from God, become like fruitles, and vvithered graſſe, caſt avvay, deſpiſed, yea curſed of al, and bleſſed by none, as the Prophet denounceth in the next verſes.\f* turned backward, that hate Sion.
\v 6 Let them be made as graſſe in the toppes of houſes: which is withered before it be plucked vp.
\v 7 Wherof the reaper hath not filled his hand, and he that gathereth the sheaues his boſome.
\v 8 And they ſayd not that paſſed by: The bleſſing of our Lord be vpon you: we haue bleſſed you in the name of our Lord.
\c 129
\cd The Iewes, or other people in tribulation for ſinne, or temporal captiuitie, crie to God to be deliuered: 4. truſting, and encoreging ech other in Gods accuſtomed mercie, aſſuredly hoping that he wil redeme & deliuer them. \f - \ft The ſixth penitential Pſalm. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. FROM \f a \fr 129.1 \ft This prayer agreeth to al true penitents, crying to God for helpe, being either in depth of ſorovv for ſinne, and ſo it is one of the Penitential Pſalmes. or the depth of feruent deſire to aſcend tovvards perfection in vertue, and from this vaile of miſerie into heauen. and ſo it is a Gradual Pſalme. or in the depth of temporal paines, and ſo it is a ſpecial prayer for ſoules in Purgatorie, offered by the Church in their behalfe.\f* the depthes I haue cried to thee ô Lord:
\v 2 Lord heare my voice: Let thine eares be intent, to the voice of my petition.
\v 3 If thou shalt obſerue iniquities ô Lord: Lord \f b \fr 129.3 \ft None iſ able to abide the rigour of Gods iuſtice.\f* who shal ſuſteyne it?
\v 4 Becauſe with thee there is \f c \fr 129.4 \ft But al muſt relie vpon his mercie.\f* propiciation: and for \f d \fr 129.4 \ft For thy promiſes made in the law, that thou wilt remitte ſinnes to the penitent, geue more grace to them that ſeeke it, and mitigate alſo the paines due for ſinnes.\f* thy law I haue expected thee ô Lord. My ſoule hath expected in his word:
\v 5 my ſoule hath hoped in our Lord.
\v 6 From \f e \fr 129.6 \ft The hope of penitents is like to the watches of the day time, from morning vntil night, which are more comfortable, then watches of the night:\f* the morning watch euen vntil night: let Iſrael hope in our Lord.
\v 7 Becauſe with our Lord there is mercie: and with him \f f \fr 129.7 \ft The greateſt comforth is in Chriſt our Redemer, vvhoſe plentiful Redemption bringeth more abundance of grace.\f* plenteous redemption.
\v 8 And he shal redeme \f g \fr 129.8 \ft Chriſts Redemption being ſufficient for al the vvorld, is effectual only to true liuing members of the Catholique Church.\f* Iſrael, from al his iniquities.
\c 130
\cd Anie iuſt ſoule in humble confidence offereth his innocencie, as a ſpiritual and gratful ſacrifice to God, 3. exhorting al Gods ſeruants euer to hope in him. \f - \ft Confidence of innocencie. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle of \f a \fr 130.1 \ft Dauid by Gods ſpecial grace hauing a ſincere minde tovvards al men, euen towards his enimies, and an humble hart, not deſiring anie thing ambiciouſly, but al to the honour of God, propoſeth his owne example, for others to imitate: that they may vvith him offer the ſacrifice of humilitie, and innocencie vnto God, from whom al good thinges procede.\f* Dauid. LORD my hart is not exalted: \f b \fr 130.1 \ft Al this vvith a thankful mind to God vvho gaue this grace.\f* neither are mine eies loftie. Neither haue I walked in great matters: nor in meruelous thinges aboue me.
\v 2 If I was not humbly mynded: but exalted my ſoule: As \f c \fr 130.2 \ft As children after they are vveaned come ſtil vvillingly to their mother, ſo doth the childe of God relie vpon Gods helpe, though he alwayes feele not the ſame ſwetnes:\f* the weaned childe is toward his mother, \f d \fr 130.2 \ft & according to his demeanur herein, he expecteth reward.\f* ſo retribution in my ſoule.
\v 3 \f e \fr 130.3 \ft King Dauid, or anie other being for his vertue aduanced, and revvarded by God, is a good example to moue others to do the like, ſo shal they receiue like reward.\f* Let Iſrael hope in our Lord, from henceforth now and for euer.
\c 131
\cd The Pſalmiſt earneſtly prayeth God to geue him leaue, & to shew him where, to build a Temple. But more eſpecially prayeth for, and prophecieth the coming of Chriſt, the promiſed Sonne of Dauid, 14. ſignifying Gods promiſe therof, and of eſtablishing his Church. \f - \ft Chriſt's coming to reſtore man. The 5. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. REMEMBER \f a \fr 131.1 \ft It is an vſual thing that Dauid, Moyſes, & other Prophetes ſpeake of themſelues in the third perſon.\f* Dauid ô Lord, and al his meekenes:
\v 2 As he ſware to our Lord, \f b \fr 131.2 \ft King Dauid deſiring, and ſo farre as lay in him, promiſing to build a Temple to God, vvith great inſtance prayed that he might performe the ſame. But God diſpoſing othervviſe, that not he, but his ſonne ſhould build it, he neuertheles prepared the matter, workemen, and money, ſhevved the forme, and diſpoſed the Leuites how to ſerue therin.\f* vowed a vowe to the God of Iacob.
\v 3 \f c \fr 131.3 \ft Moreouer by vow depriued himſelf of entering into his owne houſe, or taking his ordinarie reſt, til he might (if it ſo pleaſed God) know the place where it ſhould be built.\f* If I shal enter into the tabernacle of my houſe, if I shal aſcend into the bed of my couch.
\v 4 If I shal geue ſleepe to mine eies, and ſlumbering to mine eie liddes:
\v 5 And reſt to my temples: vntil I finde a place for our Lord, a tabernacle for the God of Iacob.
\v 6 Behold we haue heard of it \f d \fr 131.6 \ft It vvas reueled to Dauid, that the Temple ſhould be built, in that part of Ieruſalem, vvhich looketh tovvards Bethleem (othervviſe called Ephrata) vvhere our Sauiour vvas borne.\f* in Ephrata: we haue found it in \f e \fr 131.6 \ft VVithin Ieruſalem, which is compaſſed with vvoodes. In this viſion alſo the whole forme of the Temple vvas reueled vnto him, as he teſtifieth. \+it 1. Par. 28. v. 19\+it*. Al thinges, quoth he, came vvritten with the hand of our Lord vnto me: that I might vnderſtand al the vvorkes of the paterne.\f* the fildes of the wood.
\v 7 \f f \fr 131.7 \ft Holie Dauid moued vvith exceding deuotion, repared to the place, vvhere Gods Temple should be built:\f* We wil enter into his tabernacle: we wil \f g \fr 131.7 \ft & adored God, vvhere the Propitiatorie, as a footſtoole repreſenting Gods preſence, ſhould ſtand. What meruel then if deuotion moue Chriſtians, to viſite the holie places, vvhere our Sauiour, God and Man was Incarnate, vvas borne, ſuffered death, vvas buried, aſcended into heauen, or anie other place where his feete ſtood.\f* adore in the place where his feete ſtood.
\v 8 \f h \fr 131.8 \ft O God, leauing Silo, Gabaon, and the like places, come into thy holie Temple.\f* Ariſe Lord into thy reſt, thou and \f i \fr 131.8 \ft With the Arke of conenant, vvhere thou ſanctifieſt thy people. And here againe the Prophet illuminated vvith a higher Myſterie, and inflamed with more deuotion, prayeth for Chriſtes coming into the world, and that after his Paſſion he wil riſe, not only in glorie of ſoule, but alſo of bodie, prefigured by the Arke of teſtimonie, which vvas in the tabernacle, and after in the Temple.\f* the arke of thy ſanctification.
\v 9 Let thy Prieſtes be clothed with \f k \fr 131.9 \ft Grant therfore that thy Prieſtes, vvhich muſt offer ſacrifice in this ſacred place, be indued with vertues, and good life worthie of their degree:\f* iuſtice: & let thy \f l \fr 131.9 \ft and the Leuites who are ordained to ſerue there, be likevviſe made fitte for their diuers functions, both in the old and new Teſtament.\f* ſainctes reioyce.
\v 10 \f m \fr 131.10 \ft And ſeing thou haſt geuen ſuch meeknes, deuotion, zele, ſinceritie, and other vertues making him a man according to thyne owne hart, and therupon promiſed to eſtablish his ſede,\f* For Dauid thy ſeruants ſake, \f n \fr 131.10 \ft differ not to ſend thy promiſed Meſſias, Chriſt our Redemer.\f* turne not away the face of thy Chriſt.
\v 11 Our Lord \f o \fr 131.11 \ft Henceforth to the end of this Pſalme, the Prophet relateth Gods reuelation to him: That he hath truly promiſed with an oath,\f* hath ſworne truth to Dauid, and \f p \fr 131.11 \ft and vvil performe,\f* he wil not diſapoint it: \f q \fr 131.11 \ft to ſette one of Dauids ſonnes vpon his Throne: which vvas not only fulfilled in Salomon, who reigned in great peace, and builded the Temple, but eſpecially in Chriſt the Sonne of Dauid, to whom our Lord God gaue the ſeate of Dauid his father, & he shal reigne in the houſe of Iacob for euer, and of his kingdom there shal be no end. Luc. 1. v. 32. 33. By this promiſe S. Peter alſo proueth Chriſts Reſurrection. Act. 1. v. 30.\f* Of the fruite of thy wombe I vvil ſet vpon thy ſeate.
\v 12 \f r \fr 131.12 \ft Concerning the children of Chriſt, members of his kingdom the Church, the promiſe is conditional, if they obſerue Gods commandments, they ſhal ſit vpon his ſeate: be coheyres of his kingdom. \+it Rom. 8. v. 17\+it*.\f* If thy children shal keepe my teſtament, and theſe my teſtimonies which I wil teach them: Their children alſo euen for euer, shal ſit vpon thy ſeate.
\v 13 Becauſe our Lord hath choſen Sion: he hath choſen it for an habitation to himſelf.
\v 14 This is my \f s \fr 131.14 \ft Sion, the Catholique Church, is a perpetual place where God dwelleth.\f* reſt for euer and euer: here wil I dwel becauſe I haue choſen it.
\v 15 Bleſſing I wil bleſſe her \f t \fr 131.15 \ft The Church vvanting Chriſts viſible preſence, is replenished with manie bleſſinges,\f* widow: her \f v \fr 131.15 \ft and al her humble children are releeued vvith holie Sacraments.\f* poore I wil fil with breades.
\v 16 Her \f w \fr 131.16 \ft The Church hath alwayes ſome holie Prieſtes,\f* Prieſtes I wil clothe with ſaluation: and her \f x \fr 131.16 \ft and ſome holie people.\f* ſainctes shal reioyce with ioyfulnes.
\v 17 \f y \fr 131.17 \ft Of this Church, elected by God, Chriſt is the Protector, and ſtrong defence.\f* Thither wil I bring forth a horne to Dauid, I haue prepared \f z \fr 131.17 \ft and the ſame Church shal be a perpetual lampe, wherby others may come to him.\f* a lampe to my Chriſt.
\v 18 His enemies I wil clothe with confuſion: but vpon him shal my ſanctification florish.
\c 132
\cd Fraternal concord is commended to al in the Church, as neceſſarie and delectable in it ſelf, and bleſſed of God. \f - \ft Fraternal concord. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle \f a \fr 132.1 \ft Dauid being a moſt peaceable man, euen tovvard his enimies, is added by Eſdras in this title, as an example for others to imitate.\f* of Dauid. BEHOLD \f b \fr 132.1 \ft That concord is both good and pleaſant, nedeth no other proofe, but only to conſider, & to behold the fruict therof in euerie communitie, and eſpecially in the Church of God.\f* how good, and how pleaſant a thing it is, for brethren to dwel in one:
\v 2 \f c \fr 132.2 \ft The ointment, wherwith Aaron, and other High Prieſtes vvere conſecrated, was precious, and moſt odoriferous, ſignifying grace bought by Chriſts blood, and producing vertues, vvhich make ſvvete ſauoure:\f* As oyntement on \f d \fr 132.2 \ft vvas povvred vpon the High Prieſts head, who being head of the Church,\f* the head, which ranne downe vpon the beard, \f e \fr 132.2 \ft vnitie and concord deſcended from him to other Prieſtes,\f* the beard of Aaron, which ranne downe vnto \f f \fr 132.2 \ft and ſo to the people, euen to the lovveſt, and meaneſt in Gods Church.\f* the hemme of his garment:
\v 3 \f g \fr 132.3 \ft An other ſimilitude to declare the excellencie of concord: As the dew of mount Hermon, vvhich is perpetually couered vvith ſnow, that falleth from the heauen, thence deſcending refresheth & fructifieth the hil of Sion: ſo mutual concord, and fraternal charitie amongſt the faithful, nourisheth each other, making them fructful in al good workes.\f* As the dew of Hermon, which runneth downe vpon mount Sion. Becauſe \f h \fr 132.3 \ft VVhere there is vnitie God geueth abundance of grace, as where the Apoſtles vvith other faithful vvere geathered, and continued in prayer, the Holie Ghoſt came vpon them al. And the multitude of beleuers [then increaſing] had one hart and one ſoule. \+it Act. 2\+it*. &. \+it 4. v. 32\+it*.\f* there hath our Lord commanded bleſſing, and life euen for euer.
\c 133
\cd Al, and eſpecially clergie men that ſerue the Church, are inuited to prayſe God by day and by night, ſo shal they be bleſſed of God. \f - \ft God continually to be praiſed. The 1. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A gradual Canticle. LOE \f a \fr 133.1 \ft This laſt Gradual Pſalme, as a concluſion exhorteth al to praiſe God:\f* now bleſſe our Lord, al ye the ſeruantes of our Lord: which ſtand in the houſe of our Lord, in the courtes of the houſe of our God.
\v 2 In the nightes lift vp your handes vnto the holie places, and bleſſe ye our Lord.
\v 3 Our Lord out of Sion bleſſe thee, who \f b \fr 133.3 \ft for to this end he made al creatures in heauen & in earth.\f* made heauen and earth.
\c 134
\cd God who only is omnipotent, and fountaine of al goodnes, is of al to be praiſed, 3. He is the ſpecial protector of his elected people. 15. Contrariwiſe falſe goddes are impotent, vaine, and can not helpe thoſe that ſerue them. 19. Only the Church doth rightly praiſe him. \f - \ft God's perfection and goodneſs. The 1. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Allelu ia. PRAYSE ye the name of our Lord, \f a \fr 134.1 \ft Gods ſeruantes.\f* ye ſeruantes prayſe our Lord.
\v 2 Ye \f b \fr 134.2 \ft Clergie that ſerue in the Temple,\f* that ſtand in the houſe of our Lord, in \f c \fr 134.2 \ft and ye Leuite that attend to his ſeruice, muſt praiſe him for theſe cauſes.\f* the courtes of the houſe of our God.
\v 3 Prayſe ye our Lord, becauſe our Lord is \f d \fr 134.3 \ft his goodnes.\f* good: ſing ye to his name, becauſe it is \f e \fr 134.3 \ft his benignitie.\f* ſweete.
\v 4 Becauſe our Lord \f f \fr 134.4 \ft he hath adopted vs his children.\f* hath choſen Iacob to himſelf, Iſrael for his owne poſſeſſion.
\v 5 Becauſe I haue knowne that our Lord is \f g \fr 134.5 \ft he is omnipotent, as appeareth by his workes, here and elſvvhere recited : and manie wayes knovvne to the world.\f* great, and our God aboue al goddes.
\v 6 Al thinges whatſoeuer our Lord would he hath done, in heauen, in earth, in the ſea, and in al the depthes.
\v 7 Bringing forth clowdes from the vttermoſt of the earth: lightenings he hath turned into rayne. Who bringeth forth the windes out of his treaſures:
\v 8 who ſtroke the firſt begotten of Ægypt from man euen to beaſt.
\v 9 He ſent forth ſignes, and wonders in the middes of thee ô Ægypt: vpon Pharao, and vpon al his ſeruantes. \x - \xt Exo. 11.\x*
\v 10 Who ſtroke manie nations: and ſlew ſtrong kinges:
\v 11 Sehon the king of the Amorrheites, & Og the king of Baſan, and al the kingdomes of Chanaan. \x - \xt Ios. 12. Num. 21. Ios. 13.\x*
\v 12 And he gaue their land for inheritance, for an inheritance to Iſrael his people.
\v 13 Lord thy name is for euer: Lord thy memorial is vnto generation and generation.
\v 14 Becauſe our Lord shal iudge his people: and wil be intreated toward his ſeruantes.
\v 15 The idoles of the Gentiles are ſiluer, and gold, the workes of mens handes.
\v 16 They haue mouth, and shal not ſpeake: they haue eies, and shal not ſee.
\v 17 They haue eares, and shal not heare: for neither is there breath in their mouth.
\v 18 Let them that make them become like to them: and al that haue confidence in them.
\v 19 Ye houſe of Iſrael bleſſe our Lord: ye houſe of Aaron bleſſe our Lord.
\v 20 Ye houſe of Leui bleſſe our Lord: you that feare our Lord, bleſſe ye our Lord.
\v 21 Bleſſed be our Lord out of Sion, who dwelleth in Ieruſalem.
\c 135
\cd God being meruelous in himſelf, 4. hath shewed his powre and goodnes in his workes, as wel in general to al the world, 10. as in particular towards his elected people. \f - \ft God's marvelous works. The 2. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Allelu ia. CONFESSE ye to our Lord \f a \fr 135.1 \ft VVhoſe goodnes is incomparable.\f* becauſe he is good: \f b \fr 135.1 \ft This ſecond part of euerie verſe, firſt pronounced by the Prieſtes, confeſſing & praiſing Gods mercie aboue al his workes, was ſtil repeted by muſicians, or other aſſiſtants, in maner as now in Litanies [ ô Lord deliuer vs ] and [ VVe beſech thee to heare vs ] And as Gloria Patri &c. is added in the end of Pſalmes, and our Lords prayer, and the Angelical ſalutation are often repeted in diuers Offices, and in the Roſarie.\f* becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 2 Confeſſe ye to \f c \fr 135.2 \ft VVhoſe diuine powre created al other thinges.\f* the God of goddes: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 3 \f d \fr 135.3 \ft By this inuitation to praiſe our Lord God, thriſe repeted, we profeſſe the Bleſſed Trinitie, who is one God in ſubſtance, and three Diuine Perſons.\f* Confeſſe ye to \f e \fr 135.3 \ft VVhoſe dominion is ouer al the world.\f* the Lord of lordes: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 4 Who \f f \fr 135.4 \ft Only God can do true miracles ; to wit, workes aboue the courſe of al nature.\f* onlie doth great meruels: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 5 Who made the heauen \f g \fr 135.5 \ft In admirable wiſdom.\f* in vnderſtanding: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 6 Who eſtablished \f h \fr 135.6 \ft Made the waters aboue nature to remaine vvithin their bondes, & not to couer the earth.\f* the earth ouer the waters: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 7 Who made the great lightes: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 8 The ſunne to rule the day: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 9 The moone, and ſtarres to rule the night: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 10 Who ſtroke Ægypt with their firſtbegotten: becauſe his mercie is for euer. \x - \xt Exo. 11.\x*
\v 11 Who brought forth Iſrael out of the middes of them: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 12 In a mightie hand, and loftie arme: becauſe his mercie is for euer. \x - \xt Exo. 13.\x*
\v 13 Who diuided the Read ſea into diuiſions: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 14 And brought forth Iſrael through the middes therof: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 15 And he ouerthrew Pharao, and his hoſt in the Read ſea: becauſe his mercie is for euer. \x - \xt Exo. 14.\x*
\v 16 Who led his people through the deſert: becauſe his mercie is for euer. \x - \xt Num. 21.\x*
\v 17 Who ſtroke great kinges: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 18 And ſlewe ſtrong kinges: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 19 Sehon the king of the Amorrheites: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 20 And Og the king of Baſan: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 21 And he gaue their land for an inheritance: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 22 For an inheritance to Iſrael his ſeruant: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 23 For in our humiliation he was mindful of vs: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 24 And he redemed vs from our enemies: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 25 Who geueth foode to \f i \fr 135.25 \ft Al Gods benignitie, as wel of particular benefites towards his people, as general to them and al others procedeth from his mercie.\f* al fleshe: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\v 26 Confeſſe ye to the God of heauen: becauſe his mercie is for euer. Confeſſe ye to the Lord of lordes: becauſe his mercie is for euer.
\c 136
\cd The Prophet deſcribeth how lamentably the people in captiuitie of Babylon, wil bewaile the want of meanes to ſerue God, and of their natiue ſoyle : 7. with iuſt deſire of their enimies punishment. \f - \ft The Jews' lamentation in captivity. The 4. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Pſalme of Dauid \f a \fr 136.1 \ft By adding to this title [ for Ieremie ] the Septuagint Interpreters ſignifie that this Pſalme treateth of the ſame captiuitie, in which Ieremias writte his Lamentations.\f* for Ieremie. VPON the riuers \f b \fr 136.1 \ft Nere the riuers in Chaldea ; wherof Babylon was the head citie,\f* of Babylon, there we \f c \fr 136.1 \ft the Iewes remained mourning,\f* ſate and wept: whiles we \f d \fr 136.1 \ft remembring the holie rites and ſeruice of God, which had bene in Sion, wherof they were depriued in the captiuitie.\f* remembred Sion.
\v 2 On the willowes in the middes therof, we hanged vp \f e \fr 136.2 \ft Al their muſical inſtruments, as hauing no vſe of them.\f* our inſtrumentes.
\v 3 Becauſe there they that led vs captiue, \f f \fr 136.3 \ft Either in earneſt, or in ſcorne the Chaldees willed them to ſing, as they were accuſtomed in their countrie.\f* demanded of vs wordes of ſonges. And they that led vs away: Sing ye an hymne to vs of the ſonges of Sion.
\v 4 \f g \fr 136.4 \ft They excuſed themſelues, and refuſed to ſing ſacred Pſalmes before prophane people, neither had they mind to ſing in that mourning ſtate of captiuitie.\f* How shal we sing the ſong of our Lord in a ſtrange land?
\v 5 \f h \fr 136.5 \ft The people shew not only their feruent preſent deſire to ſerue God in Ieruſalem, but alſo their firme purpoſe ſtil to deſire the ſame, wiſhing that if they forget it, or loſe this affection, their right handes, or whatſoeuer is moſt deare, or neceſſarie for them, may be forgotten, not conſerued, but ſuffered to perish,\f* If I shal forget thee ô Ieruſalem, let my right hand be forgotten.
\v 6 \f i \fr 136.6 \ft If I loſe this affection, let me alſo loſe the vſe of my tongue.\f* Let my tongue cleaue to my iawes, if I doe not remember thee: If I shal not ſet Ieruſalem in the beginning of my ioy.
\v 7 Be mindful ô Lord of \f k \fr 136.7 \ft The Idomeans incenſed the Chaldees to be cruel againſt the Iewes, wherof they pray for iuſt reuenge, and withal the Pſalmiſt prophecieth that it wil be reuenged, which Iſaias alſo prophecieth, \+it c. 21. v. 11\+it*.\f* the childrem of Edom, in \f l \fr 136.7 \ft for their reioycing in Ieruſalems miſerie.\f* the day of Ieruſalem: \f m \fr 136.7 \ft The voice of the Idumeans, inciting the Babylonians vtterly to deſtroy Ieruſalem.\f* That ſay: Raſe it, raſe it, euen vnto the foundation therof.
\v 8 \f n \fr 136.8 \ft A prophecie that the people of Babylon should alſo be punished, for their crueltie againſt the Iewes, wherof Iſaias likewiſe prophecieth c. 13.\f* Daugther of Babylon miſerable: bleſſed is he, that shal repay thee thy payment, which thou haſt payed vs.
\v 9 Bleſſed is he, \f o \fr 136.9 \ft God wil bleſſe, or reward them that shal ſeuerly afflict the Babylonians,\f* that shal hold, and \f p \fr 136.9 \ft not ſparing their children. Morally he is bleſſed, that mortifieth his owne paſſions, cutteth of firſt il motions, or punisheth venial ſinnes, that they grow not ſtrong within his ſoule, and ſo draw it to committe mortal ſinne. S. Aug. hic. & S. Greg in fine expoſ. Pſal. 4. pænit.\f* shal dash thy litle ones againſt the rocke.
\c 137
\cd The whole Church, or anie iuſt perſon, rendreth thankes to God for his benefites 4. praying that al kinges and kingdomes may do the ſame, 6. becauſe God being high reſpecteth, and aduanceth the humble. \f - \ft Thankes to God for benefites. The 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 To \f a \fr 137.1 \ft This Pſalme is a fitte forme of thankes for Dauid, or anie other ſeruant of God.\f* Dauid him ſelf. I wil \f b \fr 137.1 \ft I wil render thankes and praiſe.\f* confeſſe to thee ô Lord in my whole hart: \f c \fr 137.1 \ft God euer heareth the prayer that is rightly made, for which the ſupplicant is therfore to geue thankes.\f* becauſe thou haſt heard the wordes of my mouth. In \f d \fr 137.1 \ft Angels are preſent where the faithful pray, obſerue our prayers, and offer them to God, if they be ſincere, as the prayers of Tobias, and Cornelius.\f* the ſight of Angels I wil ſing to thee:
\v 2 I wil adore \f e \fr 137.2 \ft VVhen there was not acceſſe to the Temple, yet the Iewes praying in captiuitie, turned themſelues tovvards the Temple.\f* toward thy holie temple, and wil confeſſe to thy name. \f 1 \fr 137.2 \ft \+it 1. Cor. v. 10. Tob. 12. Act. 10. Pſal. 24. v. 10.\+it*\f* For thy mercie, and thy truth: becauſe thou haſt magnified aboue euerie thing \f f \fr 137.2 \ft The Name and Maieſtie of GOD, The VVord (which is the Sonne of God) alſo the name of IESVS, is magnified aboue al names, or temporal thinges :\f* thy holie name.
\v 3 In what day ſoeuer I shal inuocate thee, heare me: thou wilt multiplie ſtrength in my ſoule.
\v 4 Let \f g \fr 137.4 \ft Sovvner or later Kinges and Princes of al kingdomes and nations, haue bene or ſhal be conuerted to Chriſt.\f* al the kinges of the earth ô Lord confeſſe to thee: becauſe they haue heard al the wordes of thy mouth:
\v 5 And let them ſing in the wayes of our Lord: becauſe great is the glorie of our Lord.
\v 6 Becauſe our Lord is high, and he beholdeth low thinges: and high thinges he knoweth \f h \fr 137.6 \ft God knovveth proud men, not as his freindes or ſeruantes, but farre of, as ſtrangers and enimies.\f* far of.
\v 7 If I shal walke in the middes of tribulation, thou wilt quicken me: and vpon the wrath of mine enimies thou haſt extended thy hand, and thy right hand hath ſaued me.
\v 8 Our Lord wil repay for me: ô Lord thy mercie is for euer: deſpiſe not the workes of thy handes.
\c 138
\cd Gods knowlege, 7. and preſence (10. without the helpe, or hinderance anie thing) extendeth to al thinges, times, and places. 17. he geueth exceeding great honour to his ſainctes, 20. the wicked, as enimies to God are iuſtly hated, 23. the iuſt pray for Gods perpetual direction. \f - \ft God's ſpecial providence of his ſervants. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto \f a \fr 138.1 \ft By this part of the title [to the end] is ſignified (as is noted Pſal. 4.) that the matter conteined in the Pſalme, perteyneth to the newe Teſtament.\f* the end, a Pſalme of Dauid. LORD thou \f b \fr 138.1 \ft God vvho knovveth al thinges moſt abſolutly and perfectly, vvithout diſcourſe or ſearching, yet, as it vvere, maketh experimental trial of his ſeruants, to make them in ſome ſorte to know him, and to know themſelues. And ſo here, holie Dauid or other faithful man, acknowlegeth Gods Omniſcience, that is, perfect knovvlege of al thinges, without exception, paſt, preſent, & to come : al vvorkes, wordes, thoughtes, and whatſoeuer can be, though it neuer vvas nor ſhal be, in general and in particular.\f* haſt proued me, and haſt knowen me:
\v 2 thou haſt knowen my ſitting downe, and my riſing vp.
\v 3 Thou haſt vnderſtood my cogitations far of: my path, and \f c \fr 138.3 \ft The vttermoſt meaſure and reach of myne intention.\f* my corde thou haſt ſearched out.
\v 4 And thou haſt foreſene al my wayes: becauſe there is not a word in my \f d \fr 138.4 \ft The word holden in by the tongue, and not vttered by mouth, is not hidden from God.\f* tongue.
\v 5 Behold ô Lord thou haſt knowen al the laſt thinges, & them of old: thou haſt formed me, and haſt put thy hand vpon me.
\v 6 Thy knowledge is \f e \fr 138.6 \ft By experiece we ſee that Gods knowlege exceedeth our reach.\f* become meruelous of me: it is made great, and I can not reach to it.
\v 7 \f f \fr 138.7 \ft As Gods knowlege comprehendeth al thinges, ſo his preſence extendeth it ſelfe to al places, neither is conteined in place, but excedeth al place, in his diuine immenſitie.\f* Whither shal I goe from thy ſpirit? and whither shal I flee from thy face?
\v 8 If I shal aſcend into heauen, thou art there: if I deſcend into hel, thou art preſent.
\v 9 If I shal take my winges early, and dwel in the extreme partes of the ſea:
\v 10 Certes thither alſo shal thy hand conduct me: and thy right hand shal hold me.
\v 11 \f g \fr 138.11 \ft The Prophet alſo in the perſon of anie curious imaginatiue man, examineth and findeth that no darknes, nor couer can hide anie thing from God.\f* And I ſayd: Perhaps darknes shal treade ouer me: and the night is mine illumination in my delightes.
\v 12 For darkenes shal not be darkened from thee, and the night shal be lightened as the day: as the darkenes therof, ſo alſo the light therof.
\v 13 Becauſe thou haſt poſſeſſed \f h \fr 138.13 \ft Nothing ſemeth more hidden, then a mans entrals.\f* my reynes: thou haſt receiued me from \f i \fr 138.13 \ft or a child in the mothers wombe.\f* my mothers wombe.
\v 14 I wil confeſſe to thee, becauſe thou art terribly magnified: thy workes are meruelous, & my ſoule knoweth excedingly.
\v 15 My \f k \fr 138.15 \ft Or bones in the flesh.\f* bone is not hid from thee, which thou madeſt in ſecrete: and my ſubſtance in the lower partes of the earth.
\v 16 \f l \fr 138.16 \ft Or mans bodilie imperfection before his birth,\f* Mine \f 1 \fr 138.16 \ft \+it Colem, Embryonem.\+it*\f* imperfection thine eies haue ſene, & in thy booke \f 2 \fr 138.16 \ft \+it Of knowlege\+it*.\f* al shal be written: \f m \fr 138.16 \ft dayly formed by God, not by man. \+it Iob. 10. v. 8. 2. Mac. 7. v. 21, 23\+it*.\f* daies shal be formed, & no man in them.
\v 17 \f n \fr 138.17 \ft Aboue al conſiderations it moſt excedeth, that God ſo high and infinite, honoreth his humble poore ſeruants ſo exceedingly, that it ſemeth to themſelues, farre more then can be due. For he rewardeth euen ouer & aboue merites ; which merites alſo are founded in Gods mercie geuen without merite.\f* But to me thy \f 1 \fr 138.17 \ft Newe tranſlaters peruert this place, tranſlating [thoughts] for [frendes] contrarie to the Hebrevv, Greke, and Latin, and al ancient Fathers, only pretending, that the ſame word in the Chaldee tongue alſo ſignifieth thoughts.\f* frendes ô God are become honorable exceedingly: their principalitie is exceedingly ſtrengthned.
\v 18 \f o \fr 138.18 \ft The number alſo of Sainctes vvhom God hath choſen, called, iuſtified and vvil glorifie, excede mans conceipt. Apoc 7.\f* I wil number them, and they shal be multiplied aboue the ſand: \f p \fr 138.18 \ft Incenſed with this excellent glorie, and deſiring to be of this innumerable multitude, by thy grace I haue riſen from ſinne, and in confidence of thy perpetual helpe, I ſtand and hope to perſeuere in thy ſeruice.\f* I roſe vp and I am yet with thee.
\v 19 \f q \fr 138.19 \ft And if it be ſo, yea ſeing it is ſo, that as thou ô God doeſt exalt thy Sainctes, to exceeding and vnſpeakable honour : ſo thou haſt alſo decreed to puniſh obſtinate ſinners, vvith eternal death and damnation :\f* If thou shalt kil ſinners ô God: \f r \fr 138.19 \ft I renounce al vvicked aſſociation, gette ye avvay from me al cruel bloudie men, that would drawe me into euerlaſting torments.\f* ye men of blood depart from me.
\v 20 \f s \fr 138.20 \ft Away from me, you that thinke,\f* Becauſe you ſay in thought: they \f t \fr 138.20 \ft the glorious manſions in heauen, prepared and promiſed to the iuſt, are vaine hopes, and in vaine expected.\f* shal receiue thy cities in vayne.
\v 21 \f v \fr 138.21 \ft This hate of ſuch ſinners the iuſt shal confidently plead, and happie are they that shal be able truly to alleage for themſelues in the day of iudgement, that they hated al, whom God hateth,\f* Did not I hate them, that hate thee ô Lord: and \f w \fr 138.21 \ft yea hated them vvith feruent zele, that are Gods enimies.\f* pyned away becauſe of thine enemies?
\v 22 \f x \fr 138.22 \ft Stil the Prophet inculcateth this neceſſarie perfect hatred,\f* with perfect hatred did I hate them: they are become \f y \fr 138.22 \ft and enmitie towards Gods enimies.\f* enemies to me.
\v 23 \f z \fr 138.23 \ft For that none in this life (without ſpecial and extraordinarie reuelation) knoweth certainly their owne ſtate, whether they be worthie of Gods loue or hatred \+it (Eccle. 9\+it*.) the iuſt ſubmitte themſelues to Gods examination of their hart and actions :\f* Proue me ô God, and know my hart: examine me, and know my pathes.
\v 24 And \f a \fr 138.24 \ft humbly praing God, that if they be in the way of iniquitie,\f* ſee, if the way of iniquitie be in me: \f b \fr 138.24 \ft he wil voutſafe to reduce and guid them into the right way of euerlaſting life.\f* and conduct me in the euerlaſting way.
\c 139
\cd The iuſt diuerſly afflicted by the wicked, pray to be defended, 7. repoſe their confidence in God, 10. who wil adiudge the reprobate to eternal punishment, 13. and reward the good with the fruition of himſelf. \f - \ft Eternal pain of the wicked, and joy of the bleſſed. The 10. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Vnto the end, a Pſalme of Dauid.
\v 2 DELIVER me ô Lord from the euil man: from the vniuſt man reſcue me.
\v 3 Which \f a \fr 139.3 \ft Vnquiet euil diſpoſed men ſtil deuiſe wicked plottes,\f* haue deuiſed iniquitie in their hart: \f b \fr 139.3 \ft and neuer ceaſe to make diſcord, and debates.\f* al the day they did appoint battels. They haue whet their tongues as that of a ſerpent:
\v 4 the venome of aſpes is vnder their lippes.
\v 5 Kepe me ô Lord from the hand of the ſinner: and from vniuſt men deliuer me.
\v 6 Who haue deuiſed to ſupplant my ſteppes: the proude haue hid a ſnare for me: And they haue ſtreched out ropes for a ſnare: they haue layd \f 1 \fr 139.6 \ft \+it ſcandalum\+it*.\f* a ſtumbling blocke for me nere the way.
\v 7 \f c \fr 139.7 \ft In time of tentation prayer is moſt neceſſarie.\f* I ſayd to our Lord: Thou art my God: heare ô Lord the voice of my petition.
\v 8 O Lord, Lord \f d \fr 139.8 \ft Man is not able to reſiſt temptations,\f* the ſtrength of my ſaluation: \f e \fr 139.8 \ft vnleſſe God by his grace, as by a helmet, defend him from yelding conſent.\f* thou haſt ouershadowed my head in the day of battel.
\v 9 Yeld me not ô Lord from \f f \fr 139.9 \ft Suffer me not to fal from that which I now deſire (which is to be conſtant in vertue) not to conſent to ſinners perſwaſions.\f* my deſire, to the ſinner: they haue deuiſed againſt me, forſake me not, \f g \fr 139.9 \ft VVho then would triumph ouer me.\f* leſt they perhaps be proude.
\v 10 \f h \fr 139.10 \ft The ſumme of their miſcheuous deuiſes,\f* The head of their compaſſe: \f i \fr 139.10 \ft conſiſting in their ſubtle perſuaſions with ſwete wordes,\f* the labour of their lippes \f k \fr 139.10 \ft ſhal ouerwhelme, and bring themſelues to perdition.\f* shal couer them.
\v 11 \f l \fr 139.11 \ft Hel fire shal be their lotes ;\f* Coales shal fal vpon them, \f m \fr 139.11 \ft into which they shal fal,\f* thou shalt caſt them downe into fyre: in miſeries \f n \fr 139.11 \ft & euer be in intolerable torments.\f* they shal not ſtand vp.
\v 12 A man ful of tongue shal not be directed in the earth: euils shal take the vniuſt man into deſtruction.
\v 13 \f o \fr 139.13 \ft I haue learned by good inſtructions, and by experience, that in the end thou ô God wilt comfort the iuſt, who are now afflicted, and punish the vniuſt, that liue in temporal delightes, as it happened to Lazarus, and the glutton.\f* I haue knowne that our Lord wil do the iudgement of the needie: and the reuenge of the poore.
\v 14 But as for the iuſt, they shal confeſſe to thy name: and the righteous shal dwel with \f p \fr 139.14 \ft Eternal glorie conſiſteth in ſeeing God.\f* thy countenance.
\c 140
\cd The Church prayeth that her children may auoide ſinful wordes, 4. not make excuſes of ſinnes committed, not communicate with others in ſinne, nor to harken to flatterers : 6. but to pray that they may amend ( the Pſalmiſt by the way prophecieth that manie shal be conuerted) 8. though ſometimes perſecution be great, the Church faileth not. \f - \ft The Church prayeth and prevaileth. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Pſalme of Dauid. LORD I haue cried to thee, heare me: attend to my voice, when I shal crie to thee.
\v 2 \f 1 \fr 140.2 \ft In theſe wordes the Church prayeth, offering incenſe in her ſolemne Offices.\f* Let my prayer be directed as \f a \fr 140.2 \ft As the ſume of incenſe is ſwete and aſcendeth vpwards : ſo the Church prayeth that her petitions may be gratful, and aſcend to God.\f* incenſe in thy ſight: the eleuation of my handes as \f b \fr 140.2 \ft In the old teſtament morning ſacrifice was rather more ſolemne, and more frequent, but the Prophet ſemeth to allude vnto Chriſts Sacrifice, which he was to offer towards euening on the Croſſe ; and the ſame alſo in vnbloudie maner, the euening before his Paſſion, in the Euchariſt.\f* euening ſacrifice.
\v 3 Set ô Lord \f c \fr 140.3 \ft A iuſt care and conſideration what to ſpeake, before the mouth be opened.\f* a watch to my mouth: and \f d \fr 140.3 \ft lippes muſt not be alwayes ſtopt, for it is a ſinne ſometimes not to ſpeake, but as a dore that is to be opened, and shut at due ſeaſons, for example ; opened to confeſſe our ſinnes, and Gods truth :\f* a doore round about to my lippes.
\v 4 Decline not my hart \f e \fr 140.4 \ft shutte from wordes of malice, and from excuſing ſinnes committed.\f* into wordes of malice, to make excuſes in ſinnes. With men that worke iniquitie: and I wil not communicate with the chiefe of them.
\v 5 The iuſt shal rebuke me in mercie, and \f f \fr 140.5 \ft Admonition of the iuſt, is a profitable reprehenſion,\f* shal reprehend me: but let not the oyle of a ſinner \f g \fr 140.5 \ft but the fawning of flatterers is pernicious.\f* fatte my head. Becauſe, \f h \fr 140.5 \ft The Church ceaſeth not to pray for ſinners,\f* yet alſo my prayer is in their \f i \fr 140.5 \ft though as yet they take pleaſure in their ſinnes,\f* good pleaſures:
\v 6 \f k \fr 140.6 \ft but the very chiefeſt of them, which ſeme moſt potent, if they remaine obſtinate to their death, shal then periſh, as men throwne againſt rockes.\f* their Iudges are ſwalowed vp ioyned to the rocke. \f l \fr 140.6 \ft They ſhal then heare and vnderſtand, that the prayers of the Church\f* They shal heare my wordes \f m \fr 140.6 \ft are effectual, obtaining grace of conſtancie to her children, not to feare anie perſecution, nor anie kind of death ; obtayning alſo iuſtificatiô of their cauſe, vvhen the perſecuters shal ſee that the wordes, and doctrin of the Church are true, and preuaile in true iudgement, as true, modeſt, peaceable, not ſeditious, turbulent, nor againſt the commonwealth.\f* becauſe they haue preuailed:
\v 7 \f n \fr 140.7 \ft As much earth ſticking together is made fructful, by breaking it into ſmal mould, ſo the children of the Church by perſecution, bring forth more fruict then before. S. Aug.\f* as the groſſenes of the earth is broken out vpon the earth. Our \f o \fr 140.7 \ft Some perſecuters are ſo cruel as to rage againſt the bones, and other reliques of Martyrs,\f* bones are diſſipated \f p \fr 140.7 \ft caſting them into the vileſt places they can, willing if they could, to throw them into hel,\f* nere to hel:
\v 8 \f q \fr 140.8 \ft but the Church and al her members repoſe confidence in God.\f* for to thee ô Lord, Lord are mine eies; in thee haue I hoped, take not away my \f 1 \fr 140.8 \ft \+it life\+it*.\f* ſoule.
\v 9 Keepe me from \f r \fr 140.9 \ft Therefore ſhe prayeth that her children be not entrapped by guilful deceiptes,\f* the ſnare, which they haue ſet for me: and from \f s \fr 140.9 \ft nor ouerthrowne by anie ſtumbling blocke caſt in their way.\f* the ſcandals of them that worke iniquitie.
\v 10 \f t \fr 140.10 \ft In the end, al perſecuters and other wicked shal be caught in their owne nette of perdition,\f* Sinners shal fal in his net: \f v \fr 140.10 \ft the Church is ſingularly protected euen to the end.\f* I am alone vntil I paſſe.
\c 141
\cd Holie Dauid being fled into a caue, and beſeeged round about by Sauls armie, explicating his diſtreſſe, 6. prayeth to be deliuered: \f - \ft Dauids prayer in extreme diſtreſſe. The 8. key.\f*
\s1 The prophetical sense of this Psalme.
\ip Al this happened in figure of Christ, of vvhom prophetically S. Augustin S. Hilarie, Cassiams, Cassiodorus, and others expound the vvhole Psalme. Amongst others S. Beda briefly in these vvordes. VVheras in the title, Vnderstanding is premised to Prayer, therby is signified, that Dauid in his distresses, and in the denne whither he fled, vnderstood vvhat our Lord should suffer of the Ievves, and hovv he vvould pray to his Father. In the first part our Lord crieth to his Father, complaining of the detestable deceiptes of Iudas the persecutor. In the second he prayeth to be deliuered from the prison of hel (Limbus, vvhere he vvas free) because the faith of al the Sainctes depended on his Resurrection.
\p
\v 1 OF \f a \fr 141.1 \ft This Pſalme in forme of a prayer, ſhevveth vvhat cogitations Dauid had in extreme danger.\f* vnderſtanding to Dauid, when he was in the caue, a prayer. 1. Reg. 24. \x - \xt 1 Reg. 24.\x*
\v 2 VVIth my voice I \f b \fr 141.2 \ft Not with exterior voice, for ſo he should haue detected himſelfe, but with feruour of ſpirite.\f* haue cried to our Lord: with my voice I haue prayed to our Lord:
\v 3 I powre out my prayer in his ſight, and I pronounce my tribulation before him.
\v 4 \f c \fr 141.4 \ft VVhen by reaſon of extremitie, I was not able to thinke hovv to helpe my ſelfe,\f* When my ſpirit faileth of my ſelf, and \f d \fr 141.4 \ft thou ô God knovving my actions and demeanour, didſt deliuer me.\f* thou haſt knowne my pathes. In this way, which I walked, they hid a ſnare for me.
\v 5 I looked toward \f e \fr 141.5 \ft I looked for helpe :\f* the right hand, and \f f \fr 141.5 \ft and diligently looked about me,\f* ſaw: and \f g \fr 141.5 \ft but none would ſeme to know me, when I required their helpe.\f* there was none that would know me. \f h \fr 141.5 \ft Endeuoring to ſaue my ſelf by flight, I found no ſecure place, for being in a caue or hole of a montaine, the whole armie beſeeged me ; al ſeeke to take avvay my life, none to ſaue it.\f* Flight hath failed me: and there is none to require my ſoule.
\v 6 \f i \fr 141.6 \ft Thus left deſolate of al mans helpe, and deſtitute of al vvorldlie shift, I cried to thee ô Lord,\f* I haue cried to thee ô Lord, I haue ſayd: Thou art \f k \fr 141.6 \ft my only hopeful refuge,\f* my hope, \f l \fr 141.6 \ft neither do I deſire to liue for anie vvorldlie reſpect, but hauing choſen thee ô God for my portion, and inheritance,\f* my portion \f m \fr 141.6 \ft I deſire to be out of this deſert place, and to be in the land, vvhere is right vſe of religious diuine ſeruice.\f* in the land of the liuing.
\v 7 Attend to my petition: becauſe I am \f n \fr 141.7 \ft afflicted.\f* humbled exceedingly. Deliuer me from them that perſecute me: becauſe they are made ſtrong ouer me.
\v 8 Bring forth my ſoule out of priſon, \f o \fr 141.8 \ft Dauids deſire of libertie, was eſpecially to this end, that he might haue conuenient place, and other meanes to ſerue and praiſe God :\f* to confeſſe vnto thy name: \f p \fr 141.8 \ft the good, and wel diſpoſed people of Iſrael, can not now ſerue thee as they deſire, but expect me,\f* the iuſt expect me; \f q \fr 141.8 \ft whom thou ô Lord, vvilt aduance to the kingdom, that then vve may ſerue thee, more freely and more commodiouſly.\f* til thou reward me.
\c 142
\cd King Dauid (or anie other) in ſpiritual or temporal tribulation, not truſting in his owne iuſtice, layeth open his calamitie, 5. conſidering Gods benignitie, 6. prayeth to be ſpedely deliuered; 11. and confidently aſſureth himſelfe therof. \f - \ft The ſeuenth penitential Pſalme. the 7. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Pſalme of Dauid, when Abſalom his ſonne perſecuted him. 2. Reg. \x - \xt 2. Reg. 17.\x* LORD heare my prayer: with thine eares receiue my petition in \f a \fr 142.1 \ft God hauing ſo promiſed is bond by his truth,\f* thy truth: heare me in \f b \fr 142.1 \ft and his iuſtice, to heare penitents praying for remiſſion of ſinnes.\f* thy iuſtice.
\v 2 And \f c \fr 142.2 \ft Deale not vvith me in rigour of iuſtice,\f* enter not into iudgement with thy ſeruant: \f d \fr 142.2 \ft for no mortal man is able of himſelfe to be iuſtified, abſtracting from Gods mercie.\f* becauſe no man liuing shal be iuſtified in thy ſight.
\v 3 Becauſe the enemie \f e \fr 142.3 \ft ſought my life,\f* hath perſecuted my ſoule: he \f f \fr 142.3 \ft and brought it into great danger. Spiritually, the diuel as a roring lion ſtil ſeeking vvhom he may deuour, hath tempted me vehemently.\f* hath humbled my life in the earth. \f g \fr 142.3 \ft I am preſſed with great calamities, temporal or ſpiritual.\f* He hath ſet me in obſcure places as the dead of the world:
\v 4 and my ſpirit is in anguish vpon me, within me my hart is trubled.
\v 5 \f h \fr 142.5 \ft In this caſe I conſider, how God hath hertofore shewed his goodnes towards me, and others.\f* I was mindful of old dayes, I haue meditated in al thy workes: in the factes of thy handes did I meditate.
\v 6 I haue \f i \fr 142.6 \ft Stretching forth handes a ceremonie in prayer, wherby the ſupplicant is made more attentiue, and alſo indureth ſome paine for part of ſatisfaction.\f* ſtretched forth my handes to thee: my ſoule is \f k \fr 142.6 \ft mans mind vvithout Gods illumination is drie and barren.\f* as earth without water vnto thee.
\v 7 Heare me \f l \fr 142.7 \ft In great tentations Gods grace and helpe is more preſently nedeful to preuent our weaknes, leſt vve yeld conſent.\f* quickly ô Lord: my ſpirite hath faynted. Turne not away thy face from me: \f m \fr 142.7 \ft If God leaue man vvithout ſpecial and continual grace, he vvil fal,\f* and I shal be like to them that deſcend into \f n \fr 142.7 \ft into ſinne, as into a deepe lake, from vvhence vvithout helpe he can not riſe vp againe.\f* the lake.
\v 8 Make me heare thy mercie \f o \fr 142.8 \ft In the firſt aſſault of tentation.\f* in the morning: becauſe I haue hoped in thee. Make the way knowen to me, wherein I may walke: \f p \fr 142.8 \ft Seing by thy grace I haue begunne to pray vnto thee.\f* becauſe I haue lifted vp my ſoule to thee.
\v 9 Deliuer me from mine enemies ô Lord, to thee I haue fled:
\v 10 teach me to doe thy wil, becauſe thou art my God: Thy \f q \fr 142.10 \ft The penitent thus humbling himſelfe, and praying may aſſuredly truſt that God doth remitte his ſinnes by the holie Sacraments, and iuſtifieth him, protecteth him, and wil bring him out of al dangers of ſpiritual or temporal enemies.\f* good ſpirite wil conduct me into the right way:
\v 11 for thy name ſake ô Lord thou wilt quicken me, in thine equitie. Thou wilt bring forth my ſoule out of tribulation:
\v 12 and in thy mercie thou wilt deſtroy mine enemies. And thou wilt deſtroy al, that afflict my ſoule: becauſe I am thy ſeruant.
\c 143
\cd The royal Prophet thanketh God, for al his victories, and poſſeſſion of the kingdom. 3. Admiring Gods benignitie towards man, 5. prayeth to be ſtil defended from al enimies, 9. promiſeth a new ſongue of prayſe, 11. deſcribeth the vanitie of worldlie men, 15. concluding that true felicitie is in ſeruing God. \f - \ft King David praiſeth God for his victories. The 8. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 A Pſalme of Dauid, \f a \fr 143.1 \ft Becauſe this vvas Dauids firſt, and a very notable victorie, the Septuagint Interpreters make mention of Goliath in this title.\f* againſt Goliath. BLESSED be our Lord my God, who \f b \fr 143.1 \ft God made Dauid a warier, and victorer againſt Goliath, without anie former training in armes.\f* teacheth my handes to battel; and my fingers to warre.
\v 2 My mercie, and my refuge: my defender, and my deliuerer. My protectour, and I haue hoped in him, who \f c \fr 143.2 \ft Made him afterwards king of a great people.\f* ſubdeweth my people vnder me.
\v 3 Lord \f d \fr 143.3 \ft Al mankind was vnvvorthie before Chriſt,\f* what is man, that thou art \f e \fr 143.3 \ft that God should be reueled vnto them,\f* made knowne to him? or the ſonne of man; \f f \fr 143.3 \ft eſpecially that he ſhould haue care of the progenie of men, after their ſinne.\f* that thou eſtemeſt him?
\v 4 \f g \fr 143.4 \ft In dede man in himſelf, in his ovvne nature and frailtie, is but a vaine and tranſitorie creature, paſſing from life to death, as a shadovv that can not conſiſt of it ſelfe, neither can man vvithout God.\f* Man is made like to vanitie: his dayes paſſe as a shadow.
\v 5 \f h \fr 143.5 \ft By a poetical deſcription he prayeth for Gods helpe, as if God ſhould make the heauens to bovv, and ſo deſcend; or make the montaines ſmoke, as vvhen Moyſes receiued the lavv; or declare himſelf by Meteors, as folovveth:\f* Lord incline thy heauens, and deſcend: touch the mountaynes, and they wil ſmoke.
\v 6 Lighten lightening, and thou shalt diſperſe them: shoote out thine arrowes, and thou shalt deſtroy them.
\v 7 Send forth thy hand from on high, take me out, and deliuer me from manie \f i \fr 143.7 \ft Tribulations or tentations.\f* waters: from the hand \f k \fr 143.7 \ft Children of the Church,\f* of children \f l \fr 143.7 \ft but of euil life.\f* ſtrangers.
\v 8 Whoſe mouth hath ſpoken \f m \fr 143.8 \ft They ſpeake in vanitie that promiſe to kepe Gods law, and performe it not.\f* vanitie: and their right hand is the right hand of iniquitie.
\v 9 O God I wil ſing to thee a new ſong: in \f n \fr 143.9 \ft In that principal inſtrument, apt for a new ſongue, and for extraordinarie benefites.\f* the pſalter of ten ſtringes, I wil ſing to thee.
\v 10 Who geueſt ſaluation to kinges: who haſt redemed Dauid thy ſeruant from the malignant ſword:
\v 11 deliuer me, And reſcue me out of the hand of \f o \fr 143.11 \ft Both Ievves & Chriſtians that liue not vvel, are as ſtrangers that frame to themſelues ſuch a falſe felicite, as is here deſcribed : making riches or vvorldlie pleaſures their God.\f* children ſtrangers, whoſe mouth hath ſpoken vanitie: and their right hand, is the right hand of iniquitie.
\v 12 Whoſe ſonnes, are as new plantes in their youth. Their daughters comly trimmed: decked about after the ſimilitude of a temple.
\v 13 Their ſtorehouſes ful, flowing out of this into that. Their ewes ful of yong, abunding in their going forth:
\v 14 their oxen are fatte. There is no ruine of wal, nor \f 1 \fr 143.14 \ft \+it tranſmigration\+it*\f* paſſage, nor crie in their ſtreates.
\v 15 They haue ſaid, that it is a happie people, which hath theſe things: \f p \fr 143.15 \ft True happines conſiſteth not in vvorldlie things.\f* bleſſed is the people, whoſe \f q \fr 143.15 \ft But in preferring God before al.\f* God is our Lord.
\c 144
\cd God is, and for euer ough̄ to be praiſed, 3. for his immenſiue, infinite, glorious Maieſtie, meruelous workes, merciful benefites; for his powre, wiſdom, iuſtice, 19. who wil reward the good, and deſtroy the wicked. \f - \ft God's Majeſty excelleth all things. The 1. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 \f a \fr 144.1 \ft By this title, Eldras ſignifieth that the Holie Ghoſt, vvho indited al the Pſalmes to Gods praiſe, more ſpecially in theſe ſeuen laſt ſuggeſted to Dauid, and by him to al Gods ſeruants, that al their other ſeruice muſt tend, and be directed to the praiſe of God : and that therin vve muſt continevv, and finally reſt, as in the ſabbath of the ſeuenth day, ſignified (as S. Beda ſuppoſeth) by theſe ſeuen laſt Pſalmes of praiſe, eternally praiſing our Lord God. For vvhich principal end both Angels and Men, yea and al other creatures vvere made.\f* Prayſing, to Dauid himſelfe. I Wil exalt thee my God \f b \fr 144.1 \ft King, is the proper epitheton of Chriſt, the Sonne of God, to vvhom, in his humanitie, God the Father promiſed the Church of al nations for his kingdom. \+it Pſal. 2\+it*. in vvhom alſo the vvhole Bleſſed Trinitie is praiſed.\f* the king: and I wil bleſſe thy name \f c \fr 144.1 \ft Al the time of this vvorld they praiſe God,\f* for euer, and \f d \fr 144.1 \ft & after in eternitie.\f* for euer and euer.
\v 2 Euerie day wil I bleſſe thee: and wil praiſe thy name, for euer, and for euer and euer.
\v 3 Great is our Lord and exceding laudable, and of his greatnes there is no end.
\v 4 Generation and generation shal praiſe thy workes: and they shal pronounce thy powre.
\v 5 They shal ſpeake the magnificence of the glorie of thy holines: and shal tel thy meruelous workes.
\v 6 And they shal tel the force of thy \f e \fr 144.6 \ft Of vvonderful and miraculous thinges, vvhich ſtrike terrour into mens mindes.\f* terrible thinges: and shal declare thy greatnes:
\v 7 They shal vtter the memorie of the abundance of thy ſwetnes: and in thy iuſtice they shal reioyce.
\v 8 Our Lord is pitiful and merciful: patient and very merciful.
\v 9 Our Lord is ſweete to al: and his \f f \fr 144.9 \ft The effectes of Gods mercie in redeming, and recalling ſinners, are eminent aboue al other vvorkes.\f* commiſerations are ouer al his workes.
\v 10 Let al thy workes o Lord confeſſe to thee: and let thy \f g \fr 144.10 \ft Therfore the ſanctified haue ſpecial cauſe to praiſe God.\f* ſainctes bleſſe thee.
\v 11 They shal tel the glorie of thy kingdom: and shal ſpeake thy might.
\v 12 That they may make thy might knowne to the children of men: and the glorie of the magnificence of thy kingdom.
\v 13 Thy kingdom is a kingdom \f h \fr 144.13 \ft Chriſts kingdom the militant Church is ſignfical, but much more the triumphant vvhich is eternal.\f* of al worldes: and thy dominion in al generation and generation. Our Lord is faithful in al his wordes: and holie in al his workes.
\v 14 Our Lord \f i \fr 144.14 \ft God is readie of his part to lift vp al.\f* lifteth vp al that fal: and ſetteth vp al that are bruiſed.
\v 15 The eies of al hope in thee ô Lord: and thou geueſt their meate in time conuenient.
\v 16 Thou openeſt thy hand: and filleſt \f k \fr 144.16 \ft He geueth neceſſarie thinges to al liuing creatures, euen to brute beaſtes.\f* euerie liuing creature with bleſſing.
\v 17 Our Lord is iuſt in al his wayes: and holie in al his workes.
\v 18 Our Lord is neere to al that inuocate him: to al that inuocate him in truth.
\v 19 He wil doe the wil of them that feare him, and wil heare their prayer; and ſaue them.
\v 20 Our Lord keepeth al that loue him: and he wil deſtroy al ſinners.
\v 21 My mouth shal ſpeake the prayſe of our Lord: and let al flesh bleſſe his holie name for euer, and for euer and euer.
\c 145
\cd The Pſalmiſt exciteth himſelf, and al others to praiſe God for his ſingular regard, and prouidence of al that truſt in him: 3. shewing that neither princes, nor other men are able to helpe, 5. but God can and doth continually releue al ſortes of neceſſities. \f - \ft Al are exhorted to praiſe God and truſt in his aſſured prouidence. The 3. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia, \f a \fr 145.1 \ft The Septuagint added the names of theſe two Prophetes, for the like reaſon as they added Ieremie, \+it Pſal. 136\+it*. becauſe Aggæus and Zacharias prophecying in the reduction of the people from captiuitie, exhorted them, as here the Pſalmiſt doth, to truſt in Gods prouidence, and preferre his ſeruice before vvorldlie cares.\f* of Aggæus, and Zacharie.
\v 2 MY ſoule prayſe thou our Lord, I wil praiſe our Lord in my life: I wil ſing to my God as long as I shal be. Put not confidence in princes:
\v 3 in \f b \fr 145.3 \ft In one Sonne of Man [Chriſt] is ſaluation (ſaith S. Auguſtin) and in him, not becauſe he is the ſonne of man, but becauſe he is the Sonne of God.\f* the ſonnes of men, in whom there is no ſaluation.
\v 4 His ſpirit shal goe forth, and he shal returne into \f c \fr 145.4 \ft He ſaith not, that the ſpirit or ſoule shal turne into the earth, but the ſoule shal depart from the bodie, and ſo euerie one (in whom worldlie men put their truſt) shal according to the bodie, returne into his earth.\f* his earth: in that day al their cogitations shal perish.
\v 5 Bleſſed is he, whoſe helper is the God of Iacob, his hope in our Lord his God:
\v 6 who made heauen and earth, the ſea, and al thinges, that are in them.
\v 7 Which keepeth truth for euer, doth iudgement for them that ſuffer wrong: geueth foode to the hungrie. Our Lord looſeth the fettered:
\v 8 our Lord illuminateth the blind. Our Lord lifteth vp the bruiſed, our Lord loueth the iuſt.
\v 9 Our Lord keepeth ſtrangers, the pupil, and widow he wil receiue: and the wayes of ſinners he wil deſtroy.
\v 10 Our Lord wil reigne for euer thy God ô Sion, in generation and generation.
\c 146
\cd God is alſo to be praiſed by his peculiar people, for particular benefites. 4. & for his omnipotent powre, wiſdom, goodnes, in creating, and gouerning this whole world, 11. and moſt ſpecial benignitie towards thoſe that truſt in him. \f - \ft God's excellency in creating and governing the world. The 2. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. PRAYSE ye our Lord becauſe Pſalme \f a \fr 146.1 \ft It iſ good to ſing Pſalmes of praiſe to God.\f* is good: to our God let there be pleaſant, and comelie praiſe.
\v 2 Our Lord building vp Ieruſalem: \f b \fr 146.2 \ft A prophecie of the reſtauration of Ieruſalem after the captiuitie.\f* wil gather together the diſperſions of Iſrael.
\v 3 Who \f c \fr 146.3 \ft Remitteth ſinnes to the penitent.\f* healeth the contrite of hart: and bindeth vp their ſores.
\v 4 Who \f d \fr 146.4 \ft Beſides experience of euerie one, that shal behold the firmament in a clere night, the holie Scripture \+it (Gen. 15. v. 5\+it*.) ſheweth, that the ſtarres are innumerable to man. For albeit Ptolomæy and other Aſtronomers numbereth certaine more notorious ſtarres, which ſerue eſpecially for ſome knowlege in the ſcience of Aſtronomie, numbering \+it 349\+it*. ſuch in the Zodiach; \+it 316\+it*. in the South part therof; and \+it 360\+it*. on the North part, which are in al \+it 1025\+it*. Yet al acknowlege that no man can come nere to anie probable coniecture of the whole number, nor is able to attaine anie perfect knovvlege of their natural influences, and ſpecial proprietes. And therfore the Pſalmiſt propoſeth here the admirable, and vnſearchable knowlege of God : who both moſt exactly knovveth the number,\f* numbereth the multitude of ſtarres: and \f e \fr 146.4 \ft and ſo perfectly their nature, that his diuine Omniſcience geueth to euerie ſtarre a proper name, according to their ſingular differences and proprieties.\f* geueth names to them al.
\v 5 Great is our Lord, and great is his ſtrength: and of his \f f \fr 146.5 \ft Thinges ſubiect to Gods knowlege and wiſdom are innumerable.\f* wiſdom there is no number.
\v 6 Our Lord receiuing the meeke: & humbling ſinners euen to the ground.
\v 7 Sing ye to our Lord in confeſſion: ſing ye to our God on harpe.
\v 8 Who \f g \fr 146.8 \ft Al theſe and the like benefites do shew Gods incomparable greatnes, wiſdom, and goodnes.\f* couereth the heauen with cloudes: and prepareth rayne for the earth. Who bringeth forth graſſe in the mountaines: and herbe for the ſeruice of men.
\v 9 Who geueth to beaſtes their foode: and to \f h \fr 146.9 \ft Both ſacred and prophane auctors teſtifie, that rauens ſeing their yong ones, either without fethers, or to haue vvhitiſh, vnlike to theirs, as ſuſpecting that they are not their owne birdes, but of ſome other kinde, leaue them deſtitute of meate; therfore God the auctor of nature, and conſeruer of al kindes of creatures, by his ſpecial prouidence, feedeth them : either by a certaine dew hanging nere them in the ayre, as Iſidorus ſuppoſeth; or by litle beaſtes, or flees, ſent by Gods prouidence, vvhich they catching into their mouthes, are nouriſhed and brought vp, as S. Chryſoſtom teacheth, ſer. in Heliam; or by vvhat other meanes ſoeuer, al agree that yong rauens are neglected by their parents, and are fedde meruelouſly by Gods ordinance; by which example the Pſalmiſt ſhevveth, that much more God hath care of men : eſpecially of ſuch men (ſaith S. Chryſoſtom.) as honour him vvith hymnes and praiſes, whom alſo he hath called to be his peculiar people, and his ovvne portion or inheritance.\f* the young rauens that cal vpon him.
\v 10 He shal not haue pleaſure in the ſtrength of an horſe: nor in the legges of a man shal he be wel pleaſed.
\v 11 Our Lord is wel pleaſed toward them that feare him: and in them, that hope vpon his mercie.
\c 147
\cd Againe God is to be praiſed for his goodnes towards his peculiar people, 4. yea towards al the world: 7. but moſt abundantly tovvards his Church. \f - \ft God's providence eſpecially towards the Church. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. O \f a \fr 147.1 \ft Ieruſalem in the latter part of Dauids time, al the time of Salomon, and part of other kings reignes til the captiuitie, had peace & proſpered. Againe after the captiuitie, the Citie was repared, the Temple reedified, and the whole land receiued and enioyed manie bleſſinges. But al this was no more then a figure of the excellent benefites here prophecied, and more euidently verified in Chriſts Catholique Church : partly here militant in the whole world, and eſpecially in the glorious Ieruſalem, and Sion, the perfect viſion of peace, and contemplation of God, in eternal life.\f* Ieruſalem praiſe our Lord: praiſe thy God ô Sion. \f 1 \fr 147.1 \ft The Hebrews ioyne this Pſalme with the precedent.\f*
\v 2 Becauſe he \f b \fr 147.2 \ft In compariſon of other cities, and peoples of the world, the gates of Ieruſalem were ſtrongly fenſed,\f* hath ſtrengthened the lockes of thy gates: he \f c \fr 147.2 \ft and the citizens bleſſed : much more the Church of Chriſt is built vpon a ſure rock, her faithful children indued with al ſpiritual graces ; and moſt ofal, heauen it ſelfe is free from al danger of calamitie, and the Sainctes are moſt ſecure & moſt happie, enioying eternal fruition of God.\f* hath bleſſed thy children in thee.
\v 3 Who \f d \fr 147.3 \ft Hath geuen peace in thy borders,\f* hath ſet thy borders peace: and filleth thee with \f e \fr 147.3 \ft and the very beſt corne, and al other fruictes : wine, oyle, milke, honey, and the reſt. Allegorically in the Church, reconciliation with God, by remiſſion of ſinnes, and peace of conſcience, in the Sacraments of Baptiſme & Penance ; vvith the moſt ſpiritual food of Chriſts Bodie and Bloud in the Euchariſt, and graces of other Sacraments. In heauen moſt aſſured peace and ioy without end.\f* the fatte of corne.
\v 4 Who ſendeth forth \f f \fr 147.4 \ft This perteyneth moſt ſpecially to Euangelical doctrine, preached\f* his ſpeach to the earth: his word \f g \fr 147.4 \ft and quickely receiued in al the world. Rom 10.v.18.\f* runneth ſwiftly.
\v 5 Who geueth \f h \fr 147.5 \ft Snow nouriſheth the earth, making it vvarme by Antiperiſtaſis, as is euident in natural Philoſophie,\f* ſnow as vvool: ſcatereth \f i \fr 147.5 \ft and noyſome ayre is changed into clere weather. In the Church by penance & auſtere life men are purged from ſinnes and vices, euil ſpirites are alſo driuen avvay. If your ſinnes ſhal be as ſcarlet, they shal be made vvhite as ſnow, and if they be redde as vermilion, they ſhal be vvhite as vvool. \+it Iſaie. 1. v. 18\+it*.\f* miſt as ashes.
\v 6 He caſteth \f k \fr 147.6 \ft Yea ſome that are hardned in ſinne, as yſe, or chryſtal, shal be melted, broken, or made fitte to be ingraffed in Gods Church. So S. Peter was admonished by a viſion \+it (Act. 10. v. 13.)\+it* to kil and eate.\f* his cryſtal as morſeles: before the face of his cold \f l \fr 147.6 \ft Othervviſe without Gods grace geuing remorſe and ſorow, no man can ouercome his owne vices.\f* who shal endure?
\v 7 He \f m \fr 147.7 \ft But Gods word preached,\f* shal ſendforth his word, and shal melt them: \f n \fr 147.7 \ft and his grace touching mens hartes,\f* his ſpirit shal blowe, and \f o \fr 147.7 \ft innumerable are conuerted.\f* waters shal flowe.
\v 8 Who declareth his word to \f p \fr 147.8 \ft The Church only enioyeth theſe ſpiritual benefites.\f* Iacob: his iuſtices, and iudgements to Iſrael.
\v 9 He hath \f q \fr 147.9 \ft Conſidering that al mankind was in the maſſe of ſinne, and that God letteth manie iuſtly perish, thoſe to whom he geueth his grace to iuſtification, are ſpecially bond to praiſe him.\f* not done in like maner to anie nation: and his iudgements he hath not made manifeſt to them. \f r \fr 147.9 \ft And therfore the Prophet concludeth this Pſalme, and the reſt folovving, vvith Alleluia.\f* Allelu-ia.
\c 148
\cd Al creatures ſpiritual and corporal, are inuited to praiſe God, their Creator and Conſeruer, 13. as incomparably excellent. \f - \ft Our Creator to be praiſed by all creatures. The 2. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. PRAYSE ye our Lord \f a \fr 148.1 \ft Al ye heauenlie ſpirites praiſe God for the excellencie of your nature.\f* from the heauens: praiſe ye him in the high places.
\v 2 Prayſe ye him al his Angels: prayſe ye him \f b \fr 148.2 \ft And for your innumerable multitude.\f* al his hoſtes.
\v 3 Prayſe ye him \f c \fr 148.3 \ft Al creatures, wanting ſenſe or reaſon, shewforth the Maieſtie and excellencie of their Creator.\f* ſunne and moone: prayſe him al ye ſtarres, and light.
\v 4 Prayſe him ye heauens of heauens: and the waters that are aboue the heauens,
\v 5 let them praiſe the name of our Lord. Becauſe he ſayd, and they were made: he commanded, and they were created.
\v 6 He eſtablished them for euer, and for euer and euer: he put a precept, and it shal not paſſe.
\v 7 Prayſe our Lord from the earth: ye dragons, and al depthes.
\v 8 Fyre, haile, ſnow, yſe, ſpirit of ſtormes: which doe his worde:
\v 9 Mountaines, and al litle hilles: trees that beare fruite, and al ceders.
\v 10 Beaſtes, and al cattel: ſerpentes, and fethered fowles:
\v 11 \f d \fr 148.11 \ft Againe God is to be praiſed for the diuerſitie of ſtates in men, wherby the whole communitie, is conſerued & gouerned.\f* Kinges of the earth, and al peoples: princes, and al iudges of the earth.
\v 12 Yongmen, and virgins: old with yong let them prayſe the name of our Lord:
\v 13 becauſe the name of him \f e \fr 148.13 \ft God only, & no falſe imagined god made & diſpoſed al thinges in order.\f* alone is exalted.
\v 14 The confeſſion of him aboue heauen, and earth: and he hath exalted \f f \fr 148.14 \ft More eſpecially for that God hath ſo fortified his Church.\f* the horne of his people. An hymne to al his \f g \fr 148.14 \ft Sanctified children,\f* ſainctes: to the children of Iſrael, a people \f h \fr 148.14 \ft that by grace and free wil, which he geueth them, approch vnto him.\f* approching vnto him. \f i \fr 148.14 \ft Al this conſidered the Pſalmiſt concleding with Alleluia, inuiteth al to praiſe our Lord.\f* Alleluia.
\c 149
\cd The Church is moſt ſingularly bond to praiſe God, 4. for the grace, ſanctitie, victorie, glorie, 7. and iudicial powre, which he geueth to his Sainctes. \f - \ft The Church muſt euer praiſe God. The 6. key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. SING ye to our Lord a new ſong: let his praiſe be in \f a \fr 149.1 \ft God our Lord whom al creatures are bond to praiſe, only accepteth thoſe mens praiſes, that liue in his holie Church.\f* the Church of ſainctes.
\v 2 Let Iſrael be ioyful in him, that made him: and let the children of Sion reioyce in their king.
\v 3 Let them praiſe his name in quire: on tymbrel, and pſalter let them ſing to him:
\v 4 Becauſe our Lord is wel pleaſed in his people: and he wil exalt the meeke vnto ſaluation.
\v 5 The ſainctes shal reioyce in glorie: they shal be ioyful in \f b \fr 149.5 \ft eternal reſt.\f* their beddes.
\v 6 The \f c \fr 149.6 \ft Hiegheſt praiſes of God shal be continually vttered by Sainctes in glorie : for ſtil as praiſes paſſe by their tongues and mouthes, more like praiſes ſhal ſuccede from their throte, and hartie affection, ſo out of the abundance of the hart their mouth shal ſtil ſpeake Gods praiſe.\f* exaltations of God in their throte: and \f d \fr 149.6 \ft Glorified Sainctes shal alſo haue iudiciarie povvre. Firſt, al in general shal like and approue Gods iuſtice in punishing the vvicked. Secondly the vvicked ſhal be iuſtly condemned in compariſon of the bleſſed, vvho paſſed through, and ouercame the like, yea and greater tribulations and tentations then thoſe, by vvhich the damned vvere ouercome. Thirdly, certaine more excellent Sainctes, namely the Apoſtles. and al thoſe that forſaking proprietie of temporal goodes, geue that they haue to the poore, as ſome religious Orders doe ; or into a Communitie as the Apoſtles, and manie primitiue Chriſtians did \+it (Mat. 4. v. 20. cap. 19. v. 27. Act. 4. v. 34. 1. Cor. 6. v. 3\+it*.) shal ſitte in iudgement ſeates, aſſeſſorie iudges with Chriſt, and iudge thoſe that render account, of wel or euil ſpending the temporal landes or goodes, which they poſſeſſed in this vvorld. So teach S. Auguſtin S. Ierom. S. Beda, and others.\f* two edged ſwordes in their handes.
\v 7 To doe reuenge in the nations: chaſtiſements among the peoples.
\v 8 To binde their kinges in fetters: and their nobles in yron manicles.
\v 9 That they may doe in them the iudgement that is \f e \fr 149.9 \ft Decreed by God.\f* written: \f f \fr 149.9 \ft This iudicial povvre is part of Sainctes glorie.\f* this glorie is to al his ſainctes. Alleluia.
\c 150
\cd God abſolutly moſt excellent is to be praiſed, 3. with al ſortes of inſtruments, and by al other meanes. \f - \ft God moſt excellent and moſt laudable. The firſt key.\f*
\p
\v 1 Alleluia. PRAYSE ye our Lord \f a \fr 150.1 \ft Al ye Angels and men that are in the holie and higheſt heauen praiſe our Lord.\f* in his holies: prayſe ye him in \f b \fr 150.1 \ft Al ye creatures that are in, and vnder the firſt moueable firmament praiſe our Lord.\f* the firmament of his ſtrength.
\v 2 Prayſe ye him in \f c \fr 150.2 \ft And you eſpecially (Gods peculiar people) amongſt whom, and for whom, diuine miracles haue bene wrought praiſe our Lord,\f* his powers: prayſe ye him \f d \fr 150.2 \ft with al your poſſible endeuoure, for though his infinite Excellencie exeedeth the powre of al creatures to praiſe him ſufficiently, yet it reſteth that you may infinitly extend your wil and deſire to praiſe our Lord, according to the multitude of his greatnes.\f* according to the multitude of his greatnes.
\v 3 \f e \fr 150.3 \ft Out of this your great and infinite deſire, let your tongues ſound and ſing diuine praiſes, as wel vvith voice, as muſical inſtruments.\f* Prayſe ye him \f f \fr 150.3 \ft VVherof ſix moſt vſual in the Tabernacle and Temple vvere theſe : Trumpet, Pſalter, Harpe, Timbrel, Organ and Cymbal.\f* in the ſound of trumpet: prayſe ye him on pſalter, and harpe.
\v 4 Prayſe ye him on timbrel and \f g \fr 150.4 \ft By the vvay the Pſalmiſt interpoſeth agane tvvo eſpecial thinges, vvhich make perfect harmonie, vvithout vvhich no inſtrument is gratful to God : Vnitie amongſt his ſeruants, ſignified by the Quire of conſonant voices :\f* quire: prayſe ye him on \f h \fr 150.4 \ft and mortification of paſſions, ſignified by Stringes, vvhich are made of dead beaſtes bovvels.\f* ſtringes, and organ.
\v 5 Prayſe ye him on wel ſounding cymbals: prayſe ye him on cymbales of iubilation:
\v 6 let euerie \f i \fr 150.6 \ft Man created of corruptible bodie and immortal ſoule, is finally admonished to praiſe our Lord, ouer and aboue the praiſes of al other corporal creatures ; vvho alſo is more eſpecially bond therto then Angels, becauſe God hath voutſaffed to make himſelfe Man, to redeme man that vvas loſt by ſinne, and to endew him vvith nevv grace, and ſo bring him to euerlaſting glorie, vvhere vvith holie Angels, men alſo for euer & euer ſhal praiſe our Lord, vvith hart, voice, and iubilation of ſpirite, ſinging as the Pſalmiſt concludeth, Alleluia.\f* ſpirit praiſe our Lord. Alleluia.
\s1 S. Augustin in the conclusion of his Enarrations upon the Psalms.
\ip S. Augustin in the conclusion of his Enarrations or Sermons upon the Psalms, explicateth a mystery in the number of an hundred and fifty, signifying the concord of the two Testaments. For in the old testament they kept the Sabbath, which is the seventh day: in \f - \ft The number of Psalms signifieth the agreement of the old and new Testament.\f* the new we keep our Lord's day, after the sabbath, that is, the eighth: which seven and eight (making fifteen) multiplied by ten, signifying the Law of ten commandments, rise unto \it 150.\it*
\ip Again, seven multiplied by seven make \it 49.\it* whereto one (to wit the eight) being added make fifty, which multiplied by three , signifying the B. Trinity, make \it 150.\it* Neither \f - \ft Three fifties signify Penance. Mercy with justice, and Praises of God.\f* seemed it without cause to this great Doctor, that the first fifty end with a Psalm of Penance, craving mercy & remission of sins: the second with Mercy & Justice, which God joineth in the Redemption, Justification, and Salvation of men: the last with Divine Praises, signifying, that by condemning sins in ourselves, through God's mercy we may be justified, and so begin in this life, which is to be perfected in the next, to praise our Lord, as S. Paul admonisheth with Psalms, Hymns, and Spiritual Songs. Concluding with the two verses, appointed by S. Damasus Pope, to be added in the end of all Psalms, and is observed ever since his time by tradition in the whole Church. \f - \ft \+it Colloss. 3. Circa An. Do 380.\+it*\f*
\ip Glory to the Father, and to the Son, and to the Holy Ghost: As it was in the beginning, and now, and ever, into worlds of worlds. \it (in eternity without end.)\it* Amen. \f - \ft Gloria Patri added by tradition.\f*
